    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Administration - A Guide for Unsecured Creditors</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bankruptcy - A guide for Unsecured Creditors</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Compulsory Liquidation - A Guide for Unsecured Creditors</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Creditors Voluntary Liquidation - A Guide for Unsecured Creditors</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Is a Voluntary Arrangement Right for me?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  "/legal/": {
    "id": 49042,
    "slug": "legal",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Information Required by the Provision of Services Regulations 2009</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Complaints</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You can view our full complaints procedure&nbsp;here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Contact Details</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt Ltd<br>Langley House,<br>Park Road,<br>London N2 8EY<br>England &amp; Wales</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tel: 0800 074 6757</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Email: info@companydebt.com</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are a limited company registered under number &nbsp;06352368.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our VAT registration number is 124 7192 23</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Our Services</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are licensed insolvency practitioners. You can access an overview of the services that we provide&nbsp;here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Professional Indemnity Insurance</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have professional indemnity insurance and are fully covered by Nexus Underwriting Ltd]. Their contact address is 150 Ledenhall Street, London, EC3V 4QT. This insurance governs advice provided in the territories of England &amp; Wales.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  "/complaints-procedure/": {
    "id": 49024,
    "slug": "complaints-procedure",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>General Insolvency Services</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have set out our complaints procedure below. Hopefully, before taking your complaint further you will attempt to resolve it internally with ourselves.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":60725,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Complaints-Procedure-1024x341.jpg\" alt=\"Complaints Procedure\n\" class=\"wp-image-60725\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If we have still not been able to resolve your concerns, you can complain to the Insolvency Practitioners Association at any time through the Insolvency Services\u2019 website&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/complain-about-insolvency-practitioner\">here</a>. They will assess your complaint and forward it to the Recognised Professional Body (RPB) we are registered with.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your complaint relates to a service that has been provided in relation to your personal financial circumstances, as we are the holder of a Full&nbsp;Permission from the Financial Conduct Authority (Full&nbsp;Permission number: 678781), your best course of action is to make your complaint directly to the Financial Ombudsman Service&nbsp;<a href=\"http://www.financial-ombudsman.org.uk/\">here</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Our Step by Step Complaints Procedure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Step 1</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As a first step, please address your complaint in writing to the Insolvency Practitioner(s) appointed on the matter. We will acknowledge receipt of your complaint in writing and respond in writing within a period of 21 days from receipt. This period of time allows us to conduct an in depth investigation of what has transpired.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once you have received our reply, if you do not consider that your complaint has been dealt with adequately or fully, or where you do not wish to complain to the Insolvency Practitioner(s) you've been dealing with directly, please follow step 2.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Step 2</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Please write to a partner who has not been dealing with your case hitherto. We will acknowledge receipt of your complaint in writing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At this level, we commit to responing n writing to all complaints at this level within a period of 28 days of receipt. This period of time allows for a further investigation of the matters that have been raised (where appropriate).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Step 3</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Should we not be able to resolve the situation to your satisfaction, you are entitled to complain to either the Insolvency Complaints Gateway or Financial Ombudsmen as outlined above.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You are also entitled to try and resolve the complaint or claim through the EU Online Dispute Resolution website&nbsp;<a href=\"http://ec.europa.eu/index_en.htm\">here</a>. You can use this platform to try and resolve a dispute arising from services or advice we have provided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/creditors-guides-to-insolvency-practitioners-fees/": {
    "id": 48170,
    "slug": "creditors-guides-to-insolvency-practitioners-fees",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<p>You are a trade creditor owed \u00a342,000 by a company that went into liquidation in March. Six months later, the IP\u2019s progress report lands in your inbox. Asset realisations of \u00a358,000. Office-holder remuneration of \u00a331,400. Disbursements of \u00a34,200. Estimated dividend to unsecured creditors: 4p in the pound.</p>\n<!-- /wp:post-content -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your share of the dividend, before you spend a minute on the file, is \u00a31,680. The fees consumed more than half the realised value of the estate, and the SIP 9 disclosure that arrived with the original notice was four pages of generalities about hourly rates. You wonder, fairly, whether anyone is checking.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That checking is your job, not the regulator\u2019s. We see this exact arithmetic on cases we triage every week, and the gap between what creditors are entitled to do about IP fees and what creditors actually do about them is the editorial point of this guide.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Insolvency Practitioner Fees at a Glance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Quick Answer: How IP Fees Are Set and Approved</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Insolvency practitioner fees in UK liquidations and administrations are set on one of three bases (time-cost, fixed, or percentage of asset realisations), disclosed up front under Statement of Insolvency Practice 9 (SIP 9), and approved by creditors at a decision procedure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where creditors are dissatisfied, the formal challenge route is an application to court under rule 18.34 of the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, within eight weeks of the fee decision being notified to us.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who Approves Insolvency Practitioner Fees</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In a Creditors\u2019 Voluntary Liquidation, the body of unsecured creditors approves the fee basis and quantum at the decision procedure, by simple majority in value of those voting. Where a liquidation committee has been formed, the committee takes that role on behalf of creditors. In administrations, the same logic applies but secured creditors weigh in earlier.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If neither creditors nor a committee approve, the IP must apply to the court for the basis to be fixed. That happens less often than you would expect, because most IPs work hard to get a fee resolution passed at the first decision rather than carry court-fixing costs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Main Creditor Right Around IP Fees</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The right that matters is the right to challenge. Under rule 18.34, any creditor (regardless of debt size) can apply to court to vary the basis or amount of the office-holder\u2019s remuneration if it appears to be excessive. The right is real and it is statutory.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The honest editorial line we take is that the right is also, for most small creditors, economically unusable. Court fees, solicitor time, and the IP\u2019s own costs of defending the application come out of the estate before any reduction lands. Below a certain debt size, in our experience, the maths kills the challenge before it starts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to Do Next If You Disagree with IP Fees</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Read the SIP 9 disclosure first, before you read the progress report. Calculate the fees as a percentage of realised assets and as a percentage of your expected dividend. If those two figures look wrong, the next step is to write to the IP requesting more detail rather than going straight to court.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the response is thin, the practical lever is to coordinate with other creditors before the next progress report. Coordinated objection at a fee resolution costs nothing and changes outcomes more often than rule 18.34 ever does.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Are Insolvency Practitioner Fees?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Time-Cost Basis (the Most Common Method)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On a time-cost basis, the IP charges by hours worked at published charge-out rates, with each grade of staff (partner, manager, senior, administrator) priced separately. A partner rate of \u00a3475 per hour and an administrator rate of \u00a3140 per hour is typical for a mid-sized firm. The fee is the sum of hours multiplied by rates, drawn against the estate as work is done.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Time-cost is the default in complex cases (administrations, contested liquidations, anywhere with antecedent transaction work) because nobody knows in advance how many hours the case will eat.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is also the basis most likely to drift if creditors are not paying attention. A case that estimates \u00a335,000 of fees can run to \u00a355,000 across two progress reports without any individual entry looking unreasonable. We see that pattern often enough that it shapes our default scrutiny.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Fixed-Fee Basis</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A fixed fee is a single quoted sum for the whole of the office-holder\u2019s work, sometimes split into stages (pre-appointment, appointment to first dividend, final). It is most common in straightforward CVLs where the asset position is clear and the IP is confident the case will not throw up surprises.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From a creditor\u2019s point of view, fixed fees are the easiest to assess. You know the total before you vote, and the IP carries the risk of overrun. The trade-off is that an experienced IP only quotes a fixed fee on cases they know will not exceed it, so the quote tends to be priced for comfort rather than for the median case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Percentage of Asset Realisations Basis</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On this basis the IP takes a fixed percentage of what they realise from the estate. Typical scales are higher percentages on the first slice (often 20% to 25% of realisations up to a threshold) and lower percentages thereafter. It is the natural choice for cases dominated by recoveries from book debts, plant and machinery sales, or property realisations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The alignment is good in theory: the IP is rewarded for getting more in. The problem in practice is that on small estates, where the percentage produces a fee that does not cover the IP\u2019s actual time, you sometimes see a hybrid resolution that mixes percentages with a time-cost top-up, and that hybrid is harder for creditors to scrutinise than either method alone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How IP Fees Are Set and Disclosed (The SIP 9 Standard)</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">SIP 9: What the IP Must Disclose Up Front</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Statement of Insolvency Practice 9, issued by the Recognised Professional Bodies through R3, is the regulatory transparency standard for IP remuneration.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before creditors are asked to approve fees, the IP must disclose the proposed basis, an estimate of the work to be done, the expected fee in money terms, and (on time-cost cases) the charge-out rates for every grade of staff who will work on the file.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The disclosure must also explain what creditors will get if the work goes ahead, in cash and as a likely dividend. SIP 9 is not optional; it is the standard the IP\u2019s authorising body audits against, and a thin SIP 9 disclosure is the single clearest signal that the fees are worth a closer look.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Time-Cost Schedules and Hourly Rates</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On a time-cost case, the SIP 9 schedule must show every charge-out rate, the estimated hours by grade, and a narrative explanation of what the work involves.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A good schedule reads like a project plan: investigation of antecedent transactions, statutory reporting, asset realisation, distribution, closure. A weak one collapses everything into a one-line \"case administration\" entry that says nothing about which grade of staff is doing the work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are reading the schedule as a creditor, the question to ask is not \"is the rate too high\" (rates broadly track firm size and location) but \"is the partner rate being charged for work that should sit with a senior or an administrator.\" Creep at the partner level is where time-cost cases lose proportion fastest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Estimated Fees vs Actual Drawn Fees</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>SIP 9 requires a fee estimate at the outset and a comparison of actual drawn fees against that estimate at every progress report. Where the actual exceeds the estimate, the IP must explain why and, in many cases, must seek further creditor approval before drawing the additional sum.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the disclosure where small problems become visible. A 10% overrun explained by an unforeseen book-debt dispute is normal. A 40% overrun explained as \"increased complexity\" without specifics is the moment to write back asking what the increased complexity actually was.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The progress report is the document SIP 9 polices most directly, and creditors who do not read it forfeit the protection it was written to give. Our experience is that the cases where fees end badly almost always trace back to a progress report nobody on the creditor side opened.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Creditors Approve Insolvency Practitioner Fees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Resolution at Decision Procedure (Approval Threshold)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fee resolution is a separate vote at the <a href=\"/insolvency/creditors-meeting/\">creditors\u2019 decision procedure</a>, taken alongside the appointment resolution. It passes on a simple majority in value of the unsecured creditors voting. The chair, normally the IP, admits or rejects claims for voting purposes and records the result on the chairperson\u2019s report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One practical point creditors miss: the fee resolution can be voted on separately from the appointment. You can confirm the IP and reject the proposed basis, or approve the basis and ask for a different fee cap, or reserve the question for a creditors\u2019 committee. Many creditors treat the package as all-or-nothing; it is not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Liquidation Committee Approval (Where One Exists)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A liquidation committee is a small group of creditors (three to five) elected to oversee the IP between formal decisions. Where a committee exists, fee approval is the committee\u2019s job rather than the general body of creditors\u2019. The committee meets, asks questions, and votes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Committees are the most effective fee-control mechanism the rules provide, and they are the most under-used. If your debt is large enough to justify the time and you trust two or three other creditors, requisitioning a committee at the first decision is the lowest-cost way to keep meaningful control over the case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When the Court Sets the Fees</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If neither the creditors nor a committee approves, the IP applies to court for the basis to be fixed under rule 18.23. The court applies a reasonableness test against the work done, the value and nature of the property dealt with, and the complexity of the case. Court-fixed fees are the exception, and the cost of the application is itself an expense of the estate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From a creditor\u2019s perspective, the court route is rarely the one creditors trigger. It is the route an IP triggers when creditor engagement is so low they cannot get any resolution passed. If you are a creditor watching that happen, it is a signal that creditor coordination has failed and the case is drifting, not a sign that the court will protect the dividend for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Creditors Can Challenge IP Fees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Application Under Insolvency Rule 18.34</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rule 18.34 of the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 lets any creditor (or member, in solvent liquidations) apply to court to vary the basis or amount of remuneration where it is excessive. The application is to the court that has jurisdiction over the insolvency, supported by a witness statement explaining why the fees are excessive and what reduction is sought.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The court can reduce the fee, vary the basis, or fix a different sum. Successful challenges are rare in reported cases because the court applies the same reasonableness test as on a fee-fixing application: the work actually required, the complexity, the value at stake. A challenge based on \"the fees feel high\" rather than on a costed alternative analysis tends to fail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Time Limit for Challenge</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The deadline is eight weeks from the date the relevant fee determination is sent to the creditor. Miss the eight weeks and the right is gone for that period of fees, although fresh fees notified later carry their own eight-week window. Diary the date the moment the progress report arrives; do not assume you can challenge later when the dividend is finalised.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eight-week clock is one of the practical reasons creditors who only react when the final dividend lands have already lost the right to do anything about the fees that produced it. SIP 9 disclosure is what triggers the clock, which is why it is worth reading on the day it arrives, not three months later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Grounds That Tend to Succeed (and Those That Don\u2019t)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grounds that have produced reductions in reported challenges include partner-level work being charged when administrator-level work was the appropriate grade, fees that exceed the SIP 9 estimate without a documented explanation, time entries with no narrative beyond \"case administration\", and disbursements charged at firm cost-recharge rates without disclosure that they include a margin.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grounds that fail are usually about quantum without analysis: \"the fees are too high relative to the dividend\" without a comparable case benchmark, or \"we expected more recovery\" without evidence the IP failed to pursue an asset. The court is interested in process and proportion, not in your disappointment with the outcome.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When It Is Actually Worth Challenging IP Fees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Calculate Fees as a Percentage of Expected Dividend Before Deciding</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first calculation we run is fees over expected dividend, not fees over realisations. If the IP\u2019s remuneration is consuming more than 30% of the cash that would otherwise reach unsecured creditors, the fees are, in our view, at least worth questioning. Below 15%, the cost of challenge almost never recovers itself for us.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The crude rule we apply when triaging cases is this: a \u00a340,000 unsecured creditor in an estate where total fees are \u00a330,000 against \u00a345,000 of realisations is paying around \u00a36,000 of fees out of their pocket through dividend dilution. A 20% reduction in fees, if achievable, recovers around \u00a31,200. That number sets the ceiling on what the challenge can rationally cost.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Audit the SIP 9 Disclosure for Hourly-Rate Creep</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Open the SIP 9 schedule and check three things: how many partner hours appear, what those hours were spent on, and whether the same task could plausibly have been done by a senior or administrator.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the partner column is weighted heavily towards routine statutory work (writing the progress report, preparing notices, filing returns) rather than antecedent transaction analysis or contentious creditor work, that is the hourly-rate creep the rules are designed to expose.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This audit costs nothing, takes around an hour for a competent finance person, and is the basis a court would expect a serious challenge to begin from. Without it, you are arguing on impression rather than on evidence, and that is the version of the case we routinely watch fail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Coordinate with Other Creditors If Combined Debt Exceeds 25%</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A single small creditor challenging fees alone is the worst-positioned challenger. A coordinated group representing more than a quarter of the unsecured debt by value can call a creditors\u2019 meeting under the rules, requisition a committee, and table a fresh fee resolution without going near rule 18.34. That is the cheaper, faster, more effective path nine times out of ten.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are sitting at \u00a342,000 in an estate of \u00a3600,000 of unsecured claims, ring the next two largest creditors before you write to your solicitor. The conversation we recommend is short: do you agree the fees look high, are you willing to put your debt behind a coordinated objection, and which of us writes to the IP first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>See our note on <a href=\"/insolvency/insolvent-company-owes-me-money/\">recovering from an insolvent company</a> for the wider playbook on creditor coordination.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Mistakes Creditors Make About IP Fees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Treating the First Notice as the Last Chance to Engage</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Many creditors read the first decision procedure notice, register the headline fee figure, and then file the rest of the correspondence unread. The fee resolution is rarely the moment the case actually moves; it is the progress reports six and twelve months later, where actual drawn fees are reconciled against the estimate, that you have the cleanest evidence base for an objection.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you only ever look at the first notice, you have no idea whether the case stayed inside the estimate or quietly broke through it. The progress report is where SIP 9 does most of its work, and creditors who skip it are choosing not to use the protection.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Confusing Disbursements with Remuneration</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disbursements are payments the IP makes on behalf of the estate (statutory advertising, agents\u2019 fees, search fees, sometimes specific solicitor costs). They are not part of the IP\u2019s remuneration.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Category 1 disbursements (direct external costs with no margin) do not need separate creditor approval; Category 2 disbursements (recharges that include an internal cost element such as travel or photocopying) do.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mistake we see is creditors lumping disbursements and remuneration together when calculating the fee burden, which produces an inflated number, undermines the objection, and makes the challenge easier for the IP to defend. Read the SIP 9 disclosure as it is written. Disbursements deserve their own scrutiny, but a different one from remuneration.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related IP Fee Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/creditors-meeting/\">Guide to Creditors\u2019 Meetings in Insolvency</a>: the decision procedure where the fee resolution is voted on.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/find-a-liquidator-near-me/\">Find a Liquidator Near Me</a>: how creditors and directors choose which IP to instruct in the first place.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-an-insolvency-practitioner/\">What Is an Insolvency Practitioner?</a>: the role and regulation of the office-holder whose fees you are scrutinising.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/preferential-non-preferential-creditors/\">Preferential and Non-Preferential Creditors</a>: where unsecured creditors sit in the distribution after fees and disbursements come out.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions About IP Fees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"4d2e9f01-7a6b-4c12-9e3a-5d8f6b1c2e7a\",\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e1. What is SIP 9 and why does it matter to creditors?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Statement of Insolvency Practice 9, issued by R3 on behalf of the Recognised Professional Bodies, is the disclosure standard every IP must follow when seeking fee approval. It requires the IP to set out the basis, the estimated work, hourly rates by grade, and the expected fee in money terms before creditors vote. It is the transparency layer that makes informed creditor approval possible.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e2. How are IP fees actually paid?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Fees are drawn from the asset realisations of the insolvent estate, ahead of the dividend to unsecured creditors but after fixed-charge holders are paid out of their security. They are not paid by the directors personally, except in the limited case where directors have funded the appointment with a private contribution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e3. Can creditors negotiate the fee basis before approval?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. The fee resolution is a vote you can amend or counter-propose. A creditor or group of creditors can table an alternative fee resolution (a different basis, a different cap, a fixed fee instead of time-cost) and ask the chair to put it to the vote. The IP cannot refuse to take the alternative resolution; the creditors decide.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e4. What happens if the IP exceeds the fee estimate?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>SIP 9 requires the IP to disclose the overrun in the next progress report and explain why. If the original resolution was for a capped time-cost fee, the IP must seek further creditor approval before drawing above the cap. If the resolution was uncapped, the overrun is permitted but visible, and creditors can challenge the additional fees within eight weeks of the disclosure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e5. Is there a typical range for IP fees in a small CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>For a straightforward CVL with limited assets and no contentious work, fixed fees of \u00a33,500 are common. Time-cost cases of similar complexity often run to \u00a38,000 to \u00a315,000 once all stages are done. Larger or contested cases, with antecedent transaction work, can reach \u00a340,000 or more without anything looking unreasonable on the schedule.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e6. How do creditors form a liquidation committee?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A committee is requested at the decision procedure, by a creditor proposing the formation and seconding from the floor. Membership is normally three to five elected creditors. Once formed, the committee takes over fee approval, can require additional information from the IP, and meets on a schedule the committee itself sets.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e7. What is the deadline to challenge IP fees in court?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Eight weeks from the date the fee determination is sent to the creditor under rule 18.34 of the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016. Each fresh fee notification carries its own eight-week window, so the right does not disappear permanently when the first window closes; it disappears for that period of fees.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":7,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e8. Can a creditor see the IP\u2019s detailed time records?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, where the request is reasonable. A creditor can ask the IP for further information about the fees under rule 18.9, and the IP must respond within fourteen days or apply to court if they consider the request unreasonable. Detailed time records are often the most useful disclosure for assessing whether partner-level work was being done at administrator-level cost or vice versa.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":8,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e9. Do disbursements need separate creditor approval?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Category 1 disbursements (direct external costs paid to third parties with no internal margin) do not. Category 2 disbursements (recharges that include an internal cost allocation, such as mileage, photocopying, or shared firm overheads attributed to the file) do require separate approval and must be disclosed to creditors as such under SIP 9.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":9,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e10. What happens if creditors complain about the IP rather than the fees?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Conduct complaints go to the IP\u2019s authorising body (the IPA, ICAEW, or another Recognised Professional Body) through the Insolvency Service\u2019s complaints gateway. That route is separate from the rule 18.34 fee challenge, and conduct complaints can be made even after the eight-week fee challenge window has closed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":10,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e11. Are IP fees the same in administration as in liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The framework is the same, but the approval body changes. In administrations with a likely return to secured or preferential creditors only, the secured (or preferential) creditors approve the fees rather than the unsecureds. In administrations where unsecureds will receive a dividend, the fee approval logic mirrors the CVL pattern.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":11,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e12. Can creditors recover their costs if a fee challenge succeeds?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Sometimes. Where the court finds the fees were excessive, the creditor\u2019s costs of the application can be ordered against the estate or, in unusual cases, against the office-holder personally. The default expectation is that the costs come out of the estate, which is why coordinated challenges by larger creditor groups have a better cost-benefit profile than individual small-creditor applications.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/how-much-does-liquidation-cost/\">How Much Does Liquidation Cost?</a>: what a CVL actually costs and what the fixed fee covers.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: the standard route for an insolvent company placed into liquidation.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/secured-vs-unsecured-creditors/\">Secured and Unsecured Creditors</a>: who gets paid first, and where IP fees sit in the order.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/cant-afford-to-liquidate/\">Can't Afford to Liquidate?</a>: how to fund a liquidation when there is no cash left in the company.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"methodology-block\">\n<p><strong>Methodology &amp; Disclosure.</strong> The fee-structure descriptions and SIP 9 framing on this page are checked against the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 (Part 18, remuneration) and SIP 9 (Statement of Insolvency Practice 9, R3, RPB-issued) as at the last-reviewed date. Editorial review by Christopher Andersen, ICAEW-licensed insolvency practitioner since 2014.</p>\n<p>The judgement most likely to be challenged in this article is our editorial line that rule 18.34 challenges are economically unusable for creditors below a certain debt size.</p>\n<p>We have left it because the arithmetic of court fees, solicitor time, and the IP\u2019s defended-application costs against the realistic dividend recovery on a small unsecured claim does not work, and we would rather say so than mis-sell a remedy. Larger creditors and coordinated groups remain a different story for us.</p>\n<p>The cost-benefit framing above is the working we show small creditors who ask about rule 18.34 in the wrong economic situation. Softer descriptions elsewhere on the internet do not show that working, which is why we are showing it here.</p>\n</aside>\n\n<aside class=\"sources-block\">\n<p><strong>Sources &amp; References.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents\">Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, Part 18 (remuneration of office-holders), in particular rules 18.16 to 18.38 covering fee bases, approval, fee estimates, requests for information, and challenge by creditors</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">Insolvency Act 1986, sections 36 (remuneration of receivers), 115 (expenses of voluntary winding up), and 176ZA (expenses ranking) on the statutory framework for office-holder remuneration</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.r3.org.uk/technical-library/england-wales/sips/\">Statement of Insolvency Practice 9 (SIP 9): Payments to Insolvency Office Holders and their Associates, issued by R3 on behalf of the Recognised Professional Bodies. The transparency standard every IP must follow when seeking and reporting on remuneration</a> - r3.org.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/complain-about-insolvency-practitioner\">Insolvency Service guidance for creditors on insolvency practitioner fees and complaints</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/insolvency-practitioners-recognised-professional-bodies/recognised-professional-bodies\">Recognised Professional Bodies (ICAEW, IPA, ACCA) regulatory guidance on the chairing of decisions, conflicts of interest, and the auditing of SIP 9 disclosures</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/insolvency-act-1986/": {
    "id": 47772,
    "slug": "insolvency-act-1986",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Insolvency Act 1986 is the statute that tells you what happens when a UK company, or an individual, cannot pay its debts. It consolidated nearly a century of scattered law, the Bankruptcy Act 1914, the Companies Act 1948 winding-up provisions, the reforms proposed by the Cork Report of 1982, into a single working framework.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are a director whose company is drifting toward insolvency, almost every consequence you are worried about has its source in this Act: wrongful trading liability, compulsory winding-up, the moratorium in administration, the avoidance of transactions at undervalue, the ranking of creditors in the distribution waterfall.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Understanding which part of the Act applies to your situation is what determines which options you still have.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide walks through the sections that matter most in practice. It is a map, not a textbook. Where a section has generated a separate body of case law, section 214 wrongful trading, section 123 the insolvency test, section 127 void dispositions, you will find links through to our detailed guides.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The aim is to let you navigate the Act with a working understanding of what each part is for, who it protects, and where the personal-liability triggers sit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How the Insolvency Act 1986 Is Structured</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Act has three main parts a director needs to locate:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The First Group of Parts (Parts I\u2013VII)</strong> covers company insolvency: company voluntary arrangements, administration, receivership, the three forms of liquidation, and the general provisions applicable to every corporate process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The Second Group of Parts (Parts VIII\u2013XI)</strong> covers individual insolvency: individual voluntary arrangements, bankruptcy, and the equivalent provisions for individuals.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The Third Group of Parts (Parts XII\u2013XIX)</strong> contains the cross-cutting provisions: preferential debts, malpractice offences, insolvency practitioners' regulation, and miscellaneous and supplementary provisions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two major amendments have reshaped the Act since it was passed. The Enterprise Act 2002 overhauled administration, abolished administrative receivership for most new floating charges, removed the Crown's preferential status (reinstated in part for HMRC in 2020), and introduced the prescribed part for unsecured creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 added the Part A1 moratorium, the restructuring plan under Part 26A of the Companies Act 2006, and a suite of pandemic-era temporary measures, most of which have now expired. If your company's charges predate the Enterprise Act, your bank may still hold an administrative-receivership right; you should check that with your adviser.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Corporate Insolvency Procedures in the Insolvency Act 1986</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Act sets out five substantive corporate processes. As a director choosing between them, you are choosing between different trade-offs on control, continuity, and creditor protection. Our licensed IPs walk through those trade-offs with every director we advise before any process is initiated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement, Part I (sections 1\u20137B)</strong>. A proposal to creditors to compromise unsecured debts on agreed terms, approved by 75% in value. Directors stay in control. Used where the business is viable but the historic debt is not. See our guide to the <a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</a>.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Part A1 Moratorium, Part A1 (inserted 2020)</strong>. A 20-business-day standalone moratorium, extendable, protecting the company from creditor action while a rescue plan is developed. Managed by a \"monitor\" (a licensed IP), directors remain in day-to-day control.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Administration, Schedule B1</strong>. Replaced the old sections 8\u201327 regime via the Enterprise Act 2002. Statutory purpose: rescue the company as a going concern; or achieve a better result for creditors than winding-up; or realise property for distribution. Directors lose control to the administrator. Moratorium against enforcement is powerful and automatic.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Receivership, Part III (sections 28\u201372H)</strong>. Administrative receivership under pre-2003 floating charges still exists but is rarely used for new charges. Fixed-charge receivers (LPA receivers) over specific secured property remain common in property-lending contexts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Liquidation, Part IV (sections 73\u2013219)</strong>. Three routes: <strong>members' voluntary liquidation (MVL)</strong> for solvent companies winding up; <strong><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">creditors' voluntary liquidation</a> (CVL)</strong> for insolvent companies where directors initiate; <strong>compulsory liquidation</strong> by court order following a winding-up petition under section 122. All three end with the company dissolved and the residual assets distributed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Insolvency Test: Section 123</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 123 is the hinge of the whole Act. It sets out when a company is \"unable to pay its debts\", the trigger for most of the Act's subsequent machinery. Four tests sit inside it:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 123(1)(a)</strong>, a creditor owed more than \u00a3750 serves a statutory demand and 21 days pass without payment, security, or compromise.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 123(1)(b)\u2013(d)</strong>, unsatisfied execution of a judgment debt (England &amp; Wales, Scotland, and Northern Ireland variants).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 123(1)(e), cash-flow test</strong>. The company cannot pay its debts as they fall due.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 123(2), balance-sheet test</strong>. The company's liabilities (including contingent and prospective) exceed its assets. The leading authority is <em>BNY Corporate Trustee Services v Eurosail</em> [2013], which held that the test requires the court to be satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the company has reached \"the point of no return\".</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are wondering whether your company is insolvent, this is where to start. Our detailed <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">insolvency test guide</a> walks through each limb with worked examples. We also run through these limbs directly with directors on the first call, because knowing which test your company fails shapes what we recommend.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Director Duties and Personal Liability Under the Insolvency Act 1986</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Act is what puts real teeth on directors' duties. Four sections are the ones you need to know, and if your company is under financial pressure, you should understand all four before your next board decision:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 213, fraudulent trading</strong>. Civil liability where the business has been carried on with intent to defraud creditors. The test is high (dishonesty required) but the consequence is uncapped personal contribution.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 214, wrongful trading</strong>. The director's nightmare section. Civil liability where, before the commencement of winding-up, the director knew or ought to have concluded that there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation, and did not take every step to minimise loss to creditors. Personal contribution to the estate, measured by the increase in net deficiency caused. This is the provision that drives \"do I keep trading?\" decisions in practice.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 212, misfeasance</strong>. Summary procedure against officers who have misapplied property or breached fiduciary duty. Not a separate wrong; a quicker route to hold directors to account.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 216\u2013217, reuse of company name</strong>. Five-year restriction on a director of an insolvent company from acting for a company with a similar name, with personal liability for debts of the successor company. A trap for directors who try to \"phoenix\" without following the statutory exception procedure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You also sit inside the broader Companies Act 2006 duty regime and the <em>BTI v Sequana</em> [2022] Supreme Court framework, which confirmed that once insolvency is likely, directors must have regard to creditor interests. The Insolvency Act sections bite only on insolvency, but the duty shift starts earlier. Our guide to <a href=\"/advice/are-directors-personally-liable-for-company-debts/\">director personal liability</a> walks through the overlap.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Challenging Transactions: The Avoidance Provisions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once a company is in liquidation or administration, the IP can unwind certain transactions that occurred before the insolvency. These are the avoidance provisions and they routinely catch directors who did not realise what they were signing away:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 238, transactions at undervalue</strong>. Two-year lookback. Gifts, or transactions for significantly less than the asset was worth. Selling the van to a director's spouse at book value when market was higher, a classic.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 239, preferences</strong>. Six-month lookback (two years for connected parties). Paying one unsecured creditor ahead of others where you were influenced by a desire to produce that preferential outcome. Paying the director's parents back their loan while leaving the tax man unpaid is the textbook case.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 244, extortionate credit transactions</strong>. Rarely invoked but available.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 245, avoidance of floating charges</strong>. Floating charges granted within 12 months before insolvency (two years for connected) for past value, not fresh money, can be set aside.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Section 127, post-petition dispositions</strong>. Once a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> is presented, any disposition of the company's property is void unless validated by the court. This is why banks freeze accounts the moment a petition is advertised.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An avoidance claim does not require dishonesty; for preferences it requires an influence test that is highly fact-sensitive, and for transactions at undervalue it requires an objective value comparison.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors who sail close to the wind on these typically do so without legal advice. A ten-minute conversation before the transaction usually costs less than the five-figure clawback claim afterwards. If you are about to pay down a loan from a connected party, or transfer an asset at below market value, take advice first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Order of Distribution in the Insolvency Act 1986</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 175 and Schedule 6 set out the priority waterfall, who gets paid before whom from the liquidation estate:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fixed-charge holders</strong>, to the extent of their security.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Expenses of the liquidation</strong>, including the IP's fees, legal costs of the estate.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Ordinary preferential debts (Category 1)</strong>, employee wages up to \u00a3800 per person for the four months pre-insolvency, holiday pay, pension contributions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Secondary preferential debts (Category 2)</strong>, HMRC for VAT, PAYE, CIS, employee NICs (reinstated by Finance Act 2020 from 1 December 2020). This is the \"Crown preference\" return.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Prescribed part</strong>, a portion of floating-charge realisations ring-fenced for unsecured creditors (currently capped at \u00a3800,000 for floating charges created on or after 6 April 2020).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Floating-charge holders</strong>, to the extent of their security.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Unsecured creditors</strong>, trade suppliers, unpaid tax outside the Category 2 reinstatement, HMRC for Corporation Tax.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Shareholders</strong>, only if there is a surplus after all debts, rare in insolvent liquidation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The 2020 reinstatement of HMRC's secondary preferential status changed the economics of lending materially. Floating-charge lenders now rank behind HMRC for VAT, PAYE, and CIS, and pricing of secured lending reflects it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In our practice, this is one of the most commonly misunderstood aspects of the distribution waterfall, particularly for directors who assumed their bank's floating charge would sweep up the bulk of any realisation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Individual Insolvency: Parts VIII to XI</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Personal insolvency sits in the Second Group of Parts. Two processes a director commonly encounters:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Individual Voluntary Arrangement, Part VIII</strong>. Personal equivalent of the CVA. Compromises unsecured personal debt over (usually) five years. Used by directors whose PGs have been called. See our <a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-an-individual-voluntary-arrangement/\">IVA guide</a>.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Bankruptcy, Parts IX\u2013XI</strong>. The personal equivalent of liquidation. Assets vest in a trustee, debts discharged after 12 months (usually), serious consequences for directorship and regulated professions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on the Insolvency Act 1986</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are asking which section of the Insolvency Act 1986 bites on your situation, you are already at the stage where a licensed IP conversation will pay for itself many times over. The Act is designed so the earlier you engage with it, the more options stay open.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Leave it late, past a statutory demand, past a winding-up petition, past a freezing order, and the Act's machinery starts working against you rather than with you. The window for rescue closes faster than most directors expect.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for a confidential review. Our licensed IPs can run through whether your company meets the section 123 test, whether a section 214 wrongful trading exposure is crystallising, and what the avoidance-provision risk looks like on your recent transactions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We will also show you which of the Part I, A1, Schedule B1, or Part IV procedures is the right route for your company. Nothing is charged until you instruct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs: The Insolvency Act 1986</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-47772\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-47772-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-47772\" tabindex=\"0\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-47772\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What does the Insolvency Act 1986 do?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-47772\">\n<p>It is the primary UK statute governing what happens when a company or individual cannot pay its debts. It sets out the corporate rescue and wind-down procedures (CVA, administration, liquidation, receivership), the personal insolvency routes (IVA, bankruptcy), the director-liability triggers (wrongful trading, misfeasance).</p>\n\n\n\n<p>It also contains the avoidance provisions (preferences, transactions at undervalue) and the priority waterfall for distributing assets among creditors. If your company is under financial pressure, you need to understand all of these before your next major decision.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-47772-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-47772\" tabindex=\"0\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-47772\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is the Insolvency Act 1986 still current?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-47772\">\n<p>Yes, the Act remains the core UK insolvency statute, significantly amended by the Enterprise Act 2002 (administration overhaul, abolition of administrative receivership for most new charges) and the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 (Part A1 moratorium, restructuring plan, temporary Covid-era measures).</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Individual sections have been amended many times; always check the current consolidated text on legislation.gov.uk before relying on the bare section number in your planning.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-47772-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-47772\" tabindex=\"0\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-47772\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Which section covers wrongful trading?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-47772\">\n<p>Section 214 (for liquidation) and section 246ZB (for administration, inserted in 2015). Both impose civil liability on directors who knew, or ought to have concluded, that there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation and did not take every step to minimise creditor loss. Contribution to the estate is measured by the increase in net deficiency caused by the continued trading.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-47772-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-47772\" tabindex=\"0\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-47772\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What is a section 123 insolvency test?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-47772\">\n<p>The statutory test for when a company is \"unable to pay its debts\". Four limbs: unpaid statutory demand above \u00a3750 for 21 days, unsatisfied execution of a judgment, the cash-flow test (cannot pay debts as they fall due), and the balance-sheet test (liabilities exceed assets on a \"point of no return\" analysis per Eurosail). Satisfying any one limb triggers the Act's insolvency consequences.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-47772-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-47772\" tabindex=\"0\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-47772\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can HMRC be paid ahead of other creditors?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-47772\">\n<p>Since 1 December 2020, yes. But only for VAT, PAYE, employee NICs, and CIS. Those sit as Category 2 (secondary) preferential debts, behind employee wages (Category 1) but ahead of floating-charge holders and ordinary unsecured creditors. Corporation Tax remains unsecured. This was the \"Crown preference\" reinstatement introduced by the Finance Act 2020.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-47772-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-47772\" tabindex=\"0\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-47772\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What is a preference under section 239?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-47772\">\n<p>A payment or transaction with one creditor that puts them in a better position than they would be in on insolvent liquidation, made when the company was insolvent and influenced by a desire to produce that preferential result. Six-month lookback from the onset of insolvency; two years where the creditor is connected (a director, spouse, parent company). The IP can apply to reverse the transaction.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:post-content -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"methodology-block\" style=\"background:#f7f7f7;border-left:3px solid #cccccc;padding:18px 22px;margin:32px 0;\">\n<p class=\"methodology-label\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px 0;font-size:18px;\"><strong>Methodology &amp; Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners. Statutory references are to the Insolvency Act 1986 as amended to the date of writing, principally by the Enterprise Act 2002, the Small Business, Enterprise and Employment Act 2015, the Finance Act 2020 (Crown preference reinstatement), and the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020.</p>\n<p>Case references (Eurosail, Sequana) reflect leading Supreme Court authority at the time of writing.</p>\n<p>Company Debt's licensed IPs advise on all of the corporate and personal procedures covered by the Act. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation; nothing is charged until we agree a scope of work.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"sources-block\" style=\"background:#f7f7f7;border-left:3px solid #cccccc;padding:18px 22px;margin:32px 0;\">\n<p class=\"sources-label\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px 0;font-size:18px;\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insolvency Act 1986 (as amended) &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Enterprise Act 2002 &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2002/40/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/12/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Finance Act 2020 (Crown preference reinstatement) &mdash; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/14/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/what-is-the-insolvency-service/": {
    "id": 47550,
    "slug": "what-is-the-insolvency-service",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<p>The Insolvency Service is a government body, not a law firm, not a debt collector, and not the same as the licensed insolvency practitioner your company might appoint. Directors who confuse these roles make poor decisions at the worst possible moment. Understanding what this agency actually does, and what it has the power to do to you, is worth getting right before you need to.</p>\n\n<p>Run as an executive agency of the Department for Business and Trade, the Insolvency Service investigates director conduct, operates the Official Receiver function, and can pursue disqualification or prosecution under the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986.</p>\n\n<p>It acts in the public interest. That is a different objective from a licensed IP working for creditors, or a restructuring adviser working for you.</p>\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-the-insolvency-service-actually-does\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-the-insolvency-service-actually-does\">What the Insolvency Service Actually Does</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<p>The core functions divide into four areas that directors in financial difficulty are most likely to encounter.</p>\n\n<p>First, the Official Receiver. When a company is wound up compulsorily by court order, the Official Receiver steps in as interim liquidator. This is a civil servant within the Insolvency Service, not a private practitioner.</p>\n\n<p>The deposit creditors pay to fund this (\u00a32,600 at current rates) goes to the Insolvency Service. The Official Receiver investigates the company's affairs and reports on director conduct.</p>\n\n<p>Second, director conduct investigations. Where misconduct is identified, including wrongful trading under s.214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 or misuse of company funds, the Insolvency Service may apply to court for a disqualification order. Under s.6 of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, a finding of unfitness carries a minimum two-year ban; serious cases reach fifteen years.</p>\n\n<p>Either way, you cannot act as a director of any UK company while subject to the order.</p>\n\n<p>Third, personal insolvency and Debt Relief Orders. The Insolvency Service administers the adjudicator function for bankruptcy applications and oversees the Debt Relief Order regime for individuals with debts under \u00a330,000 and limited assets.</p>\n\n<p>Fourth, public interest winding-up petitions. Where a company is trading fraudulently or against the public interest, the Insolvency Service can petition for compulsory winding-up even without a creditor making the application. This power is used selectively but is not rare.</p>\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-the-insolvency-service-investigates-directors\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-the-insolvency-service-investigates-directors\">How the Insolvency Service Investigates Directors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<p>The letter arrives, usually headed \"Confidential: Director Conduct Enquiry,\" after a liquidator or Official Receiver has submitted a conduct report under s.7A of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986. Every licensed IP who acts as liquidator is required by law to report on director conduct. The Insolvency Service then decides whether to pursue the matter.</p>\n\n<p>If it does, you will be asked to provide a written statement, financial records, board minutes, and correspondence. The Insolvency Service has statutory powers under insolvency legislation to compel cooperation. Declining to engage is not a neutral act; it strengthens the case against you.</p>\n\n<p>Interviews are common. They are not informal conversations. Anything you say can be used in subsequent proceedings, whether a disqualification application or, in the most serious cases, a criminal prosecution under the Fraud Act 2006 or Proceeds of Crime Act 2002.</p>\n\n<p>We see directors treat these enquiries as paperwork to be minimised. That is the wrong response. Engage, prepare, and take legal advice before your first written reply, not after. We advise every director we work with who receives such a letter to instruct a solicitor before responding.</p>\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-triggers-an-insolvency-service-investigation\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-triggers-an-insolvency-service-investigation\">What Triggers an Insolvency Service Investigation</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<p>The most common triggers we see referred through the official receiver function are worth knowing in advance.</p>\n\n<p>Misuse of Bounce Back Loans: using the funds for purposes unrelated to the business, paying personal debts, or withdrawing the money as salary without board approval. The Insolvency Service received thousands of conduct reports linked to BBL misuse after 2020. Many investigations are still working through the backlog.</p>\n\n<p>Wrongful trading: continuing to incur credit when the director knew, or ought to have known, there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation. The standard is objective. Not what you believed, but what a reasonably diligent director with your knowledge and experience ought to have concluded.</p>\n\n<p>Preferential payments: paying connected parties, or one creditor ahead of others, within the lookback windows under s.239 of the Insolvency Act 1986. Six months for unconnected creditors; two years for connected parties such as family members or associated companies.</p>\n\n<p>Missing records: companies that enter liquidation without proper financial records expose directors to personal liability. \"The accountant held them\" is not a defence. We include records maintenance in our advice to directors facing financial difficulty precisely because of this risk.</p>\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-insolvency-service-is-not-your-insolvency-practitioner\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-insolvency-service-is-not-your-insolvency-practitioner\">The Insolvency Service Is Not Your Insolvency Practitioner</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<p>This distinction matters and gets blurred constantly. A licensed insolvency practitioner (the person you appoint for a CVL, CVA, or administration) is a regulated professional working within an authorised body such as the ICAEW or IPA. Their primary duty in a liquidation is to creditors, not to you as director. But they are acting in a commercial capacity, not a regulatory one.</p>\n\n<p>The Insolvency Service is a state regulator. It does not charge you directly for its investigations. It is funded by the petition deposit, asset realisations, and Consolidated Fund. Its purpose is public interest enforcement, not creditor recovery.</p>\n\n<p>Confusing the two leads directors to behave as though the Official Receiver is a neutral case administrator. The Official Receiver's conduct report goes to the Insolvency Service. The Insolvency Service decides whether to pursue disqualification or prosecution. These are sequential steps in a state enforcement process, not a service relationship you can manage with goodwill.</p>\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-directors-can-expect-during-an-insolvency-service-enquiry\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-directors-can-expect-during-an-insolvency-service-enquiry\">What Directors Can Expect During an Insolvency Service Enquiry</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<p>The initial contact is usually a formal letter requesting a written explanation of specific transactions or decisions. The Insolvency Service will have reviewed the liquidator's report and the company's financial history before writing to you. Assume they already know more than the letter reveals.</p>\n\n<p>Documents requested typically include bank statements covering the period before insolvency, board minutes or resolutions, loan agreements, supplier contracts, and correspondence with any connected parties. If your records are incomplete, say so in your first response rather than letting the gap appear later.</p>\n\n<p>If an interview follows, you can bring a solicitor. You should. The interview is recorded. The Insolvency Service investigator is trained to explore inconsistencies between your written account and the documentary evidence. A calm, factual response with supporting documents performs better than a defensive one.</p>\n\n<p>Outcomes range from no further action (where the evidence does not support misconduct) through to a disqualification undertaking, a court application, or referral to law enforcement. The Insolvency Service publishes the names and ban lengths of disqualified directors. That information is permanently searchable.</p>\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step\">Your Next Step</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<p>If you have received a letter from the Insolvency Service, or if your company is entering any formal insolvency process, take independent legal advice immediately. Not from the liquidator; their duties run to creditors, not to you. Specialist director defence solicitors are the right port of call.</p>\n\n<p>If you are earlier in the process, facing creditor pressure but not yet in formal insolvency, the clock matters. The further the company trades into insolvency, the larger the pool of transactions the Insolvency Service can scrutinise. Directors who act early almost always leave with better outcomes than those who wait.</p>\n\n<p>At Company Debt, our licensed insolvency practitioners work with directors at this stage, not after the damage is done. Call 0800 074 6757 for a confidential conversation about what your options actually are.</p>\n\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-on-the-insolvency-service\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-on-the-insolvency-service\">FAQs on the Insolvency Service</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\t\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does the Insolvency Service handle all types of insolvency, or just compulsory liquidation?</strong></p>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\n<p>The Insolvency Service's main operational role is compulsory liquidation via the Official Receiver function. It also administers the adjudicator bankruptcy route and Debt Relief Orders.</p>\n\n<p>Voluntary liquidations are managed by private licensed insolvency practitioners, though those practitioners must still report director conduct to the Insolvency Service. The Insolvency Service then decides whether to investigate further.</p>\n\n</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\t\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How long can a director be disqualified by the Insolvency Service?</strong></p>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\n<p>Under s.6 of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, the minimum period is two years and the maximum is fifteen. Less serious conduct, such as poor record-keeping or allowing insolvent trading without dishonest intent, tends to sit in the two-to-five-year range.</p>\n\n<p>Dishonesty or serious BBL fraud cases reach the upper bands. Acting as a director during a period of disqualification is a criminal offence under s.13 of the same Act.</p>\n\n</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\t\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is there a difference between the Insolvency Service and the Official Receiver?</strong></p>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\n<p>The Official Receiver is a civil servant officer of the court who sits within the Insolvency Service structure. The Insolvency Service is the parent agency; the Official Receiver is the operational role within it.</p>\n\n<p>When a compulsory winding-up order is made, the Official Receiver is automatically appointed liquidator until either a private IP takes over or the case closes. The Insolvency Service Investigations and Enforcement team then decides whether to pursue disqualification.</p>\n\n</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\t\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>If I received a Bounce Back Loan, will the Insolvency Service investigate me automatically?</strong></p>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\n<p>Not automatically. Having taken a BBL does not itself trigger investigation. What triggers it is a conduct report identifying that the loan may have been misused: withdrawn as personal funds, used to pay personal debts, or obtained on a false representation of turnover.</p>\n\n<p>If your company enters any form of insolvency, the IP or Official Receiver will examine what happened to the BBL as a matter of routine. If the records are consistent with legitimate business use, no investigation follows. If they are not, the Insolvency Service will be notified.</p>\n\n</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\t\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Are <a href=\"/advice/losing-house-if-company-goes-bust/\">personal assets at risk</a> during an Insolvency Service investigation?</strong></p>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\n<p>An investigation alone does not put your personal assets at risk. The risk to personal assets arises from what the investigation finds. A disqualification order restricts your ability to act as a director but does not automatically attach personal liability to company debts.</p>\n\n<p>Personal liability requires a separate court order, for example a wrongful trading contribution order under s.214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 or a compensation order under s.15A of the CDDA. Personal guarantees are a separate route to personal exposure entirely.</p>\n\n</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\t\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I challenge the Insolvency Service's disqualification findings?</strong></p>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-e3c166ae-c1b0-4dd9-bdb2-4f4233e23ed2\">\n\n<p>Yes. If the Insolvency Service applies to court for a disqualification order, you can defend the proceedings. The burden of proof is on the Insolvency Service to establish unfitness. Directors who engage specialist solicitors early and challenge specific allegations where the evidence is weak have a reasonable prospect of defeating the application or negotiating a lower ban period.</p>\n\n<p>Accepting a disqualification undertaking without legal advice first, because it seems easier, is a common mistake that commits you to a period you may not have had to accept.</p>\n\n</div>\n\t\t</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by a licensed insolvency practitioner. The statutory provisions cited, including the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 and the Insolvency Act 1986, have been cross-referenced against legislation.gov.uk.</p>\n<p>Figures including the Official Receiver deposit (\u00a32,600) are drawn from gov.uk and current fee schedules as at April 2026. The Insolvency Service's role is described based on its published framework; individual case outcomes vary.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a trading name of a licensed insolvency practice. We provide advice to directors and companies facing financial difficulty. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner and our duties will be to the general body of creditors in any formal insolvency process.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; executive agency of the Department for Business and Trade responsible for Official Receiver work, director conduct investigations, and public interest winding-up</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; legal basis for disqualification orders, including the s.6 unfitness test that triggers bans of two to fifteen years</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986 ss.214, 239</strong> &mdash; statutory framework for wrongful trading liability and preference claims pursued by the Official Receiver against directors</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/692/body\"><strong>Insolvency proceedings fees</strong> &mdash; current schedule of court fees and deposits, including the &pound;2,600 creditor deposit that funds the Official Receiver appointment</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/a-guide-to-the-work-of-the-official-receiver\"><strong>Official Receiver guide</strong> &mdash; gov.uk overview of the Official Receiver&rsquo;s role as interim liquidator following a compulsory winding-up order</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/search?q=disqualified+directors\"><strong>Register of disqualified directors</strong> &mdash; Companies House search for directors subject to a disqualification order or undertaking, updated continuously</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; recognised professional body that licenses and regulates many UK insolvency practitioners independently of the Insolvency Service</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>"
  },
  "/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/": {
    "id": 7674,
    "slug": "creditors-voluntary-liquidation",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<article>\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your company cannot pay its debts and rescue is no longer realistic, a Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation (CVL) is the usual director-led route for closing it in an orderly way.</p>\n<p>You appoint a licensed insolvency practitioner, the company stops trading, assets are realised, creditors are dealt with, and the company is eventually dissolved. It is by far the most common corporate insolvency: 18,525 of the 23,938 registered company insolvencies in England and Wales in 2025 were CVLs, about 77% (<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/company-insolvencies-december-2025/commentary-company-insolvency-statistics-december-2025\">Insolvency Service</a>).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We talk to directors in this position every week. The pattern is consistent: months of mounting HMRC arrears, a <a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">Time to Pay arrangement</a>, the instalment plan HMRC offers to clear arrears over months, that was refused or failed, and the quiet dread that builds each time another brown HMRC envelope lands.</p>\n<p>If that sounds familiar, you are not the first director to sit with it, and what comes next is a sequence of decisions you take with your IP, not alone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Continuing to trade while insolvent is not automatically wrongful trading: the risk under <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\">section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986</a> arises where a director knew, or ought to have concluded, that there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation and then failed to take every step to minimise loss to creditors.</p>\n<p>That is why timing matters. HMRC escalation, including a winding-up petition, can remove the voluntary option entirely, and in the cases we handle early advice generally leaves more options open: it is easier to preserve records, limit creditor losses and organise the appointment in a controlled way. Leave it until enforcement action has already started, and most of that control is gone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--scope\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>Scope</strong></p>\n<p>This guide describes the CVL procedure in England and Wales. Scotland and Northern Ireland have separate procedural rules.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__title\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation in 30 Seconds</p>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__grid\">\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">What it is</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">A voluntary liquidation procedure under section 84 of the Insolvency Act 1986 for insolvent companies, started by the directors and shareholders before a creditor forces the issue.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Who starts it</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">The directors propose the process and shareholders pass the winding-up resolution.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Who runs it</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">Directors usually instruct the proposed insolvency practitioner; creditors can nominate a different one through the statutory decision procedure.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Is court required?</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">Usually no. A CVL is an out-of-court process. An existing winding-up petition can restrict the available time, and may overtake the CVL if a winding-up order is made first.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Shareholder approval</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">A special resolution needs at least 75% by value of the shares voted.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Typical cost</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">\u00a33,500 plus VAT for a straightforward case, plus approximately \u00a3500 to \u00a31,500 in statutory and case-specific disbursements. Director redundancy may fund some or all of this, where the director genuinely qualifies as an employee.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Typical appointment speed</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">10 to 21 days from first instruction to the liquidator taking office.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Typical full closure</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">6 to 12 months for straightforward cases; 12 to 24 months or more where assets, disputes or HMRC issues are complex.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Main director risks</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">Wrongful trading, personal guarantees called in, an overdrawn director loan account, and claims that you preferred one creditor over the others or sold something for less than it was worth.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__row\">\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__q\">Best next step</p>\n<p class=\"cd-key-facts-panel__a\">Speak to a licensed IP before creditor action escalates, and confirm where the company stands.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call the Free Director Helpline on <strong>0800 074 6757</strong> for confidential, no-obligation advice from a licensed insolvency practitioner, or take our <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">30-second insolvency test</a> first if you are not yet sure where the company stands.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"is-cvl-right\">Is Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation the Right Route for Your Company?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The key question is not simply whether the company has debt. It is whether the business can realistically trade out of insolvency without making creditor losses worse. The table below is a starting point, not a substitute for advice from a licensed IP, but it reflects the questions we ask on every call.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Likely route</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Company is insolvent and no realistic rescue exists</td><td>CVL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Company is insolvent but the business is still viable</td><td><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">CVA</a> or <a href=\"/company-administration/\">administration</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Company is solvent and closing voluntarily</td><td><a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\">MVL</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Company has no debts and has stopped trading</td><td><a href=\"/liquidation/company-strike-off-and-dissolution/\">Strike-off</a> may be possible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HMRC has refused or failed a <a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">Time to Pay</a> arrangement</td><td>CVL may be appropriate if genuine recovery is not realistic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> has already been filed</td><td>Urgent IP advice; voluntary control may be closing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Company has no assets but debts remain</td><td>CVL may still be needed; strike-off is usually unsafe here</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-definition\">When Directors Should Consider a CVL</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVL applies when the company is insolvent and the directors choose to wind it up voluntarily rather than waiting for a creditor to force the issue. Insolvency has two statutory tests under section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986; failing either is enough.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cash-flow test:</strong> the company cannot pay its debts as they fall due. Tax liabilities, supplier invoices, loan repayments, rent. Most CVLs are triggered here, often after months of mounting HMRC arrears.</li>\n<li><strong>Balance-sheet test:</strong> total liabilities exceed total assets, counting contingent and prospective liabilities such as the company's own borrowing and lease obligations. A director's personal guarantee is a separate personal obligation, not a company balance-sheet liability.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Common triggers we see: HMRC Time to Pay refusals, Bounce Back Loan defaults, loss of a major customer, or a statutory demand that cannot be paid within 21 days. There is no minimum debt level, number of creditors or trading period to enter a CVL.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The threshold that matters most is the shareholder vote: under <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/84\">section 84(1)(b) of the Insolvency Act 1986</a>, a special resolution to wind up voluntarily must be approved by at least 75% by value of the shares voted.</p>\n<p>Most companies entering a CVL are owner-managed, so the director and majority shareholder are the same person and the vote is a formality. Where you have multiple shareholders who disagree, the 75% threshold becomes a real constraint and you may need to negotiate or look at alternative routes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dormant companies with outstanding liabilities can also enter a CVL, although <a href=\"/liquidation/company-strike-off-and-dissolution/\">strike-off</a> may be more appropriate where debts are negligible and no creditor is likely to object.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-process\">How the Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation Process Works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The CVL follows a fixed statutory sequence, the same one we take every director through. The steps cannot be skipped or reordered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<div class=\"cd-step-cards\">\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 1</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Speak to an Insolvency Practitioner</strong></p>\n<p>You instruct an authorised insolvency practitioner regulated by a recognised professional body such as the ICAEW, IPA, ICAS or Chartered Accountants Ireland. The Insolvency Service oversees the regulatory framework. The IP assesses your position, prepares the documents you need, and advises you on timing.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 2</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Board Decision</strong></p>\n<p>You hold a board meeting and resolve that the company cannot continue trading and should enter liquidation.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 3</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Shareholder Resolution</strong></p>\n<p>Shareholders pass a special resolution (at least 75% by value of the shares voted) under section 84 IA 1986. For many owner-managed private companies, the special resolution can be passed as a written resolution rather than at a physical shareholder meeting, which speeds up the process.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 4</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Creditor Notification</strong></p>\n<p>The insolvency practitioner sends creditors the prescribed information and notice of the decision procedure through which they can participate in the liquidator's appointment; the precise timetable is governed by the Insolvency Rules 2016, which replaced physical creditor meetings. In practice this is handled by correspondence (deemed consent), though creditors can request a virtual meeting.</p>\n<p>Creditors have the right to appoint a different liquidator, though in practice the IP proposed by the directors is confirmed in most cases.</p>\n<p>Separately from that creditor decision procedure, the winding-up resolution must be <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents\">filed at Companies House within 15 days of being passed and advertised in The Gazette within 14 days</a>. The Gazette is the government's official journal of public notices, free for anyone to search. This filing and advertisement duty is a distinct statutory obligation, not part of the creditor notice above.</p>\n<p>The Gazette notice is the part directors dread here: a public record they picture a neighbour, a supplier's credit controller, or a future employer stumbling across. In practice it is a formal legal listing almost nobody reads for pleasure, not an announcement posted through the letterboxes on your street.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 5</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Statement of Affairs</strong></p>\n<p>You prepare and verify a Statement of Affairs by a statement of truth: a document listing every asset, every liability, every creditor with addresses and amounts owed, and any security held over the company's assets.</p>\n<p>It must be complete and accurate. Material omissions, false representations and failures to comply with statutory requirements can carry civil or criminal consequences. We cross-check against bank statements, VAT returns, and HMRC records, so discrepancies surface fast.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 6</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Liquidator Appointed</strong></p>\n<p>Once appointed, the liquidator takes full control. Director powers end on appointment day: you stop signing for the company, paying its bills, or answering for it, and after years of carrying the thing that hand-over can feel abrupt even when it is the relief you came for.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 7</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Assets, Creditors and Conduct Review</strong></p>\n<p>The liquidator realises assets, investigates the company's affairs, adjudicates creditor claims, distributes funds in the statutory order, and reports on director conduct to the Insolvency Service.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cd-step-card\">\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__number\">Step 8</p>\n<p class=\"cd-step-card__title\"><strong>Company Dissolved</strong></p>\n<p>Once the liquidation work is complete, the company is struck off the register and formally dissolved. Timing depends on complexity: 6 to 12 months for straightforward cases, longer where assets, disputes or HMRC issues are involved.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-directors-need-to-prepare\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-directors-need-to-prepare\">What Directors Need to Prepare</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Some of this you should already have to hand; the rest is drawn up with us once you instruct. Directors who arrive with clean records save time and money; where records are incomplete, the liquidator must reconstruct the position from bank statements and third-party records, and that time is billed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most directors we act for pull this together the night before the first call, at the kitchen table with a box file and a laptop, quietly dreading what the numbers will confirm. Gathering it is rarely as hard as the sitting-with-it beforehand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Bring to the first meeting</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Management accounts and recent bank statements.</li>\n<li>Aged creditors and debtors listings.</li>\n<li>HMRC correspondence, including VAT and PAYE.</li>\n<li>Payroll records.</li>\n<li>Loan and lease documents, including any Bounce Back Loan paperwork.</li>\n<li>Asset details and personal-guarantee paperwork.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Documents prepared with the IP</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Board minutes</strong> recording the decision that the company is insolvent and should enter liquidation.</li>\n<li><strong>Shareholder resolution</strong> (at least 75% by value of the shares voted) to wind up the company.</li>\n<li><strong>Statement of Affairs</strong> listing every asset, liability, creditor, and any security held over the company's assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Director questionnaires</strong> covering trading history, reasons for insolvency, and any transactions that may need investigation.</li>\n<li><strong>Statutory notices and filings</strong> to Companies House and the Insolvency Service.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We handle the statutory filings; your job is to hand over accurate, complete data for us to file from.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-cost\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation Costs and Timelines</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><span id=\"cvl-timeline\"></span>Two separate clocks run here. The appointment clock covers first instruction to the liquidator taking office; the case clock runs from appointment to dissolution. The appointment-speed range is our own, drawn from the cases we handle, not a statutory timetable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical figure</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Standard CVL fee</td><td>\u00a33,500 + VAT</td><td>Fixed fee for a straightforward case; more complex cases with debtors, staff or disputed assets are scoped and quoted individually.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disbursements</td><td>\u00a3500\u2013\u00a31,500</td><td>Bonding, statutory advertising and case-specific expenses.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Director redundancy claim</td><td>Can cover some or all of the fee where the director qualifies</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/redundancy-your-rights/redundancy-pay\">Statutory weekly cap \u00a3751 from 6 April 2026; max statutory redundancy \u00a322,530 (\u00a3751 \u00d7 30)</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>From instruction to liquidation start</td><td>10 to 21 days</td><td>From first instruction to the liquidator taking office.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time to dissolution (no significant assets)</td><td>6 to 12 months</td><td>From appointment to final dissolution.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time to dissolution (complex case)</td><td>12 to 24 months+</td><td>Property sales, contested claims, ongoing litigation, or HMRC disputes.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most common question we hear is \"how can I afford this if the company has no money?\" It is usually asked by someone staring at a business account that will not cover this month's wages, let alone a liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors who were genuinely employed by the company, not merely officeholders paid through dividends, may be able to claim statutory redundancy from the Redundancy Payments Service after the liquidator is appointed. It is a legitimate entitlement under the Employment Rights Act 1996, not a loophole.</p>\n<p>The Insolvency Service assesses whether the director was genuinely an employee, using evidence such as the employment terms, payroll records and how the working arrangement operated in practice; the liquidator supplies the case reference and relevant information.</p>\n<p>A director who cannot evidence a genuine employment relationship, for example where remuneration consisted solely of dividends and there was no express or implied employment contract, will not qualify.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-company-debts\">What Happens to Company Debts in Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVL deals with the company's liabilities in a fixed statutory order. What happens to each debt, and whether a director carries personal exposure, depends on how it was structured before liquidation. Check your own position against the table below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The debt that keeps a director awake, in the cases we see, is rarely the VAT bill. It is the personal guarantee signed years ago for a lease or an overdraft, the one that quietly puts the family home behind the company's borrowing. Liquidation closes the company; it does not touch that signature.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Debt type</th><th>What usually happens</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>HMRC debt</td><td>Treated as a company debt; HMRC ranks as a secondary preferential creditor for certain taxes (VAT, PAYE, employee NICs) since 1 December 2020.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Supplier debt</td><td>Usually unsecured unless the supplier holds security (for example, retention of title).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/\">Bounce Back Loan</a></td><td>Unsecured unless misuse is found. The government guarantee protects the lender, not the director.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bank loan</td><td>Depends on whether the loan is secured and whether a personal guarantee was given.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personal guarantee</td><td>An enforceable personal guarantee may be called in despite the company's liquidation, subject to its wording and any available defences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overdrawn director loan account</td><td>A debt owed by the director to the company. The liquidator will normally pursue repayment.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lease arrears</td><td>The landlord becomes an unsecured creditor for arrears; ongoing lease liability usually needs separate advice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Employee claims</td><td>Employees can claim unpaid wages, holiday pay and redundancy through the Redundancy Payments Service.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secured lending</td><td>The secured creditor has priority over the specific charged assets ahead of the general creditor pool.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--risk-warning\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>Personal Exposure</strong></p>\n<p>A CVL deals with company debts. It does not automatically remove personal guarantees, an overdrawn director loan account, or liability arising from misconduct such as wrongful trading or preferences. Those obligations survive the company's dissolution.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-director-risks\">What Happens to Directors in Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVL triggers a mandatory review of director conduct. This is routine and is not, in itself, an allegation of wrongdoing: the liquidator reports on the conduct of every director of every insolvent company. What the review makes of your position depends on the company's records, its transactions, and how you acted in the run-up to liquidation.</p>\n<p>The conduct review looks specifically for trading on after the position was clearly hopeless, BBL misuse, unrecorded director loan repayments, or active concealment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Statutory basis</th><th>What the Liquidator Will Examine</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrongful trading</td>\n<td>Section 214 IA 1986</td>\n<td>The liquidator will consider when the director knew or ought to have concluded that insolvent liquidation could not reasonably be avoided, and what steps were then taken to minimise creditor losses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Misfeasance (misusing company money, or breaking a duty owed to the company)</td>\n<td>Section 212 IA 1986</td>\n<td>Keep money and property properly applied and recorded. The liquidator can ask the court to make a director repay or restore misapplied funds. Record the rationale for payments made near insolvency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disqualification</td>\n<td>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, ss.6 and 7</td>\n<td>The Insolvency Service considers the conduct report and any evidence of unfit conduct; cooperation assists the investigation but does not erase earlier misconduct.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preferences</td>\n<td>Section 239 IA 1986</td>\n<td>A payment or transfer that put a creditor, surety or guarantor in a better position, with a desire to prefer them. Look-back from the onset of insolvency: 6 months, or 2 years for connected parties.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transactions at undervalue</td>\n<td>Section 238 IA 1986</td>\n<td>Do not transfer assets below market value before liquidation. The look-back is a fixed 2 years and, unlike preferences, does not change with connection; the liquidator can apply to unwind it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overdrawn director loan account</td>\n<td>Director's contractual debt to the company</td>\n<td>The liquidator will normally seek repayment. Any last-minute dividend, salary adjustment, asset transfer or set-off used to remove the balance will be examined and may be challenged.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is usually where a director's real fear sits: the worry that personal ruin comes automatically with the company's failure. It does not.</p>\n<p>It helps to separate what happens in every CVL from what needs particular facts before it becomes real. The left column is routine and applies to every director; the right becomes a live risk only on specific findings. Early advice and full cooperation help establish what actually happened, but do not remove liability where there was genuine misconduct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Happens in every CVL</th><th>Requires particular facts</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Conduct report to the Insolvency Service</td><td>Wrongful trading claim</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Books and records review</td><td>Preference claim</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Asset and transaction review</td><td>Transaction at undervalue claim</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Director questionnaire</td><td>Disqualification proceedings</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cooperation duty under section 235</td><td>Personal contribution order</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-employees\">What Happens to Employees?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most directors, letting the staff go is the part they dread most, harder than the filings or the calls with HMRC. The mechanics, at least, are well worn and the people affected are protected by two separate routes.</p>\n<p>Most employees are made redundant when trading ceases or shortly after the liquidator is appointed, though timing can differ where limited trading continues to preserve value. Within the liquidation estate, arrears of wages rank as a preferential debt, capped at \u00a3800 per employee under Schedule 6 of the Insolvency Act 1986, the part of the Act that sets the order creditors are paid in.</p>\n<p>Separately, employees claim statutory redundancy, unpaid wages, holiday pay and notice pay from the Redundancy Payments Service.</p>\n\n<p>Those payments come from the National Insurance Fund, the government pot that covers these claims when an employer cannot.</p>\n\n<p>They are capped by the \u00a3751 statutory weekly pay limit from 6 April 2026, which puts the maximum statutory redundancy payment at \u00a322,530. We handle the redundancy claim process directly with you if you are also an employee of the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-alternatives\">CVL vs Other Closure and Rescue Routes</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVL is one of several routes for a UK limited company, and the right one turns on the honest test we come back to on every call. Strip out the debt for a moment and ask whether there is still a business underneath that wins work and covers its own costs. If there is, a CVA or administration may protect it; if the debt was the only thing holding the doors open, a CVL is the cleaner answer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Best used when</th><th>Main director consideration</th><th>Related guide</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation (CVL)</td>\n<td>Insolvent, voluntary action available, business not rescuable.</td>\n<td>Director keeps choice of IP and timing; redundancy may fund some or all of the cost where the director can evidence a genuine employment relationship.</td>\n<td>This page</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compulsory liquidation</td>\n<td>A creditor (usually HMRC) has petitioned the court.</td>\n<td>Director loses control; Official Receiver runs the case.</td>\n<td><a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">Compulsory guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</td>\n<td>Insolvent but the business is viable; cash-flow gap repayable over 3\u20135 years.</td>\n<td>Requires 75% creditor vote; director keeps running the company under supervision.</td>\n<td><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">CVA guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administration</td>\n<td>Business has rescuable value, or a moratorium is needed urgently to stop creditors chasing.</td>\n<td>Administrator takes control; fees come out of the estate ahead of creditors.</td>\n<td><a href=\"/company-administration/\">Administration guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strike-off</td>\n<td>Solvent, no creditors, no material assets.</td>\n<td>Any creditor can object and restore the company; not safe where debts exist.</td>\n<td><a href=\"/liquidation/company-strike-off-and-dissolution/\">Strike-off guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Members' Voluntary Liquidation (MVL)</td>\n<td>Solvent with retained profit; tax-efficient closure wanted.</td>\n<td>Capital-gains treatment with potential Business Asset Disposal Relief.</td>\n<td><a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\">MVL guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--recovery-path\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>Recovery Path</strong></p>\n<p>The route turns on whether a viable business survives under the debt: unviable points to a CVL, viable to a CVA or administration. Either way the choice is time-sensitive: once a winding-up petition forces the issue, the voluntary window is closing and the later you act, the fewer options remain.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-directors-should-do-next\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-directors-should-do-next\">What Directors Should Do Next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The hardest step is usually the first one: making the call, and the quiet fear that asking questions somehow puts you on HMRC's radar. It does not. A call to us, or to any licensed IP, is confidential and triggers nothing. What triggers enforcement is arrears left unanswered, not the director who finally picks up the phone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Here is what we recommend, in order.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol>\n<li>If the company is still trading but clearly insolvent, call a licensed IP this week. Early engagement helps you understand your duties, preserve the options still available and avoid increasing creditor losses, and keeps a controlled CVL open rather than forced compulsory liquidation.</li>\n<li>If a winding-up petition or statutory demand has arrived, act within days, not weeks. A CVL can sometimes still be started before the petition hearing, but every day narrows the window.</li>\n<li>If trading has stopped and debts remain, the company still needs a formal closure route. Leaving it dormant removes neither the liabilities nor the risk of compulsory action.</li>\n<li>Pull together the records list above before your first IP call. Clean records cut costs and reduce risk in the conduct review.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are not sure where the company stands, take our <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">30-second insolvency test</a> first, or call the Free Director Helpline on <strong>0800 074 6757</strong> for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"cvl-faq\">Frequently Asked Questions About Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"7674-cvl-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan directors choose the insolvency practitioner in a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Directors propose the IP, and creditors have the right to appoint a different one during the decision procedure. In practice, creditors rarely exercise this right in small-company CVLs unless concerns about IP independence have been raised.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDo directors need to attend a creditors' meeting?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Physical meetings are no longer mandatory. The Insolvency Rules 2016 replaced them with a decision procedure that can be conducted by correspondence (deemed consent). You may need to answer questions from the liquidator or creditors in writing; cooperation here is part of the conduct review the liquidator files later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow long does a CVL take?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Typically 10 to 21 days from first instruction to the liquidator taking office. Full closure then takes 6 to 12 months where there are no significant assets, and 12 to 24 months or more for complex cases involving property, disputes or HMRC issues.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow much does a CVL cost?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Typically \u00a33,500 plus VAT, with \u00a3500\u2013\u00a31,500 in disbursements (bonding, statutory advertising and case-specific expenses). Directors who are also genuine employees with at least two years' continuous service may be able to claim statutory redundancy, which can fund some or all of the fee.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens if the company has no assets?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A CVL can proceed even without realisable assets. The IP's fees must still be paid, typically funded by the directors' redundancy claim where they qualify, or by direct payment from the directors where they do not. Strike-off can sometimes suit a no-asset, no-creditor case, but where unpaid creditors exist a strike-off is usually contested and the company is restored.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens to HMRC debt in a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC debt is treated as a company liability. HMRC ranks as a secondary preferential creditor for certain taxes, including VAT and PAYE, since 1 December 2020, so it is usually paid ahead of ordinary unsecured creditors from what the liquidator recovers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eAre Bounce Back Loans treated differently in a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Bounce Back Loans are unsecured debts and rank alongside other unsecured creditors. The government guarantee protects the lender, not the borrower.</p>\n<p>Directors are not personally liable unless they gave a personal guarantee or the loan was misused; the liquidator reviews how the loan funds were applied, and BBL misuse is one of the conduct issues the review looks at most closely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":7,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill I be personally liable after a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A CVL deals with liabilities through the company's insolvent estate; it does not make them vanish, and unpaid balances generally remain the company's, not the director's, merely because the company cannot pay them.</p>\n<p>Personal guarantees, an overdrawn director loan account, and misconduct findings (such as wrongful trading or preferences) can still create personal exposure. Where personal guarantees are called in, or personal insolvency follows, that is separate from the company's liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":8,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes a CVL affect my personal credit rating?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A CVL does not directly appear on a director's personal credit file; the company's credit history is separate from yours. Where personal guarantees are called in, or personal insolvency follows (bankruptcy or an IVA), those will affect your personal credit rating. Director disqualification is a public record held by the Insolvency Service, but it is not a credit-file entry.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":9,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I start a new company after a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>For a director who has spent years building a trade around a name, the real fear is being told they can never use it again. The restriction is narrower than that.</p>\n<p>You can form a new company immediately, unless you have been disqualified. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/216\">Section 216 of the Insolvency Act 1986</a> generally restricts reusing the same or a confusingly similar company name for 5 years, unless a statutory exception applies or the court grants permission.</p>\n<p>Breaching that restriction is a criminal offence, carrying up to two years' imprisonment, a fine, and personal liability for the new company's debts incurred while the prohibited name was in use. This does not restrict directorship itself.</p>\n<p>If a disqualification order is made, you cannot act as a director of any company for the period specified (2 to 15 years under section 6 of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":10,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens to employees in a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Most employees are made redundant when trading ceases or shortly after the liquidator is appointed, though timing can differ where limited trading continues to complete work or preserve value. In the liquidation estate, wage arrears rank as a preferential debt capped at \u00a3800 per employee under Schedule 6.</p>\n<p>Separately, they claim statutory redundancy, unpaid wages, holiday pay and notice pay from the Redundancy Payments Service, capped by the \u00a3751 weekly limit from 6 April 2026, giving a maximum statutory redundancy payment of \u00a322,530.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":11,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan HMRC block a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC does not vote on the shareholders' resolution to wind up, so it cannot block that decision itself. It can, though, exercise creditor rights in the liquidator-appointment decision, and it can continue an existing winding-up petition or enforcement action.</p>\n<p>An advanced petition can therefore remove your practical control of timing, unless it is withdrawn or the CVL is commenced before the hearing date.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":12,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs a CVL better than compulsory liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Where both routes remain available, a CVL normally gives the director more control over timing and the choice of proposed IP. It does not reduce the liquidator's statutory duty to review conduct: conduct is assessed on the facts regardless of route. Compulsory liquidation hands control to the Official Receiver and removes that choice entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"related-guides\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"related-guides\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are the guides we point directors to most often, depending on where the company stands.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/\">Company Liquidation</a>: full overview of the three statutory liquidation routes and how the CVL fits in.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">Compulsory Liquidation</a>: what happens once a creditor petitions the court.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\">Members' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: the solvent equivalent for tax-efficient closure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">Company Voluntary Arrangement</a>: rescue route where the business is viable.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-administration/\">Company Administration</a>: rescue route with statutory moratorium.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">Winding-Up Petitions</a>: how to defend or respond to a creditor's petition before a CVL is no longer available.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">HMRC Time to Pay</a>: what a Time to Pay arrangement involves and when HMRC will refuse one.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-vat/\">Can't Pay VAT</a>, <a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-paye/\">Can't Pay PAYE</a> and <a href=\"/hmrc/problems-paying-corporation-tax-hmrc/\">Can't Pay Corporation Tax</a>: what to do before HMRC arrears escalate to a CVL.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/advice/directors-personal-guarantees/\">Directors' Personal Guarantees</a>: how personal guarantees survive a company's liquidation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/what-happens-to-employees/\">What Happens to Employees in Liquidation</a>: redundancy rights and the National Insurance Fund.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/what-happens-to-directors-in-liquidation/\">What Happens to Directors During Liquidation</a>: companion guide on the conduct review process.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">30-Second Insolvency Test</a>: confirm where your company stands.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association.</p>\n<p>Statutory references draw on the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 84, 123, 210, 214, 216, 235, 238, 239, and Schedule 6), the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 (Part 3 procedure, deemed consent), the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 (sections 6, 7, 7A), and the Employment Rights Act 1996 (Part XII RPS claims).</p>\n<p>Cost ranges reflect cases handled directly by our practice and vary with case complexity. The \u00a3751 weekly statutory pay cap reflects the rate from 6 April 2026.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner and are paid from company assets or director funding (commonly the statutory redundancy claim, where eligible). Last reviewed 17 July 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; primary legislation governing UK corporate insolvency, including ss.84 (voluntary winding-up), 123 (insolvency tests), 210 (material omissions from a statement of the company's affairs), 214 (wrongful trading), 216 (re-use of company name), 235 (cooperation duty), 238 (transactions at undervalue), 239 (preferences), and Schedule 6 (preferential debts)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents\"><strong>Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016</strong> &mdash; procedural rules for CVL appointment, deemed-consent decisions, and Statement of Affairs requirements</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; framework for conduct review (s.7A) and disqualification orders (ss.6 and 7) following liquidation</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/18/contents\"><strong>Employment Rights Act 1996</strong> &mdash; Part XII insolvency-of-employers framework that pays director and employee redundancy claims via the Redundancy Payments Service</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/redundancy-your-rights/redundancy-pay\"><strong>Statutory weekly redundancy pay cap</strong> &mdash; \u00a3751 from 6 April 2026; applied to RPS claims and the \u00a322,530 maximum statutory redundancy payment</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/insolvency-service-official-statistics\"><strong>Insolvency Service: Company Insolvency Statistics</strong> &mdash; monthly data on CVLs, compulsory liquidations, administrations, and CVAs in England and Wales</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/find-an-insolvency-practitioner\"><strong>Find an Insolvency Practitioner (gov.uk register)</strong> &mdash; verify a liquidator's licence and regulatory body before instructing</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n</article>"
  },
  "/insolvency/what-is-a-creditor/": {
    "id": 46674,
    "slug": "what-is-a-creditor",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Notice to Creditors lands on the supplier's mat on a Tuesday morning, addressed to accounts, in a brown window envelope. Inside, it says a liquidator has been appointed to your debtor, and that your \u00a312,000 unpaid invoice is now a claim in a queue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What happens to your invoice, and where you sit in the queue, depends on which category of creditor you are. The Insolvency Act 1986 and the Insolvency Rules 2016 define those categories precisely. The difference between a preferential claim and an unsecured one can be the difference between 8 pence in your pound and nothing at all.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below, we explain what a creditor is in UK insolvency, the classes the law recognises, how proofs of debt work, where set-off and retention of title fit, and the practical points that shape recovery. Directors reading this from the other side of the ledger will find the same framework applies: the rights your creditors have are the duties you owe.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-is-a-creditor-in-uk-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-is-a-creditor-in-uk-insolvency\">What Is a Creditor in UK Insolvency?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A creditor is any person or entity owed money, goods, or services by a company. In UK insolvency, the Insolvency Act 1986 and the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 give the word a technical meaning: anyone with a provable claim against the company at the relevant date.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three elements matter. First, there has to be a debt. Second, it has to be owed by the company, not by a director personally. Third, it has to be provable, not contingent on some event that may never happen, or at least capable of being estimated if contingent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When a company is in financial distress, the creditor body stops being \"people we pay\" and becomes the statutory class to whom the directors now owe their primary duty under <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25. That shift changes how every subsequent decision is scored.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"creditor-classes-recognised-in-uk-insolvency-law\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"creditor-classes-recognised-in-uk-insolvency-law\">Creditor Classes Recognised in UK Insolvency Law</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK insolvency law splits creditors into classes, and the class determines your place in the distribution order set out in Schedule 6 of the Insolvency Act 1986:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fixed-charge secured creditors</strong>, typically a bank with a mortgage over property. They are paid out of the asset over which they hold security before any other class.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Preferential creditors, first tier</strong>, employees for wages up to \u00a3800 per person for the four months before insolvency, plus accrued holiday pay.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Secondary preferential creditors</strong>, HMRC for VAT, PAYE, employee NIC, CIS deductions, and student-loan deductions. Reinstated from 1 December 2020 under the Finance Act 2020.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Prescribed part</strong>, a top-slice of net property subject to a floating charge set aside for unsecured creditors. Capped at \u00a3800,000 for floating charges created on or after 6 April 2020 (\u00a3600,000 for older floating charges).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Floating-charge secured creditors</strong>, paid after preferential creditors and prescribed part, out of floating-charge assets.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Unsecured creditors</strong>, trade suppliers, non-preferential tax (Corporation Tax, penalties, interest), landlords for unpaid rent. Paid rateably out of what remains.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Shareholders</strong>, paid last, out of any residual surplus after all creditors are satisfied. In most insolvencies that means nothing.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The gap between classes is stark. In a typical liquidation, secured and preferential creditors recover most of what they are owed, while preferential and non-preferential creditors at the bottom of the waterfall often recover a few pence in the pound, or nothing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"proof-of-debt-how-creditors-claim-in-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"proof-of-debt-how-creditors-claim-in-insolvency\">Proof of Debt: How Creditors Claim in Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once a company enters a formal insolvency procedure, creditors must prove their debt to participate in any distribution. Rule 14.3 of the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 sets out the form.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A valid proof of debt includes:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The creditor's name and address</strong>, and a contact for correspondence.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The total amount claimed</strong>, calculated as at the relevant date (the date the company entered insolvency).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Whether any VAT is included</strong>, and whether VAT bad-debt relief will be claimed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Whether the debt is secured</strong>, preferential, or unsecured, and the basis for any security asserted.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Supporting documents</strong>, invoices, contracts, statements, sufficient to evidence the claim.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The officeholder may admit the proof in full, admit it in part, or reject it. A rejected creditor can challenge the decision in court within 21 days. We see rejections most often on two grounds: missing documents, and debts dated after the relevant date (post-insolvency trading with the officeholder, which is a different class altogether).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"set-off-and-mutual-credits\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"set-off-and-mutual-credits\">Set-Off and Mutual Credits</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where a creditor owes the insolvent company money and the company owes the creditor money, the two are netted off under rule 14.25 of the Insolvency Rules 2016. Only the net balance is payable or claimable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The conditions for insolvency set-off:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Mutual dealings</strong> existed between the creditor and the company before the relevant date.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Both amounts are in the same right</strong>, a corporate debt cannot be set off against a personal one of the director.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Neither debt arose from post-insolvency transactions</strong>, which are governed by different rules.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Set-off is mandatory, not optional. A supplier who is owed \u00a330,000 by the company but also owes the company \u00a38,000 on another invoice has a net claim of \u00a322,000, not a \u00a330,000 claim and an \u00a38,000 debt. The practical effect is to give a trading counterparty better recovery than a pure creditor in the same position, because set-off operates as a de facto security.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"contingent-creditors-landlords-guarantees-and-product-claims\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"contingent-creditors-landlords-guarantees-and-product-claims\">Contingent Creditors: Landlords, Guarantees, and Product Claims</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Not all creditors are holding an overdue invoice. Contingent creditors have a claim that may or may not crystallise. They are still creditors, and their claims still have to be valued.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Typical contingent claims:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Landlords</strong>, with a continuing lease and rent payable over the remainder of the term, valued on the basis of the likely shortfall net of re-letting.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal-guarantee beneficiaries</strong>, where a director has given a guarantee to a supplier; the supplier has a claim against the company as primary obligor and against the director as guarantor.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Product-liability claimants</strong>, with a claim arising before the relevant date but not yet determined in amount.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC on an open enquiry</strong>, where the final assessment has not issued.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Claimants in pending litigation</strong>, where the outcome is uncertain.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The officeholder values contingent claims on a just estimate under rule 14.14 of the Insolvency Rules 2016. The claimant can challenge the estimate if they disagree. In our cases, the contingent creditors most often under-valued are unresolved HMRC enquiries and onerous lease liabilities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"retention-of-title-and-trust-claims\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"retention-of-title-and-trust-claims\">Retention of Title and Trust Claims</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Some \"creditors\" are not creditors at all in the insolvency sense. A supplier with a valid retention of title clause is the owner of the goods, not a creditor for the price, as long as the goods are identifiable and unpaid. The goods fall outside the estate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Retention of title works when:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The clause is incorporated</strong> in the supply contract, typically by signed terms or proven course of dealing.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The goods are identifiable</strong>, either individually or by reference to a stock category.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The goods remain unpaid</strong>, in whole or in the extended form the clause uses.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The goods have not been consumed or processed</strong> beyond recognition.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trust claims sit in a similar position. Money held on an express trust for a client is not an asset of the company. Customer pre-payments held in a dedicated trust account, for instance, may fall outside the estate altogether, depending on how the account is structured. The officeholder's lawyer will interrogate the documentation closely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"creditors-meetings-and-creditor-committees\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"creditors-meetings-and-creditor-committees\">Creditors' Meetings and Creditor Committees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In a <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">creditors' voluntary liquidation</a> or administration, creditors have structured rights of participation. The 2017 reforms replaced the old creditors' meeting with a decision procedure, usually by deemed consent or correspondence, with physical meetings called only where a minimum creditor threshold requests one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What creditors can do:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Vote on the appointment of the liquidator</strong> in a CVL, or ratify the administrator's proposals in administration.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Request further information</strong> from the officeholder on decisions taken in the insolvency.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Elect a creditors' committee</strong>, which oversees fees, strategy, and asset realisations. Typically three to five creditors, unpaid, with access to records.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Challenge officeholder decisions</strong>, fees, decisions, conduct, under the applications route in Schedule B1 or the Insolvency Rules.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a creditor owed \u00a312,000 in a \u00a33 million estate, the committee rarely repays the time. For a creditor owed \u00a3300,000, a seat on the committee is where the fee-control and asset-sale conversations actually happen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-as-a-creditor-or-director\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-as-a-creditor-or-director\">Your Next Step as a Creditor or Director</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The real question is rarely \"what class am I?\". It is \"what can I recover, and what does it cost to pursue it?\". Two groups read this page, and your next step differs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If you are a supplier who has received a Notice to Creditors</strong>, file your proof of debt on time with the supporting invoices and contract. Check for retention of title, set-off, and personal guarantees from the directors. Small claims rarely justify committee involvement; larger ones almost always do.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If you are a director facing creditor pressure</strong>, you are already in the zone of insolvency. Your primary duty is now to creditors, and decisions you make now will be reviewed later. Talk to a licensed insolvency practitioner this week.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed IPs can advise creditors on proofs and committee strategy, and advise directors on the next-step options. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-on-creditors-in-uk-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-on-creditors-in-uk-insolvency\">FAQs on Creditors in UK Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"46674-creditor-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the difference between a secured and an unsecured creditor?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A secured creditor holds a fixed or floating charge over company assets and is paid out of that asset before unsecured creditors. An unsecured creditor has no charge and is paid rateably from whatever remains after secured and preferential creditors. In most liquidations, secured creditors recover substantially more than unsecured ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow do I submit a proof of debt?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Complete the proof of debt form supplied by the officeholder, or the prescribed form under rule 14.3 of the Insolvency Rules 2016. Include the total owed as at the relevant date, whether VAT is included, whether security is claimed, and supporting invoices or contracts. Submit within the deadline set in the officeholder's notice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I claim set-off against a company in liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, where mutual dealings existed before the relevant date and both sides are in the same right. Insolvency set-off under rule 14.25 is mandatory, the creditor claims the net balance. Set-off can substantially improve recovery for a creditor who also owed the company money.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens to a personal guarantee in a company insolvency?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The company's insolvency does not discharge the director's guarantee. The creditor can claim against the company in the liquidation and pursue the director personally for any shortfall. Directors with personal guarantees often face the hardest recovery path.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is a contingent creditor?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A creditor whose claim depends on a future event, an unresolved dispute, a continuing lease, an open HMRC enquiry, a pending court case. The officeholder values the claim on a just estimate under rule 14.14 of the Insolvency Rules 2016.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow long does it take for creditors to receive a distribution?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Simple liquidations with straightforward asset realisations may distribute within 12 to 18 months. Complex cases with litigation, contentious proofs, or overseas assets can run for several years. The officeholder must progress the case reasonably and report at least annually.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team for UK directors managing the issue covered above.</p>\n<p>We have drawn on the relevant statutory framework cited inline in the article (see Sources below) and on current Insolvency Service and HMRC guidance where applicable.</p>\n<p>Where we describe how the issue plays out operationally, including the practical sequencing of director responses and the personal-liability tail of late action, we draw on cases handled across our advisory referral network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a UK insolvency advisory firm. We refer directors to licensed insolvency practitioners where formal procedures are appropriate. Nothing on this page is a substitute for tailored legal or insolvency advice on your specific facts.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; Schedule 6 lists preferential debts; sections 175 and 176A set the order of priority and ring-fence a prescribed part for unsecured creditors</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/part/14\"><strong>Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, Part 14</strong> &mdash; procedural framework for proofs of debt, set-off, valuation of claims, and distributions to creditors in a liquidation or administration</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/14/contents\"><strong>Finance Act 2020</strong> &mdash; reinstated HMRC as a secondary preferential creditor from 1 December 2020 for PAYE, NICs, VAT and CIS held on behalf of others</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2020/211/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986 (Prescribed Part) Order 2020</strong> &mdash; raised the prescribed-part cap to &pound;800,000, the slice reserved for unsecured creditors out of floating-charge realisations</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/": {
    "id": 46579,
    "slug": "bounce-back-loan-fraud",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- TITLE: Bounce Back Loan Fraud &amp; Misuse: What UK Company Directors Need to Know -->\n<!-- POST ID: 46579 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70172 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The question you most likely arrive at this page with is not \"am I a criminal?\", it is the quieter, more corrosive \"did I do something wrong without realising?\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An invoice paid out of the wrong account. A turnover figure rounded up because the accountant was on holiday. A personal bill settled from the business card in a week when nothing else was moving.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two or three years on, with your business shakier than it was in 2020, those moments surface in a different light for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So here is the honest sort of the categories for you. Not every breach of the <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/\">Bounce Back Loan</a> scheme rules is fraud. Not every misuse attracts prosecution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The scheme was handed out fast, under pressure, with light checks, and a proportion of the trouble sits in grey (misjudgement, poor record-keeping, honest misreading of the rules) rather than outright dishonesty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But the grey still carries consequences for you, and the part that tips into fraud carries far heavier ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What follows is the distinction the Insolvency Service, the courts, and (occasionally) the police actually use when they look at Bounce Back Loan conduct. In our practice, knowing where you sit on that map is the first step to doing something useful about it, and we spend a lot of our time helping directors map it honestly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Counts as Bounce Back Loan Fraud, and What Only Counts as Misuse</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Misuse and fraud are related, but they are not the same thing, and the gap between them matters enormously to you. Where you sit on this map shapes everything from your civil exposure to whether you face a police interview.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bounce Back Loan misuse is any use of the funds that sits outside the scheme rules. The governing rule is narrow and unforgiving: the money must be used to provide an economic benefit to the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Official guidance expressly prohibits using the loan for personal purposes. A director paying themselves a modest salary the business can genuinely afford is fine.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director using the loan to settle a personal credit card, buy a family car, or bankroll a holiday is not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fraud is a separate, sharper category. Under the <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/35/contents\">Fraud Act 2006</a>, fraud requires dishonesty plus intent to make a gain or cause loss. In Bounce Back Loan cases it typically looks like:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Knowingly inflating turnover on the application to secure a larger loan than the business was entitled to.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Applying for a second Bounce Back Loan for the same business through a different lender.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Applying for a loan on behalf of a company with no meaningful trading activity.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Deliberately moving loan funds to a personal account and later claiming it was a business payment.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pivot point is dishonesty. A director who misunderstood the self-certification question about turnover and rounded up, and who used every penny in the business, is in a different position from one who multiplied last year's turnover by four because \"the whole country was doing it\". Both have issues. Only the second reliably fits a fraud charge.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Genuine mistakes still need dealing with. They do not vanish because they were honest, and an Insolvency Practitioner will still flag them. But the route out of honest misuse is usually voluntary correction, repayment, and documented explanation, not the criminal-justice ladder.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"b38500b4-c47f-4fde-8345-410b55e42770\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Complete the form today to know how much it may cost and understand your next steps\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why Bounce Back Loan Fraud Matters Even Years After the Business Stopped Trading</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The enforcement window on Bounce Back Loan misconduct is not closing. The Insolvency Service continues to pursue director disqualification cases on BBL grounds, and the Crown Prosecution Service is still bringing fraud charges on older applications. The government spent roughly \u00a347bn under the scheme and carries a political incentive to pursue the tail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For directors, that means three things are still on the table:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Civil claims</strong>, brought by an Insolvency Practitioner after the company enters liquidation, seeking a personal contribution from the director for losses caused by misconduct.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Disqualification</strong>, a 2 to 15-year ban on acting as a director, frequently with an attached compensation order.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Criminal prosecution</strong>, in serious cases involving dishonesty, especially where the sums are substantial or the pattern is systematic.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reputational weight of any of those is significant. Even a civil <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/misfeasance/\">misfeasance</a> finding stays on the public record; a disqualification is published on the register and widely indexed; a criminal conviction travels into personal life in ways a company strike-off never does.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of that is intended as scare copy. It is the reason why understanding where your conduct sits on the map, and doing the uncomfortable repair work early, is worth far more than hoping the file stays closed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Waiting Actually Costs in a Bounce Back Loan Fraud Investigation</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The instinct when you suspect a problem is to hope it goes away. In Bounce Back Loan cases, that instinct is usually the most expensive choice on the menu.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company later becomes insolvent, a liquidator or administrator will examine how the loan was obtained and how the money moved. Bank statements, applications, director's loan accounts, and dividend history are all reviewed as a matter of course. Whatever you did not volunteer will be surfaced anyway, but without the mitigation that voluntary disclosure brings.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the officeholder finds issues, the options include claims for misfeasance or, in more serious cases, fraudulent trading under the <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/insolvency-act-1986/\">Insolvency Act 1986</a>. <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/directors-disqualification/\">Director disqualification</a> proceedings can follow. In the worst cases the matter is passed to <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/hmrc/hmrc-criminal-investigations/\">criminal investigation</a>, particularly where dishonesty and sums above \u00a3100,000 are involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trying to dissolve the company quietly is usually the clumsiest version of this. Lenders object, the strike-off is suspended, and the attempt itself gets read as evidence of evasion. It is a worse outcome than simply not acting.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"0c33e347-9bb0-4fca-8ac0-23de83cc1119\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Complete the form today to know how much it may cost and understand your next steps\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How a Bounce Back Loan Fraud Investigation Usually Starts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investigations rarely begin with a dawn raid. They begin with paperwork, usually years after the loan was drawn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The four common starting points:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Lender review</strong>, the bank identifies irregularities in recovery, finds that turnover on the application does not match filed accounts, or spots transfers outside the scheme rules.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Insolvency Practitioner report</strong>, every IP has a statutory duty to report director conduct to the Insolvency Service. BBL misuse is a specific question on that return.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Strike-off objection</strong>, a lender challenges a director's attempt to dissolve the company while the loan is outstanding, triggering a review.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Referral from another investigation</strong>, an HMRC VAT enquiry, a Companies House compliance action, or a criminal case involving the same director flags the BBL as a related concern.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enforcement is led by the <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/\">Insolvency Service</a>, which runs the bulk of director disqualification work. Serious cases are referred to criminal agencies. The National Investigation Service and regional police economic crime units pick up the Bounce Back Loan cases that cross the fraud threshold.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A dissolved company is not a closed chapter. Where dishonesty or public interest is identified, investigations can be opened against former directors of companies that are no longer on the register.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Civil Consequences of Bounce Back Loan Misconduct for Directors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The civil side is where most directors end up, and where the practical damage gets done. It does not generate headlines, but it often generates the bigger personal bill.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The main civil outcomes:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Misfeasance claim</strong>, an officeholder asks the court to order the director to repay losses caused by breaches of duty, including mishandling of BBL funds.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fraudulent trading</strong>, where the court finds the business was carried on with intent to defraud creditors. The bar is high, but the consequences are serious: personal liability for the company's debts, without an upper cap.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Preference claim</strong>, a reversal of payments made unfairly to favoured <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-a-creditor/\">creditors</a> (often the director's own loan account) in the run-up to insolvency.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Disqualification and compensation</strong>, a ban plus a court order requiring the director to contribute personally to creditor losses.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical scale is sobering. The <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-the-insolvency-service/\">Insolvency Service</a> has obtained compensation orders in BBL misuse cases of up to six figures where the loan funds were clearly misapplied, and bans in the upper end of the 2 to 15-year range for the most serious conduct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Bounce Back Loan Fraud: Criminal Offences and Penalties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The criminal route is reserved for cases with clear dishonesty. It is narrower than the civil tracks but carries the heaviest consequences.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most common charges in BBL cases:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fraud by false representation</strong>, false turnover figures, false declarations about trading status, false statements about sole lender status.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fraud by failing to disclose information</strong>, hiding that a second BBL application had been made or that the business had <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/cease-trading/\">ceased trading</a>.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>False accounting</strong>, manufactured invoices or book entries used to support the application or the scheme-compliant use of funds.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Conspiracy to defraud</strong>, where multiple parties are involved, typically seen in networks of shell companies.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On conviction, fraud offences can carry custodial sentences of up to ten years. Courts can also make confiscation orders under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, which allow the state to recover not just the misused loan amount but any assets acquired with the proceeds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Voluntary repayment of the loan does not prevent prosecution where fraud is established, but it is a meaningful mitigating factor at sentencing. The same is true of early cooperation. The directors who come out of these cases with the lightest outcomes almost all disclosed, repaid, and engaged long before they had to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"00ff2c76-4834-43fe-80de-6c198c85751f\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Complete the form today to know how much it may cost and understand your next steps\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Practical Steps for Directors Worried About Bounce Back Loan Misuse</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are reading this because something has been sitting uncomfortably at the back of your mind, the right response is not panic and it is not silence. It is a structured set of practical steps, in roughly this order.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Get a regulated adviser before anyone else.</strong> A solicitor with director-conduct experience, or a licensed insolvency practitioner, not a general accountant. The early conversation is privileged where legal, and it shapes every later step.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Pull the paperwork.</strong> The original application, the bank statements from the six months each side of the draw-down, the director's loan account movements, and the accounts filed for the relevant year. Do not edit or \"tidy\" anything.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Map the use of funds honestly.</strong> What went on payroll, rent, stock, and working capital, and what did not. The gap between the two is where the conversation lives.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Correct what can be corrected.</strong> If the turnover on the application was overstated, the Voluntary Repayment Scheme exists for exactly this. If money was personally extracted, reinstating it to the company before any insolvency changes the shape of any later challenge materially.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Document the reasoning.</strong> Contemporaneous board minutes and director notes carry weight months and years later. Honest records made now are worth more than explanations reconstructed in a witness box.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Engage, do not evade.</strong> Where a lender, the Insolvency Service, or an appointed IP comes calling, cooperate under advice. Avoidance always reads badly.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this makes your problem disappear. What it does is shift your story from one about concealment to one about correction, and that change of frame is often the difference between a closed file and a disqualification hearing for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Real Bounce Back Loan Enforcement Patterns</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The public record on BBL enforcement now runs to thousands of director disqualifications and a smaller, growing roll of criminal convictions. A few patterns recur strongly enough that they are worth naming, because most fresh enquiries we see on this page echo one of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Turnover inflated to obtain a higher loan.</strong> The most common disqualification pattern. Not every inflation is dishonest, but the ones that double or treble the genuine figure reliably cross the line.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Loan funds used for personal expenditure.</strong> Mortgage payments, car finance, personal credit cards, spouse's salary where no meaningful work was done.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Multiple loans taken through different lenders.</strong> Explicitly prohibited by the scheme rules, and almost always pursued criminally where identified.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Loans drawn down by companies that had ceased trading.</strong> These almost never have an innocent explanation and tend to reach the criminal track.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Outcomes vary with the sums involved, your cooperation, and the sharpness of the dishonesty. At the softer end, a short disqualification and a compensation order. At the harder end, custodial sentences in the three to seven-year range, plus confiscation orders. Our role in the middle ground is usually to keep clients out of the harder end by pushing for voluntary correction early.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on a Bounce Back Loan Concern</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If something on this page has identified itself to you, the inflated turnover, the personal spend, the second loan, the dormant company, the useful work is not more Googling.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a one-hour conversation with a regulated adviser who knows this specific area.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From that conversation you will come out knowing whether you are in civil territory, in criminal territory, or, as often as not, in the grey where voluntary correction puts the issue to bed before it starts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The directors we see do that first, before any letter arrives, almost always walk away with the least damage. The ones who wait for the letter usually pay a multiple of what honest early action would have cost. Call us free on 0800 074 6757; our team will run through your specific facts in confidence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by our editorial team and reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners. We reflect UK insolvency, fraud, and proceeds of crime law as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<p>Our statutory references are the Bounce Back Loan Scheme rules (British Business Bank), the Insolvency Act 1986, the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, the Fraud Act 2006, the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, and the Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021.</p>\n<p>We update our position as the case law evolves, and our licensed IPs apply these rules to live cases every week.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where a CVL or Administration is the right answer, we can act as the licensed IP under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.british-business-bank.co.uk/ourpartners/coronavirus-business-interruption-loan-schemes/bounce-back-loans/\">British Business Bank - Bounce Back Loan Scheme rules</a> - british-business-bank.co.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 212, 213, 214, 238, 239)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\">Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/35/contents\">Fraud Act 2006</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2002/29/contents\">Proceeds of Crime Act 2002</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2021/34/contents\">Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\">Insolvency Service - guidance for directors and disqualification</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Bounce Back Loan Fraud</h2>\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-46579\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>If some of the loan was used for personal bills, is that automatically fraud?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-46579\">\n<p>No. Using funds for personal purposes is a breach of scheme rules, which is misuse. Fraud requires dishonesty and intent. The distinction matters: misuse tends to land in civil proceedings, fraud in criminal. Either can still have serious consequences, but the tracks are different.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Am I personally liable if the company goes insolvent?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-46579\">\n<p>The loan is a company debt. Directors become personally exposed only where misconduct, misfeasance, or fraud is established. Legitimate business failure without those factors does not produce personal liability for the BBL.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does the government guarantee remove my obligation to repay?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-46579\">\n<p>No. The guarantee reimburses the lender after recovery. The business remains contractually liable for the loan throughout.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What if the company has already been dissolved?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-46579\">\n<p>Dissolution does not close the door on investigation. The Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021 specifically extended investigatory powers to cover former directors of dissolved companies where BBL misuse is suspected.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I face criminal charges for unintentional misuse?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-46579\">\n<p>Criminal fraud requires dishonest intent. Genuine, documented mistakes are not fraud. They can still produce civil consequences and need to be addressed properly, but the criminal threshold is not met without dishonesty.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How long can I be investigated for Bounce Back Loan misuse?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-46579\">\n<p>Disqualification proceedings must generally be brought within three years of the company becoming insolvent, but the clock starts on insolvency, not on the loan draw-down. Fraud offences have no time limit for prosecution.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-6\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-6-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What if my reported turnover was only slightly incorrect?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-6-faq-46579\">\n<p>Minor errors, made in good faith, are rarely treated as fraud. Significant overstatements, especially where the correct figure would have led to a materially smaller loan or no loan at all, are treated very differently. Voluntary correction through the repayment scheme is the cleanest response.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-7\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-7-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can insolvency wipe out the loan debt?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-7-faq-46579\">\n<p>A clean liquidation, with no misconduct findings, does write off the remaining BBL balance as part of the insolvency. Misconduct breaks that protection. Fraud breaks it harder.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-8\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-8-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is there an amnesty for Bounce Back Loan fraud?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-8-faq-46579\">\n<p>No. Voluntary repayment and cooperation are mitigating factors, and can be decisive at the civil end of the spectrum, but there is no formal amnesty programme.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-9\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-9-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Do the rules differ for sole traders?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-9-faq-46579\">\n<p>Sole traders are personally liable for the loan from the outset, because there is no separate legal entity. The same fraud and misuse principles apply to the application and use of funds; only the liability structure on repayment differs.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-46579-10\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-10-faq-46579\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-46579\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Will partial repayment reduce potential penalties?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-10-faq-46579\">\n<p>Partial repayment is a recognised mitigating factor, particularly where the repayment is of the misused portion and is accompanied by cooperation and early advice. It is not a guarantee of immunity, and it does not turn fraud into misuse. It can materially soften the outcome at both the civil and criminal end.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-script-loader-46579\",\"className\":\"ub-hide\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":0,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eFAQ script loader\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>FAQ script loader.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/what-happens-if-i-default/\">What Happens If I Default on a Bounce Back Loan?</a>: the difference between not paying and misusing.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-wrongful-trading/\">Wrongful Trading</a>: the conduct test a liquidator applies to directors.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: the formal route to close a company with a BBL.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/company-strike-off-and-dissolution/\">Company Strike-Off and Dissolution</a>: why dissolution is not an escape from an investigated loan.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/advice/overdrawn-directors-loan-accounts/\">Overdrawn Directors' Loan Accounts</a>: where BBL funds drawn personally usually end up.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->"
  },
  "/liquidation/what-happens-to-directors-in-liquidation/": {
    "id": 46222,
    "slug": "what-happens-to-directors-in-liquidation",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<article>\n<!-- TITLE: What Happens to a Director When a Company Goes Into Liquidation? -->\n<!-- POST ID: 46222 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70117 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/liquidation/what-happens-to-directors-in-liquidation/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When an insolvent company enters liquidation, its directors normally lose control of the business and the liquidator takes over its assets and affairs. You hand over the company's records and property, answer reasonable questions and cooperate. That much is the same in almost every case. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/insolvency-and-the-directors-of-a-limited-company\">GOV.UK sets out the position</a> for directors of an insolvent company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What does not happen automatically is the part directors lie awake over. Liquidation does not, by itself, make you personally liable for the company's debts, disqualify you, make you bankrupt or stop you running another company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Personal exposure needs a separate legal basis: a personal guarantee, an overdrawn director's loan account, wrongful or fraudulent trading, misfeasance, a prohibited-name breach or a qualifying HMRC notice. GOV.UK's <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/director-information-hub-understanding-the-difference-between-personal-and-company-debts\">Director Information Hub</a> draws the same line between company debts and personal ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The office-holder does have to report on the conduct of the company's directors, normally within three months of the formal insolvency. That report is routine in an insolvent liquidation. It is not a finding that you did anything wrong, and it is not the same as being investigated. GOV.UK explains the duty in <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/report-misconduct-as-a-director-of-a-company-in-liquidation\">its guidance on reporting director conduct</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide covers insolvent liquidation, mainly <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a> and <a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">compulsory liquidation</a>, in England, Wales and Scotland. A solvent <a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\">Members' Voluntary Liquidation</a> is a different thing, and Northern Ireland has its own separate insolvency legislation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-happens-to-a-director-in-liquidation-the-short-answer\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-happens-to-a-director-in-liquidation-the-short-answer\">What Happens to a Director in Liquidation: the Short Answer</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By the time a director calls us, the questions have moved past the company itself. They are about the house, the family, and whether this follows them for years. Here is the position on the questions that matter most, before any of the detail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Your question</th><th>The usual position</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Do I stay in control of the company?</td><td>No. The liquidator takes control, and you stop acting for the company unless they authorise it</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/insolvency-and-the-directors-of-a-limited-company\">GOV.UK</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Do I become personally liable for the company's debts?</td><td>No, not automatically. A separate contractual, statutory or court basis is normally needed</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/director-information-hub-understanding-the-difference-between-personal-and-company-debts\">Director Information Hub</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Will my conduct be reported?</td><td>Yes. In an insolvent liquidation the office-holder normally reports within three months</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/report-misconduct-as-a-director-of-a-company-in-liquidation\">Reporting director conduct</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Does the report mean I am being investigated?</td><td>No. A further Insolvency Service investigation is a separate stage that does not always follow</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/insolvent-company-investigations-what-happens-when-a-company-is-liquidated\">Insolvent company investigations</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can I be a director again?</td><td>Yes, unless you are disqualified, bankrupt or otherwise restricted</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/insolvency-and-the-directors-of-a-limited-company\">GOV.UK</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can I use the old company's name?</td><td>Restrictions can apply for five years after an insolvent liquidation</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/re-use-of-company-names/re-use-of-company-names\">Re-use of company names</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can I claim redundancy?</td><td>Possibly, if you were genuinely an employee and meet the eligibility tests</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/claim-redundancy\">GOV.UK redundancy</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-happens-immediately-when-a-liquidator-is-appointed\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-happens-immediately-when-a-liquidator-is-appointed\">What Happens Immediately When a Liquidator Is Appointed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From the moment the liquidator is appointed, your authority to run the company ends. You cannot make payments, sign contracts or move company money without their say-so. In place of your old executive role comes a narrower and non-negotiable one: the duty to cooperate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Section 235 of the Insolvency Act 1986</strong> requires you to give the office-holder the information about the company's affairs they reasonably need, attend on them when reasonably required, and deliver up the records and property that belong to the company. This is a legal duty, not a request, and failing it is treated seriously. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/235\">Insolvency Act 1986, s235</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>What you normally lose</th><th>What you must still do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Control of company bank accounts and assets</td><td>Deliver books, records, passwords and asset information</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Power to trade, contract or pay without authority</td><td>Answer reasonable questions and attend when required</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Authority to sell or transfer company property</td><td>Disclose guarantees, connected-party dealings and loan accounts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Day-to-day decision-making for the company</td><td>Preserve records and cooperate throughout the case</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In practice the liquidator asks for bank statements, board minutes, financial records, creditor and debtor lists, details of any asset transfers, and an account of specific decisions: why this supplier was paid in March, say, when the VAT was already three months late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There may be an interview, or, in a compulsory case, a questionnaire and a meeting to talk the last two years through with the official receiver. You are entitled to a solicitor at a formal interview, and if the sums or the questions are serious, use one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Having your records in order and handing them over promptly helps demonstrate cooperation and the steps you took to protect creditors. Our <a href=\"/liquidation/list-of-liquidation-documents/\">liquidation documents checklist</a> shows what to gather.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"does-liquidation-make-a-director-personally-liable\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"does-liquidation-make-a-director-personally-liable\">Does Liquidation Make a Director Personally Liable?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No, not automatically. A limited company is a separate legal person, so its debts do not become yours simply because it goes into liquidation. Each route in the table below needs a separate fact to be true before you are exposed at all. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/director-information-hub-understanding-the-difference-between-personal-and-company-debts\">Director Information Hub: personal and company debts</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Possible exposure</th><th>Created automatically by liquidation?</th><th>When it can arise</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Ordinary company trade debt</td><td>No</td><td>Stays a company liability unless a separate route applies</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/director-information-hub-understanding-the-difference-between-personal-and-company-debts\">GOV.UK</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personal guarantee</td><td>No. It was already a separate obligation</td><td>A valid guarantee covers borrowing, rent, supplies or another debt</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/personal-guarantees\">GOV.UK</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overdrawn director's loan account</td><td>The debt already exists</td><td>You owe money to the company and the liquidator seeks repayment</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/fact-sheet-directors-loan-accounts/fact-sheet-directors-loan-accounts\">Insolvency Service</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wrongful trading</td><td>No</td><td>The s214 test is met and a court orders a contribution</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\">IA 1986, s214</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fraudulent trading</td><td>No</td><td>The business was run with intent to defraud creditors</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/213\">IA 1986, s213</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Misfeasance or breach of duty</td><td>No</td><td>Company money or property was misapplied or mishandled</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/212\">IA 1986, s212</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transaction at an undervalue or preference</td><td>No</td><td>The statutory conditions for a remedy are met</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/238\">IA 1986, s238</a> / <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/239\">s239</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>HMRC personal or joint liability</td><td>No</td><td>A specific statutory notice and its conditions apply</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/national-insurance-manual/nim12203\">HMRC</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prohibited company name</td><td>No</td><td>Sections 216 to 217 are breached</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/216\">IA 1986, s216</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Acting while disqualified</td><td>No</td><td>You take part in management in breach of an order or undertaking</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/15\">CDDA 1986, s15</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Personal guarantees.</strong> Liquidation does not cancel a personal guarantee, and may prompt the lender to enforce it, depending on the terms of the guarantee and the underlying facility.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But whether you actually owe, and how much, depends on the document, often one signed across a lender's desk years back, when the facility felt like a formality. Dig it out and check who it is owed to, what it covers, whether liability is capped, and what it says about interest, costs and security.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our guide to <a href=\"/advice/directors-personal-guarantees/\">directors' personal guarantees</a> works through how to check yours, including the terms that decide whether, and how far, it can be enforced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Overdrawn director's loan account.</strong> If you have drawn more from the company than you put in or were paid, the overdrawn balance is an asset of the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The liquidator will normally consider recovery of the balance, and may pursue repayment or agree an appropriate settlement, having weighed the evidence, the costs and the likely return to creditors. We cover the options in <a href=\"/advice/overdrawn-directors-loan-accounts/\">overdrawn directors' loan accounts</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"wrongful-trading-fraudulent-trading-and-misfeasance\"} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"wrongful-trading-fraudulent-trading-and-misfeasance\">Wrongful trading, fraudulent trading and misfeasance</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Wrongful trading (section 214)</strong> is the one that worries directors most.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It applies where you carried on trading after the point when you knew, or ought to have concluded, that there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation or administration, and did not take every step you should have to minimise the loss to creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a court is satisfied of that, it can order you to contribute to the company's assets. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\">IA 1986, s214</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is no automatic sum. It does not follow that a \u00a350,000 increase in the deficiency means a \u00a350,000 bill. The court decides whether a contribution is right at all and, if so, how much, weighing when you should have acted, what you knew, and what steps you took.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is fact-specific, and it is not a routine consequence of liquidation. Our guide on <a href=\"/liquidation/when-should-a-director-stop-trading/\">when to stop trading</a> explains where the line falls.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--legal-exposure\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>What most directors get wrong about wrongful trading</strong></p>\n<p>The fear is that any trading while insolvent is automatically wrongful. It is not: the test is whether there was a reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation, and a properly advised rescue or controlled wind-down can involve trading on.</p>\n<p>Risk increases where trading continues, creditor losses grow, and you cannot show a reasonable, documented basis for your decisions and the steps you took to minimise loss.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Fraudulent trading (section 213)</strong> is different in kind. It requires proof of dishonest intent, a purpose of defrauding creditors: hiding assets, raising false invoices, taking deposits for orders you knew you could not fill. It is distinct from ordinary commercial failure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where it is met, the court can order a personal contribution and the same conduct can be prosecuted as a crime. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/213\">IA 1986, s213</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/993\">Companies Act 2006, s993</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Misfeasance (section 212)</strong> is not another name for wrongful trading.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a statutory route through which a liquidator, or another eligible applicant, can seek repayment, restoration or compensation from a director who has misapplied or retained company money or property, or breached a duty, for example by taking excessive remuneration or authorising a payment that was not in creditors' interests. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/212\">IA 1986, s212</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Preferences and transactions at an undervalue sit alongside these. If the company favoured one creditor, or sold an asset for less than it was worth, the liquidator can ask the court to unwind it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Neither is automatically a crime and neither automatically makes you personally liable; each has statutory conditions the office-holder has to establish. We explain the fuller picture in <a href=\"/advice/are-directors-personally-liable-for-company-debts/\">whether directors are personally liable for company debts</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"can-hmrc-make-a-director-personally-liable\"} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"can-hmrc-make-a-director-personally-liable\">Can HMRC make a director personally liable?</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sometimes, but not through one general power, and not just because the company owed tax. These are three separate mechanisms with different conditions, and it is worth keeping them apart because they are routinely muddled together.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it concerns</th><th>What it does not mean</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/national-insurance-manual/nim12203\">NIC personal liability notice</a></td><td>Unpaid company National Insurance where non-payment is attributable to a director's fraud or serious neglect</td><td>Unpaid NICs do not routinely transfer to every director</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/overview-of-joint-and-several-liability-notices-for-tax-avoidance-tax-evasion-and-repeated-insolvency\">Finance Act 2020 joint-and-several-liability notice</a></td><td>Specified avoidance, evasion and repeated-insolvency cases meeting the statutory conditions</td><td>It does not apply merely because a company has tax arrears</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/compliance-handbook/ch75500\">VAT-related personal liability</a></td><td>Personal liability for specified penalties involving deliberate conduct</td><td>Unpaid VAT itself does not automatically become your personal debt</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"will-your-conduct-be-reported-and-will-you-be-investigated\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"will-your-conduct-be-reported-and-will-you-be-investigated\">Will Your Conduct Be Reported, and Will You Be Investigated?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is where two separate stages are often confused. A conduct report is routine. A formal investigation is not, and they are better seen as a sequence than a single verdict hanging over you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1. Office-holder review</td><td>Records, transactions, assets and the causes of failure are examined</td><td>A normal part of every insolvent liquidation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. Director conduct report</td><td>The office-holder normally reports within three months, covering conduct in the preceding three years</td><td>A report, not a verdict or an automatic accusation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Possible further action</td><td>The Insolvency Service may seek more and consider disqualification where evidence warrants</td><td>Further investigation and proceedings are not automatic</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The report is built from the facts of how the company was run: when it became insolvent and when the directors knew or should have known; whether trading continued past the point of no return; whether there were preferences or transactions at undervalue; whether records and filings were kept up; whether Crown debts were allowed to grow; and whether the directors cooperated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From that, the Insolvency Service decides whether the conduct crosses the threshold of unfitness. GOV.UK sets out how this works in <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/insolvent-company-investigations-what-happens-when-a-company-is-liquidated\">insolvent company investigations</a>, and we cover it in depth on our <a href=\"/liquidation/director-conduct-review/\">director conduct review</a> page.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two things not to do. Do not read the conduct report as a finding of guilt: it is a routine part of the process, and does not by itself mean disqualification will follow. And do not treat silence as the all-clear.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is no reliable published timetable for a decision, so the honest answer to \"how long until I know?\" is that it varies, and hearing nothing for a while is not confirmation that a file is closed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-three-clocks-that-get-confused\"} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-three-clocks-that-get-confused\">The three clocks that get confused</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most pages, and a fair few advisers, blur three separate time limits into one. They are not the same clock, and getting them straight tells you what actually applies to you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Clock</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Three-year conduct look-back</td><td>The report covers each relevant director's conduct in the three years before insolvency, and can reach former and shadow directors</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/7A\">CDDA 1986, s7A</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Three-month report deadline</td><td>The office-holder normally submits the conduct report within three months of the formal insolvency</td><td><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/report-misconduct-as-a-director-of-a-company-in-liquidation\">Reporting director conduct</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Three-year proceedings limit</td><td>Section 6 disqualification proceedings must normally begin within three years of the company becoming insolvent, subject to the court allowing a later application</td><td><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/7\">CDDA 1986, s7</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"can-a-director-be-disqualified-after-liquidation\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"can-a-director-be-disqualified-after-liquidation\">Can a Director Be Disqualified After Liquidation?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disqualification is not an automatic consequence of liquidation. It can follow where conduct is judged unfit or another statutory ground applies. Under <strong>section 6 of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong>, the period runs from <strong>two to 15 years</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/6\">CDDA 1986, s6</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It comes about by a court order or a voluntary undertaking. The kinds of conduct that can count as unfit include trading on well past the point of no return, failing to keep proper records, not paying over Crown deductions, and preferring connected creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those are examples of what a court may weigh, not proof of anything in your case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The window to start proceedings normally runs for three years from the company becoming insolvent, subject to the court's permission for a later application, not from the conduct report and not from dissolution. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/7\">CDDA 1986, s7</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During a disqualification you cannot act as a director, or take part in promoting, forming or managing a company, without the court's permission. Acting in breach is a criminal offence, and it can make you personally responsible for the relevant company debts incurred while you were acting in contravention, not for every debt the company ever had. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/15\">CDDA 1986, s15</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the Insolvency Service contacts you about your conduct, take specialist advice quickly. You can offer an undertaking rather than fight the case in court, which avoids the cost and publicity of a hearing. Be wary, though, of anyone who promises to negotiate the length down as if it were a haggle: the outcome turns on the facts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our guide to <a href=\"/advice/directors-disqualification/\">directors' disqualification</a> covers the process, and the <a href=\"/liquidation/whats-the-risk-of-being-disqualified-as-a-director/\">real level of risk</a> for an ordinary director.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"can-you-start-or-run-another-company-after-liquidation\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"can-you-start-or-run-another-company-after-liquidation\">Can You Start or Run Another Company After Liquidation?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Normally, yes. Liquidation does not stop you forming, owning or managing another company. The main exceptions are disqualification, bankruptcy restrictions, and the rules on reusing the insolvent company's name. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/insolvency-and-the-directors-of-a-limited-company\">GOV.UK: insolvency and the directors of a limited company</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two cautions matter. First, you cannot transfer the old company's assets, business, goodwill, data or contractual rights to a new company without a properly authorised transaction at an appropriate value; customers, of course, remain free to decide whom they trade with. Second, and this one trips up honest directors, there is the prohibited-name rule.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under <strong>section 216 of the Insolvency Act 1986</strong>, if you were a director in the 12 months before an insolvent liquidation, you can be restricted for <strong>five years</strong> from being involved in a business that uses the same name as the old company, or a name so similar it suggests a connection, unless a statutory exception applies or the court gives permission.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Breach is a criminal offence, and it can make you personally responsible for the relevant debts of the successor business. <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/216\">IA 1986, s216</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/217\">s217</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The exceptions are technical and worth advice before you trade or advertise under a familiar name. Starting again is legitimate, provided the assets, name and business transfer are handled lawfully. We cover the ground in <a href=\"/advice/what-are-phoenix-companies/\">phoenix companies and reusing a company name</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"can-a-director-claim-redundancy-pay\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"can-a-director-claim-redundancy-pay\">Can a Director Claim Redundancy Pay?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You may be able to. A director who was also genuinely an employee of the company can claim statutory redundancy and other insolvency payments from the Redundancy Payments Service. Being a director, holding shares, or being paid through PAYE does not settle it on its own: the service looks at the real working relationship and the evidence behind it. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/claim-redundancy\">GOV.UK: claim money when a business is insolvent</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where you qualify, the payments can include redundancy pay, unpaid wages within statutory limits, holiday pay and statutory notice pay.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For dismissals on or after <strong>6 April 2026</strong>, the weekly pay used to work these out is capped at <strong>\u00a3751</strong> (checked July 2026), and statutory redundancy counts a maximum of 20 years' service. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/calculate-your-redundancy-pay\">GOV.UK: calculate your redundancy pay</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You normally have six months from dismissal to apply through the Redundancy Payments Service. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/claim-redundancy\">GOV.UK: claim money when a business is insolvent</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The evidence the service looks for is the ordinary stuff of employment: a contract, payslips and PAYE records, a real set of duties and hours, holiday arrangements, and signs the relationship went beyond simply holding the office of director.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you were on the tools all week, or doing the books at the kitchen table on a Sunday as well as sitting on the board, that working reality is what the claim turns on. We set out the detail, including the specific rules for an <a href=\"/director-redundancy/\">MVL and other closures, on our director redundancy guide</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-should-a-director-do-before-liquidation\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-should-a-director-do-before-liquidation\">What Should a Director Do Before Liquidation?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Much of what determines your personal position is decided before a liquidator is ever appointed, in the weeks when the payroll only just clears and the VAT return you cannot pay is already sitting drafted on the screen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once insolvency looks likely, your duty shifts towards creditors: protect the company's assets, avoid making their losses worse, and get advice. That is not the same as the blunt instruction to down tools the moment you are insolvent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Continuing to trade is not automatically wrongful, and a properly advised rescue, sale or controlled wind-down may involve trading on. Trading blindly, without a plan or advice, is what raises the risk. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/director-information-hub-director-duties-upon-insolvency\">Director Information Hub: duties on insolvency</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\">IA 1986, s214</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Preserve the records: accounts, bank statements, contracts, payroll and digital access.</li>\n<li>Protect company property, and do not move or conceal assets.</li>\n<li>Do not repay connected parties or favour selected creditors without advice.</li>\n<li>Do not take new deposits or credit where you are unlikely to deliver and losses would grow.</li>\n<li>Write down the reasons for material board decisions, and keep the forecasts and advice you relied on.</li>\n<li>Find your personal guarantees and get copies of the signed documents.</li>\n<li>Establish your director's loan account balance.</li>\n<li>List recent asset disposals, dividends and payments to connected parties.</li>\n<li>Get advice from a licensed insolvency practitioner promptly.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most useful step is also the earliest: taking advice while options still exist. Evidence of when you sought advice may help show what information was available and what steps you took to minimise creditor losses. Our guide on <a href=\"/advice/directors-duties-to-creditors/\">directors' duties to creditors</a> explains what that shift actually requires of you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-usually-does-not-happen-to-a-director\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-usually-does-not-happen-to-a-director\">What Usually Does Not Happen to a Director</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is worth stating the reassuring side plainly, because the fear runs ahead of the facts. These are the assumptions we find ourselves correcting again and again.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>The worry</th><th>The usual position</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>\"I have to pay every company debt myself\"</td><td>No. A separate personal-liability route is normally needed first</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"The conduct report means I have been found guilty\"</td><td>No. Reporting is routine and is not a finding of misconduct</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"I am automatically disqualified\"</td><td>No. Disqualification is not an automatic consequence of liquidation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"I can never run another company\"</td><td>No, unless you are disqualified, bankrupt or otherwise restricted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Liquidation automatically takes my home\"</td><td>No. That needs a separate obligation, security, judgment, bankruptcy or enforcement route</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"My credit record is automatically wrecked\"</td><td>Liquidation does not itself transfer company debts to your credit record. Separate events, such as guarantee enforcement, personal defaults, court judgments or personal insolvency, may affect it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Choosing a CVL admits wrongdoing\"</td><td>No. Choosing a CVL can be the responsible course once insolvent liquidation is unavoidable</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"is-compulsory-liquidation-or-an-mvl-different\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"is-compulsory-liquidation-or-an-mvl-different\">Is Compulsory Liquidation or an MVL Different?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The core position, loss of control and a duty to cooperate, is the same across the insolvent routes. What changes is who is driving and how much say you had in the timing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Procedure</th><th>Solvent?</th><th>Where the director stands</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a></td><td>No</td><td>Control passes to the liquidator; conduct reporting and the personal-exposure routes apply</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">Compulsory liquidation</a></td><td>No</td><td>A court petition, often brought by a creditor, drives the timetable; the official receiver normally acts initially; the same duty to cooperate applies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\">MVL</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>The company expects to pay its debts in full, with interest, within 12 months; the insolvent-conduct framing does not normally apply</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In a <strong>compulsory liquidation</strong>, there may still be a questionnaire and an interview with the official receiver. The core position is unchanged; the timing simply was not yours to set, the way it would be in a CVL.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An <strong>MVL is a solvent liquidation</strong>. It is supported by a declaration that the company can pay its debts in full, with interest, within 12 months, so the risk-heavy conduct framing on this page is not the normal starting point. If your company is solvent and you are closing it down, an MVL is a different route entirely. <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/liquidate-your-company\">GOV.UK: liquidate a company</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-to-do-now\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-to-do-now\">What to Do Now</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have read this far, you have a rough sense of where you stand. The next step is to check it against your own facts with someone who does this for a living, before creditors or HMRC set the timetable for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are not sure whether you carry any personal exposure, that is exactly the thing to check first. Speak to a licensed insolvency practitioner about the company's position, any guarantee or loan account, and the safest next step.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Advice can clarify the options straight away; formal protection from creditor action depends on which procedure you use and how far things have gone, so an early conversation is not the same as pressing a button that stops HMRC. <a href=\"/liquidation/\">Expert liquidation advice</a> is the place to start.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"frequently-asked-questions\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"7d2c8a91-3e74-4f63-bd85-c1f4a2e8d70e\",\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill I lose my house if my company is liquidated?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Liquidation does not itself transfer ownership of your home or make it available to company creditors. It may become exposed where there is a separate personal liability, security or enforcement route, for example a secured personal guarantee, a personal judgment, a charging order or personal bankruptcy.</p>\n<p>Limited liability normally separates your assets from the company's debts, but it does not protect you from a separate personal obligation or liability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes liquidation affect my personal credit rating?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The company's liquidation is the company's insolvency, not yours, and does not by itself transfer its debts to your personal credit file. Your personal credit can still be affected where a separate personal liability arises, for example an enforced guarantee, a judgment against you, or personal bankruptcy. If you are worried about a specific account, check your own credit report directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I be a director of another company during or after liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, unless you are disqualified, subject to bankruptcy restrictions, or the new company would breach the section 216 rule on reusing the old company's name. Being a director of a company in liquidation does not by itself stop you holding other directorships. If you are later disqualified, all of them are affected.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I use the same company name again?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not freely. If you were a director in the 12 months before an insolvent liquidation, section 216 can restrict you for five years from using the old company's name or a name so similar it suggests a connection, unless an exception applies or the court permits it. Breach carries criminal and personal-liability consequences, so take advice before trading under a familiar name.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the difference between wrongful and fraudulent trading?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Wrongful trading (section 214) is a civil claim. It turns on continuing to trade when you should have concluded insolvent liquidation was unavoidable, and failing to minimise creditor losses. No dishonesty is required.</p>\n<p>Fraudulent trading (section 213) requires proof of dishonest intent and carries both civil and criminal consequences. The two are distinct, and ordinary commercial failure is not fraud.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes resigning as a director before liquidation protect me?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. Resignation does not erase conduct during your time in office. Wrongful trading, preferences, misfeasance, a personal guarantee, an overdrawn loan account and section 216 name issues all survive it. The office-holder can still investigate a former director, and the Insolvency Service can disqualify one, for the same period. We cover this in <a href=\"/liquidation/what-happens-if-a-director-resigns-before-liquidation/\">resigning before liquidation</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a director be prosecuted after liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Only in serious cases involving dishonesty or a clear breach, such as fraudulent trading, acting while disqualified, or breaching the prohibited-name rule. Ordinary company debt, and an honest business that simply failed, are not criminal and do not lead to prosecution.</p>\n<p>We cover the rare end of the spectrum in <a href=\"/liquidation/can-directors-go-to-prison-for-company-debt/\">can directors go to prison for company debt</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":7,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes this guidance apply in Northern Ireland?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>This page covers England, Wales and Scotland. Northern Ireland has its own separate insolvency legislation and procedures, so the specific section numbers and processes differ, even though the broad shape is similar. Take local advice if the company is registered in Northern Ireland.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"related-guides\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"related-guides\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are the pages we point directors to once we have talked through where they actually stand. Each goes deeper than we can here on a single exposure or decision.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"/advice/are-directors-personally-liable-for-company-debts/\">Are directors personally liable for company debts?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/advice/directors-personal-guarantees/\">Directors' personal guarantees</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/advice/overdrawn-directors-loan-accounts/\">Overdrawn directors' loan accounts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/advice/directors-disqualification/\">Directors' disqualification</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/director-conduct-review/\">Director conduct review</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/when-should-a-director-stop-trading/\">When should a director stop trading?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/director-redundancy/\">Director redundancy</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/advice/what-are-phoenix-companies/\">Phoenix companies and reusing a company name</a></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>Written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association.</p>\n<p>This guide covers company liquidation in England, Wales and Scotland; Northern Ireland has separate legislation.</p>\n<p>Statutory references draw on the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 212 to 214, 216 to 217, 235, 238 to 239), the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 (sections 6, 7, 7A and 15), the Companies Act 2006 (section 993), the Social Security Administration Act 1992 (section 121C), the Finance Act 2020 and Insolvency Service, HMRC and GOV.UK guidance.</p>\n<p>Legislation, official guidance and financial thresholds last checked 30 July 2026. The \u00a3751 statutory weekly pay cap applies to dismissals on or after 6 April 2026 and is reviewed each April.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner, and we may receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/insolvency-and-the-directors-of-a-limited-company\"><strong>GOV.UK &mdash; insolvency and the directors of a limited company</strong> &mdash; what happens to directors when a company becomes insolvent</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/director-information-hub-understanding-the-difference-between-personal-and-company-debts\"><strong>Director Information Hub &mdash; personal versus company debts</strong> &mdash; the line between company liabilities and personal exposure</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/report-misconduct-as-a-director-of-a-company-in-liquidation\"><strong>Insolvency Service &mdash; reporting director conduct</strong> &mdash; the office-holder's three-month conduct report</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/insolvent-company-investigations-what-happens-when-a-company-is-liquidated\"><strong>Insolvency Service &mdash; insolvent company investigations</strong> &mdash; how the review and any further investigation work</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, s214</strong> &mdash; wrongful trading and the court's contribution power</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/213\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, s213</strong> &mdash; fraudulent trading</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/212\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, s212</strong> &mdash; misfeasance and breach of duty</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/216\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, ss216 to 217</strong> &mdash; restriction on re-use of an insolvent company's name and personal liability for breach</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/235\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, s235</strong> &mdash; the director's duty to cooperate with the office-holder</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/6\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, ss6, 7, 7A and 15</strong> &mdash; unfit-conduct disqualification, the proceedings window, conduct reporting and liability for acting while disqualified</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/company-director-disqualification\"><strong>GOV.UK &mdash; company director disqualification</strong> &mdash; how disqualification and undertakings work</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/personal-guarantees\"><strong>GOV.UK &mdash; personal guarantees</strong> &mdash; how a guarantee survives the company's insolvency</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/fact-sheet-directors-loan-accounts/fact-sheet-directors-loan-accounts\"><strong>Insolvency Service &mdash; directors' loan accounts fact sheet</strong> &mdash; recovery of an overdrawn loan account</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/national-insurance-manual/nim12203\"><strong>HMRC NIM12203</strong> &mdash; National Insurance personal liability notices</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/overview-of-joint-and-several-liability-notices-for-tax-avoidance-tax-evasion-and-repeated-insolvency\"><strong>HMRC &mdash; joint-and-several-liability notices</strong> &mdash; Finance Act 2020 director liability conditions</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/claim-redundancy\"><strong>GOV.UK &mdash; claim money when a business is insolvent</strong> &mdash; redundancy and other payments for director-employees</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/calculate-your-redundancy-pay\"><strong>GOV.UK &mdash; calculate your redundancy pay</strong> &mdash; the statutory weekly cap and service limits</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/liquidate-your-company\"><strong>GOV.UK &mdash; liquidate a company</strong> &mdash; solvent MVL and the declaration of solvency</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n</article>"
  },
  "/content-usage-guidelines/": {
    "id": 45771,
    "slug": "content-usage-guidelines",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Our Editorial Policy</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>All articles on insolvency are written by our inhouse licensed insolvency practitioners whose biographies and accreditations can be seen here: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/meet-the-team/</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":60739,\"sizeSlug\":\"full\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img src=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Company-Rescue-Options-for-Businesses-in-Financial-Difficulty-6.jpg\" alt=\"Editorial Policy &amp; Content Usage Guidelines\" class=\"wp-image-60739\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our mission is to provide the most comprehensive, well structured and easily understandable information on debt, insolvency and wider business matters on the web.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We always provide links to primary source material such as HM &amp; Revenue's own website, Acts of the UK Parliament, Scottish Parliament, Welsh Parliament and the Northern Ireland Assembly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3><strong>What Company Debt Content Can You Use? And How?</strong></h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>You are welcome to share links to any of our content by email and social media. </li><li>Feel free to republish in full any Company Debt-original images (such as charts, graphs, infographics, videos, or SlideShares) by copying or embedding them and including them in your content, as long as you honor the Content Attribution Policy below. (Note: This does&nbsp;<em>not&nbsp;</em>include any stock images we've purchased for use in our content.)</li><li>Unfortunately, we can't<strong> </strong>let you republish in full any of our text-based content on the web. Why? Well, duplicate content is just bad for SEO, and Google will not appreciate it.</li><li>You cannot claim our content as your original ideas (i.e. using our content and publishing it on a channel as your own). We love that you want to share it, but please just share the original link instead. Thank you!</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3><strong>Content Attribution Policy</strong></h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li><strong>Attribute</strong> Company Debt as the source.</li><li><strong>Link to the original</strong> Company Debt source you're referencing:<ul><li>For references to a Company Debt blog article, link to the URL of the specific blog article you're referencing.</li><li>For references to Company Debt downloadable content offers behind a form, please link to the landing page URL with the form for that individual offer.</li></ul></li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->"
  },
  "/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-staff-wages/": {
    "id": 44055,
    "slug": "cant-pay-staff-wages",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Failing to run payroll on time is not comparable to missing any other creditor. An unpaid supplier sends a chasing email. Unpaid employees go to the CAB by lunchtime, the BBC regional newsroom by tea, and employment tribunal services the next morning. We see the commercial damage compound at a pace no other single event in a limited company's life can match.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below we cover what a UK company director is legally required to do when your business cannot fund payroll, how the Redundancy Payments Service safety net interacts with formal insolvency, and where the director duty shift under the Insolvency Act 1986 narrows your options for continued trading.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"why-inability-to-pay-staff-wages-signals-company-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"why-inability-to-pay-staff-wages-signals-company-insolvency\">Why Inability to Pay Staff Wages Signals Company Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Failing to meet payroll is one of the clearest external markers of cash-flow insolvency. Under section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986, your company is deemed unable to pay its debts when it cannot pay debts as they fall due. Staff wages are the textbook case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The warning-sign pattern, usually visible weeks before the first missed payroll:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Other essential creditors being rolled.</strong> Rates, utilities, VAT, all being deferred while payroll is protected. Once payroll itself starts slipping, the order has collapsed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Mounting HMRC arrears</strong> from PAYE and VAT. HMRC is the most informed single creditor about a company's position, and persistent <a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-paye/\">PAYE arrears</a> attract immediate attention.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Supplier arrears spreading.</strong> Trade credit withdrawn by one supplier quickly spreads across others through credit agencies.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Repeated use of overdraft at ceiling.</strong> The bank sees the pattern before most directors acknowledge it, and facility review usually follows.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When these cluster, the director-duty shift under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006 has usually already occurred. Your obligation is now to creditors as a whole. In our experience, staff are one of the most protected categories of creditor in UK insolvency law, and they know it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duties-when-a-company-cannot-pay-staff-wages\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duties-when-a-company-cannot-pay-staff-wages\">Director Duties When a Company Cannot Pay Staff Wages</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Missing payroll is not just a cash management issue. It carries specific statutory consequences for you as the director, and in our view those consequences are among the most serious a director can face.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Continued trading without a realistic rescue plan becomes wrongful trading</strong> under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986. Personal contribution to creditor losses is the remedy.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Employees accruing further wages</strong> in a period when the director knew or ought to have known those wages could not be paid adds directly to wrongful-trading exposure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Selective payment of some employees</strong> while others go unpaid is a potential preference claim under section 239, particularly where the paid employees are connected persons (directors themselves, family members).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal injection of funds by the director</strong>, paying wages from a director's personal account, creates a director's-loan position that the liquidator will scrutinise, and can be unwound if made when insolvency was known.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Wrongful trading, specifically</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Arises where directors continue trading when there is no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Tested objectively, what a reasonably diligent director would have known.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Personal contribution order to cover the losses caused by the continued trading.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Disqualification under the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 for up to 15 years is a common accompanying sanction.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Documented contemporaneous advice from a licensed Insolvency Practitioner is the material that defends you against a later wrongful-trading claim. In our practice, the directors who fare best are those who take that advice once their payroll is at risk, not after it has failed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"employee-rights-when-the-employer-cannot-pay-wages\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"employee-rights-when-the-employer-cannot-pay-wages\">Employee Rights When the Employer Cannot Pay Wages</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK employment law provides a specific safety net where your company enters formal insolvency. Your employees become preferential creditors for unpaid wages up to a statutory cap, and can claim certain statutory entitlements from the Insolvency Service's Redundancy Payments Service (RPS), paid from the National Insurance Fund.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the Redundancy Payments Service covers</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Unpaid wages</strong>, up to 8 weeks, subject to the statutory weekly cap (\u00a3751 from 6 April 2026).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Holiday pay</strong>, up to 6 weeks of accrued-but-unpaid holiday from the 12 months preceding insolvency.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Statutory redundancy pay</strong>, for employees with at least 2 years' continuous service, calculated on the statutory formula.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Statutory notice pay</strong>, one week per year of service, up to a maximum of 12 weeks.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your employees need a case reference number from the appointed Insolvency Practitioner to make RPS claims. Typical processing time is around 6 weeks from a complete application. Enhanced contractual redundancy above statutory is not covered; it remains an unsecured claim against the insolvent employer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"short-term-options-when-staff-wages-cannot-be-paid\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"short-term-options-when-staff-wages-cannot-be-paid\">Short-Term Options When Staff Wages Cannot Be Paid</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before formal insolvency becomes the answer, three short-term options can bridge a genuine cash-flow gap in your business. Each has specific limits and consequences you need to understand. We cover them below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Renegotiate supplier and tax payment terms</strong> to free up cash for payroll. HMRC Time to Pay arrangements can release cash quickly where the underlying business is sound. Supplier deferral risks credit withdrawal.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Short-term finance</strong>, overdraft extension, invoice finance, commercial loan. Useful only where the underlying business is viable; borrowing to fund payroll in a structurally-loss-making business compounds the problem.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Transparent communication with employees.</strong> Most employees will accept a short, specific delay (a week or two) with a clear reason and date. Open communication preserves goodwill and reduces tribunal claims; silence produces the opposite.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are bridges, not solutions. If your cash-flow problem is structural, bridging extends the period during which your wrongful trading exposure accumulates. The hard question, whether your business is viable if the historic debt is addressed, has to be answered honestly. Our advice is to get that answer before any of these short-term moves.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"formal-insolvency-options-when-the-company-cannot-fund-payroll\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"formal-insolvency-options-when-the-company-cannot-fund-payroll\">Formal Insolvency Options When the Company Cannot Fund Payroll</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where your business is not viable in its current form, three formal options address the position. Each one changes your position as a director and your employees' position as creditors. We work across all three routes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVA is a legally binding agreement to repay your historic creditors a percentage over 3\u20135 years while your company continues to trade. It requires 75% creditor approval by value. Well-suited where your underlying business is viable and the cash-flow problem is restructuring historic debt rather than current operations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Administration</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Administration imposes a statutory moratorium on creditor action while an administrator pursues rescue or sale of the business. Where the trading operation is worth preserving but the current corporate vehicle is not, a pre-pack administration can transfer the business (and employees under TUPE) to a new entity overnight.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation (CVL)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a> is the orderly wind-down route where rescue is not viable. Directors initiate it, a licensed IP takes control, employees are made redundant (unlocking RPS access), and assets are realised and distributed. Properly conducted, a CVL protects director conduct and closes the position cleanly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVL triggered when your payroll can no longer be funded is the single most effective action to limit your personal-liability exposure and unlock your employees' rights to the RPS safety net. Waiting produces worse outcomes for everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-when-you-cannot-pay-staff-wages\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-when-you-cannot-pay-staff-wages\">Your Next Step When You Cannot Pay Staff Wages</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your payroll is at genuine risk this month, the right sequence is: an immediate conversation with a licensed IP (same-day or next-day), an honest review of your cash-flow position, and a decision on whether to bridge-and-trade-on, restructure through CVA, move to administration, or initiate CVL.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed insolvency practitioners and business rescue specialists can explain your responsibilities as a director, outline the options available for the business, and help you plan the best way forward. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for immediate, confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"payroll-arrears-faqs\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"payroll-arrears-faqs\">Payroll Arrears FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"5d69fb07-5cd3-47e4-a678-088fc0e64714\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I pay some employees but defer others when money is short?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Selective payment creates two specific risks. First, employment tribunal claims from the deferred employees for unlawful deduction of wages (section 13, Employment Rights Act 1996). These succeed routinely and carry modest compensation plus costs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Second, if the deferred employees include anyone connected to you as the director, or the paid employees are connected persons, the payment can be unwound as a preference under section 239 of the Insolvency Act 1986 in any later liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if I personally inject funds to pay staff wages?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Short-term bridging is legitimate where your underlying business is sound and the gap is genuinely temporary. Two cautions: the director's loan account position needs to be documented properly (a formal loan, not a capital contribution masquerading as one).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If insolvency subsequently follows within 6\u201324 months, the payment and any repayment to you as director can be examined as a preference. Make the loan at arm's length, minute it, and take advice before the injection if there is any doubt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eAre directors personally liable for unpaid staff wages?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Generally no. For a limited company, wages are a company debt, not your personal debt. Personal exposure arises through: personal guarantees on specific employment-related obligations (rare); wrongful trading findings; fraudulent trading; or misfeasance.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You can also face personal liability where HMRC issues a Personal Liability Notice for unpaid PAYE/NICs. Ordinary inability to pay wages in a properly-managed insolvency does not itself produce personal liability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhen should I speak to an Insolvency Practitioner?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>As soon as payroll is at foreseeable risk, not after it has failed. The decisive factor in limiting director liability and maximising employee protection is acting within the window where a structured process (CVA, administration, CVL) is still available. Same-day or next-day IP advice is common and usually free at initial consultation; delay is the single largest cost in these cases.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill employees receive their full owed wages in a liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Your employees are preferential creditors for up to 4 months' wages capped at \u00a3800 per employee under Schedule 6 of the Insolvency Act 1986, paid ahead of floating-charge holders and unsecured creditors from company assets.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Above that, the Redundancy Payments Service pays up to 8 weeks of unpaid wages at the statutory weekly cap, 6 weeks of holiday pay, and statutory redundancy and notice pay. Any balance above these caps remains an unsecured claim in the liquidation, recoverable only from residual assets after other creditors are paid.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"methodology-amp-disclosure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"methodology-amp-disclosure\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team, reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners, and reflects UK employment and insolvency law as at the last-reviewed date. Statutory caps (\u00a3751 weekly pay cap from 6 April 2026; 4 months' wages capped at \u00a3800 preferential) are current for the 2026/27 year.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Statutory references are drawn from the Insolvency Act 1986, Companies Act 2006, Employment Rights Act 1996, and Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where we recommend a CVA, Administration, or CVL, we can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; sections 123 (inability to pay debts), 214 (wrongful trading once payroll fails), 239 (preference risk where some staff are paid), 212 (misfeasance) and Schedule 6 (employee preferential debts)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/172\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 172</strong> &mdash; the director duty shift to creditors as a whole, triggered the moment payroll cannot be funded with confidence</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/18/contents\"><strong>Employment Rights Act 1996</strong> &mdash; Part II unlawful deduction of wages claims from deferred staff, Part XI redundancy entitlements, Part XII payments from the National Insurance Fund on employer insolvency</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; the up-to-15-year disqualification sanction that routinely follows wrongful trading findings tied to unpaid wages</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/claim-redundancy\"><strong>GOV.UK: Claim for redundancy and money you're owed</strong> &mdash; the Redundancy Payments Service portal staff use to recover unpaid wages, holiday pay, notice pay and statutory redundancy once the company enters formal insolvency</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-afford-to-repay-business-loan/": {
    "id": 43917,
    "slug": "cant-afford-to-repay-business-loan",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Business loan defaults rarely arrive in isolation. By the time the standing order has bounced twice and the relationship manager has moved to \"can we schedule a call this week\", the cash flow has usually been tightening for six to nine months. The loan is the visible symptom, not the cause.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This page sets out what happens when your limited company cannot meet its business loan repayments, the enforcement path, the specific personal-liability risks (mainly through personal guarantees rather than the loan itself), and the negotiation, restructuring, and formal-insolvency options available to you at each stage of distress.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"recognising-the-early-signs-of-business-loan-default\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"recognising-the-early-signs-of-business-loan-default\">Recognising the Early Signs of Business Loan Default</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The signals that the loan is on a trajectory to default usually show up weeks or months before the first formal missed payment:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Covenant breach on debt-service cover ratio</strong> (typically 1.25x on commercial facilities). Technical default often precedes payment default by a full quarter.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Relationship manager proactively suggesting review</strong>. Banks almost always signal concern before they act.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Other essential creditors being delayed</strong> to protect the loan payment. Rates, suppliers, and VAT slipping.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Directors injecting personal funds</strong> to cover payments.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Reliance on the overdraft facility</strong> sitting at ceiling.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any one is a reason to forecast your next 90 days. Two or more together is a reason to open the conversation with your lender before the formal default notice arrives. In our experience, directors who raise the issue proactively get materially better terms than those who wait for the demand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-happens-when-you-default-on-a-uk-business-loan\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-happens-when-you-default-on-a-uk-business-loan\">What Happens When You Default on a UK Business Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The lender's recovery toolkit depends on the facility structure. For a standard unsecured commercial loan:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Default notice</strong> under the loan agreement, giving a period (typically 14\u201330 days) to remedy the breach.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Acceleration</strong>, the lender declares the full outstanding balance immediately due.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Formal demand for repayment</strong>, often accompanied by a pre-action letter from the lender's solicitors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>County court claim</strong> for the debt. Judgment in default if the company does not defend.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Statutory demand</strong>, for debts over \u00a3750 unpaid for 21 days. Creates a presumption of insolvency.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">Winding-up petition</a></strong> where the presumption is not rebutted. Advertisement in The Gazette freezes company bank accounts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For secured loans (typically over \u00a3100,000, or anything secured on specific assets), the lender has additional direct-enforcement routes: realisation of security, appointment of a receiver, or, for a qualifying floating-charge holder, out-of-court appointment of an administrator under Schedule B1 of the Insolvency Act 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-liabilities-and-personal-guarantees-on-a-business-loan\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-liabilities-and-personal-guarantees-on-a-business-loan\">Director Liabilities and Personal Guarantees on a Business Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A business loan is a company debt. Limited liability is the default. Personal liability arises through specific, enumerated routes, not through the loan itself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal guarantees</strong> on the loan, the most common route. Commercial banks routinely require PGs on loans to smaller companies and first-time borrowers. Facility-specific vs all-monies wording matters enormously: an all-monies PG given on one facility can be called on any other debt the company owes the same lender.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Secured personal assets</strong>, a charge over the director's property (family home, second property) pledged as collateral.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading</strong> under section 214, personal contribution to losses caused by continuing to trade after insolvency was unavoidable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fraudulent trading</strong> under section 213, personal liability for company debts, uncapped, where the business was carried on with intent to defraud creditors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Misfeasance</strong> under section 212, including preference payments where a connected-party creditor (often the director's own loan account) was paid ahead of the lender.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first two sit on your loan documentation. The last three sit in conduct. Both need review as soon as default is in view for your company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"practical-options-to-manage-unmanageable-business-loan-debt\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"practical-options-to-manage-unmanageable-business-loan-debt\">Practical Options to Manage Unmanageable Business Loan Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the underlying business is viable but the loan service is temporarily unaffordable:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Renegotiate with the existing lender</strong>. Term extension, interest-only period, temporary payment holiday, covenant reset. Lenders almost always prefer a continuing loan on modified terms to enforcement; the economics of forbearance vs recovery favour forbearance in most cases.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Refinance with a new lender</strong>. Challenger banks and specialist commercial lenders often price aggressively for refinance business. Typically takes 8\u201312 weeks and may attract early-repayment fees on the existing loan.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Consolidate multiple facilities</strong>. Where the company carries a loan, overdraft, invoice finance, and asset finance, consolidation into a single secured facility can reduce total debt service and simplify covenant management.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Asset-based refinancing</strong>. Sale-and-leaseback on equipment, property, or vehicles can release capital to pay down the loan, at the cost of an operating lease going forward.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Structured debt restructure</strong>. Where multiple creditors are involved, <a href=\"/advice/business-restructuring/\">business restructuring</a> through a coordinated informal agreement can reset the debt profile across the board.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Each of these preserves your limited-liability shield. They work only where the underlying trading business can support the revised debt service. Restructuring a loan on top of a structurally loss-making business buys time without solving your problem. We see this pattern often; the restructure question and the viability question need to be answered together.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-to-seek-professional-insolvency-advice-on-a-business-loan\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-to-seek-professional-insolvency-advice-on-a-business-loan\">When to Seek Professional Insolvency Advice on a Business Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The triggers for a licensed insolvency practitioner conversation:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Default notice received and cannot be remedied within the cure period.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Lender has accelerated the debt or issued a statutory demand.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Multiple creditor pressures (loan plus PAYE plus VAT plus supplier arrears).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Personal guarantee exposure is material and the company position is deteriorating.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Director is considering personal injection of funds to cover payments.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The formal routes available when informal options are exhausted:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</strong>, binding 3\u20135 year repayment plan with unsecured creditors (secured loans sit outside unless the secured creditor agrees).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Administration</strong>, statutory moratorium, rescue or sale of the business.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a> (CVL)</strong>, orderly wind-down where the business is not viable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVL cleanly closes the company-level liability on the business loan. It does not close out a personal guarantee; the PG remains a personal debt and is pursued separately by the lender against your personal assets. If your loan carries a PG, this needs parallel planning before you enter any formal process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-business-loan-default\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-business-loan-default\">Your Next Step on Business Loan Default</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two early questions determine everything that follows. First: what does your loan documentation say about personal guarantees and security? Pull your loan file before having the conversation. Second: is your underlying business viable if the loan service is restructured? That is the question a licensed IP answers from your management accounts in an hour.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pattern we see most often: directors who inject another \u00a330k of personal savings to cover two more repayments, buy three months, and are back in the same meeting with the same lender, minus the \u00a330k. Propping up an unviable debt structure rarely buys time in any useful sense.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed insolvency practitioners and business rescue specialists can assess the position, explain the negotiation, restructuring, and formal-insolvency options, and handle any personal-guarantee exposure in parallel with the corporate position. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"business-loan-arrears-faqs\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"business-loan-arrears-faqs\">Business Loan Arrears FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"43917-loan-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs there a difference between a CVA and a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, and the difference is structural. A CVA is a rescue mechanism, the company continues to trade while paying creditors a percentage over 3\u20135 years. A CVL is a closure mechanism, the company ceases trading, assets are realised, proceeds distributed to creditors, and the company is dissolved. A CVA keeps the business; a CVL ends it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan limited company directors be held personally liable for an unpaid business loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not by default. A company business loan is a company debt. Personal liability arises through specific routes: personal guarantees on the loan (common on smaller facilities), charged personal assets, wrongful trading, fraudulent trading, or misfeasance. Review the loan documents and the conduct picture, both determine whether personal exposure is real in the specific case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow quickly can creditors move to wind up the company for an unpaid loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>From first formal default to petition typically runs 60\u2013120 days, but the timeline compresses where security is involved. A secured lender can appoint a Law of Property Act receiver within days of default on a charged asset, without any court hearing. Unsecured lenders work through statutory demand and petition, which takes longer but produces a similar outcome.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if the loan was a Bounce Back Loan or a government-backed scheme loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Bounce Back Loans were advanced without personal guarantees and cannot be enforced against a director's main home or main vehicle under the scheme rules. CBILS and RLS loans over \u00a3250,000 typically required a personal guarantee limited to 20% of the facility. Check the specific loan documentation, scheme rules vary and have changed over time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCould I face criminal charges for unpaid business loan debts?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not for the inability to pay itself, default is a civil matter. Criminal exposure arises from specific conduct: fraud on the loan application (Fraud Act 2006), fraudulent trading under section 213 of the Insolvency Act 1986, or misappropriation of loan funds. Ordinary business failure, honestly conducted, does not create criminal exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs it ever too late to seek help on a business loan default?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Rarely. The menu of options narrows over time, informal restructure becomes formal insolvency; CVA becomes administration; administration becomes compulsory liquidation, but some option exists at almost every stage until the company is formally wound up.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Even after a winding-up petition is advertised, administration can be invoked with the court's leave. The earlier your intervention, the better your options; but the worst outcome is inaction, not late action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if the lender will not agree to a repayment plan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Two alternative routes. First, refinance with a different lender, moves the problem rather than solving it, but sometimes opens better terms for your business. Second, enter a formal process (CVA, administration) that imposes terms the lender must accept through the statutory framework.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The presence of a licensed IP in our conversation with the lender often changes their position before the formal process becomes necessary.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"methodology-amp-disclosure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"methodology-amp-disclosure\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by our editorial team, reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners, and reflects UK corporate insolvency and commercial lending law as at the last-reviewed date. Statutory references are drawn from the Insolvency Act 1986, Companies Act 2006, Law of Property Act 1925, and Fraud Act 2006.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where we recommend a CVA, Administration, or CVL, we can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner under separate engagement. Our 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; statutory tests for cash-flow and balance-sheet insolvency (s 123), and the wrongful trading, misfeasance, preference, and administration provisions a director may face once loan repayments stop</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/172\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 172</strong> &mdash; directors' duty to promote the success of the company, and the point at which that duty shifts to consider creditor interests once loan default becomes likely</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo5/15-16/20/section/101\"><strong>Law of Property Act 1925, section 101</strong> &mdash; statutory power allowing a secured lender to appoint a receiver over charged assets once the loan facility falls into default</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/35/contents\"><strong>Fraud Act 2006</strong> &mdash; the framework for fraud by false representation, relevant where a director draws on or applies for borrowing while knowing the company cannot repay</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/": {
    "id": 43758,
    "slug": "bounce-back-loan-support-hub",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most Bounce Back Loans were taken out quickly and with limited advice. The questions that follow tend to arrive later: what happens if the company can't repay, whether the director carries personal liability, and whether the company can be closed with the loan still outstanding. This hub covers those questions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Repayment, Default and Director Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<div class=\"cd-hub-grid\">\n  <a class=\"cd-hub-card\" href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/what-happens-if-i-default/\">\n    <div class=\"cd-hub-card__title\">What happens if I default on a Bounce Back Loan?</div>\n    <span class=\"cd-hub-card__desc\">The government guarantee, lender options, and your exposure</span>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"cd-hub-card\" href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/directors-liability-for-bounce-back-loans/\">\n    <div class=\"cd-hub-card__title\">Directors' liability for Bounce Back Loans</div>\n    <span class=\"cd-hub-card__desc\">When personal liability arises from a government-backed loan</span>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"cd-hub-card\" href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/can-i-lose-my-house-with-a-bounce-back-loan/\">\n    <div class=\"cd-hub-card__title\">Can I lose my house with a Bounce Back Loan?</div>\n    <span class=\"cd-hub-card__desc\">Personal assets and the BBL \u2014 what is actually at risk</span>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"cd-hub-card\" href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/\">\n    <div class=\"cd-hub-card__title\">Bounce Back Loan fraud</div>\n    <span class=\"cd-hub-card__desc\">What counts as misuse and how it is investigated</span>\n  </a>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Closing a Company with a Bounce Back Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<div class=\"cd-hub-grid\">\n  <a class=\"cd-hub-card\" href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/dissolving-a-company-with-bounce-back-loan/\">\n    <div class=\"cd-hub-card__title\">Dissolving a company with a Bounce Back Loan</div>\n    <span class=\"cd-hub-card__desc\">Why strike-off is blocked and what to do instead</span>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"cd-hub-card\" href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/cant-pay-coronavirus-business-interruption-loan-cbils/\">\n    <div class=\"cd-hub-card__title\">Can't pay a CBILS loan</div>\n    <span class=\"cd-hub-card__desc\">Options when a Coronavirus Business Interruption Loan can't be repaid</span>\n  </a>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/dissolving-a-company-with-bounce-back-loan/": {
    "id": 43745,
    "slug": "dissolving-a-company-with-bounce-back-loan",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- POST ID: 43745 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70167 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/dissolving-a-company-with-bounce-back-loan/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The form is short, the fee is \u00a313, and Companies House will, in theory, strike a dormant company off the register within three months of filing. That is the story directors are told about DS01, and it is the story they try to apply to a company that still has a live Bounce Back Loan on the books.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It almost never works. Lenders routinely object, the strike-off is suspended, the company remains on the register in a state of limbo, and the attempt itself becomes a piece of evidence that a later Insolvency Service investigation reads as evasion rather than tidiness.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below you will find why you cannot dissolve a company with an outstanding <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/\">Bounce Back Loan</a>, what actually happens when directors try, and the route that does work: a <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>, including how the BBL balance is treated inside it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why You Cannot Dissolve a Company With an Outstanding Bounce Back Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Voluntary strike-off under section 1003 of the <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/what-is-the-companies-act-2006/\">Companies Act 2006</a> is designed for one specific situation: a solvent company that has genuinely ceased to trade, has no outstanding liabilities, and wants a clean exit from the register. It is an administrative process, not an insolvency one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A Bounce Back Loan is an outstanding liability. It does not matter that the government underwrote the lender. It does not matter that no personal guarantee was given. The loan remains a debt of the company until it is repaid or written off through a formal insolvency process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 1006 of the Companies Act gives creditors and interested parties the right to object to a proposed strike-off. Lenders routinely exercise that right on BBL cases. In practice:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The DS01 application is filed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Companies House publishes notice in The Gazette (the required public notice of intended strike-off).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The lender sees the notice and files an objection, either directly, through the British Business Bank, or through the Insolvency Service.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Companies House suspends the strike-off. The company remains on the register.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The matter is, at that point, visible to the <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-the-insolvency-service/\">Insolvency Service</a>, which can open a director-conduct investigation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The entire exercise produces no benefit, costs you \u00a333, and signals to the people whose opinion matters most that you have tried to walk away from a live creditor. It is the single most consistently counter-productive move in the whole BBL-closure map, and in our experience it never produces the result directors expect.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Happens When Directors Try to Dissolve a Bounce Back Loan Company Anyway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The specific sequence of consequences:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Insolvency Service investigation</strong>. The Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021 specifically extended the Insolvency Service's investigatory powers to cover former directors of dissolved companies where BBL misuse is suspected. Attempted strike-off of a company with unpaid BBL is a pattern that triggers that power directly.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Reinstatement to the register</strong>. Even if the strike-off somehow gets through, creditors can apply to restore the company for up to twenty years. Reinstatement is routine on BBL defaults, and the restored company is then pursued through the insolvency process the director tried to avoid.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal conduct findings</strong>. Where misuse of the loan is established (inflated turnover, personal expenditure from loan funds, repayment of director's loan account from BBL), the Insolvency Service can obtain a <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/directors-disqualification/\">director disqualification</a> order of 2 to 15 years, often with a compensation order attached.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Criminal referral</strong>. Serious cases, typically those involving <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/\">Bounce Back Loan fraud</a>, multiple loans, or sums above \u00a3100,000, are passed to criminal investigation agencies. Custodial sentences of three to seven years, plus confiscation orders under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, sit at the hard end of this track.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Reputational consequences</strong>. Disqualifications are published on the public register, indexed by name, and routinely surface on background checks for years afterwards.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of that is the outcome you were trying to achieve by filing DS01. All of it is the outcome you make more likely by filing it. Our advice to any director in this position: do not file the DS01 without taking specialist advice first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Closing a Bounce Back Loan Company Through Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The route that works, the formal mechanism designed for exactly this situation, is a Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation. A CVL is the standard insolvency process for a company that cannot pay its debts and has no realistic prospect of being rescued. Our licensed IPs conduct these regularly and the process is well-mapped.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The CVL mechanics:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Directors resolve to place the company into liquidation.</strong> A board resolution, followed by a shareholders' resolution, initiates the process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A licensed <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-an-insolvency-practitioner/\">Insolvency Practitioner</a> is appointed</strong> to act as liquidator.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Creditors are formally notified</strong>, including the BBL lender, and a decision procedure is held (usually a deemed consent procedure or a virtual meeting) for creditors to approve the liquidator's appointment.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The liquidator takes control</strong>, realises company assets, investigates director conduct, and distributes proceeds to creditors in the statutory order.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Where no misconduct is found</strong>, the remaining BBL balance is written off as part of the liquidation, and the company is formally dissolved.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The last point is the one directors underestimate. A CVL conducted with clean books does exactly what you hoped dissolution would do: ends the company, writes off the unpaid BBL, and closes the file. The difference is that the CVL does it lawfully. Our licensed IPs conduct this process regularly and can guide you through each step.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Protecting Yourself Through a Bounce Back Loan CVL</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The CVL protects you from personal liability on the BBL itself. It does not protect you from misconduct findings if misconduct is present. Your behaviour during the lead-up to liquidation is the single biggest determinant of the outcome, and in our practice we help directors understand that distinction before the process begins.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Pull the paperwork cleanly.</strong> The original BBL application, the bank statements from the six months either side of the draw-down, the director's loan account, and the management accounts for the period. Do not edit, \"tidy\", or backdate anything. The liquidator reconstructs the position from bank records regardless.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Document use-of-funds honestly.</strong> Where the loan went to payroll, rent, stock, and working capital, that is straightforward. Where it went to personal expenditure or to clear a director's loan account, that is the conversation to have with the IP before the liquidation begins, not after.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Correct what can be corrected.</strong> If the application overstated turnover, the Voluntary Repayment Scheme lets you hand back the excess proactively. If loan funds were personally extracted, reinstating them to the company before insolvency changes the shape of any subsequent challenge materially.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Engage with the lender and the IP, do not evade.</strong> Cooperation is consistently the lowest-cost path through this. Evasion produces the worst outcomes almost every time.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Contemporaneous records over reconstructions.</strong> Board minutes made at the time, documenting the reasoning for decisions, carry enormous evidential weight later. Reconstructions from memory, a year into a conduct investigation, carry almost none.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on a Bounce Back Loan Company Closure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are considering closing a company with an outstanding BBL, the call that actually changes the outcome is a confidential conversation with our licensed insolvency practitioners before any form is filed. Bring the loan agreement, the last two sets of accounts, the bank statements from six months either side of the draw-down, and a rough use-of-funds map.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From that conversation, you come out with three things: a clear view on whether a CVL is the right route; an estimate of fees (CVL fees for smaller companies typically run \u00a33,000\u2013\u00a36,000, often recoverable from company assets); and, if the use-of-funds review raises any misuse concerns, a plan for voluntary correction before the formal process starts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of those conversations is improved by waiting for a strike-off objection to force it. Our licensed IPs handle this initial review as a free confidential call.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Dissolving a Company With a Bounce Back Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"e9cbccce-6786-49d4-bd9f-21e923d2866c\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I dissolve my company if I have partly repaid the Bounce Back Loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. Under section 1003 of the Companies Act 2006, voluntary strike-off requires the company to have no outstanding liabilities. Any remaining BBL balance is an outstanding liability, regardless of how much has been repaid. A CVL is the appropriate route where the balance cannot be cleared.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens if my Bounce Back Loan strike-off application is rejected?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The strike-off is suspended, not deleted. The company remains on the register, the BBL remains live, and the attempted strike-off becomes visible to the Insolvency Service. At that point the director's options are: reinstate the Companies House filings that have lapsed during the strike-off attempt (annual accounts, confirmation statement), then either pay the BBL down or enter CVL.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eAm I personally liable if the company fails to repay the Bounce Back Loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not by default. BBLs were advanced without personal guarantees, and cannot be enforced against a director's main home or main vehicle under the scheme rules. Personal liability arises only where misuse of funds is established, where inflated turnover on the application amounts to fraud, or where wrongful trading or <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/misfeasance/\">misfeasance</a> is found during the CVL investigation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes it matter if I used the Bounce Back Loan for personal expenses?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, materially. Use of BBL funds for personal expenditure is a breach of the scheme rules and, in insolvency, a breach of director's duty. Consequences range from a liquidator's claim for a personal contribution (civil) to fraud prosecution (criminal), depending on the amounts and the evidence of intent. Voluntary repayment of the misapplied portion before liquidation is the cleanest mitigation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow does dissolution differ in Scotland or Northern Ireland?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The underlying principles are the same, a company with an outstanding BBL cannot be struck off. Procedural detail varies: Scottish insolvency is handled through the Accountant in Bankruptcy framework, Northern Ireland through the Insolvency Service NI. The statutory rights of BBL lenders to object to strike-off are equivalent across the UK.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if I cannot afford Bounce Back Loan liquidation fees?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Most licensed IPs will quote against realisable company assets first, meaning fees come out of the liquidation estate rather than the director's pocket. Where assets are insufficient, some IPs accept staged payment from the director. A handful decline cases they cannot fund; a brief free call with two or three IPs usually produces a workable quote.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCould a Bounce Back Loan affect my ability to start another company?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>An unpaid BBL, on its own, does not prevent a director from starting or running another limited company. A disqualification order arising from BBL misuse does. And that disqualification can last up to fifteen years, covering director and de facto director roles in any UK company during that period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association.</p>\n<p>Statutory references draw on the Companies Act 2006 (sections 1003 to 1012), the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 84 to 90 on CVL, 212, 213 and 214), the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, and the 2021 Dissolved Companies Act.</p>\n<p>Last reviewed May 2026.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner. We may also receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, sections 1003 to 1012</strong> &mdash; voluntary strike-off, bona vacantia and the framework for court restoration of dissolved companies</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 84 to 90</strong> &mdash; the Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation framework</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 212 to 214</strong> &mdash; misfeasance, fraudulent trading and wrongful trading liability for directors</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2021/34/contents\"><strong>Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021</strong> &mdash; direct Insolvency Service powers over dissolved-company directors from 15 December 2021, specifically targeting BBL misuse</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; the framework for director disqualification (2 to 15 years) and compensation orders</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.british-business-bank.co.uk/finance-options/debt-finance/recovery-loan-scheme/bounce-back-loan/\"><strong>British Business Bank &mdash; Bounce Back Loan Scheme</strong>: official scheme guidance and lender list</a> - british-business-bank.co.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>The Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; published guidance on dissolved-company investigations and BBL fraud prosecutions</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/what-happens-if-i-default/": {
    "id": 43675,
    "slug": "what-happens-if-i-default",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- TITLE: Can't Repay Your Bounce Back Loan? A UK Director's Guide to Risks &amp; Solutions -->\n<!-- POST ID: 43675 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70171 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/what-happens-if-i-default/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You sit down on a Sunday evening, spreadsheet open, and the numbers confirm what you already suspected: the next Bounce Back Loan payment is not going to clear. You start googling at 11pm.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>By midnight you have read eight contradictory articles, one terrifying forum thread, and a headline suggesting directors are being chased personally for loans the government supposedly \"wrote off\". None of it squares.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So here is the honest version. Defaulting on a <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/\">Bounce Back Loan</a> is serious, but it is not the catastrophe the louder corners of the internet imply. The risks that matter are narrower and more specific than \"you'll lose everything\". Acting early almost always reduces them. Ignoring the lender almost always widens them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What follows is what we tell directors who ring us at 8am on a Monday after that spreadsheet moment: what you actually owe, what you do not, where the real exposure sits, and the routes that still make sense when cash flow has stopped pretending.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Bounce Back Loan Default Picture, at a Glance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>You still owe the loan.</strong> The government guarantee protects the lender, not you. A <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/\">Bounce Back Loan</a> is not quietly written off because the business cannot pay.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Limited company directors are <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/what-is-limited-liability/\">not personally liable</a> by default.</strong> Personal assets sit behind the corporate veil unless there is fraud, misrepresentation, or misuse of funds.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Sole traders are personally on the hook</strong>, but scheme rules bar enforcement against your main home and main vehicle. Separate personal insolvency law can still reach other assets.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Missing payments is not a crime.</strong> Default alone is not illegal. Trouble lives in how the loan was obtained and how the money was used, not in the missed direct debit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Pay As You Grow (PAYG) is available and underused.</strong> Term extensions, interest-only periods up to 18 months, and a single six-month payment holiday can buy real breathing space.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Silence is the most expensive response.</strong> Lenders escalate non-engagement faster than they escalate honest hardship.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If the company is <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency-calculator/\">insolvent</a>, liquidation lawfully deals with the debt.</strong> Where no misconduct is found, the remaining BBL balance is written off as part of the process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Dissolving a company with an unpaid BBL almost always fails.</strong> Lenders object. The company stays on the register. The issue follows you.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Inflated turnover and personal spending have teeth.</strong> <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/directors-disqualification/\">Disqualification</a>, compensation orders, and occasional criminal referrals are real, and they almost always surface during insolvency, not from the bank spotting it first.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Voluntary repayment and early advice reduce exposure more than any clever restructure.</strong> Regulators and insolvency officeholders treat proactive correction very differently from evasion.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"1298dc10-c7da-4ca6-ba5c-c3ccd626f7a8\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Complete the form today to know how much it may cost and understand your next steps\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the Bounce Back Loan Government Guarantee Actually Does</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The single most damaging myth in this area is the idea that the government guarantee behaves like an insurance policy for the borrower. It does not. It is a contract between the lender and HM Treasury. The loan itself remains a normal commercial debt sitting on your business's balance sheet.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In practical terms:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The <strong>borrower (your business)</strong> is contractually bound to repay.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The <strong>lender</strong> must make reasonable recovery efforts before calling on the guarantee.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The <strong>guarantee reimburses the lender</strong>, not the borrower, once recovery is exhausted.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a limited company, the director's position hinges on whether the corporate veil holds. Limited liability is the default setting. It is lifted in narrow, well-defined circumstances, fraud, knowingly misleading declarations on the application, using the loan outside the business, not because a payment was missed or a business simply did not work out.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The streamlined self-certification route taken on most Bounce Back Loan applications does not change any of this. Your obligation to repay exists because you signed a loan agreement, not because of how thoroughly the bank stress-tested your numbers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Two concepts worth fixing in your head:</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Corporate veil</strong>, the legal separation between company and director. It is pierced only where the court finds serious misconduct; ordinary business failure is not that trigger.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Misfeasance</strong>, improper conduct by a director: using loan money for personal purposes, making false declarations, or treating some creditors preferentially when insolvency is near.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Get those two clear and most of the scare-story headlines fall away on their own.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:embed {\"url\":\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JUsR8WX-mpc\",\"type\":\"video\",\"providerNameSlug\":\"youtube\",\"responsive\":true,\"align\":\"center\",\"className\":\"wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-embed aligncenter is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio\"><div class=\"wp-block-embed__wrapper\">\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JUsR8WX-mpc\n</div></figure>\n<!-- /wp:embed -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Really Happens When You Default on a Bounce Back Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lenders working Bounce Back Loans have to follow specific recovery principles. They are not licensed to behave like a hard-nosed unsecured creditor chasing an overdraft. Expect a structured sequence: missed payment letters, phone calls, a formal demand, and only then legal action if engagement never materialises.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That does not make default consequence-free. If you ghost the bank:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Formal recovery escalates on a predictable timeline.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>County court claims or winding-up petitions become realistic if the debt is large enough and unengaged.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Where recovery proves impossible, the lender calls on the government guarantee.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A guarantee claim does not quietly close the file. Any subsequent recoveries are passed back to HM Treasury. The debt is not \"sold\" in the consumer-finance sense, and it is not automatically transferred to a collections agency, but it does keep a pulse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The more decisive risk is what surfaces in insolvency, not in the collections queue. Inflated turnover figures, suspicious transfers in the months before liquidation, loan money that quietly paid off a director's personal credit card, these are the issues an Insolvency Practitioner or <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-the-insolvency-service/\">the Insolvency Service</a> looks at, and they carry different consequences from a missed payment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The honest hierarchy of risk, from most to least common:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Escalating recovery costs and legal pressure from non-engagement</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Director scrutiny during insolvency where irregularities exist</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Personal liability, disqualification, or compensation orders where misconduct is found</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Early engagement does not fix every problem, but it meaningfully shrinks the first category and often prevents the second from ever being triggered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Pay As You Grow: Bounce Back Loan Relief That Actually Helps</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Pay As You Grow (PAYG) is the most under-used piece of the Bounce Back Loan scheme. It is not a bailout. It is a set of contractual flexibilities that let a director who is still trying to trade out of trouble breathe without tripping straight into default.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The three options:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Term extension</strong>, extend the loan from six years to ten. Monthly payments fall, lifetime interest rises. Useful when the cash flow dip is structural rather than temporary.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Interest-only periods</strong>, up to six months at a time, available up to three times across the life of the loan (18 months in total). During these windows you pay interest only on the outstanding balance.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Full payment holiday</strong>, a single six-month period where nothing is paid. Interest accrues. It is a one-shot option, so spend it carefully.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>All three are requested through your lender and credit-reported as being on a PAYG arrangement rather than in default. That distinction matters when you are trying to keep supplier terms and card-machine facilities intact.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If, on reflection, you believe the original application overstated turnover, the Voluntary Repayment Scheme lets you hand back the excess proactively. It is an awkward conversation, but it is an order of magnitude easier than the one you will have with an Insolvency Practitioner who spots the discrepancy three years later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Dealing With a Bounce Back Loan Through Company Liquidation</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company is genuinely insolvent, the Bounce Back Loan sits in the same queue as any other unsecured business debt. It is not senior, it is not preferential, and it is not separately enforceable against you as a director unless misconduct is found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once insolvency becomes likely, your legal duties change. You are no longer primarily acting for shareholders; you are acting for creditors as a whole. In practical terms, that is usually when a <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation (CVL)</a> enters the conversation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Inside liquidation:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>A licensed Insolvency Practitioner (or the Official Receiver) reviews company books and bank statements.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Director conduct is assessed, with particular focus on how the BBL application was made and how the money was spent.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Genuine business failure, without misconduct, does not automatically produce personal consequences.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"7f1d8b17-e57e-4500-a35f-2cd78f9d3f39\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Complete the form today to know how much it may cost and understand your next steps\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The CVL process, stripped of jargon</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Directors resolve to place the company into liquidation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>A licensed Insolvency Practitioner is appointed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Creditors are notified; assets are realised.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The company is dissolved once the process is complete.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trying to dodge this by filing for voluntary strike-off is the most common misstep we see. Lenders almost always object, the dissolution is suspended, and nothing has been resolved, except that the director has now signalled a willingness to avoid <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-a-creditor/\">creditors</a>, which is a fact that follows them into any later insolvency review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where no misconduct is found during the CVL, any remaining Bounce Back Loan balance is written off. That is the lawful route to the outcome many directors mistakenly believe dissolution will deliver.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Bounce Back Loan Default for Sole Traders: Different Rules, Different Pressure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sole traders do not have a corporate veil to sit behind. The Bounce Back Loan is personal business debt, and the responsibility to repay is personal too.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The scheme does bake in a meaningful protection:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Lenders and the government will not enforce a Bounce Back Loan against your main home or your main vehicle.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Read that carefully. It applies to enforcement <em>under the scheme</em>. It does not override general personal insolvency law. If the debt drives you into bankruptcy, a trustee in bankruptcy can still look at property equity, vehicle value, and personal assets through the ordinary bankruptcy rules. The scheme protection is real, but it is not a force field.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For many sole traders the more useful route is an <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-an-individual-voluntary-arrangement/\">Individual Voluntary Arrangement (IVA)</a>. A properly structured IVA is a legally binding deal with creditors that can:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Keep you out of bankruptcy.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Protect the family home where equity is modest.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Cap repayments at what you can genuinely afford over a defined period.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is not the right answer for every sole trader, an IVA only holds if the proposed payments are realistic, but it is often the middle path between trying to limp on and being pushed into bankruptcy by an unresponsive creditor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Bounce Back Loan Misuse Turns Default Into Something Worse</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most of the serious personal outcomes we see do not come from businesses failing. They come from how the loan was obtained or spent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The patterns that attract scrutiny:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Turnover figures on the application that cannot be supported by the filed accounts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Loan funds moved directly to a director's personal account, or used to clear personal debts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Unusual transfers to connected parties in the months leading up to insolvency.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Repaying a director's loan account from BBL money while other creditors went unpaid.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The two relevant legal concepts here are <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/misfeasance/\">misfeasance</a> (improper use of company powers) and preference (favouring one creditor, usually yourself, over others when insolvency is near). Both are assessed in insolvency, not in the collections process. That is why so many directors are surprised by them, the investigation happens long after the missed payments have stopped mattering.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you recognise any of these patterns in your own conduct, the response is not to quietly hope it is never noticed. The Insolvency Practitioner will notice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Voluntary repayment of overclaimed amounts, documented explanations, and candid early advice dramatically soften the outcome. Transparency is not a moral flourish here; it is the practical difference between a closed file and a director disqualification hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step If You Cannot Repay Your Bounce Back Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The directors we see come out of Bounce Back Loan default best tend to do the same three things, in the same order. They speak to their lender about PAYG before the first missed payment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>They get a proper, regulated view from us or another regulated adviser on whether the company is actually insolvent rather than just tight. And if liquidation is the honest answer, they speak to our licensed Insolvency Practitioner before the situation makes the decision for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The ones who come out worst almost all have one thing in common. They waited.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is no version of this problem that improves by being ignored. Acting now gives you options. Acting later narrows them, often to the ones you least wanted. If you want a confidential view of where your position sits, call us free on 0800 074 6757 and we will run the numbers with you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"1e92cad6-a0ae-4b70-b4f4-52ffeedf99dc\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Complete the form today to know how much it may cost and understand your next steps\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Bounce Back Loan Default</h2>\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-43675\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-43675-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-43675\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-43675\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Will the government pay my Bounce Back Loan if I can\u2019t?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-43675\">\n<p>No. The guarantee reimburses the lender once recovery is exhausted. You, or your business, remain contractually responsible for the debt.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-43675-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-43675\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-43675\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is defaulting on a Bounce Back Loan a criminal offence?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-43675\">\n<p>No. Default alone is a civil matter. Criminal exposure arises from fraud on the application or deliberate misuse of the loan, not from an inability to pay.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-43675-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-43675\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-43675\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I dissolve my company to escape a Bounce Back Loan?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-43675\">\n<p>In practice, no. Lenders routinely object to strike-off where a BBL is outstanding. The dissolution is paused, the debt remains, and your attempt becomes part of the record.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-43675-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-43675\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-43675\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does Pay As You Grow reduce what I owe?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-43675\">\n<p>No. PAYG restructures the repayment profile; it does not reduce the principal. Term extensions in particular add to total interest paid over the life of the loan.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-43675-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-43675\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-43675\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Will a Bounce Back Loan default show up on my personal credit file?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-43675\">\n<p>Bounce Back Loans are business lending, so a limited company default is not reported on a director's personal credit file directly. Sole traders are different: the debt is personal and can affect the personal file. Indirect effects (for example, on any personal guarantee, overdraft, or connected borrowing) can show up in both cases.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-43675-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-43675\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-43675\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can an Insolvency Practitioner pursue me personally for a BBL?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-43675\">\n<p>Only where misconduct is found. A CVL does not, by itself, generate personal liability. Misfeasance claims, preference claims, or disqualification proceedings can, but those require evidence of specific wrongdoing, not simply the fact that the loan went unpaid.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/\">Bounce Back Loan Fraud and Misuse</a>: where a default stops being a default and starts being misuse.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: closing a company that still has a Bounce Back Loan.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/how-much-does-liquidation-cost/\">How Much Does It Cost to Liquidate a Company?</a>: what closure costs, and how to fund it.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/company-strike-off-and-dissolution/\">Company Strike-Off and Dissolution</a>: why striking off with a live BBL does not work.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n  <!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">HMRC Time to Pay</a>: if there are tax arrears alongside the loan.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide is written by our editorial team and reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners. It reflects UK insolvency law and the British Business Bank's published Bounce Back Loan Scheme rules as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<p>Our statutory references are the Insolvency Act 1986 (wrongful trading under section 214, misfeasance under section 212, preferences under section 239, transactions at undervalue under section 238) and the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986.</p>\n<p>We also draw on the Fraud Act 2006 (fraud by false representation where application turnover was overstated), the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 (confiscation following fraud convictions), and the Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021 (investigation of dissolved-company directors).</p>\n<p>Pay As You Grow, the no-enforcement-against-main-home rule, and the \u00a325,000 recovery threshold reflect the published British Business Bank Bounce Back Loan Scheme terms. Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm; where a CVL or Administration is the right answer, we can act as the licensed IP under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 212, 213, 214, 238, 239)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\">Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/35/contents\">Fraud Act 2006</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2002/29/contents\">Proceeds of Crime Act 2002</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2021/34/contents\">Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.british-business-bank.co.uk/ourpartners/coronavirus-business-interruption-loan-schemes/bounce-back-loans/\">British Business Bank - Bounce Back Loan Scheme</a> - british-business-bank.co.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bounce-back-loan-scheme\">HMRC - Bounce Back Loan misuse and director liability guidance</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-43675\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->"
  },
  "/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/directors-liability-for-bounce-back-loans/": {
    "id": 43580,
    "slug": "directors-liability-for-bounce-back-loans",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The question directors actually arrive at this page holding is not \"am I personally liable\" in the abstract. It is the quieter, more specific version: if my company cannot repay the Bounce Back Loan, is there a realistic route by which that problem reaches my personal bank account, my home, or my name on a register somewhere?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For the majority of directors who ran the loan through a limited company and used it for the business, the honest answer is no. Bounce Back Loans were advanced without personal guarantees, and the scheme rules prohibit enforcement against a main home or main vehicle.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ordinary business failure does not, on its own, pierce limited liability. We are clear on that, and we see it misunderstood often.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The narrower-but-real routes by which you can become personally liable on a BBL sit on a defined list. We cover each of them below so you know exactly where the exposure is.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-the-bounce-back-loan-scheme-structures-director-liability\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-the-bounce-back-loan-scheme-structures-director-liability\">How the Bounce Back Loan Scheme Structures Director Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Bounce Back Loan Scheme was built specifically to distribute capital quickly under COVID conditions. That design had three features directly relevant to personal liability:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>No personal guarantees.</strong> Unlike almost every other piece of commercial borrowing, the BBL deliberately stripped the PG out. Directors did not sign a personal contract alongside the company loan agreement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Government 100% guarantee</strong> to the lender. The bank's loss in default is underwritten by HM Treasury, which removed the usual commercial pressure to chase a director personally where the company could not pay.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Scheme-level protection on main home and main vehicle.</strong> The rules expressly prohibit enforcement of a BBL against a director's principal dwelling or primary vehicle. This protection applies to scheme enforcement; it does not override general personal-insolvency law.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For you as a limited company director, that protection layers on top of the ordinary corporate veil. The company is a separate legal person; its debts are its own; your personal assets sit behind that separation. Unless a specific route out of the default protection applies, you are not personally liable on the BBL.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are a sole trader, your BBL is personal from the outset because there is no separate legal entity. The scheme-level main-home and main-vehicle protection still applies, but general personal-insolvency rules remain live and you carry the full weight of that.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-specific-routes-to-personal-liability-on-a-bounce-back-loan\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-specific-routes-to-personal-liability-on-a-bounce-back-loan\">The Specific Routes to Personal Liability on a Bounce Back Loan</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Four specific routes take a director out of the default protection. They do not overlap cleanly; a single set of facts can trigger one, two, or all four.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Fraud or dishonest conduct on the application</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Knowingly inflating turnover figures to secure a larger loan, applying for a second Bounce Back Loan for the same company through a different lender, or applying on behalf of a company with no meaningful trading activity: these are forms of <a href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/bounce-back-loan-fraud/\">Bounce Back Loan fraud</a> under the Fraud Act 2006.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are on this track, criminal exposure is real: custodial sentences of up to ten years in serious cases, plus confiscation under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, which can reach assets including your family home.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The threshold is dishonesty. If you made a genuine misreading of the self-certification question, you are in a different legal position from a director who multiplied turnover by four because \"everyone was doing it\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Wrongful trading under section 214</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Continuing to trade past the point where insolvent liquidation was unavoidable, without taking every step a reasonably diligent director would take to minimise creditor losses, is wrongful trading under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The consequence is a court order requiring you to contribute personally to the losses caused by the continued trading.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Wrongful trading is a civil test based on negligence, not intent. The objective question is what a reasonably diligent director would have done, not what you actually believed at the time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Misfeasance under section 212</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Specific breaches of director's duty that caused loss to the company: misapplying company property, making unlawful distributions, paying connected parties in preference to trade creditors when insolvency was near. Misfeasance claims are brought by the liquidator and can produce personal contribution orders.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In BBL cases, the most common misfeasance pattern is using loan funds to clear a personal credit card or repay your director's loan account while trade creditors went unpaid. Both patterns are readily visible in the bank statements and both are standard findings in our IP reviews.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Separate personal guarantees on other borrowing</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the one most commonly missed. The BBL did not take a PG, but your company may have other facilities (commercial mortgage, invoice finance line, overdraft) that did. If the company fails, those other guarantees are called, producing personal liability on those debts, not on the BBL itself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical effect on your personal finances can be the same whether your exposure came via a BBL misconduct route or a separate PG on a different facility. In our experience, directors are often surprised to find their personal guarantee on the overdraft is the real exposure, not the BBL.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-the-insolvency-service-investigates-bounce-back-loan-cases\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-the-insolvency-service-investigates-bounce-back-loan-cases\">How the Insolvency Service Investigates Bounce Back Loan Cases</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investigations start with paperwork, not with dawn raids. The common entry points:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a> appointment.</strong> Every licensed Insolvency Practitioner has a statutory duty under section 7A of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 to file a conduct return on directors within three months of appointment. BBL misuse is a specific section of that return.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A strike-off objection.</strong> A lender objecting to a DS01 filing where a BBL is outstanding routinely triggers an Insolvency Service review.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A referral from another investigation.</strong> An HMRC VAT enquiry, a Companies House compliance action, or a separate criminal matter involving the same director can flag the BBL for examination.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Lender-identified irregularities.</strong> Recovery teams noting turnover on the application that does not match filed accounts, or transfers to personal accounts soon after draw-down.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What investigators review in practice: the application itself against your filed accounts and VAT returns; bank statements either side of draw-down to reconstruct use of funds; your director's loan account and any movements inside it; and the trading timeline to establish whether the business was genuinely active when you drew the loan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Possible outcomes, in rough order of severity: closed file with no action; director disqualification of 2 to 15 years, often with a compensation order; criminal referral to the National Investigation Service or a regional police economic-crime unit for prosecution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The bulk of cases resolve at the civil end, and your cooperation throughout matters. The criminal track is reserved for clear dishonesty and larger sums.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"protecting-yourself-against-bounce-back-loan-personal-liability\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"protecting-yourself-against-bounce-back-loan-personal-liability\">Protecting Yourself Against Bounce Back Loan Personal Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The protective steps sit on a short, actionable list.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Keep the paperwork.</strong> The application, bank statements for six months either side of draw-down, your director's loan account, management accounts for the period. The IP reconstructs the position from bank records regardless. Having your documents ready saves weeks and demonstrates cooperation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Segregate personal and business finances.</strong> Clean separation makes the misfeasance and preference defences easier. Personal card on business account, business card on personal account, informal cash drawings, all create ambiguity that reads badly in an IP's report.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Seek licensed insolvency advice early.</strong> The wrongful-trading defence rests on your taking every step a reasonably diligent director would take. Documented advice from a licensed IP is that defence. Our experience is clear: advice taken when the position is still recoverable is much cheaper than advice taken after the <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> arrives.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Correct mistakes voluntarily, not reluctantly.</strong> Inflated turnover on the application can be unwound through the Voluntary Repayment Scheme. Personal expenditure can be reinstated to the company. Both are significantly easier to frame as honest correction if done before the IP asks the question.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Avoid the dissolution shortcut.</strong> Attempting to strike the company off the register while a BBL is outstanding is the single most counter-productive move available. Lenders object, the Insolvency Service reads it as evasion, and the attempt itself becomes evidence at the subsequent conduct review.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-bounce-back-loan-personal-liability-risk\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-bounce-back-loan-personal-liability-risk\">Your Next Step on Bounce Back Loan Personal Liability Risk</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are in the majority position (clean BBL use, straightforward business failure, no misconduct concerns) the default protection does what it says. Document your use of funds, keep the paperwork, and move to a CVL if closure is the right answer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you recognise any of the risk patterns (overstated turnover, personal use of loan funds, a second loan on a different lender, or continued trading well past obvious insolvency), book an hour with a licensed IP before any formal process begins. An hour now, while the position is still recoverable, is the cheapest hour you will buy on this problem.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In our experience, the conversations that begin before the letter arrives consistently produce lighter outcomes than the ones that begin after.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice on your specific position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"bounce-back-loan-personal-liability-faqs\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"bounce-back-loan-personal-liability-faqs\">Bounce Back Loan Personal Liability FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"4bb7b293-0b3c-4a38-9567-fdd82a4b76b4\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan the bank seize my personal assets if my company defaults on the Bounce Back Loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. BBLs were advanced without personal guarantees, and the scheme rules prohibit enforcement against a director's main home or main vehicle. Banks have no contractual route to personal enforcement on a BBL alone. Personal exposure comes through insolvency misconduct findings, not through the loan agreement itself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if I used the Bounce Back Loan for personal expenses?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Personal expenditure from BBL funds is a breach of the scheme rules and, in insolvency, a breach of director's duty. A liquidator can pursue a personal contribution for the misapplied amount. Where the amounts are small and the explanation is credible, this often resolves civilly. Where the amounts are substantial or clearly dishonest, a fraud investigation is the harder end of the track.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCould I face personal liability if I dissolve my company without repaying the Bounce Back Loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. The Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021 specifically extends investigatory powers to former directors of dissolved companies where BBL misuse is suspected. Attempted strike-off with a live BBL is a pattern that triggers that power directly. Personal liability can follow from the conduct review, even though the company no longer exists.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill my personal credit score be affected if my company defaults?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not directly on a limited company BBL without a personal guarantee. Indirect effects arise where a separate personal guarantee is called on other borrowing, where a charging order is obtained against your property in subsequent personal-liability proceedings, or where a bankruptcy petition is issued.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A clean CVL on a limited-company BBL does not, by itself, affect your personal credit file.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow quickly does the Insolvency Service act if it suspects Bounce Back Loan misuse?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Conduct reviews at the civil end typically run six to eighteen months from IP appointment to disqualification proceedings. Criminal investigations are slower and more targeted. The time-lag matters: misconduct from 2020 is still being prosecuted in 2025 and 2026, and you should not assume the window has closed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"methodology-amp-disclosure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"methodology-amp-disclosure\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team, reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners, and reflects UK insolvency and BBL-scheme rules as at the last-reviewed date. Statutory references are drawn from the Fraud Act 2006, Insolvency Act 1986, Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, and the Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where we recommend a CVL, we can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/35/contents\"><strong>Fraud Act 2006</strong> &mdash; criminal framework engaged where a BBL application involved knowingly inflated turnover, a duplicate loan, or a non-trading company</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; sections 212 misfeasance, 213 fraudulent trading and 214 wrongful trading, the three civil routes to personal contribution orders after a BBL failure</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; section 7A requires every IP to file a conduct return within three months of appointment, the entry point for most BBL investigations</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2021/34/contents\"><strong>Rating (Coronavirus) and Directors Disqualification (Dissolved Companies) Act 2021</strong> &mdash; extends Insolvency Service investigation and disqualification powers to directors of struck-off companies, closing the dissolution shortcut</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2002/29/contents\"><strong>Proceeds of Crime Act 2002</strong> &mdash; confiscation regime that can reach personal assets, including a family home, where BBL fraud results in a criminal conviction</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/advice/unenforceable-personal-guarantee/": {
    "id": 43522,
    "slug": "unenforceable-personal-guarantee",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The letter from the bank tends to arrive in the same week everything else does. Invoice Finance have pulled the facility. Two big clients have gone quiet. And now, here it is: a formal demand under the personal guarantee you signed in 2019, with a ten-day deadline and a number you cannot find in any bank account you currently own.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That letter is the question most directors come to this page holding. Is there any chance this is unenforceable? Can I fight this?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The honest answer, in most cases, is no. Personal guarantees given to UK lenders with proper process are usually enforceable. But \"usually\" is not \"always\", and a small number of defences genuinely bite when they apply.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The more useful answer we give in our practice is a separate one: even where your guarantee is enforceable, it is rarely enforceable on the terms of the original demand, and the negotiation window is wider than most directors realise.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What follows sets out both sides for you. The narrow grounds on which a personal guarantee is actually unenforceable, and the broader set of responses we would consider with you when the guarantee is enforceable but the demand is not the end of the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When a Personal Guarantee Is Genuinely Unenforceable</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The defences that make a personal guarantee unenforceable are technical, narrow, and fact-specific. Broad arguments about fairness rarely win in court. The grounds that actually work have been litigated enough times to have recognisable shapes:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Material misrepresentation at the time of signing.</strong> The lender told you another director was signing as a co-guarantor and they were not; the lender stated the facility had a ceiling that was immediately exceeded; the lender described the guarantee's scope in terms that did not match the document. Where the false statement was material to your decision to sign, the guarantee can be rescinded.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Duress or undue influence.</strong> The guarantee was signed under pressure that went beyond ordinary commercial negotiation, or in a context where the lender knew or ought to have known the guarantor was not freely consenting. Spousal guarantees signed in the presence of the director-spouse have been rescinded on this basis in well-known authorities.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fraud on the part of the lender or a third party.</strong> Rare, but where established, fatal to enforcement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>An unfair term under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999</strong>((Trusted Source - UK Government - <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1999/2083/contents/made\">Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999</a>)) or, for more recent guarantees, the Consumer Rights Act 2015. Only the court can declare a term unfair, but the mere credible prospect that a term is unfair can shift a negotiation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Formal defects.</strong> A guarantee not evidenced in writing is unenforceable under section 4 of the Statute of Frauds 1677. A guarantee unsigned by the guarantor, or signed only in a form that does not meet the statute, is similarly vulnerable. These are less common than directors hope, because banks' standard documents almost always satisfy the formalities.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most modern lender practice closes the obvious gaps. It is now standard for a lender to require the guarantor to take independent legal advice, to sign in the presence of a witness, and to confirm understanding of the obligation in writing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where those steps were completed, the defences above narrow sharply. The directors we see succeed in rescinding a personal guarantee usually do so on misrepresentation or undue influence grounds, not on formality or fairness arguments.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where more than one director signed, the guarantee will almost certainly be on a joint-and-several basis. The lender can pursue all guarantors or pick one. In practice, they pursue the one with the most accessible equity, usually the house. A co-guarantor's defence does not protect the others from enforcement; it only strengthens their own position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Enforceable Is a Personal Guarantee in Practice</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enforcement is not instant, and the gap between demand and execution is where almost all useful work happens.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The typical path from missed company payment to enforced guarantee runs:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Default letter to the company, followed by a formal demand.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Demand under the personal guarantee once the company default is established.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Pre-action correspondence, usually allowing a short window to respond with a proposal.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>County court or High Court claim if no agreement is reached.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Judgment, followed by enforcement, most commonly a charging order on property equity, occasionally bankruptcy proceedings where equity is insufficient.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequence can take six to eighteen months from missed payment to enforced judgment, depending on the lender's appetite and the borrower's response. The window is not a loophole; it is the structure of civil debt recovery. But it does mean the \"ten days to pay\" wording on a demand letter is almost never the real deadline.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three formalities materially affect whether a given personal guarantee is enforceable at all:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>In writing</strong>, the Statute of Frauds requires a guarantee to be evidenced in writing to be enforceable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Signed</strong>, by the guarantor or their duly authorised agent. Printed signatures satisfy the requirement, but unsigned documents do not.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Consideration</strong>, ordinary contract formation rules apply. Offer, acceptance, consideration, intention to create legal relations, capacity.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A guarantee signed as a deed does not need separate consideration, and in practice most bank guarantees are executed as deeds specifically to close that formation argument. Worth checking on the document in front of you before making any argument on consideration.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Long a Personal Guarantee Stays Enforceable</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The standing position is simple and unflattering to the guarantor. Absent a specific clause to the contrary, the guarantor remains liable until the underlying business debt is released. Repayment in full discharges both. A formal release from the lender discharges both. Nothing else reliably does.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That has practical consequences:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Resignation as a director does not cancel the guarantee.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The business ceasing to trade does not cancel the guarantee.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The business being liquidated does not cancel the guarantee, in fact, it typically triggers the call on it.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Some guarantees include a cancellation clause allowing the guarantor to terminate on notice in defined circumstances, often limited to future advances rather than the existing balance. These exist more often in invoice finance and asset-backed lending than in clearing bank overdrafts, but they are worth checking in the document.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Limitation periods cap how long the lender has to bring proceedings. For a guarantee executed as a simple contract, the limitation period under the Limitation Act 1980 is six years from the date of default. Where the guarantee is executed as a deed, the period is twelve years. The clock starts on default, not on signing, which is why decade-old guarantees can still produce live claims.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Is a Personal Guarantee Enforceable After a Director Resigns?</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Yes. This is the single most common misunderstanding in the area. Resignation changes nothing about the guarantee.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical steps for a director planning an exit from a company with guaranteed borrowing:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Ask the lender to release the guarantee. Make the request in writing and get the response in writing. A conversation with a relationship manager is not a release.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Ask any incoming director to execute a replacement guarantee. Banks are more willing to substitute than to release.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>If release is refused, consider whether the facility itself can be refinanced away from the guarantee, a new facility without PG, or a facility secured on company assets only. Clean refinance is the cleanest exit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A lender is not obliged to release a guarantee on a director's resignation, and where the company's position is weak they almost certainly will not. But where the business is in good health and meeting its terms, a structured request often succeeds where an informal ask fails.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Getting Out of an Enforceable Personal Guarantee</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the guarantee is enforceable, the question is not whether to pay but on what terms. Four routes are worth looking at in sequence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal Guarantee Insurance.</strong> Where you took out PG insurance at the time of signing, it typically covers around 70% of the guaranteed liability on a call. Policies are more common on invoice finance and asset-backed lending than on bank overdrafts. Check the original paperwork before assuming it does not apply.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Negotiation.</strong> Lenders enforce through the courts only when the expected recovery exceeds the cost. Where equity is modest, other assets are limited, or the guarantor is prepared to make an early structured settlement, lenders will commonly accept 40\u201370% of the demanded amount on a lump-sum basis. The negotiation works best when it is led by a professional with experience of the specific lender, and when it is backed by a credible statement of means.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Individual Voluntary Arrangement.</strong> An IVA is a formal, legally binding arrangement with the guarantor's creditors, spreading repayment over three to five years and writing off unpaid balances at the end. An IVA stops enforcement once approved. It damages credit materially for six years, but where the alternative is bankruptcy the trade-off is usually worth making.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Bankruptcy as the last resort.</strong> In extreme cases, bankruptcy is the cleanest route to a structured outcome. The trustee takes over the position, creditors are treated pari passu, and the guarantor is discharged (usually after twelve months) with the guarantee debt extinguished. It is the worst option on the menu, but where the demands exceed realisable assets by a wide margin, it is sometimes the only route that closes the file.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One decision that almost always makes things worse: trying to trade out of the underlying business position by selling company assets cheaply to connected parties, moving funds around, or continuing to trade past the point of insolvency in the hope that the guarantee will expire.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Each of those shows up in any subsequent insolvency review and converts the guarantee problem into a misfeasance or preference claim stacked on top. The guarantee is bad. The guarantee plus a director-conduct finding is significantly worse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Unenforceable Personal Guarantee FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"43522-pg-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I argue my personal guarantee is unfair and therefore unenforceable?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Possibly, but only the court can actually declare a term unfair. The practical value is usually in pre-action leverage rather than a final judgment. Lenders sometimes prefer a negotiated settlement to the cost and risk of litigating a marginal enforceability point.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes taking independent legal advice before signing mean I cannot challenge the guarantee?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>It narrows the defences available. Independent advice is how lenders inoculate against later undue influence or non-disclosure arguments. It does not, however, rescue a guarantee that was given on the basis of a material misrepresentation by the lender.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs a personal guarantee enforceable if the business is in liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. Liquidation does not release the guarantor; it typically triggers the call on the guarantee. The liquidator pursues the company assets; the lender pursues the guarantor separately.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if another director was supposed to sign but did not?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>If you signed on the understanding that another director was also signing, and that representation was material to your decision, the guarantee may be rescindable on misrepresentation grounds. The argument is fact-specific and requires evidence of the representation, not just your belief.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I negotiate a lower settlement on an enforceable personal guarantee?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Usually yes. Lenders enforce through the courts only where the expected recovery exceeds the cost. Lump-sum settlements at 40\u201370% of the demanded amount are reasonably common where the guarantor's realisable assets are limited and the settlement is credible.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#ffffff\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the time limit for a lender to enforce a personal guarantee?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Six years from default for a simple contract, twelve years where the guarantee was executed as a deed. Most bank guarantees are executed as deeds specifically for this reason.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on an Unenforceable Personal Guarantee Question</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise small business directors on personal guarantees called in at the point of business insolvency on a regular basis. Most of the useful work is not the unenforceability argument, it is the sequencing of the business position, the guarantee response, and, where relevant, the personal insolvency route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have received a demand, the two things we would tell you to do before you reply are: pull the original guarantee document and read it carefully (particularly the scope clause and any cancellation rights), and get a confidential view from an experienced adviser on where the specific lender typically settles.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The demand is the start of your negotiation, not the end of it. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for a confidential view before you respond to the letter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by our editorial team and reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners and the commercial solicitors we work alongside on guarantee enforcement matters. It reflects UK contract and insolvency law as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our statutory references are the Statute of Frauds 1677 (writing requirement for guarantees), the Misrepresentation Act 1967, the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999 and the Consumer Rights Act 2015 (unfair-terms controls where the guarantor signed as a consumer), and the Limitation Act 1980 (enforcement windows). Our case-law reference cites <em>Royal Bank of Scotland plc v Etridge (No 2)</em> [2001] UKHL 44 on undue influence and the requirement for independent legal advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where a called PG sits alongside company insolvency, we can act as the licensed IP under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation; nothing is charged until a scope is agreed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/aep/Cha2/29/3/contents\"><strong>Statute of Frauds 1677, section 4</strong> &mdash; the writing requirement that makes an oral or unsigned personal guarantee unenforceable against the guarantor</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1967/7/contents\"><strong>Misrepresentation Act 1967</strong> &mdash; statutory basis for rescinding a guarantee where the lender made a material false statement that induced the director to sign</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1977/50/contents\"><strong>Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977</strong> &mdash; controls onerous or unusual clauses in business guarantees that were not fairly brought to the guarantor's attention</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1999/2083/contents/made\"><strong>Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999</strong> &mdash; the older consumer-facing fairness regime relevant to guarantees signed before October 2015</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2015/15/contents\"><strong>Consumer Rights Act 2015</strong> &mdash; current fairness test applied to guarantees where the guarantor signed in a non-business capacity outside their trade</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1980/58/contents\"><strong>Limitation Act 1980</strong> &mdash; the six- and twelve-year limitation periods that can render a stale guarantee claim unenforceable depending on whether it was executed as a deed</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKHL/2001/44.html\"><strong>Royal Bank of Scotland plc v Etridge (No 2) [2001] UKHL 44</strong> &mdash; leading House of Lords authority on undue influence and the lender's duty to ensure a guarantor receives independent legal advice</a> - bailii.org</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":63061,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"custom\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Quick-Quote-1024x598.png\" alt=\"Quick Quote for Closing a Company\" class=\"wp-image-63061\" /></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/what-happens-if-a-company-cannot-pay-its-debts/": {
    "id": 42739,
    "slug": "what-happens-if-a-company-cannot-pay-its-debts",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is usually a Tuesday. The VAT return is sitting open on a screen, the payroll run is two days away, and a director realises the maths does not work this month. That is the moment most of our calls begin. The mistake we see most often in the next 48 hours is treating the realisation as a cash-flow problem when, legally, it has already become an insolvency event.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once a UK company cannot pay its debts as they fall due, or its liabilities exceed its assets, your duties as a director shift from shareholders to creditors. Continuing to trade past that point without taking advice is what creates personal liability. This guide names the moment, sets out the four scenarios you might actually be in, and tells you what to do before the next payment run.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"company-cannot-pay-debts-at-a-glance\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"company-cannot-pay-debts-at-a-glance\">Company Cannot Pay Debts at a Glance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When a UK limited company cannot pay its debts as they fall due, it is technically insolvent under section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986. From that point director duties shift from shareholders to creditors under the BTI v Sequana principle, and continuing to trade without taking active steps to minimise creditor loss puts the directors personally at risk under section 214 (wrongful trading). The realistic options are a Time to Pay arrangement, an informal creditor negotiation, a CVA, administration, or a creditors&#8217; voluntary liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Quick Answer: When a Company Cannot Pay Its Debts</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your company cannot pay its debts as they fall due, it is cash-flow insolvent under section 123(1)(e) of the Insolvency Act 1986. From that moment, your duty as a director shifts to protecting creditor interests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You must stop incurring credit you cannot pay back, get written advice from a licensed insolvency practitioner, and choose between rescue, sale, or a formal procedure within days, not weeks.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When \"Cannot Pay\" Becomes an Insolvency Event</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>\"Cannot pay\" is not one situation. It is four, and the legal consequences are different for each. The contrarian point most directors miss: balance-sheet insolvency, not cash-flow insolvency, is what most often triggers wrongful-trading liability later, because it is the test that survives even when you are still scraping payments together.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Main Director Risk When the Company Cannot Pay</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The main risk is wrongful trading under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986. If you keep trading after the point where you knew, or should have known, there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation, a court can order you to contribute personally to the shortfall. The risk is sharper when your decision-making is undocumented.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to Do Next If the Company Cannot Pay</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hold a minuted board meeting within seven days that records the cash position, the liabilities, and the advice taken. Stop selective payments to favoured creditors. Get written insolvency-practitioner advice before the next supplier or bank payment goes out. Then choose your route: rescue, restructure, sell, or close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-cannot-pay-actually-means-in-uk-law\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-cannot-pay-actually-means-in-uk-law\">What \"Cannot Pay\" Actually Means in UK Law</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK insolvency law does not treat \"we cannot pay this month\" as a single legal status. Under section 123 of the <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/123\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Insolvency Act 1986</a>, a company is deemed unable to pay its debts in two distinct ways. The distinction matters because each test triggers different director duties and different creditor remedies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cash-Flow Insolvency Test (s.123(1) IA 1986)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 123(1)(e) is the headline test most directors recognise. Your company is cash-flow insolvent if it cannot pay its debts as they fall due. A statutory demand for more than \u00a3750 left unpaid for 21 days is enough to prove it. So is an unsatisfied judgment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The test is mechanical. It does not care whether the underlying business is viable. If you missed last month's PAYE because the receivable from your largest customer landed late, you were technically cash-flow insolvent at that moment, even if everything cleared two weeks later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Balance-Sheet Insolvency Test (s.123(2) IA 1986)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 123(2) is the test most directors do not appreciate until afterwards. Your company is balance-sheet insolvent when its liabilities, including contingent and prospective liabilities, exceed its assets. You can be balance-sheet insolvent and still meeting payments. You can pass the cash-flow test on any given Tuesday and still be balance-sheet insolvent every day of the year.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the test that bites later. When an administrator or liquidator looks back at when the duty to creditors first arose, they often anchor on balance-sheet insolvency, especially where directors took on more credit while deficits were widening. We see this in almost every wrongful-trading review we run.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Difference Between Temporary Cash Gap and Insolvency</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A temporary cash gap is when funds are landing, just not in the right week. The receivable is contracted, the buyer is solvent, and the gap is bridgeable through forbearance, an overdraft headroom call, or a director loan. It is not insolvency, but it can become insolvency quickly if forbearance is refused.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The dividing line is honest evidence. If you can show, on paper, that the cash will arrive within a defined window and you have written agreement from creditors to wait, you are managing a gap. If you cannot, you are insolvent and need to act on that footing. Our triage on how to save a struggling business covers the bridging steps when the gap is genuinely short term.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-to-tell-which-scenario-the-company-cannot-pay-falls-into\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-to-tell-which-scenario-the-company-cannot-pay-falls-into\">How to Tell Which Scenario the Company Cannot Pay Falls Into</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You cannot decide what to do next until you know which of the four scenarios you are in. The diagnosis is forensic, not optimistic. The three documents below are what an insolvency practitioner will look at first, and they are the documents we ask for on every initial call.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">13-Week Cash-Flow Forecast as the Trigger Document</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A 13-week cash-flow forecast, week by week, with realistic collection dates and committed outgoings, is the document that decides scenario (a) versus (b). If the forecast shows a positive closing cash balance every week with reasonable haircuts on receipts, you are managing a gap. If any week dips below zero, you are cash-flow insolvent in that week.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Build the forecast yourself, in a spreadsheet you can defend later. Do not let optimism creep in. If your largest customer has paid late three months running, model them at 30 days late, not 0.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Statement of Assets and Liabilities as the Balance-Sheet Test</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For the balance-sheet test, prepare a statement of assets and liabilities at realisable, not book, values. Plant and machinery at auction value, not depreciated cost. Stock at trade-sale prices. Debtors net of an honest provision. Then list every liability, including contingent ones: dilapidations, personal guarantees called, HMRC penalties accruing, redundancy if you closed the doors today.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If liabilities exceed assets on this honest basis, you are balance-sheet insolvent. This is the moment most directors discover they crossed the line months earlier. See our framework on the insolvency test for worked examples.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Creditor Pressure as the External Trigger</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Creditor pressure is the third diagnostic. A statutory demand, a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> advertised in the Gazette, or a single creditor refusing further forbearance is an external signal that the cash-flow test will be deemed proven if challenged.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are at this point, the question is no longer whether you are insolvent but how creditors will be paid. Our guide on dealing with creditor pressure covers the immediate triage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-options-are-available-when-the-company-cannot-pay\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-options-are-available-when-the-company-cannot-pay\">What Options Are Available When the Company Cannot Pay</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your options collapse into three categories once you have diagnosed the scenario honestly. Each category protects creditors differently and exposes you to different residual risks. The comparison below is the version we walk directors through on a first call.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Informal Agreement or Repayment Plan</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have a temporary cash gap or one large creditor causing the pressure, an informal arrangement may hold. Time to Pay with HMRC is the most common. Bilateral forbearance with a landlord or trade creditor works where the relationship has history. The catch: informal agreements offer no protection from the next creditor who refuses to play along.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rescue or Restructuring Procedure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the underlying business is viable but the debt load is not, a formal rescue procedure binds creditors and gives you a window to trade through. A Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA) restructures debt by 75% creditor vote.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Administration imposes a moratorium and gives an administrator the steering wheel. The Restructuring Plan under Part 26A of the Companies Act 2006 can cram down dissenting classes. Detail on each procedure sits in our guide to rescuing a business from insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sale, Closure or Insolvency Procedure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the business is not viable, the route is sale or orderly closure. A pre-pack administration sells the business as a going concern at administrator-controlled valuation. A <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a> (CVL) closes the company at the directors' initiative. Compulsory liquidation, by contrast, is forced on you by a winding-up petition and removes you from the steering wheel entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"hasFixedLayout\":false} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Best for</th><th>Director control</th><th>Creditor protection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Time to Pay (HMRC)</td><td>Tax-only cash gap, otherwise solvent</td><td>Full</td><td>None against other creditors</td></tr><tr><td>CVA</td><td>Viable trading, unsustainable debt</td><td>Retained, supervised</td><td>Binds 75% by value</td></tr><tr><td>Restructuring Plan</td><td>Complex creditor classes, secured debt</td><td>Retained, court-supervised</td><td>Cross-class cram down</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Rescue or going-concern sale needed</td><td>Lost to administrator</td><td>Statutory moratorium</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-Pack Administration</td><td>Going-concern sale, speed needed</td><td>Lost to administrator</td><td>SIP 16 disclosure to creditors</td></tr><tr><td>CVL</td><td>Not viable, orderly closure wanted</td><td>Lost to liquidator</td><td>Pari passu distribution</td></tr><tr><td>Strike-off</td><td>No debts, no trading, no assets</td><td>Full until strike-off</td><td>Creditors can object</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-risks-should-directors-watch-when-the-company-cannot-pay\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-risks-should-directors-watch-when-the-company-cannot-pay\">What Risks Should Directors Watch When the Company Cannot Pay</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The personal-liability risks below are not theoretical. Each one has its own statutory basis, its own evidence trail, and its own track record in the Insolvency Service investigation files. The risks compound. Directors who walk into one usually walk into two or three.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"hasFixedLayout\":false} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Statutory basis</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wrongful trading</td><td>s.214 Insolvency Act 1986</td><td>Personal contribution to creditor losses if you traded past the no-reasonable-prospect point</td></tr><tr><td>Fraudulent trading</td><td>s.213 Insolvency Act 1986</td><td>Personal liability and possible criminal sanction for trading with intent to defraud creditors</td></tr><tr><td>Preferences</td><td>s.239 Insolvency Act 1986</td><td>Court can reverse payments to a creditor (often a connected party) made in the 6 months before insolvency</td></tr><tr><td>Transactions at undervalue</td><td>s.238 Insolvency Act 1986</td><td>Court can reverse asset transfers below market value made in the 2 years before insolvency</td></tr><tr><td>Personal guarantees</td><td>Contract law</td><td>Bank, landlord, or supplier can pursue your personal assets directly</td></tr><tr><td>Director loan account overdraft</td><td>Companies Act 2006 / IA 1986</td><td>Liquidator will demand repayment of overdrawn DLA in full</td></tr><tr><td>Director disqualification</td><td>CDDA 1986</td><td>Up to 15 years' ban from directorship for unfit conduct</td></tr><tr><td>Misfeasance</td><td>s.212 Insolvency Act 1986</td><td>Personal liability for breach of fiduciary duty to creditors</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-directors-should-do-when-the-company-cannot-pay\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-directors-should-do-when-the-company-cannot-pay\">What Directors Should Do When the Company Cannot Pay</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Call a Solvency Review Board Meeting Within 7 Days and Minute the Cash Position</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Convene a board meeting with one item on the agenda: solvency. Minute the current cash position, the 13-week forecast, the statement of assets and liabilities, and the advice taken. The minute is the document that defends you later. We have seen well-run boards survive an investigation almost entirely on the strength of contemporaneous minutes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Stop Selective and Connected-Party Payments Immediately</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From the realisation moment, every payment must be defensible to a future liquidator. Paying the bank because you fear the overdraft being pulled, while letting HMRC slide, is a preference under section 239. Paying yourself or a related party in priority to trade creditors is the textbook claim a liquidator will run. Stop both immediately.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Get Written Insolvency-Practitioner Advice Before the Next Creditor Pays Out</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Verbal reassurance from your accountant is not enough. Get advice in writing from a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by one of the recognised professional bodies, dated and on letterhead. The advice itself, even if you choose not to take the recommended route, is evidence you took your duty to creditors seriously. Our <a href=\"/insolvency/insolvent-company-investigations/\">director investigations guide</a> covers what investigators look for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"mistakes-directors-make-when-the-company-cannot-pay\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"mistakes-directors-make-when-the-company-cannot-pay\">Mistakes Directors Make When the Company Cannot Pay</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Paying the Bank or Landlord Before HMRC Out of \"Goodwill\"</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The instinct is human: keep the bank sweet, keep the landlord on side. The legal effect is a preference. Once you are insolvent or about to become so, every creditor you pay is a creditor you put ahead of HMRC and the wider unsecured pool. A liquidator will unwind it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Selling Assets to a Connected Party at Book Value</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Transferring the van fleet to a phoenix company at written-down book value, weeks before the insolvency, is one of the most common transactions at undervalue we see. It almost always reverses. If you need to sell assets to a connected party, you need an independent valuation and you need it on file before, not after, the transfer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Trading On Because \"Next Month Will Be Better\"</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The single most expensive mistake. Each month traded after the no-reasonable-prospect point widens the creditor deficit you may be ordered to contribute to personally. Hope is not a defence to a wrongful-trading claim. Documented evidence of why the prospect was reasonable, week by week, is. If you do not have that evidence, you are exposed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"related-guides-for-when-the-company-cannot-pay\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"related-guides-for-when-the-company-cannot-pay\">Related Guides for When the Company Cannot Pay</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>How to save a struggling business: operational triage when the gap is genuinely short term.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Rescue your business from insolvency: formal procedure routing across CVA, Administration, Restructuring Plan.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Dealing with creditor pressure: what to do when a single creditor is forcing the pace.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/cease-trading/\">Cease trading</a>: the holding-pattern frame once you have stopped trading but not yet entered a procedure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Company Voluntary Arrangement: detailed CVA mechanics, voting thresholds, and supervisor role.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"frequently-asked-questions-when-a-company-cannot-pay-its-debts\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"frequently-asked-questions-when-a-company-cannot-pay-its-debts\">Frequently Asked Questions When a Company Cannot Pay Its Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"6ca2dafc-a723-42ab-9c15-d3503cbc0a88\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"hasFAQSchema\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"hasFAQSchema\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs my company insolvent if I missed one VAT payment?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Technically, you were cash-flow insolvent at the moment the payment was due and unpaid, under section 123(1)(e) of the Insolvency Act 1986. Whether that is a one-off cash gap or evidence of a deeper problem depends on your 13-week forecast and your balance-sheet position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>One missed payment that you can show was a timing issue is recoverable. A pattern of missed payments, with no clear bridge to solvency, is not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan directors be held personally liable when a company cannot pay its debts?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, in several distinct ways. Wrongful trading under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 is the most common, and exposes you to personal contribution to creditor losses. Personal guarantees expose you on specific debts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Overdrawn director loan accounts must be repaid in full. Misfeasance under section 212 covers breach of fiduciary duty more broadly. Each route has its own evidence trail and its own remedy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow does HMRC behave when a company cannot pay PAYE or VAT?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC's first move is usually a Time to Pay arrangement if you ask early and the figures are credible. If you ignore the debt, expect penalties, interest, and ultimately a winding-up petition.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC has been the petitioning creditor on more than half of compulsory liquidations in recent years. They are not lenient, but they are predictable. Engaging early, with a realistic proposal, almost always produces a better outcome than waiting for enforcement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if I have personally guaranteed a company loan?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>If the company defaults, the creditor can pursue you personally for the guaranteed amount. The guarantee survives the insolvency of the company; it does not vanish.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Some lenders will negotiate a reduced settlement once they see the wider creditor picture, but you must engage the lender directly. We commonly negotiate guarantee positions in parallel with the company-level procedure, because the two outcomes interact.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eShould I stop trading immediately to avoid wrongful trading?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not necessarily. Stopping trade abruptly can itself increase creditor losses if the business has saleable goodwill or going-concern value.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The correct answer is to stop trading on the same basis you were yesterday, take written advice within days, and let the practitioner advise on whether to continue trading under controlled conditions, sell the business, or close. Continuing without advice is the dangerous route, not continuing under advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes a CCJ mean my company is insolvent?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A County Court Judgment is evidence of a debt, not automatic proof of insolvency. But an unsatisfied judgment, where the creditor has tried and failed to enforce, is one of the statutory routes to deeming the company unable to pay its debts under section 123. If you have an unsatisfied CCJ, the creditor's next move is often a winding-up petition, and you should treat it as the late stage it is.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow quickly should I get insolvency advice?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Within days of the realisation, not weeks. The director-protection value of advice falls sharply once trading continues past the no-reasonable-prospect point. Most insolvency practitioners, including our team, offer a free initial discussion that costs you nothing and lets you choose your next move with the law in front of you. The earliest call is the cheapest one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"methodology-block\">\n<p><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>The contrarian editorial line on this page (that balance-sheet insolvency, not cash-flow insolvency, is what most often triggers wrongful-trading liability later) was tested against the section 214 case files our practitioners have run since 2014 and against the published Insolvency Service disqualification reports.</p>\n<p>We have not softened the framing. Most directors arrive convinced cash-flow is the only legal trigger; the case files we have run since 2014 say balance-sheet exposure is what carries the personal-liability tail.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a UK insolvency advisory firm that earns fees when directors instruct one of our regulated insolvency practitioners. That is our commercial role.</p>\n<p>The editorial position above is the same advice we give on a free initial call, regardless of whether the director instructs us afterwards. Editorial review by Christopher Andersen, ICAEW-licensed insolvency practitioner since 2014. Last reviewed 30 April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"sources-block\">\n<p><strong>Sources and References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/123\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Insolvency Act 1986, section 123: definition of inability to pay debts</a> (legislation.gov.uk).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Insolvency Act 1986, section 214: wrongful trading</a> (legislation.gov.uk).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/213\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Insolvency Act 1986, section 213: fraudulent trading</a> (legislation.gov.uk).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/172\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Companies Act 2006, section 172: duty to promote success of the company (and the creditor-duty shift confirmed in BTI v Sequana)</a> (legislation.gov.uk).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</a> (legislation.gov.uk).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">UK Insolvency Service: procedure overview and disqualification reports</a> (gov.uk).</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/advice/can-personal-assets-of-directors-be-seized-from-a-ltd-company/": {
    "id": 41082,
    "slug": "can-personal-assets-of-directors-be-seized-from-a-ltd-company",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your personal assets are protected by limited liability. But limited liability has limits. If you gave a personal guarantee, have an overdrawn director's loan account, or continued trading while the company was insolvent, your home, savings, and other personal assets can be reached by creditors, the liquidator, or the court.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see directors assume that the \"limited\" in limited company means their personal assets are untouchable. For the company's debts themselves, that is correct. A trade creditor who is owed money by the company cannot send bailiffs to your house for the company's debt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But the routes to personal asset seizure are more numerous than most directors realise, and they are the routes the liquidator is specifically trained to investigate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have worked with directors who lost their family home because of a personal guarantee they signed in 2019 and forgot about. We have worked with others who kept everything because they understood where the boundaries were and stayed inside them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-when-can-a-director-s-personal-assets-be-seized\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-when-can-a-director-s-personal-assets-be-seized\">Quick Answer: When Can a Director's Personal Assets Be Seized?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your personal assets can be seized or pursued in five specific situations: (1) you gave a personal guarantee, (2) your director's loan account is overdrawn, (3) a court makes a wrongful trading contribution order under section 214, (4) HMRC issues a personal liability notice for unpaid PAYE or NICs, or (5) you are made personally bankrupt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Outside these routes, your personal assets are protected by the company's separate legal personality.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"personal-guarantees-the-direct-route-to-director-assets\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"personal-guarantees-the-direct-route-to-director-assets\">Personal Guarantees: The Direct Route to Director Assets</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A personal guarantee is a contractual promise that you will pay the company's debt if the company cannot. It is the most common way personal assets become exposed after company insolvency. Banks require them for business loans. Landlords require them for commercial leases. Some suppliers require them for trade credit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When the company enters liquidation, the creditor who holds your guarantee can pursue you directly for the guaranteed amount. If the guarantee is secured against your property (a charge registered at the Land Registry), the creditor can apply for a charging order and ultimately force a sale.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the guarantee is unsecured, the creditor can obtain a county court judgement against you and enforce through bailiffs, attachment of earnings, or a bankruptcy petition.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We tell every director to audit their guarantee position before the company enters any formal process. Check every loan agreement, every lease, and every credit facility for guarantee clauses.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have seen directors discover guarantees they did not remember signing, buried in the schedules of a facility agreement from years ago. The guarantee does not care whether you remember it. It cares whether you signed it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"overdrawn-director-s-loan-account\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"overdrawn-director-s-loan-account\">Overdrawn Director's Loan Account</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your current account with the company is overdrawn (you owe money to the company), the liquidator will demand repayment. The overdrawn balance is an asset of the company, and the liquidator has a duty to recover it for creditors. If you cannot or do not repay, the liquidator can pursue the claim through the courts and enforce against your personal assets.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see overdrawn loan accounts that accumulated gradually: \u00a3500 here for a personal expense, \u00a31,000 there for a cash withdrawal, until the balance reaches \u00a320,000 or \u00a330,000.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors often assume these will be written off in liquidation. They will not. The liquidator treats overdrawn DLAs as one of the first and easiest assets to recover, because the debt is documented in the company's accounts and is not disputable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"wrongful-trading-contribution-orders-against-directors\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"wrongful-trading-contribution-orders-against-directors\">Wrongful Trading Contribution Orders Against Directors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the liquidator brings a successful wrongful trading claim under section 214, the court orders you to contribute personally to the company's assets. The amount reflects the increase in the company's deficiency during the period you continued trading when you should have stopped. This is a personal debt enforceable against your assets.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you cannot pay the contribution order from liquid assets, the creditor (the liquidation estate, through the liquidator) can enforce against your property, savings, and other assets. In extreme cases, the inability to pay a contribution order can lead to personal bankruptcy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"hmrc-personal-liability-notices-against-directors\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"hmrc-personal-liability-notices-against-directors\">HMRC Personal Liability Notices Against Directors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC can issue personal liability notices to directors for unpaid PAYE and employee NICs that the company deducted from wages but failed to remit. These are your personal debts, not the company's. HMRC can enforce them through standard debt recovery procedures, including bailiffs, charging orders, and bankruptcy petitions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find these notices often arrive months after the company has been dissolved. The director has moved on, assumed everything was dealt with by the liquidator, and then receives an HMRC demand that reopens the entire chapter. If you owe HMRC for unremitted payroll taxes, that debt follows you regardless of what happened to the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-cannot-be-seized-the-limits-of-director-asset-exposure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-cannot-be-seized-the-limits-of-director-asset-exposure\">What Cannot Be Seized: The Limits of Director Asset Exposure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We want to be clear about what is protected as well as what is exposed:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Company debts you did not guarantee</strong> cannot be enforced against your personal assets. A trade creditor owed \u00a350,000 by the company cannot pursue you for that debt unless you signed a guarantee.</li>\n<li><strong>Tools of your trade</strong> and essential household items are protected from seizure by bailiffs under the Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013.</li>\n<li><strong>Pension funds</strong> held in approved pension schemes are generally protected from creditors and bankruptcy trustees.</li>\n<li><strong>Assets owned by your spouse</strong> in their own name are not automatically exposed, unless the asset was transferred from you to avoid creditors (which the trustee can reverse).</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress the pension protection specifically because we see directors who panic and cash in their pension to pay company debts. If the pension is in an approved scheme, it is protected from creditors. Cashing it in to pay debts that limited liability already protects you from is one of the most expensive mistakes a director can make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-directors-can-protect-personal-assets\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-directors-can-protect-personal-assets\">How Directors Can Protect Personal Assets</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Audit your guarantees.</strong> Know what you guaranteed, to whom, for how much, and whether the guarantee is secured against your property.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear your director's loan account.</strong> If it is overdrawn, plan to repay before the company enters insolvency. Repaying during insolvency creates preference risk, so act early.</li>\n<li><strong>Stop trading on time.</strong> A shorter period of wrongful trading means a smaller contribution order.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not cash in protected assets.</strong> Pensions in approved schemes are protected. Do not liquidate them to pay company debts.</li>\n<li><strong>Take personal legal advice.</strong> A solicitor specialising in director liability can assess your specific exposure and advise on protection strategies that are lawful and effective.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt connects directors with licensed insolvency practitioners and personal liability specialists. A confidential conversation will map your exposure and explain your options before the pressure arrives.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-41082-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about your personal asset exposure\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Director Personal Asset Seizure</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-41082\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-41082-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-41082\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-41082\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can bailiffs come to my home for company debts?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-41082\">\n<p>Not for the company's debts directly. Bailiffs can only enforce debts owed by you personally. If you gave a personal guarantee and a CCJ was obtained against you, bailiffs can enforce that personal judgement. If the debt is owed only by the company, bailiffs have no authority to seize your personal property.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-41082-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-41082\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-41082\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is my house safe if my company goes into liquidation?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-41082\">\n<p>Your house is safe unless you gave a personal guarantee secured against it, you face a wrongful trading contribution order you cannot pay from other assets, or you are made personally bankrupt. Check your guarantee position first. If no guarantee is secured against your property, your home is not at risk from the company's insolvency alone.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-41082-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-41082\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-41082\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can the liquidator take my personal savings?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-41082\">\n<p>The liquidator can pursue you for overdrawn director's loan accounts and wrongful trading contributions. If you do not pay voluntarily, they can obtain a court judgement and enforce against your personal assets including savings. They cannot access your personal savings directly without a court order. Pensions in approved schemes are generally protected.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-41082-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-41082\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-41082\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I transfer my house to my spouse to protect it?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-41082\">\n<p>Transferring property to avoid creditors is exactly what the Insolvency Act is designed to reverse. A trustee can claw back transfers at undervalue made within 5 years of bankruptcy. If the transfer was made to defeat creditors, the court can reverse it regardless of when it was made. The attempted concealment will be noted in any disqualification assessment.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-41082\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on the Insolvency Act 1986 (section 214 wrongful trading; Part IX bankruptcy; sections 339 to 342 transactions at undervalue), HMRC personal liability notice provisions, the Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013, and practical experience from personal asset exposure cases handled by licensed practitioners in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; section 214 wrongful trading contribution orders, Part IX personal bankruptcy, sections 339&ndash;342 transactions at undervalue and preferences clawback</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/5/section/121C\"><strong>Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992, s.121C</strong> &mdash; statutory basis for HMRC personal liability notices against directors for unpaid employee NICs</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/1894/contents/made\"><strong>Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013</strong> &mdash; bailiff enforcement procedure and the exempt goods list protecting tools of trade and essential household items</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/47/contents\"><strong>Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, ss.14&ndash;15</strong> &mdash; framework courts use when ordering sale of a jointly-owned family home to satisfy a director's personal debt</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1979/53/contents\"><strong>Charging Orders Act 1979</strong> &mdash; procedure for securing a county court judgement against a director's property after personal guarantee enforcement</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; official guidance on director conduct, wrongful trading investigations, and personal liability following company failure</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; regulator and licensed-IP search for verifying the practitioner instructed on a director-asset risk review</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/what-is-a-high-court-writ/": {
    "id": 39500,
    "slug": "what-is-a-high-court-writ",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A High Court writ, properly, a writ of control, is the enforcement order a creditor gets when they want judgment debt collected fast and with teeth. It is issued under Schedule 12 of the Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007, executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer (HCEO) rather than a County Court bailiff, and it is the fastest form of civil enforcement available in England and Wales.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If one lands on your company's doorstep, you have seven days of meaningful manoeuvring room before the HCEO can re-attend to remove goods. What you do in those seven days matters more than almost anything else in the process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What follows covers who gets writs, how a County Court Judgment becomes a High Court writ, what the HCEO can and cannot take, the fee structure that makes writs painful, and how directors in genuine cash-flow difficulty can stop or manage the process, including through <a href=\"/insolvency/insolvency-act-1986/\">Insolvency Act 1986</a> moratoria where the company is insolvent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-a-high-court-writ-actually-is\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-a-high-court-writ-actually-is\">What a High Court Writ Actually Is</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A writ of control does two things. First, it authorises a named HCEO (or their enforcement agents) to take control of the judgment debtor's goods and sell them to satisfy the debt, costs, interest, and HCEO fees.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Second, it puts a strict statutory procedure around that power, the Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013, that regulates notice, timing, what can be taken, what is exempt, and how the goods are sold. If your goods are taken, knowing the exemptions can make a material difference to what you recover.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The writ is issued by the High Court, not applied for at the County Court counter. For this reason writs are mainly used in two situations:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>High Court judgments</strong> of any value, enforced by writ of control as the default process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>County Court Judgments of \u00a3600 or more</strong>, transferred up to the High Court for enforcement under section 42 of the County Courts Act 1984. This is by far the most common route; commercial creditors routinely transfer CCJs up to escape the slower County Court bailiff process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below \u00a3600, a CCJ can only be enforced by County Court warrant, not writ. Above \u00a35,000, the case must be in the High Court in any event, it cannot stay at County Court level for enforcement. Between \u00a3600 and \u00a35,000, the creditor chooses: stay at County Court with a warrant, or transfer up for the writ.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"transferring-a-ccj-to-the-high-court\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"transferring-a-ccj-to-the-high-court\">Transferring a CCJ to the High Court</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A creditor with a CCJ of \u00a3600 or more files a Notice of Issue (Form N293A) requesting transfer of the judgment to the High Court for enforcement. The court fee is \u00a378. Once transferred, the writ is issued, usually within 48 hours, and sent to an HCEO firm chosen by the creditor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Why creditors transfer up to the High Court rather than staying with the County Court warrant:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Speed</strong>. HCEO firms are commercial operations with incentive to execute fast. A County Court warrant can queue for months; an HCEO will be at the door within two weeks.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Pressure</strong>. HCEO attendance concentrates directors' attention like almost nothing else. Many debts that sat unpaid on a CCJ for a year are settled within seven days of an HCEO Notice of Enforcement arriving.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Higher fees the debtor pays</strong>. The statutory fee scale on a writ of control is materially higher than a County Court warrant, which both increases the pressure and, where the creditor recovers fully, transfers more cost to the debtor.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Uplift in interest</strong>. A judgment transferred to the High Court carries interest at 8% under section 17 of the Judgments Act 1838, which can be material on large balances over time.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One narrow exception: consumer credit agreements regulated by the Consumer Credit Act 1974 cannot be enforced by writ of control, regardless of value. These must stay in the County Court. In our experience, some creditors do attempt to transfer regulated debts up; if you believe your debt is regulated, challenge the transfer before engaging with the HCEO on the merits.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-enforcement-stages-and-hceo-fees-on-a-high-court-writ\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-enforcement-stages-and-hceo-fees-on-a-high-court-writ\">The Enforcement Stages and HCEO Fees on a High Court Writ</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Taking Control of Goods (Fees) Regulations 2014 set a three-stage fee scale, each stage triggered by a specific step in the enforcement process:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Compliance stage, \u00a375 + 7.5% on the debt above \u00a31,000</strong>. Triggered by the Notice of Enforcement being issued. The debtor has seven clear days to pay in full or enter a payment arrangement. Almost all debts that are paid fast settle at this stage, the fee is fixed and low, and no HCEO has yet attended.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Enforcement stage, \u00a3190 + 7.5% on the debt above \u00a31,000</strong>. Triggered by the first HCEO attendance after the seven-day compliance window expires. Goods can now be taken into control, usually via a Controlled Goods Agreement allowing them to remain on site pending payment. See our guide to the <a href=\"/hmrc/controlled-goods-agreement/\">Controlled Goods Agreement</a>.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Sale stage, \u00a3495 + 7.5% on the debt above \u00a31,000</strong>. Triggered by HCEO attending to remove goods for sale. This is the expensive end.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On a \u00a320,000 judgment, full progression to sale stage adds approximately \u00a32,185 in HCEO fees on top of the debt, interest, and court costs. Settlement at compliance stage is \u00a375 + \u00a31,425 = \u00a31,500. The seven-day window matters financially as well as operationally.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Part-payment or an instalment agreement at compliance stage stops the fee clock. Part-payment after enforcement begins does not roll back the enforcement fee; the higher-stage fee is payable on top of any settlement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"hceo-powers-of-entry-seizure-and-exempt-goods\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"hceo-powers-of-entry-seizure-and-exempt-goods\">HCEO Powers of Entry, Seizure, and Exempt Goods</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The HCEO's powers under the 2007 Act and the 2013 Regulations are broad but not unlimited. The practical ones:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Entry to business premises</strong>. HCEOs may enter any business premises where goods of the debtor are believed to be. They can force entry with an executory warrant if necessary. Though forcible entry on first attendance is rare in practice.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Entry to domestic premises</strong>. Only by peaceful means (unlocked door, open window, invitation). Forced entry to a dwelling requires a separate application to the court. For a limited company, this matters where the registered office is the director's home and the HCEO attends expecting to find company goods.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Hours</strong>. 6am to 9pm, any day of the week, including Sunday if the HCEO considers it reasonable and the debt has not been paid in time.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Who can be present</strong>. A constable can accompany where there is risk of breach of the peace; a locksmith if forced entry is authorised; an independent valuer for high-value goods.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Exempt goods, the HCEO cannot take:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Tools of trade up to a total value of \u00a31,350 (an aggregate, not per item).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Items belonging to a third party, stock on consignment, leased equipment, a partner's personal vehicle.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Goods subject to a retention-of-title clause (unpaid supplier stock), or under hire-purchase or finance lease in the finance company's ownership.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Items clearly not the debtor's, identifiable personal items, customer deposits, client-account property for regulated firms.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Vulnerable-person property in domestic settings, specific Schedule 12 paragraph 10 protections.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Third-party claims are a live battleground in commercial enforcement. If the HCEO takes goods you later prove belonged to a factor, a supplier on ROT, or a finance company, the remedy is interpleader or an application to the court.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Evidence, the ROT clause, the lease schedule, the delivery note, matters. Keep it accessible. Our advisers can help you identify which goods are correctly exempt before the HCEO attends.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"stopping-or-challenging-a-high-court-writ\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"stopping-or-challenging-a-high-court-writ\">Stopping or Challenging a High Court Writ</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three main routes depending on what you are challenging:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Stay of execution</strong>. Application under CPR 83 to suspend the writ. Grounds: proposed payment by instalments the court considers reasonable; material change in circumstance; pending set-aside of the underlying judgment. The court fee is \u00a3313 for an application on notice (or \u00a3123 by consent or without notice) per the current EX50 schedule. A stay does not extinguish the debt; it pauses enforcement while a plan is put in place.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Set aside the underlying judgment</strong>. If the CCJ was issued in default (no acknowledgement of service), an application under CPR 13 can set it aside where there is a real prospect of defence and a good reason for not responding. The court fee is \u00a3313 for an on-notice application per the current EX50 schedule. Success erases the judgment and, with it, the writ.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Insolvency moratoria</strong>. An administration appointment, pre-pack or otherwise, creates an immediate statutory moratorium under Schedule B1 paragraph 43 that stops the HCEO from continuing. A CVA with moratorium, or the Part A1 standalone moratorium introduced in 2020, have similar effect. A <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">creditors' voluntary liquidation</a> (CVL) also freezes enforcement once commenced.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The insolvency route is the one directors often under-use. If the writ reflects an underlying cash-flow insolvency, the business cannot pay its debts as they fall due, and the creditor situation is broader than this one writ, the moratorium in administration or CVL is a structural solution rather than a procedural delay.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It also puts the decision on the right basis: your duties at this point have shifted toward creditor interests under <em>BTI v Sequana</em>, and engaging a licensed IP is the right response. Our team handles exactly this scenario regularly, and the earlier you call us, the more options remain open.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"high-court-writs-and-insolvency-proceedings\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"high-court-writs-and-insolvency-proceedings\">High Court Writs and Insolvency Proceedings</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The interaction between an active writ and insolvency proceedings is governed by specific Schedule 12 and Insolvency Act provisions:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Administration moratorium</strong>. Schedule B1 paragraph 43 of the Insolvency Act 1986 prevents \"steps to enforce security\" and legal process without administrator consent or court permission. An HCEO on site stops; goods already taken into control become complicated.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Liquidation</strong>. Section 126 of the Insolvency Act prevents any action or proceeding without leave of the court once winding-up proceedings have begun; section 128 makes any sale or execution commenced against the company's goods after the commencement of winding-up void, unless the court orders otherwise. For enforcement that has progressed to sale, the liquidator may recover proceeds.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">Winding-up petition</a></strong>. Once a petition is advertised, section 127 makes any disposition of company property void unless validated. HCEO continuing to enforce after advertisement risks the sale being unwound.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>CVA and Part A1 moratorium</strong>. Both create statutory protection against enforcement, subject to specific carve-outs for creditors with existing secured positions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Timing matters. A director who instructs a CVL or administration on the morning the HCEO is due to attend materially changes the outcome. Waiting until goods have been removed and sold cuts the options down sharply. Our licensed IPs can be instructed urgently; if the HCEO is due tomorrow, call us today.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-a-high-court-writ\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-a-high-court-writ\">Your Next Step on a High Court Writ</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a Notice of Enforcement has arrived and you can pay in full, pay it; you will save the enforcement-stage and sale-stage fees. If you can negotiate an instalment plan with the creditor or the HCEO firm within the seven-day compliance window, do that.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you cannot pay and cannot negotiate, the question is whether the company is insolvent on the section 123 cash-flow test, in which case the moratorium route under the Insolvency Act becomes the right response. That is the call to make today, not after the HCEO has attended.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call us free on 0800 074 6757. We will run through the writ, the underlying debt, your wider creditor position, and whether administration, CVL, CVA, or the Part A1 moratorium is the proportionate response for your company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A single writ you can pay is not an insolvency event; one writ on top of an <a href=\"/hmrc/hmrc-enforcement-action/\">HMRC time-to-pay breach</a>, overdue rent, and a factoring shortfall usually is. The first conversation is diagnostic and free.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"high-court-writ-faqs\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"high-court-writ-faqs\">High Court Writ FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"39500-hcw-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow long do I have once a High Court writ is issued?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Seven clear days from the date of the Notice of Enforcement before the HCEO can attend to take control of goods. In that window the fee charged is the compliance stage only (\u00a375 + 7.5% on the debt above \u00a31,000). Payment, or a binding instalment plan, closes the matter at compliance stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan an HCEO force entry to my business?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>To business premises, yes, HCEOs can force entry with an executory warrant if necessary, though in practice forced entry on first attendance is rare. To domestic premises, no, entry must be peaceful unless a court has separately authorised forced entry. A registered office at a home address creates a grey zone where HCEOs sometimes test the limits; check the authorisation before admitting anyone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat goods can an HCEO not take?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Tools of trade up to \u00a31,350 in aggregate value; goods belonging to a third party (stock on consignment, leased equipment, items under retention of title); goods on hire-purchase or finance lease in the finance company's ownership; identifiable personal items in a domestic setting; and vulnerable-person property within specific Schedule 12 protections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Third-party claims are a common and winnable battleground where the evidence is available. If you have leased equipment or ROT stock on site, preserve the relevant documents before the HCEO attends.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill administration stop an HCEO?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, the Schedule B1 moratorium prevents continuation of enforcement without administrator consent or court permission. An HCEO on site when administration is filed must cease; goods taken into control prior to the filing are subject to the administrator's directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Timing matters: filing administration on the day the HCEO is due to attend is the common pattern where the underlying position is irretrievable. If you are considering this route, call us before the HCEO arrives, not after.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I pay in instalments to stop a writ?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, if the creditor or HCEO firm accepts. Most HCEO firms will agree an instalment plan at compliance stage where the proposal is realistic, typically three to six months for a substantial debt, longer only exceptionally.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A formal stay of execution under CPR 83 is an alternative route where the creditor will not agree; the court fee is \u00a3313 for an on-notice application (or \u00a3123 by consent/without notice) and grounds include instalments the court considers reasonable. If you need help negotiating the instalment terms, our advisers can assist alongside your solicitor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow much does a writ add to the debt?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>At compliance stage only: \u00a375 plus 7.5% on the debt above \u00a31,000. At enforcement stage: a further \u00a3190 plus 7.5%. At sale stage: a further \u00a3495 plus 7.5%. On a \u00a320,000 judgment, full progression adds approximately \u00a32,185 in HCEO fees, plus accrued 8% judgment-rate interest and the \u00a378 transfer-up fee. Settling at compliance is materially cheaper than letting the HCEO attend.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"methodology-amp-disclosure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"methodology-amp-disclosure\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners. Statutory references are to the Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 (Schedule 12), the Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013, the Taking Control of Goods (Fees) Regulations 2014, and section 42 of the County Courts Act 1984 (transfer-up).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 17 of the Judgments Act 1838 (High Court interest rate) and the Insolvency Act 1986 (moratoria and post-petition void dispositions) also apply. Fee figures reflect the current statutory scale.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed IPs advise on the insolvency routes that interrupt a writ, administration, CVL, CVA, and the Part A1 moratorium, and coordinate with solicitors on stay-of-execution and set-aside applications where those are the right response. Our 0800 number is a free confidential consultation; nothing is charged until we agree a scope of work with you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2007/15/schedule/12\"><strong>Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007, Schedule 12</strong> &mdash; the statutory basis for writs of control, the seven-day notice, and HCEO powers to take control of goods</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/1894/contents\"><strong>Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013</strong> &mdash; procedural rules governing HCEO entry, exempt goods, controlled goods agreements, and removal timing</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2014/1/contents\"><strong>Taking Control of Goods (Fees) Regulations 2014</strong> &mdash; the fixed three-stage fee structure (compliance, enforcement, sale) that makes writs disproportionately expensive on smaller debts</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1984/28/section/42\"><strong>County Courts Act 1984, section 42</strong> &mdash; mechanism allowing CCJs of \u00a3600 or more to be transferred up to the High Court for enforcement by writ</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/schedule/B1\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, Schedule B1</strong> &mdash; the administration moratorium that halts HCEO enforcement once a notice of intention to appoint is filed</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/advice/losing-house-if-company-goes-bust/": {
    "id": 38597,
    "slug": "losing-house-if-company-goes-bust",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your limited company goes bust, your house is protected by limited liability, unless you gave a personal guarantee secured against it. That single exception is what turns a company problem into a personal one, and it is the exception that applies to far more directors than realise it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We speak to directors every week who are terrified of losing their home. In most cases, the fear is unfounded because limited liability does exactly what it says: the company's debts are the company's debts, not yours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But in roughly one in three cases we handle, the director signed a personal guarantee when they took out a bank loan, a commercial lease, or a credit facility. That guarantee is the bridge between the company's insolvency and your front door. We explain below exactly when your house is at risk and when it is not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-when-is-your-house-at-risk-from-a-limited-company\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-when-is-your-house-at-risk-from-a-limited-company\">Quick Answer: When Is Your House at Risk From a Limited Company?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your house is at risk only if one of these specific conditions applies:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>You gave a personal guarantee secured against your property.</strong> The lender registered a charge at the Land Registry. They can apply for a court order to force a sale. This is the most direct and most common route.</li>\n<li><strong>You gave an unsecured personal guarantee and cannot pay.</strong> The creditor obtains a CCJ, then a charging order against your property, then applies for an order for sale. Slower but reaches the same outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>A wrongful trading contribution order is made against you and you cannot pay.</strong> The liquidator enforces the order against your personal assets, including your share of any property. See wrongful trading exposure.</li>\n<li><strong>You are made personally bankrupt.</strong> Your interest in your home vests in the trustee in bankruptcy, who can apply for a sale after 12 months. See director bankruptcy.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If none of these apply, your house is not at risk from the company's insolvency. Limited liability protects your personal assets from the company's debts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"personal-guarantees-secured-against-your-house-the-main-risk\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"personal-guarantees-secured-against-your-house-the-main-risk\">Personal Guarantees Secured Against Your House: The Main Risk</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the scenario we see most often. You took out a business loan. The bank required a personal guarantee. The guarantee was secured by a second charge on your home, registered at the Land Registry. You signed because you needed the funding and assumed the company would repay. The company enters liquidation. The bank calls in the guarantee.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have sat with directors who did not remember the charge existed until the bank's solicitor wrote to them. The charge was buried in the facility agreement, signed years ago. The director assumed the guarantee was unsecured. It was not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a charge is registered against your property, the creditor has a direct route to enforcement: they can apply to the court for an order for sale under section 14 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise every director approaching insolvency to check the Land Registry for charges on their property. You can do this online at the Land Registry website for \u00a33. If a charge exists that you did not expect, you need personal legal advice immediately.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-your-house-is-not-at-risk-from-a-company-failure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-your-house-is-not-at-risk-from-a-company-failure\">When Your House Is NOT at Risk From a Company Failure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress the positive because the fear is often worse than the reality:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>No personal guarantee = no risk from that creditor.</strong> A trade supplier owed \u00a350,000 by the company cannot touch your home unless you guaranteed the debt.</li>\n<li><strong>HMRC cannot seize your home for company tax debts</strong> unless they issue a personal liability notice for PAYE/NICs and you cannot pay, leading to enforcement against personal assets. Corporation Tax and VAT debts are the company's, not yours.</li>\n<li><strong>Director disqualification does not affect your home.</strong> It bans you from being a director. It does not create a financial claim against your assets.</li>\n<li><strong>The company entering liquidation does not create a claim against your home.</strong> Liquidation is the company's process. Your home is only affected if a personal liability route (guarantee, wrongful trading, bankruptcy) applies.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-directors-can-protect-the-house-after-a-bust\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-directors-can-protect-the-house-after-a-bust\">How Directors Can Protect the House After a Bust</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a charge already exists against your property, your options are limited but real:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Negotiate the guarantee down.</strong> Creditors often accept reduced settlements. We have seen bank guarantees settled at 30 to 50% of face value. Engage early, before enforcement starts.</li>\n<li><strong>Challenge the guarantee.</strong> If there were procedural defects, undue influence, or material variation to the underlying facility without your consent, the guarantee may be unenforceable.</li>\n<li><strong>Consider an IVA.</strong> An Individual Voluntary Arrangement lets you repay a proportion of your personal debts over 5 to 6 years, potentially avoiding bankruptcy and forced sale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are direct about what you cannot do: transferring your property to your spouse to protect it from creditors is a transaction at undervalue that a trustee in bankruptcy can reverse within 5 years (or longer if the intent was to defeat creditors). We see directors try this. It does not work, and the attempted concealment makes your position worse. See hiding assets before insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"steps-directors-should-take-right-now-to-protect-a-house\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"steps-directors-should-take-right-now-to-protect-a-house\">Steps Directors Should Take Right Now to Protect a House</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Check the Land Registry.</strong> Search your property for registered charges. \u00a33 online.</li>\n<li><strong>Audit your guarantee position.</strong> Pull out every facility agreement and check the guarantee clauses. Our personal guarantees guide explains what to look for.</li>\n<li><strong>If a charge exists, take personal legal advice immediately.</strong> Not insolvency advice. Personal property advice from a solicitor who understands enforcement.</li>\n<li><strong>If no charge exists, recognise that your house is likely safe.</strong> Limited liability is doing its job. Focus on managing the company's insolvency properly.</li>\n<li><strong>Speak to a licensed insolvency practitioner</strong> about both the company's position and your personal exposure. Company Debt connects directors with regulated IPs. A free, confidential consultation will clarify where you stand.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-38597-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about your guarantee exposure and the family home\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Losing Your House When a Limited Company Goes Bust</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-38597\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-38597-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-38597\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-38597\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Will I automatically lose my house if my company is liquidated?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-38597\">\n<p>No. Limited liability means the company's debts are not your debts. Your house is only at risk if you gave a personal guarantee secured against it, face a wrongful trading order you cannot pay, or are made personally bankrupt. Most directors do not lose their home after company liquidation.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-38597-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-38597\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-38597\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I transfer my house to protect it from creditors?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-38597\">\n<p>No. Transferring property to defeat creditors is a transaction at undervalue that can be reversed by a trustee in bankruptcy (within 5 years, or longer if intent to defeat creditors is shown). The attempted transfer makes your position worse, not better.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-38597-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-38597\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-38597\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How do I check if there is a charge on my property?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-38597\">\n<p>Search the Land Registry online at HM Land Registry. A title register search costs \u00a33 and shows all registered charges against your property, including second charges from personal guarantees. If a charge exists that you did not expect, take legal advice immediately.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-38597-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-38597\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-38597\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can a creditor force the sale of my home if my spouse is not a guarantor?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-38597\">\n<p>The creditor can only enforce against your beneficial share of the property. Where a non-guarantor spouse co-owns, the court weighs their interest and the welfare of any minor children under section 15 of the Trusts of Land Act before granting an order for sale. The outcome turns on the specific facts. If your spouse is asking whether they are personally liable for your business debts more broadly, see our guide on <a href=\"/insolvency/personal-liability-spouses-business-debts/\">personal liability for a spouse's business debts</a>.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-38597\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on the Insolvency Act 1986 (section 214 wrongful trading; Part IX bankruptcy; sections 339 to 342 transactions at undervalue), the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 (section 14, orders for sale), the Land Registration Act 2002, and practical experience from personal guarantee and property cases handled by licensed insolvency practitioners in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. This does not influence our editorial content. References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; section 214 wrongful trading contribution orders, Part IX personal bankruptcy, and sections 339&ndash;342 on transactions at undervalue that can claw back home transfers</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/47/contents\"><strong>Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996</strong> &mdash; sections 14 and 15, the statutory route a secured creditor uses to apply for an order for sale of a jointly owned home</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2002/9/contents\"><strong>Land Registration Act 2002</strong> &mdash; governs registration of legal and equitable charges against your property, including the second charges banks take under personal guarantees</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/search-property-information-land-registry\"><strong>HM Land Registry: search property information</strong> &mdash; how to check the register for &pound;3 to confirm whether a lender has registered a charge against your home before insolvency escalates</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1979/53/contents\"><strong>Charging Orders Act 1979</strong> &mdash; the mechanism by which an unsecured creditor converts a county court judgment into a charge over your home, the slower route to enforcement</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; official guidance on personal bankruptcy, the trustee's powers over the bankrupt's home, and the twelve-month window before a sale application</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006</strong> &mdash; codifies the limited liability principle and statutory director duties under sections 170&ndash;177 that wrongful trading claims build on</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-a-commercial-lease-or-rent/": {
    "id": 31237,
    "slug": "cant-pay-a-commercial-lease-or-rent",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- TITLE: Can\u2019t Pay Commercial Rent or Lease? Legal Risks &amp; Solutions for UK Businesses -->\n<!-- POST ID: 31237 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70225 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-a-commercial-lease-or-rent/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Commercial rent is the creditor with the fastest self-help remedy in UK insolvency. A landlord with a well-drafted lease and an empty premises can change the locks peaceably, no court order, no notice period, the afternoon after the rent falls due. That is not a theoretical risk; forfeiture by peaceable re-entry is exercised routinely by commercial landlords on persistent arrears.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This page sets out the specific enforcement routes available to a UK commercial landlord, forfeiture, Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR), statutory demands, winding-up petitions, with the exact thresholds and fees, and the practical options available to you before your lease position is beyond repair.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Missed Commercial Rent Escalates in a UK Lease</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A missed rent payment is a breach of lease, triggering immediate contractual and legal consequences. The cascade:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Contractual late fees</strong>, most commercial leases carry default interest at 4\u20138% over base rate, calculated daily from due date.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Breach of covenant</strong> recorded in the landlord's file, relevant on any subsequent assignment, renewal, or reference request.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Credit-profile damage</strong>, commercial credit agencies reflect rent arrears within weeks of notification by the landlord or managing agent.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Formal notice</strong>, usually a demand for payment with a specific deadline, referencing the lease's default provisions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The speed at which this escalates separates commercial tenancy from almost every other UK debt position. Where residential tenants have extensive statutory protections, commercial tenants have comparatively few. The lease governs, and the lease almost always favours the landlord.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When Commercial Rent Arrears Signal Company Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Persistent commercial rent non-payment is a direct failure of the cash-flow test under section 123 of the <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/insolvency-act-1986/\">Insolvency Act 1986</a>. Unlike supplier debt, rent is a near-impossible creditor to stretch, the landlord has direct remedies against the premises the business operates from.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The red flags that usually accompany commercial rent arrears:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Persistent cash shortages at month-end and quarter-end.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Other essential creditors (rates, utilities, payroll) being rolled.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Director's loan account moving one direction, capital in from the director to keep payments current.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Staff numbers creeping up while revenue flattens or declines.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Recognising the pattern while rent is the only essential creditor in arrears is the moment for the rescue options discussed below. Once rates, HMRC, and suppliers are all on the queue, your position has compounded.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The CVAs and administrations we run that produce a continuing business are almost always instructed while the managing agent is still sending demand letters. Cases instructed the afternoon after the locks have been changed tend to be orderly exits, not rescues.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What a Commercial Landlord Can Do When the Rent Is Not Paid</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The specific enforcement tools available to a UK commercial landlord, in rough order of speed:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Forfeiture by peaceable re-entry</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fastest and most disruptive remedy. Under common law, a landlord can re-enter and change the locks where the lease permits forfeiture for non-payment and the premises are unoccupied, typically overnight, at weekends, or during holiday periods.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No court order is required for rent arrears (a Section 146 notice under the Law of Property Act 1925 is only required for non-rent breaches). If your premises are ever left unattended with arrears outstanding, your landlord can act that day.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once forfeited, the tenant's only route back is <strong>relief from forfeiture</strong>, an application to the court, typically requiring the arrears plus costs to be paid. The tenant must act within six months under section 38 of the Senior Courts Act 1981.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under the Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007, a landlord with <strong>at least 7 days' rent arrears</strong> (principal rent only, not service charges or insurance premiums) can instruct a certified enforcement agent. A Notice of Enforcement is served, giving 7 clear days before goods can be taken into control. CRAR applies to the premises only, not wider business assets.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>CRAR fees follow the same scale as court enforcement: \u00a375 compliance fee on instruction, \u00a3235 plus 7.5% of debt over \u00a31,500 on first enforcement visit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Statutory Demand and Winding-Up Petition</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For corporate tenants with rent debt over \u00a3750, a <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/company-cash-flow-problems/what-is-a-statutory-demand-against-a-company/\">statutory demand</a> gives 21 days to pay. Unpaid demands support a <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>. Court fee for a winding-up petition is \u00a3352, with a deposit of \u00a32,600 for the Official Receiver (current figures). Advertisement in The Gazette freezes company bank accounts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">County Court Claim</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For the arrears balance itself, a straightforward debt claim through the County Court produces judgment (CCJ) and access to the full enforcement toolkit, warrants of control, charging orders, third-party debt orders. Slower than forfeiture or CRAR, but the judgment survives subsequent assignment or change of tenant structure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Director Personal Liability on Commercial Rent Arrears</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A commercial lease signed by a limited company is, by default, a company debt protected by <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/what-is-limited-liability/\">limited liability</a>. Personal exposure routes:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/the-risks-of-signing-a-personal-guarantee/\">Personal guarantees</a> on the lease</strong>, very common on smaller tenants. Check the lease carefully; landlord standard leases often include a guarantee covering the full remaining lease term plus dilapidations.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Authorised Guarantee Agreements (AGAs)</strong> from previous tenants on assignment, a prior-tenant director can remain liable even after assigning the lease, under the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading</strong> under section 214, continuing to operate from the premises while knowing the rent cannot be paid and insolvency is unavoidable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Misfeasance or preference</strong> findings in subsequent insolvency, particularly where other creditors (often connected parties) were paid while rent went into arrears.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Personal guarantees on commercial leases are the personal-liability route most commonly missed. If you think the limited company shields you from the lease, check the document, not your memory.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A PG signed four years earlier on page eleven of a schedule can expose your personal assets, including the family home, long after default. We have sat in too many kitchens with directors reading their own signature back to them a fortnight after the landlord's solicitor served a demand for the residue of the term.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Negotiating with Your Commercial Landlord</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Landlords prefer a continuing tenant on modified terms to an empty unit on the market at a lower rent. The negotiation posture most likely to succeed:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Lead with a specific proposal.</strong> \"Temporary rent reduction to 70% for six months, with full rent resumed from month seven, and any shortfall spread over months seven to eighteen\" is the shape of a proposal landlords can accept. Vague \"can we discuss payment options\" is not.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Present financial evidence.</strong> Management accounts, three-month forecast, trade references. A landlord considering a concession needs material to justify it to their own finance director or investors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Offer an immediate partial payment.</strong> A credibility signal that the proposal is being made in good faith, not as a delay tactic.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Consider a mediator.</strong> For larger arrears or contested positions, a neutral third party often unsticks conversations that direct negotiation has stalled.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The landlord's own calculation is straightforward: cost of void (lost rent, re-letting fees, rates liability while empty) against the cost of accepting modified terms from you. Where your covenant is reasonable and the premises are suitable, you almost always win that comparison. Your job is to present the evidence that makes that case clear.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Formal Restructuring Options for Commercial Lease Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When informal negotiation is insufficient, three formal routes address the underlying insolvency position:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">CVA</a> is a legally binding agreement between the company and unsecured creditors to repay a percentage of historic debt over 3\u20135 years. Landlord rent arrears are usually included. Requires 75% approval by value of unsecured creditors voting. Well-suited where the underlying trading business is viable but carries unmanageable historic rent debt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>CVAs have been used prominently by UK retail chains (New Look, Arcadia, Pizza Express) specifically to compromise landlord rent debt. The mechanism works the same way for smaller tenants on individual leases.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Administration</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/what-is-a-pre-pack-administration/\">Administration</a> produces a statutory moratorium on landlord action, the single most useful tool when forfeiture or CRAR is imminent. The administrator pursues rescue as a going concern, sale as a going concern, or realisation for creditors. Where the lease is valuable (good location, below-market rent), an administrator can assign it to a buyer of the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation (CVL)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a> closes the position cleanly when rescue is not viable. The liquidator disclaims onerous leases under section 178 of the Insolvency Act 1986, ending the tenancy and cutting off future rent liability for the (now-defunct) company. The landlord's remaining claim becomes a standard unsecured creditor claim in the liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step When You Cannot Pay Commercial Rent</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The two questions that drive everything else: does your lease include a personal guarantee (check the document, don't rely on memory), and is the underlying trading business viable if the rent arrears are addressed? The answers determine whether the negotiation, CVA, administration, or CVL route fits.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An hour with a licensed <a href=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/insolvency/what-is-an-insolvency-practitioner/\">insolvency practitioner</a> and a property solicitor, together, usually produces a clear action plan. Our licensed IPs and business rescue specialists can explain the options, outline the risks, and guide you through the next steps. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Commercial Rent and Lease Arrears</h2>\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-31237\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31237-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-31237\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31237\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>If I cannot pay rent, can the landlord come after my personal assets?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-31237\">\n<p>Only where you have given a personal guarantee on the lease, signed an AGA on a prior assignment that is still live, or are pursued through post-insolvency personal-liability routes (wrongful trading, misfeasance). A limited-company lease without a PG protects personal assets from direct landlord action.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31237-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-31237\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31237\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What if I gave a personal guarantee on the commercial lease?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-31237\">\n<p>The landlord can pursue you personally for the guaranteed amount, usually the rent arrears plus dilapidations at lease end, plus future rent for the remaining term if the guarantee covers it. Enforcement follows standard personal-debt routes: judgment, charging order over property, bankruptcy petition where the threshold is met. Check the PG wording carefully; scope and cap vary.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31237-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-31237\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31237\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Will my business credit rating be affected by rent arrears?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-31237\">\n<p>Yes. Commercial credit agencies are notified by landlords or managing agents within weeks of arrears becoming material. A CCJ or formal enforcement step adds further damage. Re-letting or refinancing becomes materially harder while the profile shows active arrears.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31237-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-31237\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31237\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I arrange a payment plan without formal insolvency?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-31237\">\n<p>Usually yes, landlords generally prefer a continuing tenant on modified terms to an empty unit. A credible, specific written proposal with financial evidence and an immediate partial payment is the right shape. Informal arrangements stick best when recorded in a side letter or lease variation.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31237-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-31237\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31237\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How fast can a landlord start forfeiture proceedings?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-31237\">\n<p>Forfeiture for non-payment of rent can be immediate through peaceable re-entry where the lease permits and the premises are unoccupied, no notice required. Most standard commercial leases contain a forfeiture clause triggered by a specific number of days' arrears (typically 14 or 21).</p>\n\n\n<p>Where peaceable re-entry is not possible (occupied residential-attached premises, certain protected categories), the landlord applies for a possession order instead. If your lease has a forfeiture clause and your premises are ever unattended with arrears outstanding, this can happen the same day.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31237-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-31237\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31237\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Are there government relief programmes for commercial rent arrears?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-31237\">\n<p>The COVID-era moratorium on commercial landlord enforcement ended in 2022, and the Commercial Rent (Coronavirus) Act 2022 binding-arbitration scheme for ring-fenced COVID-period arrears has now closed.</p>\n\n\n<p>No ongoing general government scheme addresses current commercial rent arrears. Sector-specific support (energy, retail-and-leisure rates relief) can indirectly reduce pressure on your business but does not address rent directly.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>We wrote this guide on our editorial desk and our licensed insolvency practitioners reviewed it. It reflects UK property, landlord-tenant, and insolvency law as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<p>Our statutory references are drawn from the Law of Property Act 1925, the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995, the Tribunals Courts and Enforcement Act 2007, and the Insolvency Act 1986.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where we recommend a CVA, Administration, or CVL, we can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner under separate engagement. Our 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo5/15-16/20/section/146\">Law of Property Act 1925, section 146 (non-rent forfeiture notice)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1995/30/contents\">Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 (AGA provisions)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2007/15/contents\">Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 (CRAR, Schedule 12)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2014/1/contents/made\">Taking Control of Goods (Fees) Regulations 2014</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">Insolvency Act 1986, sections 123, 178 (disclaimer of onerous leases), 214</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2022/12/contents/enacted\">Commercial Rent (Coronavirus) Act 2022</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/courts-tribunals/insolvency-and-companies-court\">HMCTS - winding-up petitions and statutory demands</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-31237\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->"
  },
  "/advice/business-restructuring/": {
    "id": 31199,
    "slug": "business-restructuring",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Business restructuring means changing the way your company operates, owes, or is financed to avoid insolvency. It is not a single process. It is a category of interventions that range from an informal conversation with your bank to a court-supervised administration, and the right one depends on how much time you have and how severe the problem is.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We work with directors at every stage of this spectrum. Some call when the problem is three months old and fixable with a phone call to HMRC. Others call when a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> has been filed and the only option left is emergency administration.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The difference in outcome between these two groups is stark. Early restructuring preserves options, value, and control. Late restructuring preserves whatever is left after creditors have taken their share. We cannot make this point strongly enough: the earlier you restructure, the more of the business you keep.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-uk-business-restructuring-options\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-uk-business-restructuring-options\">Quick Answer: UK Business Restructuring Options</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK business restructuring options fall into three tiers, from lightest to most formal:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Tier 1, Informal:</strong> Direct creditor negotiation, HMRC Time to Pay, invoice finance, refinancing, cost reduction, and operational changes. No statutory framework, no public record, maximum director control.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Tier 2, Formal rescue:</strong> Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA), the standalone moratorium (Part A1 of the Insolvency Act), and restructuring plans under Part 26A of the Companies Act 2006. Statutory framework, binding on creditors, moderate cost.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Tier 3, Court-supervised:</strong> Administration. Full court protection, automatic moratorium, but the administrator runs the company and the cost is highest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If none of these can fix the underlying problem, the company is not restructurable. It needs to be closed through a <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a> or will face compulsory liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are honest about this because we see directors pursue restructuring options that delay the inevitable, accumulate more debt, and increase their personal wrongful trading exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"tier-1-informal-business-restructuring\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"tier-1-informal-business-restructuring\">Tier 1: Informal Business Restructuring</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Informal restructuring is the first step and the cheapest. It involves direct engagement with creditors to renegotiate terms, extend payment deadlines, or reduce the total debt burden through negotiation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>HMRC Time to Pay.</strong> The most common informal restructuring tool. HMRC's Business Payment Support Service agrees instalment plans for tax debts, typically over 6 to 12 months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that directors who contact HMRC proactively, before enforcement starts, have a significantly higher acceptance rate. A phone call this week is worth more than a solicitor's letter next month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Creditor negotiation.</strong> Direct conversations with suppliers, landlords, and lenders to restructure payment terms. This works when creditors trust you and the business is demonstrably viable. It fails when creditors have lost confidence or when one large creditor refuses to cooperate, because informal agreements are not binding.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Refinancing and asset-based lending.</strong> If the problem is cash flow rather than profitability, injecting new capital through invoice finance, asset-based lending, or commercial refinancing can bridge the gap.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see this work when the company has a strong order book and collectible debts. It does not work when the new borrowing simply displaces the problem by adding another creditor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Operational restructuring.</strong> Cost reduction, headcount adjustment, renegotiation of contracts, closure of loss-making divisions. These are management decisions, not insolvency processes, but they are often the difference between a business that needs formal restructuring and one that can trade through.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"tier-2-formal-business-rescue-and-restructuring\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"tier-2-formal-business-rescue-and-restructuring\">Tier 2: Formal Business Rescue and Restructuring</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA).</strong> A binding agreement between the company and its creditors to repay a proportion of the debt over 3 to 5 years. Requires 75% creditor approval by value. The company continues to trade under your control, supervised by a licensed IP.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find CVAs work well for businesses with a viable core and a specific, identifiable debt problem. See our guide to alternatives to liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Standalone moratorium (Part A1).</strong> Introduced by the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020, this provides eligible companies with a 20-business-day breathing space (extendable) during which creditor enforcement is suspended.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company must appoint a monitor (a licensed IP) who assesses whether rescue is likely. The moratorium does not restructure the debt. It buys time to prepare a CVA, restructuring plan, or other rescue mechanism.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Restructuring plan (Part 26A).</strong> A court-sanctioned restructuring mechanism that can bind dissenting creditor classes through cross-class cram-down. This is the most powerful restructuring tool available, but it is expensive, complex, and typically used for larger companies with sophisticated creditor structures.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see restructuring plans in cases where a CVA would fail because a single large creditor class would block it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--rescue\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Business Rescue Options</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Explore Rescue Options Before Closure</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">A CVA or restructure can keep a viable company trading while dealing with creditor pressure. Speak to a Licensed IP about the routes open to you.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/company-rescue-recovery-hub/\">Explore Rescue Options &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"tier-3-administration-as-business-restructuring\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"tier-3-administration-as-business-restructuring\">Tier 3: Administration as Business Restructuring</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Administration is the last resort for rescue. It provides the strongest protection, an automatic moratorium on all creditor enforcement, but it costs the most (from \u00a315,000) and removes director control entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The administrator runs the company and decides whether to rescue it, sell it, or transition to liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise administration only when Tier 1 and Tier 2 options have been exhausted or are not viable, when a winding-up petition is imminent and needs to be stopped, or when the business has significant going-concern value that would be destroyed by rushed liquidation. Our administration guide covers the process in full.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-to-choose-the-right-business-restructuring-option\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-to-choose-the-right-business-restructuring-option\">How to Choose the Right Business Restructuring Option</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Cash-flow problem with viable business:</strong> Start with Tier 1. HMRC TTP, creditor negotiation, refinancing.</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple creditors, need binding agreement:</strong> Tier 2. CVA or restructuring plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Creditor enforcement imminent or in progress:</strong> Tier 3. Administration or standalone moratorium.</li>\n<li><strong>Business model broken, debts structural:</strong> None. Close the company rather than restructuring debts around a failing operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We tell directors: restructuring is not rescue. Rescue is the outcome. Restructuring is the mechanism. If the mechanism does not fix the underlying problem, it delays failure at the cost of additional debt and increased personal exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The test is always the same: can this business generate more cash than it consumes? If yes, restructure. If no, close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt connects directors with licensed insolvency practitioners who can assess which tier of restructuring is appropriate for your company. A free, confidential consultation will clarify your options and the realistic prospects for each one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-31199-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about which restructuring tier fits your company\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Business Restructuring</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-31199\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31199-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-31199\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31199\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What is the cheapest way to restructure business debt?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-31199\">\n<p>Direct creditor negotiation and HMRC Time to Pay cost nothing beyond your time. These are the cheapest options and should always be tried first. CVAs cost \u00a35,000 to \u00a310,000 in professional fees. Administration starts at \u00a315,000. The cheapest effective option depends on the severity of creditor pressure and whether informal approaches will hold.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31199-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-31199\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31199\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I restructure if my company is already insolvent?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-31199\">\n<p>Yes. CVAs, administration, and restructuring plans are all available to insolvent companies. The key test is whether the business has a viable future once the debt is restructured. If the underlying business generates enough cash to service a restructured debt and cover ongoing obligations, restructuring is realistic. If it does not, restructuring delays liquidation rather than preventing it.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31199-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-31199\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31199\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What is the standalone moratorium introduced in 2020?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-31199\">\n<p>Part A1 of the Insolvency Act (introduced by the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020) gives eligible companies a 20-business-day breathing space where creditor enforcement is suspended. A licensed IP acts as monitor. The moratorium does not restructure debt. It buys time to prepare a CVA, restructuring plan, or other rescue mechanism. It can be extended with creditor consent.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-31199-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-31199\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-31199\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Do I keep control of my company during restructuring?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-31199\">\n<p>In Tier 1 (informal) and Tier 2 (CVA, moratorium, restructuring plan), you retain control under varying degrees of supervision. In administration, control passes to the administrator entirely (though light-touch administration may allow you to continue managing day-to-day operations). The more formal the process, the less control you retain.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-31199\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on the Insolvency Act 1986 (Part I CVA; Part II administration; Part A1 moratorium), the Companies Act 2006 (Part 26A restructuring plans), the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020, HMRC Time to Pay guidance, and practical experience from restructuring cases handled by licensed practitioners in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; Part I governs Company Voluntary Arrangements, Schedule B1 governs administration, and Part A1 sets out the standalone moratorium framework used in restructuring</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/part/26A\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, Part 26A</strong> &mdash; statutory basis for restructuring plans, including cross-class cram-down of dissenting creditor classes sanctioned by the court</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/12/contents\"><strong>Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020</strong> &mdash; introduced the standalone moratorium and the Part 26A restructuring plan as permanent rescue tools</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/difficulties-paying-hmrc\"><strong>HMRC: Business Payment Support Service and Time to Pay</strong> &mdash; official guidance on negotiating instalment arrangements for tax arrears before enforcement action begins</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; statutory body publishing guidance on rescue, restructuring outcomes, and director conduct following formal insolvency</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; framework for conduct review and disqualification orders where directors continue trading recklessly into insolvency</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; recognised professional body and licensing regulator; use the IP search to verify a practitioner before instructing on a restructuring</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/lpa-receivership/": {
    "id": 29543,
    "slug": "lpa-receivership",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<p>The letter arrives by tracked post from the lender's solicitors. You are told that an LPA receiver has been appointed over the commercial freehold at Unit 7 of the business park your company bought in 2019.</p>\n\n<p>The keys must be surrendered within 48 hours. Rent from the three tenants should now be paid to the receiver, not your company.</p>\n\n\n<p>LPA receivership sits in a specific pocket of English law. It is not a formal insolvency procedure, and the company is not wound up or put into administration.</p>\n\n<p>Under sections 101 to 109 of the Law of Property Act 1925, a secured lender with a fixed charge over property can appoint a receiver contractually, without going to court, purely on the basis of a default under the mortgage or charge deed.</p>\n\n\n<p>In our caseload, LPA receivership lands on property-owning SMEs and small landlords when a commercial mortgage or development loan falls into arrears.</p>\n\n<p>The mechanism is fast, the receiver owes their primary duty to the lender, and directors retain office but lose control of the charged asset. What matters for you is what the lender still holds elsewhere, and what remains after the property is sold.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-lpa-receivership-is-and-when-it-is-used\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-lpa-receivership-is-and-when-it-is-used\">What LPA Receivership Is and When It Is Used</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>An LPA receivership is the appointment of a receiver over a specific charged property under sections 101 to 109 of the Law of Property Act 1925. The lender does not need the court's permission.</p>\n\n<p>The receiver does not need to be a licensed insolvency practitioner.</p>\n\n\n<p>In our experience of these files, the appointment arrives by a one-page deed from the lender, often before directors have finished reading the default letter.</p>\n\n\n<p>The receiver's job is narrow. They take control of the charged property, manage it to preserve value, collect rents from any tenants, and usually sell the property.</p>\n\n\n<p>The proceeds are applied under section 109(8) LPA 1925: first to the receiver's costs, then to any prior charges, then to the interest on the appointing lender's debt, then to the principal, and any surplus returns to the borrower.</p>\n\n\n<p>Lenders choose LPA receivership when the asset is tangible and discrete, when speed matters, and when they want to avoid the expense and collective nature of administration.</p>\n\n\n<p>In our caseload, the three situations that produce the bulk of LPA appointments are commercial mortgage default, buy-to-let portfolio default, and property development loan default where the build has stalled.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-lpa-receivership-appointment-process-what-triggers-it\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-lpa-receivership-appointment-process-what-triggers-it\">The LPA Receivership Appointment Process: What Triggers It</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>Appointment is triggered by default under the mortgage or charge deed.</p>\n\n<p>The default clauses vary by lender, but the common triggers are missed payments (usually three consecutive), breach of financial covenants, insolvency events of the borrower, or breaches of wider obligations such as unauthorised subletting or failure to maintain.</p>\n\n\n<p>Once the default is established, the lender serves a formal demand for payment under section 103 LPA 1925.</p>\n\n<p>If the borrower does not pay within the statutory period (in practice, this step is often abbreviated or waived in the mortgage deed), the lender appoints the receiver by deed.</p>\n\n\n<p>The receiver then serves notice on any tenants directing them to pay rent to the receiver's account rather than the landlord's. They take possession of the property, secure it if vacant, arrange insurance, and begin the marketing process.</p>\n\n\n<p>A notice of appointment is filed at Companies House on form LQ01 within seven days. The whole process from demand to the receiver being on site can be as short as two weeks. That speed is the point.</p>\n\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-an-lpa-receiver-can-and-cannot-do-with-your-property\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-an-lpa-receiver-can-and-cannot-do-with-your-property\">What an LPA Receiver Can and Cannot Do With Your Property</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>The receiver's powers come from two sources. First, section 109 LPA 1925 gives statutory powers to collect rents, grant leases, and insure the property.</p>\n\n<p>Second, the mortgage deed usually grants extended powers, including the power to sell the property, to employ agents and solicitors, and to carry out repairs and improvements.</p>\n\n\n<p>Crucially, the receiver is by statute the agent of the borrower (section 109(2) LPA 1925), not the agent of the lender. This is a legal fiction with consequences.</p>\n\n\n<p>It means the borrower is responsible for the receiver's acts, and the receiver's costs are added to the secured debt rather than paid separately by the lender. It also means the receiver owes duties of care to the borrower as well as to the lender.</p>\n\n\n<p>What the receiver cannot do. They cannot deal with assets outside the charged property. Your stock in trade, your other properties, your book debts, your intellectual property, your cash at bank: untouched.</p>\n\n\n<p>They cannot take over the company's wider operations. They cannot disclaim onerous contracts in the way an administrator can under Schedule B1 IA 1986. They cannot pursue misfeasance claims against directors.</p>\n\n<p>Their remit is the charged property and nothing more.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"receiver-duties-care-in-sale-and-your-rights-as-borrower\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"receiver-duties-care-in-sale-and-your-rights-as-borrower\">Receiver Duties, Care in Sale, and Your Rights as Borrower</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>The receiver's primary duty is to the appointing lender: to realise the security and recover the debt. But the receiver also owes duties of care to the borrower and to anyone with a subsequent interest in the property.</p>\n\n\n<p>The receiver must take reasonable care to obtain a proper price in sale. A property sold at a significant undervalue can expose the receiver to a claim in negligence.</p>\n\n\n<p>In practice, \"reasonable care\" means marketing the property properly (usually through a RICS-qualified agent), accepting independent valuations, and not selling to a connected party without a clear arms-length process.</p>\n\n\n<p>It does not mean waiting for the optimal market moment. A receiver is entitled to sell in a sluggish market if the debt has to be realised, and the courts are slow to second-guess a timing decision made in good faith.</p>\n\n\n<p>As borrower, your rights are: to receive statutory receipts and payments accounts periodically; to challenge the sale in court if the price is significantly below market;</p>\n\n<p>to receive any surplus after the secured debt and the receiver's costs are paid; and to require the receiver to account for decisions.</p>\n\n\n<p>Those rights exist, but you should not overstate them. The receiver is not required to consult you on marketing strategy, and disputing every decision is usually counter-productive.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-lpa-receivership-differs-from-administration-and-liquidation\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-lpa-receivership-differs-from-administration-and-liquidation\">How LPA Receivership Differs From Administration and Liquidation</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>These three procedures are often confused because they all involve an external office-holder taking control of assets, but they operate on different scales and different legal bases.</p>\n\n\n<p>LPA receivership covers only the charged property. The company continues to trade (or not) in everything else. Directors remain in office. The receiver owes primary duty to the lender. No licensed IP is required.</p>\n\n<p>Appointment is fast and contractual. No court involvement.</p>\n\n\n<p>Administration under Schedule B1 Insolvency Act 1986 covers the whole company. Directors lose day-to-day control. The administrator is a licensed IP with duties to all creditors. There is a statutory moratorium on enforcement by other creditors.</p>\n\n\n<p>The goal is company rescue, a better outcome than liquidation, or distribution to secured and preferential creditors. Appointment can be by court or out-of-court, but is subject to the Insolvency Act framework.</p>\n\n\n<p>Liquidation (voluntary or compulsory) ends the company. The liquidator is a licensed IP. All assets are realised, the statutory waterfall under section 175 IA 1986 and Schedule 6 is applied, and the company is dissolved.</p>\n\n<p>For the fuller differences, see our guide on the <a href=\"/liquidation/\">UK liquidation process</a>.</p>\n\n\n<p>The practical difference from your perspective: LPA receivership is surgical, administration is systemic, liquidation is terminal.</p>\n\n<p>Which one the lender chooses depends on what they want. For a single property default with a solvent business underneath, LPA receivership is usually the right tool. Where the wider business is also in trouble, the lender may push for administration instead.</p>\n\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duties-when-the-lpa-receiver-arrives-at-your-property\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duties-when-the-lpa-receiver-arrives-at-your-property\">Director Duties When the LPA Receiver Arrives at Your Property</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>You remain a director of a trading company. Your section 172 Companies Act 2006 duties continue, and where insolvency is probable for the company as a whole, the creditor duty under <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25 engages.</p>\n\n<p>The receiver's presence on the charged property does not suspend those duties.</p>\n\n\n<p>Practical obligations. Cooperate with the receiver's reasonable information requests. Hand over keys, tenancy agreements, insurance documents, rent rolls, EPCs, and fire risk assessments without making the receiver chase.</p>\n\n<p>Do not interfere with the receiver's management of the property or negotiate with tenants behind the receiver's back. Continue filing at Companies House and with HMRC.</p>\n\n\n<p>Keep an eye on your wider position. An LPA receivership is often a symptom rather than the disease. If the charged property was the backstop for a wider cash-flow problem, losing it may trigger other defaults.</p>\n\n\n<p>We regularly see directors focus so tightly on the receiver that they miss the VAT arrears building on the trading side. For related director responsibilities, see our guide on <a href=\"/advice/are-directors-personally-liable-for-company-debts/\">directors' personal liability for company debts</a>.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"personal-guarantees-surplus-and-what-happens-after-the-sale\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"personal-guarantees-surplus-and-what-happens-after-the-sale\">Personal Guarantees, Surplus, and What Happens After the Sale</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>Most commercial mortgages and development loans are backed by a personal guarantee from the director or from a parent company. The PG sits outside the LPA appointment.</p>\n\n\n<p>If the property sells for \u00a31.2m and the debt is \u00a31.4m, the receiver accounts for the \u00a31.2m under section 109(8), and the remaining \u00a3200,000 becomes an unsecured shortfall.</p>\n\n<p>The lender then pursues the guarantor for that shortfall, typically by County Court proceedings.</p>\n\n\n<p>This is where LPA receivership surprises directors most. They assume that surrendering the property ends the matter. It does not. The PG remains live, and the guarantor's personal assets (often the family home) are exposed to the shortfall.</p>\n\n\n<p>In our caseload, the PG shortfall claim usually arrives within three to six months of the property sale, and by then any statutory defences to the PG have to be raised quickly or they are lost.</p>\n\n\n<p>If the property sells for more than the secured debt plus receiver's costs, the surplus returns to the company under section 109(8)(iv) LPA 1925. That happens more rarely than directors hope.</p>\n\n\n<p>Commercial property sold under receiver pressure typically achieves 80% to 92% of vacant possession value, depending on market, tenant quality, and condition.</p>\n\n<p>A \"surplus\" appears when the loan-to-value was conservative at the start and property values have since risen, and even then the receiver's fees and legal costs eat a chunk of the excess.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step\">Your Next Step</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n\n<p>Two groups of directors reach this page. The first are directors whose property equity exceeds the secured debt comfortably, who have no PG, and whose wider business is solvent. For that group, LPA receivership is inconvenient but contained.</p>\n\n<p>Let the receiver sell, collect any surplus, tighten the loan covenants on your other facilities, and move on. You do not need urgent advice.</p>\n\n\n<p>The second group are directors whose property is in negative equity, who have personally guaranteed the loan, whose trading business is also under pressure, and for whom the property sale will trigger a PG shortfall they cannot meet.</p>\n\n\n<p>For that group, the LPA appointment is not the event; it is the warning before the real event. The real event is the PG call and the risk to the family home.</p>\n\n<p>The window to restructure (refinance the PG, negotiate a capped settlement, consider an IVA, or look at <a href=\"/advice/business-restructuring/\">wider business restructuring</a>) is typically three to six months from appointment to sale.</p>\n\n\n<p>If you are in the second group, do not wait for the sale to complete. Call us on 0800 074 6757 as soon as the appointment letter arrives.</p>\n\n<p>Our licensed IPs will model the likely sale proceeds, the PG shortfall, the options on the PG, and the wider trading picture in one call.</p>\n\n<p>The difference between acting at appointment and acting after the sale is usually measured in tens of thousands of pounds.</p>\n\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-about-lpa-receivership\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-about-lpa-receivership\">FAQs About LPA Receivership</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-29543\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How fast can an LPA receiver be appointed?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-29543\">\n<p>From default to receiver on site, two to four weeks is typical. The lender serves a demand under section 103 LPA 1925, allows the stated notice period (often very short under the mortgage deed), and appoints by deed. No court involvement is required, and the appointment is effective from the moment the deed is signed.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Do I stop being a director when the receiver arrives?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-29543\">\n<p>No. Directors remain in office and continue to manage the company's wider affairs. What you lose is control of the specific charged property. The rest of the business, including any other properties not charged to this lender, continues under director management.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can the receiver sell the property for whatever they can get?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-29543\">\n<p>Not quite. The receiver owes a duty to take reasonable care to obtain a proper price, usually meaning open-market marketing through a RICS agent and an independent valuation. They are entitled to sell in a sluggish market, and the courts rarely second-guess timing, but a sale at significant undervalue without proper process can produce a claim in negligence.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What happens to tenants in a commercial property under LPA receivership?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-29543\">\n<p>Tenants' leases continue on their existing terms. Rent becomes payable to the receiver rather than the landlord. The receiver can grant new leases and agree variations under the powers in the mortgage deed. Existing tenants are generally protected by their lease and by the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 business tenancy framework.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does LPA receivership trigger insolvency of the company?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-29543\">\n<p>Not directly. LPA receivership is a property-specific enforcement, not a collective insolvency procedure. The company does not enter administration or liquidation automatically. However, cross-default clauses in other facilities often mean the receiver's appointment triggers defaults elsewhere, and the wider consequences can push the company into a formal insolvency process.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is my home at risk if I gave a personal guarantee?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-29543\">\n<p>If the property sale produces a shortfall against the secured debt, the lender will usually pursue the PG. The home is not automatically at risk, but the PG is a personal debt enforceable by County Court action, charging order, or bankruptcy petition. Defences (misrepresentation, undue influence, want of consideration) exist but are fact-specific and must be raised promptly.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-29543-6\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-6-faq-29543\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-29543\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I challenge the receiver's appointment itself?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-6-faq-29543\">\n<p>Rarely successfully. The appointment must comply with the mortgage deed and the LPA 1925, but challenges usually fail because the default was real and the procedure was followed. Better use of your resources is to engage with the receiver on valuation and marketing, and to focus on the PG position in parallel.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide was prepared by our editorial team and reviewed by a licensed insolvency practitioner within Company Debt's commercial property recovery practice.</p>\n<p>It applies sections 101 to 109 of the Law of Property Act 1925 and section 172(3) Companies Act 2006 on the creditor duty. It also reflects <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25 on the engagement of the creditor duty before formal insolvency, and Schedule B1 Insolvency Act 1986 for the administration comparison.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a UK insolvency and business rescue firm. Where we advise borrowers during an LPA receivership, our role is independent of the appointing lender and the receiver. We disclose any prior contact with either before accepting instructions.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo5/15-16/20/contents\"><strong>Law of Property Act 1925, sections 101, 103, 109</strong> &mdash; statutory power for a fixed-charge lender to appoint a receiver out of court and the section 109(8) order of application of sale proceeds</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, Schedule B1 and section 175</strong> &mdash; administration regime LPA receivership sits outside, and the liquidation waterfall that determines what remains for unsecured creditors after the receiver's sale</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 172</strong> &mdash; directors' duty to promote company success, modified once insolvency is in prospect by the creditor-regarding duty in BTI v Sequana</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1954/56/contents\"><strong>Landlord and Tenant Act 1954</strong> &mdash; business tenancy security of tenure framework governing the position of occupying tenants after an LPA receiver takes possession</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/companies-house-forms\"><strong>Companies House form LQ01</strong> &mdash; notice of appointment of a receiver or manager, filed within seven days of the receiver taking office over the charged property</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.supremecourt.uk/cases/uksc-2019-0046.html\"><strong>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA [2022] UKSC 25</strong> &mdash; Supreme Court ruling on when directors' duties shift to consider creditor interests, directly relevant once an LPA default is in prospect</a> - supremecourt.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; regulator and licensed-IP search, useful when checking the standing of a receiver instructed by the lender or sourcing independent advice</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-29543\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->"
  },
  "/hmrc/corporation-tax-penalties/": {
    "id": 28761,
    "slug": "corporation-tax-penalties",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The letter from HMRC rarely announces itself as urgent. The tone is flat: \"Our records show\u2026\", \"you may be liable\u2026\", a reference number, a figure. Directors who have not seen one before tend to file it, pay it, or query it in roughly equal proportions. Each of those responses has a different cost six months later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Corporation <a href=\"/hmrc/tax-penalties/\">Tax penalties</a> compound in a specific way. The fixed penalty for a missed filing is not the story. The behaviour-based penalty for the underlying inaccuracy is the story, and its calculation, which moves with cooperation, disclosure, and whether the error was \"careless\" or \"deliberate\", can take a five-figure bill into six figures without the director noticing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below we cover the specific Corporation Tax penalty categories, the statutory basis for each, how behaviour tiers change the amount you owe, and the practical options for reducing or appealing a penalty before it becomes enforced debt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"why-corporation-tax-penalties-matter-more-than-directors-think\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"why-corporation-tax-penalties-matter-more-than-directors-think\">Why Corporation Tax Penalties Matter More Than Directors Think</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Corporation Tax penalties live in two separate regimes that combine on any given case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fixed late-filing penalties</strong> under Schedule 18 of the Finance Act 1998: \u00a3100 at day one after the filing deadline; a further \u00a3100 after three months; 10% of the tax unpaid after six months; another 10% at twelve months.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Behaviour-based inaccuracy penalties</strong> under Schedule 24 of the Finance Act 2007: up to 30% of the potential lost revenue for careless errors, up to 70% for deliberate, and up to 100% for deliberate and concealed. These apply to the inaccuracy itself, not to late filing.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Interest</strong> on late payment of Corporation Tax at HMRC's published rate (currently 7.75% for late payment; rates track Bank of England base rate + 4%).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The combined effect is the bit most directors miss. If your company filed late, paid late, and had a small understatement, you can face a fixed filing penalty, a percentage penalty on unpaid tax, interest on the tax, and a separate behaviour-based penalty on the understatement. Four separate charges stacked on what you originally treated as \"a bit of admin we fell behind on\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you ignore any of these, the escalation path leads to HMRC enforcement action, distraint, statutory demand, and eventually a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The penalties are not collected through a separate process. They are simply tax your company owes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-main-types-of-corporation-tax-penalties\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-main-types-of-corporation-tax-penalties\">The Main Types of Corporation Tax Penalties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The penalty architecture under UK tax law splits into four categories. Each has its own statutory basis, its own trigger, and its own route to mitigation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Late filing penalties (CT600)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The CT600 corporation tax return must be filed within 12 months of the end of the accounting period. Miss that deadline and:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>\u00a3100 fixed penalty applies immediately.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>A further \u00a3100 after three months.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>At six months, HMRC estimates the tax due and adds a 10% penalty on that figure (the \"tax-geared\" penalty).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>At twelve months, another 10% tax-geared penalty on the unpaid amount.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The \u00a3100 figures double if the company has filed late in each of the last three accounting periods.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Late payment penalties on Corporation Tax</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Corporation Tax is due nine months and one day after the accounting period ends, three months before the filing deadline. Late payment attracts interest at the current HMRC rate and, where the return itself is filed more than six months late, the tax-geared penalties described above.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Inaccuracy penalties under Schedule 24</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the return contains an error that leads to an understatement of tax, HMRC can impose an inaccuracy penalty scaled to the behaviour behind the error.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Careless</strong>: up to 30% of the potential lost revenue; reducible to 0\u201315% for unprompted disclosure, 15\u201330% for prompted.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Deliberate (not concealed)</strong>: 20\u201370% of PLR; reducible to 20\u201335% for unprompted disclosure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Deliberate and concealed</strong>: 30\u2013100% of PLR; reducible to 30\u201350% for unprompted disclosure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>\"Reasonable care\" is the safe harbour. Where your company genuinely tried to get it right and the error was innocent, no penalty arises at all under Schedule 24.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Failure to notify penalties</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where a company becomes liable to Corporation Tax (typically on first trading) and fails to notify HMRC within three months of the start of the accounting period, a failure to notify penalty under Schedule 41 of the Finance Act 2008 applies, same behaviour tiers as Schedule 24, same reductions for disclosure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-behaviour-tiers-determine-corporation-tax-penalty-amounts\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-behaviour-tiers-determine-corporation-tax-penalty-amounts\">How Behaviour Tiers Determine Corporation Tax Penalty Amounts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The word that changes everything in a Schedule 24 enquiry is \"careless\" versus \"deliberate\". The gap between the two is measured in tens of thousands.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC's test for each:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Reasonable care</strong>, the taxpayer took appropriate steps to get the position right. No penalty.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Careless</strong>, the taxpayer failed to take reasonable care. Objective test; inexperience is not a defence.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Deliberate</strong>, the taxpayer knew the return was wrong and submitted it anyway.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Deliberate and concealed</strong>, deliberate error plus active steps to hide it (false records, concealment of transactions).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reductions within each tier are driven by three factors: telling (quality of your disclosure), helping (your cooperation with the enquiry), and giving (providing access to records). HMRC reduces the penalty percentage within the statutory range based on scores for each. Our experience is that proactive cooperation moves the needle materially.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Getting your behaviour category right is typically the single biggest commercial point in any enquiry. The difference between \"careless\" and \"deliberate\" on a \u00a350,000 error is the difference between a \u00a37,500 penalty and a \u00a335,000 one, before the reductions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"failure-to-notify-and-other-corporation-tax-oversights\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"failure-to-notify-and-other-corporation-tax-oversights\">Failure to Notify and Other Corporation Tax Oversights</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The category of penalty that catches first-time directors out most often is failure to notify. Your new company must notify HMRC of chargeability to Corporation Tax within three months of the start of its first accounting period. Missing that is not rare. Most accountants pick it up when the first set of accounts comes round, but the window will already have passed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Other recurring oversights that attract penalties:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Close company loans to participators</strong>, section 455 tax at 33.75% of outstanding director's loan balances nine months after the accounting period-end, often missed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Group relief errors</strong>, incorrect surrender of losses between group companies.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Transfer pricing</strong> adjustments where related-party transactions were not conducted at arm's length.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>R&amp;D tax credit claims</strong> disallowed on HMRC review, with a behaviour-based penalty where the claim was not reasonable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Each of these has its own complexity, but all run through the same Schedule 24 behaviour framework once an inaccuracy is identified.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"reducing-or-appealing-corporation-tax-penalties\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"reducing-or-appealing-corporation-tax-penalties\">Reducing or Appealing Corporation Tax Penalties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three routes to reduce a penalty, applicable in most cases:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Unprompted disclosure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disclosure made before you have any reason to think HMRC is about to discover the error. This produces the biggest reduction in penalty bands; careless errors can go to 0%. If your accountant spots an error in last year's return, filing an amendment voluntarily before any HMRC contact is often cheaper by 20\u201330 percentage points of penalty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Prompted disclosure with full cooperation</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disclosure once HMRC has opened an enquiry but before it has found the error on its own. Your reduction is smaller than unprompted but still material, often 15\u201320 percentage points depending on the quality of your cooperation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Formal appeal</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Within 30 days of the penalty notice, your company can appeal to HMRC on grounds including reasonable excuse, incorrect application of the behaviour tier, or incorrect calculation of the potential lost revenue. If HMRC upholds the penalty, you can take the appeal to the First-tier Tribunal (Tax Chamber).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most common successful appeal ground is \"reasonable excuse\": unexpected illness, bereavement, third-party failure (software, accountant), or other events outside your control. The bar is real. Generic business pressure is not a reasonable excuse in itself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"corporation-tax-penalty-case-how-the-numbers-actually-stack\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"corporation-tax-penalty-case-how-the-numbers-actually-stack\">Corporation Tax Penalty Case: How the Numbers Actually Stack</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A worked example that appears repeatedly in our caseload: a company with a \u00a340,000 Corporation Tax liability that filed nine months late and understated tax by \u00a312,000 through a careless error in R&amp;D treatment. If you are in this position, the penalties stack:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>\u00a3100 fixed late filing + \u00a3100 at three months = \u00a3200</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>\u00a34,000 tax-geared penalty (10% of \u00a340,000 at six months late)</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Interest on late payment, roughly \u00a32,300 at current rates across a year</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Careless inaccuracy penalty of 15% (prompted, moderate cooperation) on \u00a312,000 PLR = \u00a31,800</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Total penalty and interest: ~\u00a38,300 on top of the original \u00a340,000 tax. Careful pre-emption on your part (unprompted disclosure on the R&amp;D point, earlier filing even without payment) could have reduced that total by roughly half.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are not edge cases. They are the standard arithmetic of the UK Corporation Tax penalty regime when applied to a company that did not act promptly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-corporation-tax-penalties-signal-a-wider-problem\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-corporation-tax-penalties-signal-a-wider-problem\">When Corporation Tax Penalties Signal a Wider Problem</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Large accumulated HMRC debt, particularly where it sits alongside other creditor pressure, is one of the clearest external indicators of cash-flow insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Persistent late filing and repeated penalty notices are pattern evidence in any subsequent insolvency review. An Insolvency Practitioner's conduct report specifically addresses your compliance history, and we see it used against directors who thought HMRC arrears were a company problem rather than a personal one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where your company <a href=\"/hmrc/problems-paying-corporation-tax-hmrc/\">cannot pay the underlying Corporation Tax</a> and accumulating penalties, HMRC can refuse a standard Time to Pay arrangement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At that point your next steps move onto the enforcement track: HMRC enforcement, potentially Personal Liability Notices for NIC, and eventually liquidation action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A licensed insolvency practitioner involved early can often negotiate a better HMRC outcome than you would reach alone, partly because our presence signals that formal process is on the table, and partly because we can model whether a CVA or administration produces a better result for everyone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-corporation-tax-penalties\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-corporation-tax-penalties\">Your Next Step on Corporation Tax Penalties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three practical calls, in order of urgency:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Accountant</strong>, on penalty calculation and any possible unprompted disclosure on underlying inaccuracies.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Tax specialist</strong>, if the behaviour category is contested or the inaccuracy is complex.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Licensed insolvency practitioner</strong>, if the penalties sit alongside broader HMRC arrears and cash flow is tight.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed IPs and business rescue specialists can assess the position, handle the HMRC conversation, and implement a formal process where one is the right answer. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Corporation Tax Penalties</h2>\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-28761\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-28761-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-28761\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-28761\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How long do I have to appeal a Corporation Tax penalty?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-28761\">\n<p>30 days from the date of the penalty notice. The appeal is made to HMRC first; if unsuccessful, it can be taken to the First-tier Tribunal (Tax Chamber) within 30 days of HMRC's decision. Missing the 30-day window usually closes the appeal route entirely, though late appeals can be accepted on reasonable-excuse grounds.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-28761-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-28761\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-28761\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does HMRC ever waive Corporation Tax penalties?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-28761\">\n<p>Yes, in specific circumstances: where the taxpayer has a reasonable excuse (genuine events outside their control), where HMRC made an error, or where the penalty would be unjust in the circumstances. Routine business pressure does not meet the reasonable excuse threshold.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-28761-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-28761\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-28761\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Are directors personally liable for Corporation Tax penalties?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-28761\">\n<p>Not by default. Corporation Tax and its penalties are company debts. Personal liability arises only where HMRC can establish specific misconduct, Personal Liability Notices for unpaid NIC under the Social Security Administration Act 1992, or wrongful trading findings in subsequent insolvency where continued trading worsened creditor losses.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-28761-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-28761\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-28761\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What is the difference between \"careless\" and \"deliberate\" inaccuracy?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-28761\">\n<p>Careless means the taxpayer failed to take reasonable care, an objective standard. Deliberate means the taxpayer knew the return was wrong and submitted it anyway. The penalty bands differ sharply: careless up to 30%, deliberate up to 70%. Contesting the behaviour category HMRC proposes is often the single highest-value point in any enquiry.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-28761-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-28761\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-28761\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I make an unprompted disclosure after an enquiry has opened?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-28761\">\n<p>No, once HMRC has contacted the company about the relevant issue, any subsequent disclosure is \"prompted\" by definition. The reduction bands are smaller. Unprompted disclosure requires the taxpayer to volunteer the information before HMRC had any reason to suspect the error.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-28761-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-28761\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-28761\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What happens if I cannot afford to pay the Corporation Tax penalty?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-28761\">\n<p>HMRC may accept a Time to Pay arrangement covering both the underlying tax and the penalty, typically over 6\u201312 months. Where this is refused, or where the company cannot realistically service any arrangement, the penalty becomes enforceable like any other tax debt, statutory demand, winding-up petition, and the full enforcement toolkit. Early IP advice is the right call in that position.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team for UK directors managing Corporation Tax penalties or trying to avoid them.</p>\n<p>We've drawn on the Finance Act 1998 (Schedule 18 on Corporation Tax returns, assessments and related matters), the Finance Act 2007 (Schedule 24 on inaccuracy penalties), the Finance Act 2008 (Schedule 41 on failure to notify), and current HMRC guidance on Corporation Tax compliance and penalty mitigation.</p>\n<p>Where we describe how penalty assessments and behaviour-tier reductions play out operationally, including how disclosure quality affects the eventual penalty rate and how Corporation Tax arrears interact with broader insolvency procedures, we draw on cases handled across our advisory referral network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a UK insolvency advisory firm. Where Corporation Tax arrears sit alongside broader distress, we refer directors to licensed insolvency practitioners. Nothing on this page is a substitute for tailored tax or insolvency advice on your specific facts.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/36/schedule/18\"><strong>Finance Act 1998, Schedule 18</strong> &mdash; statutory basis for Corporation Tax returns and the fixed late-filing penalties of &pound;100, &pound;200, and the 10% tax-geared charges at six and twelve months</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2007/11/schedule/24\"><strong>Finance Act 2007, Schedule 24</strong> &mdash; inaccuracy penalty regime setting the 30% careless, 70% deliberate, and 100% deliberate-and-concealed bands on potential lost revenue</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2008/9/schedule/41\"><strong>Finance Act 2008, Schedule 41</strong> &mdash; failure-to-notify penalties applied where a company does not tell HMRC it is chargeable to Corporation Tax</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/compliance-checks-penalties-for-inaccuracies-in-returns-and-documents-ccfs7a\"><strong>HMRC factsheet CC/FS7a</strong> &mdash; official guidance on how inaccuracy penalties are calculated, mitigated, and suspended during a compliance check</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/compliance-handbook\"><strong>HMRC Compliance Handbook</strong> &mdash; internal manual setting out behaviour categorisation, disclosure reductions, and the quality-of-disclosure scoring used by case officers</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/difficulties-paying-hmrc\"><strong>HMRC: Time to Pay arrangements</strong> &mdash; route for negotiating an instalment plan on Corporation Tax debt before enforcement, distraint, or a winding-up petition is issued</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-28761\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/personally-liabilty-of-company-secretary/": {
    "id": 28545,
    "slug": "personally-liabilty-of-company-secretary",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The letter usually lands six to nine months after the company has gone into liquidation. It is from the appointed liquidator, addressed to you personally as company secretary, and it asks for documents, signatures, and a written explanation of what you knew and when. In our casework, most secretaries open that letter assuming it cannot really be aimed at them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The instinct that \"I'm just admin, the directors carry the risk\" is mostly true. The Companies Act 2006 puts the heavy fiduciary duties on directors, not secretaries, and limited liability protects you from ordinary company debts the way it protects the directors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Mostly true is not entirely true. From the cases we see, three specific exposures cut through the assumption: anything you signed personally, anything you filed dishonestly or carelessly, and anything you actively participated in alongside the directors. Those three are the ones the liquidator's letter is testing for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have been named in correspondence from a liquidator, the Insolvency Service, or HMRC, the question is which of those three categories your conduct sits in, and how much of it you can document was legitimate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"company-secretary-liability-at-a-glance\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"company-secretary-liability-at-a-glance\">Company Secretary Liability at a Glance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A UK company secretary has limited statutory liability for company debts. The Companies Act 2006 imposes filing and compliance duties, but day-to-day insolvency risk sits with the directors. Three carve-outs catch secretaries who go beyond filing: personal guarantees signed in your name, false or careless statutory filings (sections 1112 and 1121 CA 2006), and shadow-director conduct under section 251 IA 1986 where you actively participate in decisions a director would normally make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Quick Answer: When a Company Secretary Is Personally Liable</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A UK company secretary is personally liable in three scenarios: where you signed a personal guarantee or indemnity, where you filed false or negligent statutory documents at Companies House, or where you acted alongside the directors in misconduct that contributed to the company's losses.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Outside those three triggers, ordinary administrative duties do not pierce limited liability. The trap is that secretaries often do not realise which of their day-to-day actions counts as a \"trigger\" until after the company has failed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When Secretary Liability Becomes Real (the Trigger Events)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The triggers we see most often in liquidator investigations:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Signing a personal guarantee</strong> on a lease, bank facility, supplier credit account, or asset finance agreement on top of your secretarial duties.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Filing a confirmation statement, accounts, or share-capital filing</strong> that turns out to be inaccurate, late, or misleading.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Acting as a \"de facto director\"</strong>, meaning your real role went beyond admin and you were taking management decisions alongside or instead of the appointed directors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Main Personal Risk to Watch as a Secretary</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In our experience, the single biggest underestimated risk is the de facto director route under the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 (CDDA 1986, the statute that bans unfit officers from running companies). If a liquidator decides you were behaving as a director in substance, you can be disqualified for up to 15 years and pursued for compensation, even though your title said \"secretary\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to Do About Company Secretary Liability</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a liquidator, the Insolvency Service, or HMRC has been in touch, do three things this week: pull every document you have signed in the last three years, write a contemporaneous timeline of what you actually did versus what the directors decided, and take licensed insolvency advice before you reply.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-counts-as-company-secretary-personal-liability\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-counts-as-company-secretary-personal-liability\">What Counts as Company Secretary Personal Liability?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Company Secretary Liability Meaning</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>\"Company secretary personal liability\" means the company secretary is personally on the hook for a debt, loss, or penalty connected to the company, rather than the company itself bearing it. Limited liability is what normally keeps you separate from the company; personal liability is the exception when the law decides you should not be separate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The exception is narrow but specific. UK company law treats secretaries as \"officers\" of the company alongside directors, which means certain statutory duties and certain criminal offences apply to you directly under the Companies Act 2006 and the Insolvency Act 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Difference Between Company Liability and Secretary Personal Liability</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company liability is whatever the company itself owes: trade debts, tax, judgments, lease arrears. When the company is liquidated, those debts die with it (subject to creditor distributions from any remaining assets). They do not, by default, transfer to you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Secretary personal liability is different. It is a separate, fresh claim against you as an individual, founded either on a contract you signed personally or on conduct the law treats as your own wrongdoing. It survives the liquidation of the company and is enforceable against your house, savings, and wages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Statutory vs Contractual Exposure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Statutory exposure comes from Acts of Parliament: the Companies Act 2006, the Insolvency Act 1986, the CDDA 1986, and the Theft Act 1968 where fraud is alleged. It applies because you held the office of secretary, and you cannot contract out of it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Contractual exposure comes from documents you have personally signed: a personal guarantee, an indemnity, a director's loan agreement, a security over your home. This is the route that most often produces six-figure personal demands and where most secretaries are caught off-guard, because you signed the form years ago and forgot the wording.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-personal-liability-can-arise-from-acting-as-company-secretary\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-personal-liability-can-arise-from-acting-as-company-secretary\">When Personal Liability Can Arise From Acting as Company Secretary</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Filing Obligations Under Companies Act 2006</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sections 853A to 859Q of the Companies Act 2006 set out the filing duties that fall on every officer \"in default\", which expressly includes the secretary. The headline ones are the confirmation statement, statutory accounts, changes to PSC (people with significant control) data, and registrations of charges.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Late or false filings expose you to criminal-grade fines of up to \u00a35,000 per offence, daily default fines, and director-style disqualification under the CDDA 1986. The Companies House practice is that fines are typically pursued against the directors first, but the secretary is well within the statutory net where they signed the filing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Personal Guarantees Signed by the Secretary</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the single most common route to a personal claim we see, and it has nothing to do with secretarial conduct. Lenders, landlords, and trade suppliers routinely ask whoever signs the paperwork to add a personal guarantee, and secretaries who handle the company's contracts often sign without reading the back page.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your name is on the PG, the lender does not need to prove fault. They prove the company defaulted and demand the balance from you. The starting point for understanding what you actually agreed is the explainer on the risks of signing a personal guarantee.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Misconduct or Active Participation in Wrongdoing</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the secretary actively helps the directors do something the law forbids, the secretary becomes a participant rather than a bystander. The classic examples we see are paying out on the eve of insolvency, filing false accounts, or moving assets to a connected company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 213 (fraudulent trading) and section 214 (wrongful trading) of the Insolvency Act 1986 both reach officers other than directors where they \"knowingly\" participated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Liquidators look for this in the books. If you signed cheques, authorised transfers, or countersigned a board minute that approved a doubtful payment, expect to be asked to justify it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-assets-or-income-are-at-risk-in-secretary-liability\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-assets-or-income-are-at-risk-in-secretary-liability\">What Assets or Income Are at Risk in Secretary Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The exposure depends entirely on which trigger applies. The risk table below maps each route to the statutory basis, the typical financial scale, and what the lender or office-holder can pursue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>Statutory or contract basis</th><th>Typical scale</th><th>What is at risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Personal guarantee on a company loan or lease</td><td>The signed PG contract</td><td>\u00a310k to \u00a3500k+</td><td>Savings, equity in your home, wages (via charging order or attachment of earnings)</td></tr><tr><td>False or misleading statutory filings</td><td>Companies Act 2006, ss.853\u2013859 and ss.1112\u20131113 (false statement offence)</td><td>Up to \u00a35,000 per offence plus daily default fines</td><td>Personal fine, criminal record on conviction</td></tr><tr><td>Misfeasance (breach of duty as an officer)</td><td>Insolvency Act 1986, s.212</td><td>Compensation = the loss the company suffered</td><td>Personal compensation order, enforceable against all assets</td></tr><tr><td>CDDA 1986 disqualification</td><td>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, ss.6\u201310</td><td>2 to 15 years' ban; compensation order possible alongside</td><td>Loss of director or secretary roles, named on public register, often loss of professional licence</td></tr><tr><td>Regulatory penalty (FCA, ICO, HMRC PLN)</td><td>Sector-specific: FSMA 2000, GDPR (UK), Social Security Administration Act 1992 s.121C</td><td>\u00a35k to several million depending on regulator</td><td>Personal fine, ban from regulated roles, and tax debt transferred under a personal liability notice</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our honest read of the table is that most secretaries, in most insolvencies, never see a single line of it activated. The ones who do are almost always sitting on a personal guarantee or have done something on the misconduct side that they did not flag to themselves at the time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-company-secretary-liability-is-enforced\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-company-secretary-liability-is-enforced\">How Company Secretary Liability Is Enforced</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Liquidator Investigation and s.212 Misfeasance</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once the company enters liquidation, the appointed liquidator has a statutory duty to investigate the conduct of every officer, secretary included. Section 212 of the Insolvency Act 1986 lets the liquidator apply to court for a compensation order against any officer who has \"misapplied or retained\" company money or breached a fiduciary duty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The liquidator's first move is the formal questionnaire and document request. The standard process (document review, interviews, then enforcement decisions) is set out in our guide to insolvent company investigations. Most secretary investigations end at the document stage with no further action, but the ones that escalate do so quickly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Insolvency Service Disqualification Action (CDDA 1986)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Insolvency Service receives a conduct report from the liquidator on every failed company. If the report identifies the secretary as having acted as a de facto director or having actively participated in misconduct, the Insolvency Service can apply for disqualification under the CDDA 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CDDA ban runs for 2 to 15 years. It blocks you from being a director, secretary, or shadow director, and from being involved in the management of any company. For anyone whose career involves company office-holding, that is the more damaging consequence than the underlying compensation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Personal Guarantee Enforcement by Lender</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the trigger is a PG, the enforcement route is straight commercial litigation. The lender issues a formal demand, gives you 14 to 21 days to pay, and then sues for the balance. From County Court judgment, they progress to charging orders against your home, attachment of earnings against your wages, or a bankruptcy petition where the debt exceeds \u00a35,000.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The lender does not need a liquidator's permission and does not wait for the Insolvency Service. PG enforcement runs on its own clock and frequently arrives before the company has even finished liquidating.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-to-reduce-risk-as-a-company-secretary\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-to-reduce-risk-as-a-company-secretary\">How to Reduce Risk as a Company Secretary</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Audit Every Document You Have Signed in the Last Three Years</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Pull each contract, lease, finance agreement, supplier account, and bank facility where your signature appears. Read the back page of every one. You are looking for the words \"personal guarantee\", \"indemnity\", \"joint and several\", or any clause that names you in your personal capacity. If you find one, photocopy it and put it with your own records.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You cannot un-sign a guarantee, but knowing you have one changes how you should respond when the company gets into trouble. Secretaries who only discover the PG when the demand letter arrives have lost six months of negotiation runway.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Get Written Director Sign-Off on Statutory Filings Before You Submit</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every confirmation statement, set of accounts, PSC update, and charge filing should carry a written instruction from a named director (an email is fine) telling you to file it on the basis of the data they have provided. That email is your defence to a \"false filing\" allegation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the directors will not put it in writing, that itself is the warning sign. A secretary acting on verbal instructions only is, in our experience, the secretary the liquidator finds easiest to pursue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Document Your Objections to Decisions in Board Minutes</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you sit in board meetings and see a decision you think is wrong, record your objection in the minutes you draft. The flags we are looking out for are preferential payments to a connected creditor, undervalued asset transfers, or taking on debt while insolvent. A note that \"the secretary expressed concern about the proposed payment\" is worth more than any later witness statement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the directors refuse to let your objection appear in the minutes, send yourself a contemporaneous email setting it out. Liquidators and the Insolvency Service give significant weight to contemporaneous documentation and very little to recollections offered after the fact.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"mistakes-company-secretaries-make-about-liability\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"mistakes-company-secretaries-make-about-liability\">Mistakes Company Secretaries Make About Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Assuming the Secretary Title Is Purely Administrative</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Companies Act 2006 calls the secretary an \"officer\" of the company in the same breath as the directors. Officer status is what brings you within reach of the criminal-grade filing offences, the misfeasance jurisdiction, and the CDDA 1986 disqualification regime. Calling the role \"administrative\" is a description, not a legal status.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Treating \"I Was Just Following Instructions\" as a Defence</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is not, on its own. The defence the courts recognise is \"reasonable steps\", which means evidence you questioned the instruction, sought clarification, or escalated where the action looked wrong. Following instructions silently is the position the liquidator will frame as acquiescence, and acquiescence sits inside the misfeasance net.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Believing Resignation Wipes the Slate</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Resigning as secretary stops the clock on future obligations but does nothing to the obligations that already crystallised before your TM02 (the form you file at Companies House to record a secretary resignation).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Personal guarantees survive resignation. Misfeasance liability survives resignation. CDDA 1986 disqualification proceedings can be brought against former officers up to three years after the company entered insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"related-company-secretary-liability-guides\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"related-company-secretary-liability-guides\">Related Company Secretary Liability Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Adjacent topics that cover the specific instruments and statutes referenced above:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Insolvent Company Investigations, what the liquidator does once the company is wound up, and what each officer should expect.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The Risks of Signing a Personal Guarantee, the contract route by which most secretary claims actually arise.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Personal Liability Notices, the HMRC instrument that transfers unpaid NIC to officers personally, including secretaries with financial control.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Insolvency Act 1986 Overview, sections 212, 213, and 214 explained for officers other than directors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"frequently-asked-questions-about-company-secretary-liability\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"frequently-asked-questions-about-company-secretary-liability\">Frequently Asked Questions About Company Secretary Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"28545-secretary-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a company secretary be personally liable for company debts?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, but only in three defined situations: where the secretary signed a personal guarantee or indemnity, where they made false or negligent statutory filings, or where they actively participated in misconduct that contributed to creditor losses. Outside those three triggers, ordinary company debts stay with the company under limited liability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a company secretary be disqualified under the CDDA 1986?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. The Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 expressly catches anyone who has acted as a director in substance, and that includes a secretary whose real role went beyond administration. Disqualification runs from 2 to 15 years and blocks you from any director, secretary, or shadow director role during the ban.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs a company secretary liable for late or false filings at Companies House?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, where the secretary is named as the filing officer or signed the relevant document. Sections 853A to 859Q of the Companies Act 2006 catch every officer \"in default\", which includes the secretary, with fines up to \u00a35,000 per offence and daily default penalties. Written director sign-off on every filing is the practical defence to a false-filing allegation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes resigning as company secretary remove personal liability?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. Resignation stops further obligations from accruing but does nothing to liabilities that already exist. Personal guarantees, misfeasance claims under section 212 of the Insolvency Act 1986, and disqualification under the CDDA 1986 all survive resignation, and former secretaries can be pursued up to three years after the company entered insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan HMRC issue a Personal Liability Notice to a company secretary?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, where the secretary had material financial control over payroll or NIC payments. HMRC's PLN power under section 121C of the Social Security Administration Act 1992 reaches \"officers\" of the company, and the case law treats secretaries with hands-on control over payment decisions as inside that net.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat should a company secretary do if the company is approaching insolvency?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Three actions. First, audit every document you have signed personally and identify any guarantees or indemnities. Second, insist on written director sign-off for every filing and every payment authorisation. Third, take licensed insolvency advice in your own name, separate from the directors, so the advice is privileged to you and the file shows you took reasonable steps.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"methodology-block\">\n<p><strong>Methodology &amp; Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners. It reflects UK company-secretary liability law as it stands at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<p>Statutory references are drawn from the Companies Act 2006, the Insolvency Act 1986, and the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, with cross-checks against current Companies House guidance and Insolvency Service practice notes.</p>\n<p>Where we set out the trigger events and the typical financial scale in the risk table, we have prioritised what we see in live liquidator investigations rather than the academic edge cases. Specific case-law references are kept light deliberately: the page is for company secretaries assessing their own exposure, not for litigators preparing a tribunal bundle.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. We are not a law firm and we do not represent at tribunal. Where secretary liability has crystallised into criminal exposure or a disqualification application, you will need specialist solicitor representation alongside any insolvency support we provide.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"sources-block\">\n<p><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Companies Act 2006, Part 12 (Company Secretaries) and ss.853A to 859Q (filing duties): <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Insolvency Act 1986, s.212 (misfeasance), s.213 (fraudulent trading), s.214 (wrongful trading): <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 (applies to officers acting as de facto directors): <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\">legislation.gov.uk</a></li>\n<li>Companies House guidance on company secretary duties and filing responsibilities: <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/company-secretarial-services\">gov.uk</a></li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/personal-liability-spouses-business-debts/": {
    "id": 28526,
    "slug": "personal-liability-spouses-business-debts",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The letter arrives on a Tuesday, addressed to you personally. NatWest is demanding \u00a3140,000 under a personal guarantee your husband gave in 2019 when the company drew down a growth loan. You do not remember signing anything for the business, because you did not. What you signed, five years ago, was a mortgage variation when you added your name to the family home.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The bank's argument is that the charge over the house supports the guarantee. Your solicitor's advice is that before they can enforce against your half of the equity, they have to prove you received independent legal advice, that you understood the risk, and that the bank satisfied the eight-point protocol in <em>Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)</em> [2001] UKHL 44.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If they cannot, the security may be unenforceable against your interest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That is the shape of the question. The default position in English law is that one spouse is not liable for the other's business debts. But there are specific routes by which liability crosses the marital line, and the default only holds if none of those routes has opened.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We walk you through the routes that matter, the <em>Etridge</em> protections the bank has to clear, and what the Insolvency Act 1986 does to jointly-owned property when one spouse is made bankrupt. We see the same bundle of documents every week; in our experience the outcome is nearly always decided by which boxes the lender did or did not tick in 2018 or 2019, not by what the letter threatens now.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Default Position on Personal Liability for a Spouse's Business Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marriage, civil partnership, or cohabitation does not, on its own, make you liable for your partner's business debts. That is the starting point, and it is true whether the business is a limited company, a partnership, or a sole tradership.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The principle is an old one. A limited company has separate legal personality under <em>Salomon v A Salomon &amp; Co Ltd</em> [1897] AC 22, so its debts are its own, not its directors' and not its directors' spouses'. Our guide to <a href=\"/advice/what-is-limited-liability/\">limited liability</a> sets out the separation more fully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A sole trader's business debts are personal to them, but they do not automatically become their spouse's debts merely because the two are married.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the bank wants, when it lends to a small owner-managed company, is something extra to that default. A personal guarantee from one director, ideally both. Security over a jointly-owned property. A co-borrower on the loan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Those are the instruments that cross the marital line and create exposure for the non-trading spouse. Without one of them, you are insulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Routes by Which You Can Become Liable for Your Spouse's Business Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The routes cluster into four recognisable categories. If you are reading this letter from a lender, the first task is identifying which of these applies to your specific paperwork.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Joint borrowing.</strong> A joint business loan, a joint overdraft, or a business credit card held in joint names. Both signatories are jointly and severally liable for the full balance. The lender does not have to pursue one before the other.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Co-guarantor on a personal guarantee.</strong> You signed the PG document alongside your spouse, typically for a bank facility or a landlord. Once the company defaults, the lender can call either of you, or both, for the full amount.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Co-director of the business.</strong> If you are a director, the wrongful trading and misfeasance provisions in sections 212 and 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 apply to you personally, even if your involvement was nominal. Being \"just on the paperwork\" is not a defence once things go wrong.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Business partner (partnership or LLP member).</strong> In an ordinary partnership, every partner is jointly and severally liable for partnership debts. In an LLP, liability is limited unless personal guarantees were given. If you registered with HMRC as a partner alongside your spouse, that exposure is real.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fifth route is the one directors often overlook: security granted over the matrimonial home. That is not the same as being liable under a personal guarantee. The bank's charge over the property secures the company's debt or the guarantee; it does not make you a debtor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But if the company defaults and the guarantee is called, the bank can apply to force sale of the property to realise its security. Our guide to the personal guarantee on <a href=\"/liquidation/\">company liquidation</a> covers the enforcement sequence in detail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Etridge Protocol: Protection for Spouses Who Signed Personal Guarantees</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The leading case on spouse guarantees is <em>Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)</em> [2001] UKHL 44. The House of Lords held that when a wife (or husband) provides a guarantee or security to support a loan to the other spouse's business, the lender is put on inquiry as to the possibility of undue influence or misrepresentation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>To protect itself against later challenge, the lender must satisfy an eight-point protocol. In practice that means the non-business spouse must receive independent legal advice from a solicitor who has not acted for the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That solicitor must have seen the underlying loan agreement and the borrower's financial position, and must have explained the document's effect in a face-to-face meeting.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The solicitor then writes to the bank confirming the advice was given. Without that certificate, and without the procedural steps behind it, the bank's security is vulnerable to being set aside on the ground of undue influence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the door we ask spouses to check first when a guarantee is called. If the 2019 paperwork shows you met your own solicitor, read the bank's letter, and signed in front of them, the <em>Etridge</em> defence is weaker.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you were handed the document at the kitchen table with \"just sign here, love, it's a formality\", and no solicitor was involved, there is a case to be argued. The difference can be the equity in your half of the house.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Joint Accounts, Joint Cards, Joint Mortgages and Liability for Business Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Joint bank accounts and joint credit cards are a regular route for unintended spouse liability. The legal position is unforgiving: both parties are jointly and severally liable for the full balance, regardless of who incurred the spending.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the couple share a joint current account and one of them is running business expenses through it, the unused limit or the overdraft facility is backed by both of them. When the spouse's business fails and the account is left overdrawn, the bank can collect from either party.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Joint mortgages are more complex. The mortgage itself is a joint debt. If one spouse's business fails, that does not automatically trigger default on the mortgage unless the lender has required assignment of rental income, cross-default, or taken a second charge to support business borrowing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the mortgage is performing, the matrimonial-home protection under the Insolvency Act 1986 kicks in, which we set out below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Personal Liability When Your Spouse Becomes Bankrupt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your spouse's business fails, their personal guarantees are called, and they cannot pay, they may end up bankrupt. Bankruptcy under the Insolvency Act 1986 vests their assets in a trustee in bankruptcy, who has a duty to realise the estate for creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two questions immediately arise for you, the non-bankrupt spouse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Your own assets.</strong> The trustee has no claim on property owned solely by you, provided it is genuinely yours. Bank accounts in your sole name, a car registered to you, inheritance money in your name. Those remain yours.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Jointly-owned property.</strong> The matrimonial home is the usual concern. Where the property is held as beneficial joint tenants or tenants in common, the trustee is entitled to the bankrupt spouse's beneficial share, not yours.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 336 of the Insolvency Act 1986 and section 335A are the provisions that govern what happens next with the family home. The trustee can apply to court for an order for sale, but the court will balance the interests of the creditors, the needs of any children, and the position of the non-bankrupt spouse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>During the first year after bankruptcy, the court will typically not force sale unless exceptional circumstances exist. After one year, under section 335A(3), the interests of creditors are assumed to outweigh all other considerations unless \"exceptional\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The trustee usually ends up with a buy-out from the non-bankrupt spouse, or a forced sale with the bankrupt's half of the net equity going into the estate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Protect Yourself From Personal Liability on a Spouse's Business Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the business has been trading for a while and no call has yet been made, the protective moves sit in three areas.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Do not sign anything on business banking paperwork that you have not read.</strong> Personal guarantees, co-borrower agreements, second charges, unlimited mortgage extensions. These are the instruments by which liability crosses. If the bank wants your signature, the bank wants you on the hook.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If you must sign, insist on independent legal advice.</strong> A separate solicitor for you, not the business's solicitor. That is the <em>Etridge</em> defence you are preserving for the day the guarantee is called. The cost of an hour's advice is trivial relative to what it buys.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Keep your own assets clearly yours.</strong> A sole-name bank account for your salary. Savings in your name. An inheritance ring-fenced. Joint ownership blurs the line, and blur works against you if creditors come calling.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once a PG is called or a statutory demand is served on your spouse personally, the protective window is narrower. The focus shifts to the <em>Etridge</em> defence, to bankruptcy-vs-IVA planning on your spouse's side, and to the home's position under section 336.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our note on joint and several liability covers the HMRC angle where your spouse has signed a joint-and-several-liability notice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Personal Liability for a Spouse's Business Debts: What to Check in Your Paperwork</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before you ring a lender to protest, open the filing cabinet. The documents that determine your exposure are specific and identifiable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The original business loan agreement. Look for your name alongside your spouse's as a borrower or as a guarantor. Initialled pages count.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The overdraft facility letter. Personal guarantee clauses are often buried on page three or four under \"Security\".</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The mortgage deed and any subsequent variations. A second charge to support business lending is the instrument the bank will rely on if the guarantee is called.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Any landlord's guarantee. Commercial leases often come with spouse PGs where the business is trading from premises.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Companies House filings. If you are listed as an active director of the business, your exposure is not \"spouse of director\", it is \"director\". The two are not the same legally.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We ask spouses to assemble that bundle before the first call. Our conversation is much sharper when the paperwork is in front of us.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Guessing at which documents you signed is how most of the anxiety accumulates. The documents themselves usually tell a tighter, more navigable story than the one you have been telling yourself since the letter arrived.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on Liability for a Spouse's Business Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The verdict splits into two camps. If you have never signed a personal guarantee, never co-signed on business borrowing, are not a director or partner in the business, and the matrimonial home does not carry a second charge for the company, your exposure is likely zero.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The creditor's letter is aggressive positioning, not a legitimate claim. A short letter from a solicitor back to the lender usually closes it down.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you did sign something, the question is not whether you are liable at all but how much of the <em>Etridge</em> protocol the bank cleared at the time. That is a paperwork-led argument, and the sooner a solicitor or an IP reviews the bundle, the sooner you know where the defence sits. Leaving it until the bank has issued proceedings is the expensive route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call Company Debt free on 0800 074 6757 for a confidential review. We will walk through the documents you signed, work out whether an <em>Etridge</em> defence is available, and model the impact on the family home under sections 335A and 336 of the Insolvency Act 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We will then set out with you whether your spouse's best route is an IVA, bankruptcy, or a negotiated settlement with the lender. Nothing is charged until you instruct us.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Personal Liability for a Spouse's Business Debts</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"28526-spouse-liability-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eAm I automatically liable for my spouse's business debts?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. Marriage or civil partnership does not create automatic liability for your spouse's business debts. Liability arises only where you have signed something (a personal guarantee, a joint loan, a co-borrower agreement), are a director or partner in the business, or have property charged to secure the business's borrowing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the Etridge protocol?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The eight-point test from <em>Etridge (No 2)</em> [2001] UKHL 44. Where a spouse guarantees or secures the other spouse's business borrowing, the lender must ensure independent legal advice is given, the solicitor sees the loan paperwork, and consequences are explained face to face. A bank that cannot show compliance risks having its security set aside for undue influence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan the bank take my house if my spouse's business fails?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Only where there is a charge supporting the debt or a called guarantee, and only to the bankrupt spouse's beneficial share. The trustee's route to forced sale is sections 335A and 336 IA 1986. For the first year post-bankruptcy, the court weighs the non-bankrupt spouse's and children's interests. After a year, creditor interests presumptively prevail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens to a joint bank account if my spouse goes bankrupt?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The bank typically freezes the joint account on being notified of the bankruptcy. The trustee in bankruptcy is entitled to the bankrupt spouse's share of any credit balance. Any overdraft is joint-and-several, so the bank can pursue you for the full outstanding amount notwithstanding the bankruptcy. You should open a sole-name account before the bankruptcy order, not after.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs my spouse's sole trader debt different from company debt?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. A limited company has separate legal personality per <em>Salomon v Salomon</em> [1897] AC 22, so its debts are its own. A sole trader's business debts are personal to them. Either way, those debts do not automatically transfer to a spouse. They transfer only if you signed something creating liability, or if the debt is secured on jointly-owned property.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I be held liable as a director if I was only a director on paper?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. Being a registered director, even a \"silent\" one, means sections 212 (misfeasance) and 214 (wrongful trading) of the Insolvency Act 1986 apply to you if the company goes into insolvent liquidation. Nominal directorship is not a defence. Resignation before any warning signs crystallise is the clean position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association.</p>\n<p>Statutory references draw on the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 212, 214, 335A and 336), the Companies Act 2006 (principles of separate legal personality), and case authority including <em>Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)</em> [2001] UKHL 44 and <em>Salomon v A Salomon &amp; Co Ltd</em> [1897] AC 22. Last reviewed May 2026.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner. We may also receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/335A\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 335A and 336</strong> &mdash; the rights of a bankrupt's spouse in the family home and the court's powers on a trustee's application for sale</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 212 and 214</strong> &mdash; misfeasance and wrongful trading liability for directors</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/339\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 339 to 343</strong> &mdash; transactions at undervalue and preferences in personal bankruptcy, the route by which spouse-asset transfers can be reversed</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006</strong> &mdash; the modern framework establishing separate legal personality and limited liability</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKHL/2001/44.html\"><strong><em>Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)</em> [2001] UKHL 44</strong> &mdash; the leading House of Lords authority on undue influence in spousal guarantees and the Etridge protocol</a> - bailii.org</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKHL/1896/1.html\"><strong><em>Salomon v A Salomon &amp; Co Ltd</em> [1897] AC 22</strong> &mdash; the foundational separate-legal-personality decision</a> - bailii.org</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/find-an-insolvency-practitioner\"><strong>Find an Insolvency Practitioner (gov.uk register)</strong> &mdash; verify a practitioner's licence before instructing</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/company-cash-flow-problems/biggest-struggles-for-small-business-owners/": {
    "id": 26902,
    "slug": "biggest-struggles-for-small-business-owners",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The challenges that actually sink UK small businesses are rarely the dramatic ones. They are the quieter compliance and cash-flow pressures that compound over three or four quarters until you are running a business where every Thursday afternoon is spent firefighting something that should have been handled three months earlier.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This page is the working-director's triage list, the top nine pressures we see repeatedly across the insolvency cases we're instructed on, with the statutory detail, the specific thresholds, and the practical steps that consistently defuse each one before it reaches crisis point.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Cash Flow Pressures and Late Payment</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Healthy cash flow is the single largest predictor of business survival. Late payment is the single largest predictor of broken cash flow.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The specific tool UK businesses underuse is the statutory interest regime. Under the <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/20/contents\">Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998</a>, you are entitled to charge <strong>8% plus the Bank of England base rate</strong> on qualifying late payments, plus a fixed compensation amount (\u00a340\u2013\u00a3100 depending on invoice size).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The right applies automatically; it does not need to be in your contract. If you are not using it, you are effectively subsidising your customers' working capital.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Invoicing discipline.</strong> Issue on delivery, not monthly. Clear payment terms. Chase on day 31, not day 60.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Statutory interest applied on qualifying invoices.</strong> The threat, correctly documented, is itself a collection tool.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Prompt Payment Code sign-up</strong> and escalation to the Small Business Commissioner for persistent late-payers.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Formal recovery</strong>, statutory demand, county court claim, <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>, where informal chasing has run its course.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The mistake with big customers</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Larger customers get softer treatment on credit control, and larger customers are the most common late-payers. Your assumption that a FTSE 250 retailer will settle on terms is empirically wrong; their payment systems are often the slowest. Apply the same discipline regardless of customer size.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Tax Compliance Duties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The UK tax calendar for an owner-managed limited company runs on fixed dates that are enforced with compounding penalties. Missing the filing or payment is where HMRC's relationship with the business typically sours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Self Assessment</strong>, online return filed by 31 January following the tax year end; payment due the same date.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Corporation Tax</strong>, company tax return within 12 months of accounting period end; <strong>payment due 9 months and 1 day</strong> after accounting period end.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>VAT</strong>, quarterly returns and payments on a Making Tax Digital basis for most businesses.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>PAYE / RTI</strong>, Full Payment Submission on or before each payday.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Budgeting for tax liability</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The single most common small-business tax failure is treating corporation tax and VAT as month-end money. Ring-fencing your tax reserve in a separate account, ideally weekly, prevents the quarterly shock.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is an operational discipline, not a tax-optimisation move, and it eliminates the HMRC penalties and interest that catch unprepared directors. In our experience, this single habit change does more to prevent HMRC escalation than any other.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Employment Obligations When Hiring Staff</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring your first employee triggers a cluster of statutory obligations that most directors underestimate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Right to work checks</strong>, documented, kept on file, with clear consequences for failure (civil penalties up to \u00a345,000 for a first-time breach, \u00a360,000 for repeat offences under the 2024 regime).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Written particulars</strong> within two months, contract terms required under section 1 of the Employment Rights Act 1996.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Employer registration with HMRC</strong> before the first pay day; PAYE scheme set up; qualifying payroll software.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Employers' Liability Insurance</strong> with minimum \u00a35m cover, legally required under the Employers' Liability (Compulsory Insurance) Act 1969.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Workplace pension assessment</strong> under auto-enrolment rules.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Employment status misclassification</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Treating someone who meets the employee tests as a contractor to save NIC is a pattern HMRC now routinely checks. If your review finds misclassification, consequences include backdated employment tax, NIC, penalties (typically 30\u2013100% of tax lost), and employment rights claims the business thought it had avoided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The CEST tool on gov.uk is a starting point; a formal employment-status review is the right call where your arrangement is ambiguous.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Payroll and Workplace Pension Compliance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>PAYE, RTI, and auto-enrolment sit together as a compliance triangle. One weak corner produces penalties from two regulators. Our editorial team reviews this combination regularly because it is the most common multi-penalty trap in our caseload.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Minimum pension contributions are set by law: total contributions must be <strong>at least 8% of qualifying earnings</strong>, with a minimum <strong>3% employer contribution</strong>. The Pensions Regulator enforces compliance with civil penalties and, in serious or persistent breach cases, criminal prosecution of directors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Register as an employer with HMRC before first payment.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>HMRC-recognised payroll software (the list is published on gov.uk).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Automatic enrolment assessment on each pay reference period, not just once at setup.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Qualifying pension scheme in place before first eligible jobholder is enrolled.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Health and Safety Regulations</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK employers have a statutory duty to protect employees and others affected by the business under the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999. The HSE's five-step approach is the working framework:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Identify the hazards.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Decide who might be harmed and how.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Evaluate the risks and decide on precautions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Record findings (required for businesses with 5 or more employees).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Review and update.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HSE fines post-sentencing-guidelines can be significant, a serious breach by a small employer typically attracts fines in the \u00a35,000\u2013\u00a350,000 range; larger breaches run into six figures. Fines are calculated against turnover, not profit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Data Protection and GDPR Duties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018 apply to any business processing personal data, which is effectively every business. Small-business reality:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>ICO registration and fee</strong> (currently \u00a340\u2013\u00a32,900 depending on size and turnover).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Lawful basis for processing</strong> documented for each category of personal data.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Privacy notice</strong> covering the required transparency information.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Data breach response</strong>, reportable breaches must be notified to the ICO within 72 hours of awareness.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The maximum ICO fine is 4% of annual global turnover or \u00a317.5m, whichever is higher. For small businesses the usual enforcement is reputational via ICO action notices, but the fines are real and in our experience directors consistently underestimate this exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Finance and Funding Decisions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding decisions most small businesses face, bank overdraft, invoice finance, commercial mortgage, asset finance, director's loan, each carry different personal-liability profiles.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal guarantees</strong> are the default ask on most commercial facilities. The variables worth negotiating: cap on liability, facility-specific rather than all-monies scope, joint and several apportionment between co-guarantors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Invoice finance</strong>, typically personally guaranteed for the full facility; often the second-cheapest finance route but structurally binds the director personally.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Commercial mortgages</strong>, sometimes secured without PG on stronger covenants; Law of Property Act receivership risk on default.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Director's loans</strong>, attract section 455 tax at 33.75% on balances outstanding 9 months after accounting period end.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Director Duties and Personal Liability</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors of UK limited companies have seven statutory duties under the Companies Act 2006 (sections 171\u2013177). The two that bite in distress situations are the duty to promote the success of the company (section 172), and the duty to exercise reasonable care, skill, and diligence (section 174).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once insolvency becomes likely, the section 172 duty pivots: you are acting for creditors as a whole, not for shareholders. Continuing to trade past that pivot point, without taking every step a reasonably diligent director would take to minimise creditor losses, is wrongful trading.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The remedy is a personal contribution order against you under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986. Our licensed IPs can help you establish exactly where that duty line sits in your situation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Insolvency Options When Things Tighten</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When the cash flow pressures described above compound, the formal options available are:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA), repay historic debt over 3\u20135 years while continuing to trade.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Administration, statutory moratorium against creditor action while rescue or sale is pursued.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a> (CVL), orderly wind-down where rescue is not viable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Members' Voluntary Liquidation (MVL), solvent closure for owner-managed companies with accumulated reserves, usually on retirement or exit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on UK Small Business Pressures</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The nine challenges above are cumulative. If you address them in clusters, payroll and pensions together, tax and cash flow together, H&amp;S and data protection together, you will usually find the compliance burden manageable. Directors who treat each as an isolated quarterly problem usually find them stacking into a crisis that reaches the insolvency end of the map.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Honest framing: directors rarely call us about one of these pressures. They call when three have stacked up the same quarter, usually in the order HMRC letter, bank finance tightening, key employee exit. If two of those are already true for you, the useful call is now, not once the third lands.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the financial pressures have already tipped into insolvency territory, our licensed insolvency practitioners and business rescue specialists can explain your options, outline the risks, and help you plan your next steps. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">UK Small Business Challenges FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"26902-struggles-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the biggest cause of small business failure in the UK?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Cash flow failure, not profitability failure. Most businesses that enter insolvency were profitable on paper at some point in the 12 months before they failed. What kills them is the timing gap between invoices issued and cash received, compounded by tax payments that do not wait for the customer to settle.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow much is the statutory interest rate on late commercial payments?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Under the Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998, the statutory rate is 8% plus the Bank of England base rate, applied to qualifying late commercial payments. A fixed compensation amount (\u00a340 for invoices under \u00a31,000, \u00a370 for \u00a31,000\u2013\u00a310,000, \u00a3100 for \u00a310,000+) applies per qualifying invoice alongside the interest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhen is corporation tax due for a UK limited company?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Nine months and one day after the end of the accounting period. The company tax return (CT600) follows and must be filed within 12 months of the period end. Note the payment deadline precedes the filing deadline by three months, directors sometimes miss this and file late because they assume tax is due at filing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the minimum pension contribution under auto-enrolment?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Total contributions of at least 8% of qualifying earnings, with a minimum 3% from the employer. The employee makes up the difference, with tax relief applied. Getting the calculation wrong triggers enforcement action from The Pensions Regulator, including penalties that accumulate while the error persists.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhen should a UK small business seek insolvency advice?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>At the first sustained signal that the cash-flow test is being failed, essential overheads being delayed, HMRC arrears accumulating, supplier pressure increasing. Early licensed-IP advice expands the options available (CVA, administration, restructuring) rather than narrowing them to liquidation as the last resort. Late advice usually means liquidation is the only option left.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan small business owners be held personally liable for company debts?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>By default, no. Limited liability separates the company's debts from your personal finances. Personal liability arises through specific routes: personal guarantees on specific facilities, overdrawn director's loan accounts, wrongful or fraudulent trading findings, misfeasance claims, and HMRC Personal Liability Notices.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ordinary business failure in a properly-run limited company does not produce your personal liability. If you are unsure whether any of these routes apply to your situation, take early advice before the question becomes urgent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f9f9f9\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat should a small business director do in a data breach?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Notify the Information Commissioner's Office within 72 hours of becoming aware of a reportable breach. Notify affected individuals without undue delay where the breach is high-risk. Document the incident and response internally. Late notification is itself a breach and attracts separate penalties.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team, reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners, and reflects UK commercial, tax, employment, health-and-safety, and data-protection law as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Statutory figures (pension contribution minimum, auto-enrolment thresholds, interest rate under the 1998 Act) are accurate for the 2025/26 reference year; always check current gov.uk guidance for the year in question.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where we recommend a CVA, Administration, CVL, or MVL, we can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, Part 10 (Directors' duties, sections 171&ndash;177)</strong> &mdash; the seven statutory duties owner-managers must satisfy, including the duty to promote success and the duty to exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/20/contents\"><strong>Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998</strong> &mdash; the automatic right to charge 8% plus Bank of England base rate, plus fixed compensation, on qualifying late commercial invoices</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2008/30/contents\"><strong>Pensions Act 2008 (auto-enrolment framework)</strong> &mdash; the enrolment and contribution obligations triggered when a small business hires its first eligible jobholder</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1999/3242/contents/made\"><strong>Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999</strong> &mdash; the risk-assessment duty that applies to every employer regardless of headcount, and the written record threshold at five employees</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2018/12/contents\"><strong>UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018</strong> &mdash; the lawful-basis, breach-notification and ICO registration obligations that bind any small business processing customer or staff personal data</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 123 and 214</strong> &mdash; the cash-flow and balance-sheet tests for insolvency, and the wrongful-trading provision that exposes directors to personal liability once the point of no return passes</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/stop-or-avoid-insolvency/": {
    "id": 26888,
    "slug": "stop-or-avoid-insolvency",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is 10pm. The management accounts are open on one screen, an HMRC letter on another, and a half-drafted email to the factoring company in between. You have been round the numbers twice tonight and the answer each time has been \"next week's payroll does not work\". That is the moment most directors first Google \"how to avoid insolvency\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The honest starting point is that \"avoid\" is sometimes the wrong word. Some companies can be turned around before the balance sheet fails; others cannot, and the director has not yet accepted the diagnosis. The job of this page is to set out both routes, the informal ones that keep you out of formal process, and the formal ones that stop insolvency from running to its full conclusion.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below, we cover the turnaround moves available before the tipping point, the statutory tools that freeze the situation, and the point at which continuing to fight is itself a breach of duty. In the CVL and CVA files we handle, the companies that recover share one trait: they called a specialist inside 48 hours of the management accounts telling them payroll would not clear.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-it-means-to-stop-or-avoid-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-it-means-to-stop-or-avoid-insolvency\">What It Means to Stop or Avoid Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A company is insolvent in law when it fails the cash-flow test or the balance-sheet test in section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986. The cash-flow test asks whether you can pay your debts as they fall due; the balance-sheet test asks whether your liabilities exceed your assets, taking account of contingent and prospective liabilities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>\"Avoiding\" insolvency means acting early enough that neither test is failed. \"Stopping\" insolvency means using a formal tool to halt the consequences once the tests are being failed. The two routes look similar from the outside but are legally different.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once your company is insolvent, your duty pivots. Under <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25, you owe your primary duty to the creditor body, not the shareholders. Every decision from that point is reviewable against whether it promoted creditor recovery.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"early-stage-turnaround-options-before-formal-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"early-stage-turnaround-options-before-formal-insolvency\">Early-Stage Turnaround Options Before Formal Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are the levers you can pull before the section 123 tests are failed. They are the tools that actually avoid insolvency rather than stopping it:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Time to Pay (TTP) with HMRC</strong>, an agreed instalment plan for Corporation Tax, VAT, or <a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-paye/\">PAYE arrears</a>. Arranged directly with HMRC's Business Payment Support Service, typically 6 to 12 months, longer on BBL-related VAT.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Invoice finance or factoring</strong>, advances against sales ledger to bridge the gap between billing and payment. Fast, but it costs a percentage per invoice and ties up your debtor book.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Asset sale-and-leaseback</strong>, releasing capital tied up in machinery or vehicles, with the asset financier taking the book value and renting it back to you.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Director capital injection or shareholder loan</strong>, direct cash support, documented as a loan or a subscription for shares.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Equity raise or trade investor</strong>, slower but non-refundable capital, suitable where there is a genuine growth story to sell.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Debt restructuring with lenders</strong>, renegotiating repayment schedules, interest holidays, or amortisation profile on commercial lending.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Pay As You Grow on Bounce Back Loans</strong>, the statutory extensions and payment holidays available on BBLs issued under the 2020 scheme.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Early-stage options share a trade-off. Invoice finance keeps you trading but tethers you to a factoring fee structure that compounds month on month. A director loan helps with this week's payroll but adds to the overdraft on the director's loan account you will have to explain later. Each option buys time, and the quality of what you do with the time decides the outcome.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"negotiating-with-hmrc-time-to-pay-arrangements\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"negotiating-with-hmrc-time-to-pay-arrangements\">Negotiating with HMRC: Time to Pay Arrangements</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC is the single largest creditor in most SME insolvencies, and it is also the most structured to negotiate with. A Time to Pay arrangement, applied for through the Business Payment Support Service, can spread arrears over 6 to 12 months, sometimes longer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC will typically agree TTP where you can show:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Cash-flow projections</strong> for the period covering the instalments, prepared on a reasonable basis.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Evidence the cause of the arrears was transitory</strong>, a lost customer, a bad debt, a supply-chain disruption, rather than structural.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Current-period returns are up to date</strong>, TTP on overdue VAT will not be granted if the current return is itself unfiled.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>You have explored other funding</strong>, bank facility, director loan, equity, and cannot cover the bill from those sources.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Interest continues to run on TTP at the standard HMRC late-payment rate (Bank of England base rate plus four percentage points since 6 April 2025). A second TTP after a first has defaulted is much harder to land. HMRC's position hardens markedly once its Debt Management team has transferred the case to enforcement, so the call you make matters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"formal-tools-that-can-stop-insolvency-short-cva-and-moratorium\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"formal-tools-that-can-stop-insolvency-short-cva-and-moratorium\">Formal Tools That Can Stop Insolvency Short: CVA and Moratorium</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When the informal options are exhausted but the business is still viable, two formal tools can halt the slide into full liquidation or administration:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</strong>, a statutory compromise with creditors under Part 1 of the Insolvency Act 1986. Requires 75% creditor approval by value of those voting. Pays a compromised amount over a fixed period, typically three to five years, after which the remaining debt is written off.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Part A1 Moratorium</strong>, the standalone moratorium introduced by the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020. Gives you a 20-business-day breathing space (extendable) with a freeze on most creditor action while a restructuring plan is developed. Requires a licensed insolvency practitioner as monitor.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVA works where the business model is fundamentally viable but the legacy debt is not affordable. It leaves the directors in place, preserves trading relationships, and avoids the stigma of liquidation. The trade-off is that 25% plus one vote of the creditor body can kill the proposal, and HMRC as a secondary preferential creditor often votes against unless the offer is strong.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The moratorium is newer, less tested, and more complex. It is most useful when you need a narrow window to land a refinancing, sale, or CVA, rather than as a standalone solution. The qualifying conditions exclude companies with recent insolvency history, so it is not a universal tool.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-to-stop-a-winding-up-petition-before-it-becomes-an-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-to-stop-a-winding-up-petition-before-it-becomes-an-order\">How to Stop a Winding-Up Petition Before It Becomes an Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A creditor who is owed more than \u00a3750 and whose debt is undisputed can serve a statutory demand and, after 21 days, petition for your company's winding-up. The petition is advertised in the London Gazette seven business days after presentation, and from that moment section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986 voids any disposition of company property.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The window between presentation and advertisement is the one where you can still act effectively:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Pay the petition debt in full</strong>, if the funds exist. This removes the statutory basis for the petition.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Negotiate a withdrawal</strong>, with settlement terms and costs paid. The petitioning creditor files a notice of withdrawal, subject to the court's consent.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Contest the petition</strong>, where the debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds. The Mann v Goldstein line of authority sets the test.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Propose a CVA or administration</strong> that offers creditors a better outcome than liquidation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once the petition is advertised, the practical window closes. The bank will freeze the account on notice. Suppliers will pull credit. Your factoring facility will typically suspend. Applying for a validation order under section 127 is possible, but it is a rearguard action rather than a fix.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-to-stop-fighting-and-use-formal-process\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-to-stop-fighting-and-use-formal-process\">When to Stop Fighting and Use Formal Process</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The difficult judgement for directors is not \"can I avoid insolvency?\" but \"when does continuing to fight make things worse?\".</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 creates the wrongful trading offence. A director who continues trading after the point at which there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation can be personally liable for the increase in creditor losses from that date.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The markers that usually mean \"stop\":</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Payroll is at risk for consecutive months</strong>, not just a single late payment.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC arrears are increasing</strong>, not decreasing, despite TTP attempts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The order book has fallen below break-even</strong> and there is no realistic prospect of recovery in the next quarter.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Trade credit has been withdrawn</strong> by one or more major suppliers.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A key customer or contract has been lost</strong> without replacement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two of those markers together is enough for us to recommend an urgent meeting with a licensed insolvency practitioner. Three is the point at which continuing to trade without advice becomes the risk itself.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The option that looks like \"giving up\" may actually protect the most jobs and the most creditor value, because a <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">creditors' voluntary liquidation</a> caught early preserves optionality that a winding-up order does not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-stopping-or-avoiding-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-stopping-or-avoiding-insolvency\">Your Next Step on Stopping or Avoiding Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The real problem is rarely the numbers on the screen. It is the speed at which you act after seeing them. Two groups read this page, and your next call is different.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If you are in the early stage and no petition has been issued</strong>, you have options. Call an insolvency practitioner for a diagnostic this week, not next month. A CVA, TTP, or refinancing lands when the business is still viable; a fortnight later, the same conversation is about liquidation.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If a petition has been presented or advertised</strong>, you are no longer in \"avoid\" territory, you are in \"contain\". Do not make selective payments. Do not sign over assets. Apply for a validation order if essential trading payments must continue, and take specialist advice inside 48 hours.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed IPs can review the cash position, diagnose whether a turnaround is realistic, and arrange a CVA, moratorium, or orderly liquidation where appropriate. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-on-stopping-or-avoiding-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-on-stopping-or-avoiding-insolvency\">FAQs on Stopping or Avoiding Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"26888-avoid-insolvency-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I stop a winding-up petition after it has been advertised?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Only by paying the petition debt, negotiating a withdrawal with the petitioning creditor (subject to court consent), or successfully contesting the petition on substantial-dispute grounds. Once advertised, the bank account will typically be frozen and section 127 Insolvency Act 1986 voids any disposition of company property without a validation order.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs a CVA a good way to avoid liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A CVA works where the business is fundamentally viable but the legacy debt cannot be serviced. It requires 75% creditor approval by value, typically runs for three to five years, and leaves directors in place. It is not suitable where the order book has collapsed or the trading model itself is broken. HMRC, as a secondary preferential creditor, often votes against weak CVA proposals.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow long does a Time to Pay arrangement take to agree with HMRC?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Straightforward TTPs under \u00a3100,000 are often agreed within a single phone call to the Business Payment Support Service and a follow-up cash-flow submission. Larger or more complex cases can take several weeks and may require accountant or IP support. Current-period returns must be up to date before HMRC will agree TTP on older arrears.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eAt what point does continuing to trade become wrongful trading?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Section 214 Insolvency Act 1986 makes you liable for the increase in creditor losses from the point at which there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation. That is a judgement call, not a bright-line test. Taking contemporaneous licensed insolvency advice, and documenting the alternatives considered, is the strongest defence if a liquidator reviews the decision later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I use a Part A1 moratorium without a CVA?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, but rarely sensibly. The moratorium is most useful as a 20-business-day breathing space in which to land a refinancing, sale, CVA, or scheme of arrangement. Used without a clear plan for what happens when the moratorium ends, it simply delays the formal process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill taking early insolvency advice trigger formal process?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. A diagnostic conversation with a licensed IP is confidential and non-obligatory. Many of the companies we see after a first conversation go on to trade out through informal turnaround rather than formal process. Early advice expands your options; late advice narrows them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by our editorial team and reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners. We reflect UK insolvency law as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<p>Statutory references are drawn from the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 123, 127, 214, Part 1 on CVAs, Schedule B1), the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 (Part A1 Moratorium), and the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where a CVA, moratorium, administration, or CVL is the right outcome, we act as the licensed officeholder under separate engagement. Our 0800 number is a free confidential consultation, not a transaction.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; section 123 cash-flow and balance-sheet tests, section 127 void dispositions, section 214 wrongful trading, Part 1 CVA framework, and Schedule B1 administration regime</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/12/contents\"><strong>Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020</strong> &mdash; Part A1 standalone moratorium giving viable companies a 20-business-day payment holiday from most creditor action</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents\"><strong>Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016</strong> &mdash; procedural rules for CVA proposals, moratorium filings, and administration appointments referenced throughout this guide</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/hm-revenue-customs/contact/business-payment-support-service\"><strong>HMRC Business Payment Support Service</strong> &mdash; route for negotiating Time to Pay instalment arrangements on Corporation Tax, VAT, and PAYE arrears before petition stage</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-business-rates/": {
    "id": 26347,
    "slug": "cant-pay-business-rates",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Business rates sit in a category most directors underestimate: a priority debt collected by a local authority with procedural powers that move faster than any bank. The sequence from missed instalment to bailiff visit in England and Wales is measured in weeks, and the automatic surcharges in Scotland add ten per cent to the bill the moment a summary warrant is granted.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your company has missed a business rates instalment, you need to understand the recovery sequence in your jurisdiction and the reliefs your business may not be claiming.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The exact bailiff fee structure can turn a \u00a31,000 rates bill into a \u00a31,400 bill in a fortnight. We also cover the director-liability picture when the underlying position tips into insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"who-is-liable-for-unpaid-business-rates\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"who-is-liable-for-unpaid-business-rates\">Who Is Liable for Unpaid Business Rates</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Liability for non-domestic rates falls on the <strong>occupier</strong> of the property, the ratepayer of record, regardless of the nature of the occupation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your company is occupying a property, formal lease or informal arrangement, you are the ratepayer and you are on the hook((Trusted Source - GOV.UK - <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/introduction-to-business-rates\">Introduction to business rates</a>)).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three specific points catch directors out, and we see all three regularly:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Unoccupied properties</strong> shift liability to the owner or leaseholder. Empty property relief applies for limited periods (three months non-industrial, six months industrial), but the clock runs on calendar basis regardless of active marketing.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Partnership occupations</strong> carry joint and several liability, any one partner can be pursued for the full debt, regardless of private agreements between partners.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Multiple UK regions</strong>: Scotland's non-domestic rates are governed by separate Scottish legislation with different surcharge and enforcement rules; Northern Ireland centralises enforcement through Land &amp; Property Services. If your business operates across jurisdictions, you need to track the differences rather than assume English rules apply throughout.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"why-business-rates-arrears-escalate-faster-than-most-commercial-debt\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"why-business-rates-arrears-escalate-faster-than-most-commercial-debt\">Why Business Rates Arrears Escalate Faster Than Most Commercial Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Local authorities operate on a timetable that is almost unique in UK debt recovery: fast, procedural, and largely automatic. In our experience, directors are routinely surprised by how quickly the sequence moves. The formal recovery sequence in England and Wales:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Reminder notice</strong> after the first missed instalment, giving 7 days to settle.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Final notice</strong> demanding the full annual balance if the reminder is ignored.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Liability order</strong> from the Magistrates' Court, typically within 2\u20134 weeks of the final notice. Court costs (varying by local authority) are added to the debt.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Enforcement agents</strong> (bailiffs) instructed to recover. Compliance fee of \u00a375 on instruction, enforcement fee of \u00a3235 plus 7.5% of debt over \u00a31,500 on first visit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Goods taken into control</strong>, with possible removal and sale to settle the debt, plus sale fees.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A \u00a31,000 rates bill that you ignore can escalate to over \u00a31,400 within a month once court costs and compliance fees are added. The arithmetic is entirely mechanical, and it works against you fast.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In Scotland, the sequence is sharper: a summary warrant is granted without a court hearing and triggers an automatic <strong>10% statutory surcharge</strong> on the outstanding balance the moment it is issued. Your \u00a350,000 rates bill acquires \u00a35,000 of surcharge instantly. Sheriff Officers then enforce through bank arrestment (freezing funds), attachment of goods, or earnings arrestment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northern Ireland routes enforcement through Land &amp; Property Services and, on escalation, the Enforcement of Judgments Office, with powers to seize goods and deduct from income.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Continued non-payment at scale can trigger a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> from the local authority. If your rates debt exceeds \u00a3750 and remains unpaid for 21 days after a statutory demand, you meet that threshold.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"business-rates-reliefs-most-distressed-companies-are-not-claiming\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"business-rates-reliefs-most-distressed-companies-are-not-claiming\">Business Rates Reliefs Most Distressed Companies Are Not Claiming</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before the recovery machinery is in motion, your fastest wins almost always come from checking relief entitlement. The schemes vary by jurisdiction and change annually. Getting the current position right usually requires a direct conversation with the local authority rather than online summaries.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small Business Rate Relief</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In England: up to <strong>100% relief</strong> for properties with a Rateable Value of \u00a312,000 or less; tapering relief for RVs between \u00a312,001 and \u00a315,000. In Scotland: the Small Business Bonus Scheme provides similar relief with different thresholds((Trusted Source - GOV.UK - <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/apply-for-business-rate-relief/small-business-rate-relief\">Small Business Rate Relief</a>)).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see many small companies paying full rates when they qualify for full relief. Check your rateable value before assuming your bill is correct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Retail, Hospitality, and Leisure Relief</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Originally introduced in the COVID period and extended in stages. For 2024/25: <strong>75% relief</strong> for eligible businesses, capped at \u00a3110,000 per business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For 2025/26: <strong>40% relief</strong>, same \u00a3110,000 per-business cash cap. If your business qualifies, you should apply now((Trusted Source - GOV.UK - <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/business-rates-relief-202425-retail-hospitality-and-leisure-scheme\">Business rates relief: 2024/25 Retail, Hospitality and Leisure scheme</a>)).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The per-business cash cap catches multi-site operators. If your company runs six qualifying retail properties, you hit the \u00a3110,000 ceiling quickly, producing a material gap between the headline relief percentage and your actual benefit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Empty Property Relief</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In England, unoccupied non-industrial property qualifies for 3 months of relief; industrial property for 6 months. Anti-avoidance rules tightened in recent years have closed the intermittent-occupation loophole that your business cannot now use to reset the relief clock((Trusted Source - GOV.UK - <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/business-rates-relief/empty-property-relief\">Empty property relief</a>)).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Hardship Relief</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Discretionary, granted by local authorities where the ratepayer would otherwise suffer hardship and granting the relief is in the interests of local council tax payers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is not easily granted. Your application needs audited accounts, a recovery plan, and evidence of wider community or employment impact. It is available and under-requested((Trusted Source - GOV.UK - <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/apply-for-business-rate-relief/hardship-relief\">Hardship relief</a>)).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your relief applications should be made early and in writing, with the specific ground cited. Councils do not retroactively apply reliefs you did not claim.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"immediate-steps-when-a-company-cannot-pay-business-rates\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"immediate-steps-when-a-company-cannot-pay-business-rates\">Immediate Steps When a Company Cannot Pay Business Rates</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The sequence that stops the meter running, in priority order:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Contact the local authority before the reminder notice becomes a final notice.</strong> Local authorities almost always prefer a payment plan over the cost of formal recovery. The earlier the conversation, the better the terms.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Verify the bill is correct.</strong> Rateable Value appeals, missed reliefs, and billing errors are surprisingly common. An accepted appeal can wipe out arrears entirely.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Apply for any missed reliefs.</strong> Small Business Rate Relief, hospitality relief, empty property relief, hardship relief. Each requires a formal application.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Propose a written payment plan.</strong> A credible plan backed by cash flow evidence, with the first payment offered upfront, is routinely accepted. Plans are typically 6\u201312 months for non-domestic arrears.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Seek professional insolvency advice</strong> if the rates arrears sit alongside other creditor pressure. In our experience, rates arrears in isolation are usually manageable. Rates plus VAT plus supplier debt is a different conversation entirely, and we would want to assess the full picture before recommending a path.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"enforcement-costs-that-add-to-business-rates-debt\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"enforcement-costs-that-add-to-business-rates-debt\">Enforcement Costs That Add to Business Rates Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The enforcement fee structure in England and Wales is set in statute and is worth knowing precisely, because it changes the economics of your early engagement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Compliance stage</strong>, \u00a375 on instruction of enforcement agents.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Enforcement stage</strong>, \u00a3235 plus 7.5% of any debt over \u00a31,500, on first visit to the premises.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Sale stage</strong>, \u00a3110 plus 7.5% of debt over \u00a31,500, on removal and sale.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A \u00a35,000 rates debt, fully enforced through to sale, attracts approximately \u00a3945 of statutory fees plus the underlying court costs. That is a ~19% uplift on the original debt, paid to the enforcement company rather than the creditor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical implication is clear: a payment plan agreed before enforcement is cheaper by the entire enforcement fee stack.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you negotiate before the instruction, you save the compliance and enforcement fees entirely. If you negotiate after the first visit, you have already absorbed \u00a3310.((Trusted Source - LEGISLATION.GOV.UK - <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1989/1058/contents/made\">National Non-Domestic Rates (Administration and Enforcement) Regulations 1989</a>))</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-business-rates-arrears-signal-cash-flow-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-business-rates-arrears-signal-cash-flow-insolvency\">When Business Rates Arrears Signal Cash-Flow Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rates are classified as a priority overhead for insolvency purposes. If your company has persistent rates arrears alongside <a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-vat/\">unpaid VAT</a> and PAYE, our licensed IPs treat that as one of the three clearest external indicators of cash-flow insolvency under section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once the cash-flow test is failed, your duties as director under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006 pivot toward creditors as a whole. Continued trading past the point where insolvent liquidation is unavoidable is wrongful trading under section 214, with personal-contribution consequences for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the rates arrears sit alongside broader distress, formal options become relevant. Our licensed IPs can advise on which route fits your position:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Company Voluntary Arrangement, agreement to pay historic arrears over 3\u20135 years while continuing to trade. Requires 75% creditor approval by value.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Administration, a statutory moratorium against creditor action while a rescue or sale is pursued.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>, orderly wind-down where rescue is not viable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-unpaid-business-rates\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-unpaid-business-rates\">Your Next Step on Unpaid Business Rates</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a one-off rates shock in an otherwise solvent business, your right conversation is with the local authority, armed with a relief application and a payment plan. Most are accepted if they are credible and offered before the enforcement instruction.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where rates arrears are part of a broader solvency issue, your next step is an hour with a licensed Insolvency Practitioner.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We can work out whether your business is viable if the historic debt is addressed through CVA, whether administration produces a better outcome, or whether a CVL is the cleaner route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed IPs and business rescue specialists can assess your position and explain the options on a confidential call. Free advice on 0800 074 6757.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"business-rates-arrears-faqs\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"business-rates-arrears-faqs\">Business Rates Arrears FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"26347-rates-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I negotiate a payment plan for unpaid business rates?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. Local authorities routinely accept payment plans, particularly where the ratepayer engages before the liability order stage. Plans of 6 to 12 months are standard. The key is proposing in writing, with cash flow evidence, and offering an immediate first payment to show commitment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow much will bailiff fees add to my business rates debt?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>In England and Wales, \u00a375 at the compliance stage, \u00a3235 plus 7.5% of debt over \u00a31,500 on first enforcement visit, and a further \u00a3110 plus 7.5% on sale. A \u00a35,000 debt fully enforced attracts roughly \u00a3945 of statutory fees. In Scotland, the statutory 10% surcharge on summary warrant applies instead, plus Sheriff Officer fees.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a council pursue me personally for unpaid business rates?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>For a limited company ratepayer, no, the rates are a company debt. Directors become personally exposed only if a personal guarantee was given (uncommon on rates), or through insolvency misconduct routes (wrongful trading, misfeasance). Partnerships and sole traders are different: the ratepayer is personally liable from the outset.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan unpaid business rates lead to a winding-up petition?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, where the debt exceeds \u00a3750 and is unpaid 21 days after a statutory demand. Local authorities do issue winding-up petitions on rates debts, though it is usually the escalation path after standard enforcement has not produced payment. The petition is a serious step, it freezes company bank accounts on advertisement, so early engagement is the much better alternative.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat reliefs should a distressed business check for first?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Small Business Rate Relief is the headline check (100% for RV \u00a312,000 or less, tapering to \u00a315,000). Then Retail, Hospitality and Leisure Relief if the sector qualifies (40% for 2025/26, capped at \u00a3110,000 per business). Empty Property Relief if a property is unoccupied. Hardship Relief is the discretionary fallback, requiring formal application with supporting evidence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I appeal the Rateable Value?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, through the Valuation Office Agency's Check-Challenge-Appeal process in England. An accepted reduction is backdated to the effective date, which can wipe out substantial arrears. The process is slow (often 12\u201318 months to resolution), but it is worth initiating early where the RV is clearly out of line with comparable properties.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"methodology-amp-disclosure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"methodology-amp-disclosure\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team, reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners, and reflects UK business rates law across England, Scotland, and Northern Ireland as at the last-reviewed date. Fee figures are taken from the Taking Control of Goods (Fees) Regulations 2014. Relief percentages reflect 2024/25 and 2025/26 schemes; check current gov.uk guidance for subsequent years.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. We can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner for a recommended CVA, Administration, or CVL under separate engagement. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1988/41/contents\"><strong>Local Government Finance Act 1988</strong> &mdash; primary statute setting the non-domestic rating framework, the occupier-liability rule, and billing authority powers cited throughout this guide</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1989/1058/contents/made\"><strong>Non-Domestic Rating (Collection and Enforcement) Regulations 1989</strong> &mdash; procedural rules for reminders, summonses, and liability orders that drive the rapid English recovery sequence described above</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2014/1/contents/made\"><strong>Taking Control of Goods (Fees) Regulations 2014</strong> &mdash; fixed enforcement-agent fee scale (compliance, enforcement, sale) that converts a modest arrears figure into the 40 per cent uplift referenced in the bailiff-cost section</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/apply-for-business-rate-relief\"><strong>GOV.UK: Business rates relief schemes</strong> &mdash; official guidance on small business, retail/hospitality/leisure, charitable, hardship, and empty property reliefs distressed occupiers commonly fail to claim</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 123 and 214</strong> &mdash; the cash-flow insolvency test and the wrongful trading exposure directors face once rates arrears signal the company cannot pay its debts as they fall due</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/advice/misfeasance/": {
    "id": 26298,
    "slug": "misfeasance",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Misfeasance is a breach of your duties as a director. Under section 212 of the Insolvency Act 1986, the liquidator can bring a misfeasance claim against you to recover any loss the company suffered as a result of that breach. It is a personal liability, and it survives the company's dissolution.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that most directors have never heard the word \"misfeasance\" until the liquidator mentions it. It is not a separate offence. It is the enforcement mechanism for breaches of the seven general duties you owe as a director under the Companies Act 2006.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you breached any of those duties (failed to exercise reasonable care, prioritised your own interests, declared conflicted dividends, approved transactions that were not in the company's interests) and the company suffered a loss, section 212 lets the liquidator pursue you for that loss.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-what-is-a-misfeasance-claim\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-what-is-a-misfeasance-claim\">Quick Answer: What Is a Misfeasance Claim?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A misfeasance claim under section 212 allows the liquidator (or any creditor or contributory with the court's permission) to apply to the court for an order that a director who has breached their duties must: (1) repay or restore money or property to the company, (2) account for any personal profit made from the breach, or (3) contribute to the company's assets as compensation for the loss caused.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress that misfeasance is a civil claim, not a criminal charge. You pay money. You do not go to prison.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>But the amounts can be substantial. We have seen misfeasance claims for six-figure sums where a director authorised transactions that benefited themselves at the company's expense. The claim is separate from and in addition to any wrongful trading or disqualification proceedings.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"common-grounds-for-misfeasance-claims-against-directors\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"common-grounds-for-misfeasance-claims-against-directors\">Common Grounds for Misfeasance Claims Against Directors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see misfeasance claims brought on these grounds most frequently:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Unlawful dividends.</strong> Dividends declared when the company did not have sufficient distributable reserves under section 830 of the Companies Act 2006. The liquidator recovers the dividend as money that should never have been paid out.</li>\n<li><strong>Excessive director remuneration.</strong> Paying yourself a salary or bonus that was disproportionate to the company's financial position, particularly in the period approaching insolvency. We see directors who doubled their salary in the final year of trading, knowing the company was struggling.</li>\n<li><strong>Undeclared conflicts of interest.</strong> Transactions where the director had a personal interest that was not properly declared and authorised. The liquidator recovers any profit the director made from the conflicted transaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Transactions at undervalue with connected parties.</strong> Selling company assets to yourself, family members, or associated companies below market value. The liquidator recovers the difference between the sale price and the market value.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure to exercise reasonable care and skill.</strong> Approving a transaction without proper due diligence, failing to obtain professional advice when it was needed, or ignoring warning signs that a competent director would have acted on.</li>\n<li><strong>Overdrawn director's loan accounts.</strong> Where the withdrawals breached the Companies Act loan provisions or were not properly authorised.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that misfeasance claims are often brought alongside wrongful trading claims. The wrongful trading claim covers the period of continued trading. The misfeasance claim covers specific transactions within that period. Together, they can produce a combined personal liability significantly larger than either claim alone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"misfeasance-vs-wrongful-trading-the-director-s-difference\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"misfeasance-vs-wrongful-trading-the-director-s-difference\">Misfeasance vs Wrongful Trading: The Director's Difference</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Both are civil claims under the Insolvency Act. Both create personal liability. But they cover different things:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Misfeasance (section 212):</strong> Covers specific breaches of duty, particular transactions, decisions, or failures. The claim recovers the loss caused by the specific breach.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading (section 214):</strong> Covers continued trading beyond the point where insolvent liquidation was unavoidable. The claim recovers the increase in the company's net deficiency during the period of wrongful trading.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director can face both. A misfeasance claim for an unlawful dividend paid in March, AND a wrongful trading claim for continued trading from January to June. The liabilities are cumulative.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"who-can-bring-a-misfeasance-claim-against-a-director\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"who-can-bring-a-misfeasance-claim-against-a-director\">Who Can Bring a Misfeasance Claim Against a Director?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 212 allows claims to be brought by: the official receiver, the liquidator, any creditor of the company, or any contributory (shareholder). In practice, the liquidator brings most misfeasance claims because they have access to the company's records and the duty to investigate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see creditor-brought misfeasance claims occasionally, particularly where the liquidator has decided not to pursue a claim (perhaps because the estate cannot fund the litigation) and a large creditor decides to pursue it independently with the court's permission.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"time-limits-for-misfeasance-claims\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"time-limits-for-misfeasance-claims\">Time Limits for Misfeasance Claims</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The limitation period for misfeasance claims is 6 years from the date of the liquidator's appointment. This means a breach that occurred 5 years before liquidation can still be claimed if the claim is brought within 6 years of the liquidator being appointed. The total window can be 11 years from the date of the original breach.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We tell directors: do not assume old transactions are safe. The liquidator reviews the full trading history and can pursue claims going back years. A conflicted transaction from 2019 discovered during a 2026 liquidation is still within the limitation period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-directors-can-defend-against-a-misfeasance-claim\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-directors-can-defend-against-a-misfeasance-claim\">How Directors Can Defend Against a Misfeasance Claim</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Show the decision was reasonable at the time.</strong> Misfeasance is assessed against the information available when the decision was made, not with hindsight. If you took professional advice and acted on it, that is a strong defence.</li>\n<li><strong>Show proper authorisation.</strong> If the transaction was properly declared, authorised by the board, and documented in the minutes, the procedural compliance supports your position.</li>\n<li><strong>Show no loss.</strong> If the company did not suffer a loss from the alleged breach, there is nothing to recover. The burden is on the claimant to prove loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 1157 relief.</strong> The court can relieve a director from liability if they acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused. This is a discretionary power, not a guarantee, but it provides a safety valve for directors who made honest mistakes.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise directors facing a misfeasance claim to instruct a solicitor who specialises in insolvency litigation. Misfeasance claims are fact-intensive and the defence depends on the specific circumstances. A specialist can assess the strength of the claim and advise on whether to defend, negotiate, or settle.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-directors-can-prevent-misfeasance-claims\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-directors-can-prevent-misfeasance-claims\">How Directors Can Prevent Misfeasance Claims</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Know your duties.</strong> The seven general duties under the Companies Act are the standard you are measured against.</li>\n<li><strong>Document every significant decision.</strong> Board minutes that record what was decided, what information was considered, and what advice was taken.</li>\n<li><strong>Declare conflicts.</strong> Every interest in every transaction, formally, in the minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not pay dividends without confirming distributable reserves.</strong> Get your accountant to confirm the position before declaring.</li>\n<li><strong>Seek professional advice before making unusual decisions.</strong> A valuation before selling an asset. Legal advice before entering a connected-party transaction. Insolvency advice before the company enters the twilight zone.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt connects directors with licensed insolvency practitioners who can assess your misfeasance exposure. A confidential consultation will identify whether any past decisions create risk and what you can do about it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-26298-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about your misfeasance exposure\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Misfeasance and Director Liability</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-26298\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26298-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-26298\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26298\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is misfeasance a criminal offence?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-26298\">\n<p>No. Misfeasance is a civil claim under section 212 of the Insolvency Act. You can be ordered to pay money (repay, restore, or compensate). You cannot be imprisoned for misfeasance. Criminal liability requires fraud or deliberate concealment under separate provisions.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26298-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-26298\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26298\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How much can a misfeasance claim cost me?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-26298\">\n<p>The claim recovers the loss caused by the specific breach. Unlawful dividends = the dividend amount. Transactions at undervalue = the difference between sale price and market value. Excessive remuneration = the excess amount. Claims can range from a few thousand pounds to six figures depending on the transaction.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26298-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-26298\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26298\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I be disqualified for misfeasance?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-26298\">\n<p>Yes. The conduct underlying a misfeasance claim can also form the basis of a disqualification case. The misfeasance claim recovers the financial loss. The disqualification prevents you from being a director. Both can proceed simultaneously.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26298-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-26298\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26298\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How far back can a misfeasance claim go?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-26298\">\n<p>The limitation period is 6 years from the liquidator's appointment. Since the breach may have occurred years before liquidation, the total window from breach to claim can be up to 11 years. Old transactions are not necessarily safe.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-26298\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on the Insolvency Act 1986 (section 212, misfeasance), the Companies Act 2006 (sections 170 to 177 directors' duties; section 830 distributions; section 1157 relief from liability), and practical experience from misfeasance cases handled by licensed practitioners in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. This does not influence our editorial content or recommendations.</p>\n<p>References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026. Last reviewed by Chris Andersen, Licensed Insolvency Practitioner (IPA regulated), April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 212 and 214</strong> &mdash; the statutory route for liquidators to recover losses from directors who breached duty (s212 misfeasance) or kept trading when insolvent (s214 wrongful trading)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/part/10/chapter/2\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, sections 170 to 177</strong> &mdash; the seven general duties whose breach is enforced through a s212 misfeasance claim, including reasonable care, conflicts, and acting within powers</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/830\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 830</strong> &mdash; the distributable-profits rule used to identify unlawful dividends, a frequent ground for misfeasance claims after liquidation</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/1157\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 1157</strong> &mdash; the court's discretion to grant a director relief from misfeasance liability where they acted honestly and reasonably</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, sections 6 to 8</strong> &mdash; the disqualification regime that often runs in parallel with a misfeasance claim following the liquidator's conduct report</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; the agency that receives the liquidator's D-report and decides whether to bring disqualification proceedings against directors</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; recognised regulator whose IP search lets directors verify the liquidator investigating their conduct</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/cease-trading/": {
    "id": 26279,
    "slug": "cease-trading",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director closed the office on a Friday in late October, paid the last two staff their notice money in cash, and told us he had \"ceased trading.\" By Wednesday morning he was opening a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> from HMRC.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company had not been dissolved. It had not been liquidated. It had simply stopped operating, and in the gap between stopping and closing, the debts kept moving and so did the legal exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That gap is where most directors get into trouble. Ceasing to trade is not a closure. It is a holding pattern, and the longer you sit in it without a chosen exit route, the more director liability you accumulate.</p>\n\n<p>We have written this guide for the director sitting in that gap right now, so we will say plainly which moves protect you and which moves quietly create exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ceased Trading at a Glance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Quick Answer: What \"Ceased Trading\" Actually Means</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A company has ceased trading when it stops carrying on business activity, takes no further orders, issues no further invoices, and no longer transacts in goods or services. The company itself still exists at Companies House.</p>\n\n<p>Its debts still exist. Filing duties still exist. Director duties still exist. You have closed the shop, not the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When Ceasing to Trade Is Realistic (and when it isn't)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ceasing to trade is realistic when the company has no creditor pressure, no overdue tax, and no pending litigation, and you intend to either close it formally within months or hold it dormant for a defined commercial reason.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is not realistic when HMRC is chasing, when suppliers are unpaid, or when you need the cessation to be the answer rather than the prelude. In those cases the cessation is the start of a process, not the end of one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Main Director Risk After a Company Ceases Trading</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The dominant risk is that the clock on <a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-wrongful-trading/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">wrongful trading</a> exposure under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 does not stop simply because you stopped invoicing.</p>\n\n<p>If the company was already insolvent on the day you ceased, every payment you authorised in the wind-down can be tested later by a liquidator. Preferences and undervalues become reviewable transactions under sections 238 to 239.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to Do Next After Stopping Trade</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Decide the exit route within weeks, not months.</p>\n\n<p>The four real choices are strike-off (only if solvent and quiet), <a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Members' Voluntary Liquidation</a> (solvent with assets to extract), <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a> (insolvent), or <a href=\"/company-administration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">company administration</a> (rescue or controlled wind-down with a moratorium).</p>\n\n<p>Drift is not a fifth option, although directors often try to make it one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Does Ceased Trading Mean in UK Law?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK statute does not contain a single neat definition of \"ceased trading.\" HMRC treats the cessation date as the day commercial activity stops for corporation tax purposes, which triggers final accounts and a final corporation tax return.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Companies House treats trading status separately from legal existence: the company stays on the register until you formally close it. So in our practice we treat \"ceased trading\" as a factual position, not a legal status.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ceased Trading vs Dormant vs Dissolved</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The three states are routinely confused, and the confusion is expensive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Ceased trading:</strong> the company has stopped operating but still exists, still files, still owes whatever it owed yesterday.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Dormant:</strong> a Companies House status where there have been no significant accounting transactions in the period. Dormancy is a filing position, not a closure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Dissolved:</strong> the company has been removed from the Companies House register and has ceased to legally exist. Only dissolution ends the company.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You can be ceased and dormant at the same time. You cannot be dissolved and dormant. The trap is treating dormancy as the finish line when creditors, HMRC, or a former supplier can still revive the matter while the company sits on the register.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Difference Between Cease Trading and Strike-Off</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Strike-off under section 1003 of the Companies Act 2006 is the formal removal of a solvent, quiet company from the register, applied for on Form DS01.</p>\n\n<p>Ceasing to trade is the prerequisite condition, because the rules require no trading, no name change, and no disposal of trading stock for property in the previous three months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Strike-off is the doorway out. Ceasing trading is standing in front of the doorway. They are not the same act, and creditors can object to strike-off if debts remain.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When Ceasing to Trade May Not Be Suitable</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company owes HMRC, has unpaid suppliers chasing payment, has an outstanding bounce back loan, or is facing a statutory demand, ceasing trade as a self-help measure tends to make matters worse.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC and bank creditors will object to strike-off, the company stays on the register accruing late-filing penalties, and director conduct in the wind-down period becomes the subject of an investigation later.</p>\n\n<p>In those cases the right move is a controlled formal procedure, not a quiet exit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Assess Whether the Company Should Cease Trading</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before you stop, work the numbers honestly. The decision determines which exit routes remain open and which doors you have already closed without realising it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cashflow and Solvency Test Before Stopping</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The two statutory tests in section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986 matter here: the cashflow test (can the company pay its debts as they fall due?) and the balance sheet test (do liabilities, including contingent and prospective ones, exceed assets?).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If either is failing on the day you stop, you are ceasing while insolvent and your post-cessation conduct will be judged accordingly. Run our <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">insolvency test</a> first; the answer changes which procedure you can choose.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Creditor Position and Outstanding Liabilities</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>List every creditor in three columns: who they are, what they are owed, and what status they hold. Secured lenders, HMRC as preferential creditor for PAYE and VAT, and unsecured trade suppliers each behave differently when a company stops.</p>\n\n<p>A clean list is the first thing any insolvency practitioner will ask for, and a missing major creditor is the first thing that derails a strike-off.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Director Personal Exposure Audit</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Pull every personal guarantee, every overdrawn director's loan account, every bounce back loan agreement, and every personal credit facility taken to support the business. These follow you out of the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>They also drive the choice of exit: a director with a large overdrawn loan account ceasing trade and seeking strike-off is exactly the pattern <a href=\"/insolvency/insolvent-company-investigations/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">insolvent company investigations</a> are designed to catch.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Options Are Available Once the Company Has Ceased Trading?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once trading has stopped, the company sits in a holding pattern until you select a route out.</p>\n\n<p>The viable choices fall into three categories: an informal wind-down toward strike-off, a rescue or restructuring procedure, or a formal insolvency procedure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Informal Wind-Down or Strike-Off</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company is solvent, has paid all debts, has no live disputes, and has not traded for three months, voluntary strike-off via DS01 is cheap and quick.</p>\n\n<p>Filing fee is modest, processing takes around two months on the gazette, and the company is removed from the register.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost of getting it wrong is much higher than the cost of getting it right: HMRC objections and creditor objections both restore the company and the clock on director conduct restarts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rescue or Restructuring Procedure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the business has underlying value but has stopped because cash ran out, administration or a Company Voluntary Arrangement may still be available.</p>\n\n<p>Administration in particular brings a statutory moratorium that pauses creditor action, which is sometimes the only thing that stops a winding-up petition during the post-cessation window.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rescue is harder once you have already laid off staff and let trading contracts lapse, so timing matters. We routinely see directors arrive too late for rescue because the cessation itself was treated as the answer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Formal Insolvency Procedure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the company is insolvent and there is no realistic rescue, the orderly route is <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: directors propose, shareholders pass a 75 per cent special resolution, creditors approve the liquidator.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The alternative is compulsory winding-up under sections 122 and 124 of the Insolvency Act 1986, usually triggered by a creditor petition. CVL keeps the choice of liquidator with the directors. Compulsory does not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>When it fits</th><th>What it surrenders</th><th>Director risk</th><th>Related guide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Strike-off (DS01)</strong></td><td>Solvent, quiet, three months no trading, no creditor pressure</td><td>Speed and informality kept; no IP oversight</td><td>Objections from HMRC or creditors restore the company; conduct still reviewable</td><td><a href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/dissolving-a-company-with-bounce-back-loan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Dissolving with a bounce back loan</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dormant</strong></td><td>Pause without closure for a defined commercial reason</td><td>Annual filings continue; no extraction</td><td>Liabilities accumulate; status reverts on any transaction</td><td>Companies House dormancy guidance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MVL</strong></td><td>Solvent; assets to extract; tax-efficient closure</td><td>Costs of an IP; declaration of solvency under oath</td><td>Personal liability if declaration is wrong</td><td><a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">MVL guide</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>CVL</strong></td><td>Insolvent; rescue not viable; orderly closure preferred</td><td>Control of the liquidator's investigation</td><td>Conduct reviewed; PG and overdrawn DLAs surface</td><td><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">CVL guide</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Administration</strong></td><td>Underlying value; need moratorium; rescue plausible</td><td>Day-to-day control passes to administrator</td><td>Lower than compulsory if entered early; harder once trade has stopped</td><td><a href=\"/company-administration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Administration guide</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Risks Should Directors Watch After the Company Has Ceased Trading?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk profile changes the day you stop, and not in the way most directors expect. Ceasing to trade does not freeze director duties; it sharpens them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every payment you authorise, every asset you move, every creditor you favour during the wind-down is a transaction a future liquidator can examine.</p>\n\n<p>The table below sets out the risks we see most often when post-cessation conduct is tested in CVL or compulsory liquidation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Statutory hook</th><th>What triggers it</th><th>Director consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Wrongful trading post-cessation</strong></td><td>Insolvency Act 1986 s.214</td><td>Continuing to incur liabilities (lease, payroll, supplier orders) after insolvency was unavoidable</td><td>Personal contribution order to the company's assets</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Preferential payments during wind-down</strong></td><td>Insolvency Act 1986 s.239</td><td>Paying connected creditors, family loans, or favoured suppliers ahead of HMRC</td><td>Liquidator can claw the payment back; conduct reportable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transaction at undervalue / asset removal</strong></td><td>Insolvency Act 1986 s.238</td><td>Selling stock, vehicles or equipment to connected parties below market value</td><td>Transaction reversed; possible disqualification</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Director disqualification</strong></td><td>CDDA 1986 s.6</td><td>Pattern of misconduct or unfitness identified in liquidator's D-report</td><td>2 to 15 year ban from acting as a director</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal liability notice (HMRC)</strong></td><td>Finance Act provisions</td><td>Unpaid PAYE / NIC where HMRC attributes director responsibility</td><td>Tax debt transferred to the director personally</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal guarantee enforcement</strong></td><td>Contract law</td><td>Lender, landlord or supplier calling in a signed PG once company defaults</td><td>Personal demand; possible bankruptcy if unpaid</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pattern we see is unfair in one specific way: directors who cease and pay everyone equally are usually fine, while directors who cease and pay the bank, the landlord, or themselves first are exposed.</p>\n\n<p>The instinct to \"tidy up\" is the instinct that creates <a href=\"/insolvency/preferential-non-preferential-creditors/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">preferences between creditors</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Directors Should Do Immediately After the Company Has Ceased Trading</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Notify HMRC and Companies House Within Reasonable Time</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tell HMRC the cessation date in writing through the corporation tax account and de-register for VAT and PAYE if the cessation is permanent.</p>\n\n<p>Companies House does not require a separate \"ceased trading\" notice, but the next confirmation statement and accounts must reflect the position.</p>\n\n<p>Late notification is not a hanging offence on its own, but it adds to a pattern when conduct is reviewed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Preserve Books and Records for Liquidator Access</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Keep bank statements, sales ledgers, purchase ledgers, payroll records, board minutes, and director loan account workings intact. Six years is the statutory minimum, longer if a liquidation is on the horizon.</p>\n\n<p>If a CVL or compulsory winding-up follows, the liquidator's first request is the books, and \"we lost them in the wind-down\" is heard as evasion, not a misfortune.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Stop All Selective and Connected-Party Payments</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Pay nobody outside the ordinary run of unavoidable closing costs until you have advice.</p>\n\n<p>That includes paying yourself a final salary, settling a director's loan with a relative, repaying a friend who lent the company money, or clearing a supplier you happen to know personally.</p>\n\n<p>These are exactly the transactions sections 238 and 239 of the Insolvency Act 1986 are written to capture.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Take Written IP Advice Before Any Asset Sale</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company has saleable assets (vehicles, plant, stock, intellectual property) and there is any chance the company is insolvent, the sale must go through a process that can be defended later: independent valuation, marketing, arm's length buyer, paper trail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have seen too many post-cessation asset transfers reversed by a liquidator because the only valuation was the director's own. A short, written instruction to one of our licensed insolvency practitioners is cheaper than the unwind.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Common Mistakes Directors Make After Ceasing to Trade</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Treating Cessation as Closure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The single most expensive mistake. Directors stop trading, change their email signature, take a job elsewhere, and assume the company has gone. It has not.</p>\n\n<p>A creditor with a 12-year limitation period on a deed, an HMRC review of the final return, or a Companies House late-filing penalty will all find the company sitting exactly where it was left.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Continuing to Use the Company Bank Account for Personal Expenses</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once trading has stopped, the company bank account is not a personal float. Drawing personal sums after cessation inflates the director's loan account, which is then a debt owed back to the company that a liquidator will pursue.</p>\n\n<p>The friction here is that small sums feel harmless; in aggregate they look exactly like asset stripping.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Letting the Strike-Off Application Drift</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A DS01 with HMRC owed corporation tax will be objected to. The application is suspended, the company stays alive, late-filing penalties accrue, and the director is no further forward.</p>\n\n<p>Strike-off is not a way of avoiding HMRC; it is a way of formalising the closure of a company that has already settled with HMRC.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Cease Trading Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Insolvency test for directors</a>: work out whether the company was insolvent on the day you stopped.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: the orderly closure route when the company is insolvent.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/company-administration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Company administration</a>: when the trading stop is recoverable and a moratorium is needed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/bounce-back-loan-support-hub/dissolving-a-company-with-bounce-back-loan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Dissolving a company with a bounce back loan</a>: the specific trap for directors who took BBL funding.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-wrongful-trading/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Wrongful trading</a>: the post-cessation conduct test that catches most directors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>If your company has stopped trading and you have not yet chosen an exit route, our licensed insolvency practitioners will run through the position with you and tell you which doors are still open.</p>\n\n<p>Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/123\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, section 123</strong> &mdash; the cashflow and balance-sheet tests used to decide whether a company is insolvent on the day it ceases trading</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, section 214</strong> &mdash; wrongful trading liability for directors who continue to incur debts after insolvency was unavoidable, including the post-cessation period</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/238\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 238 and 239</strong> &mdash; transactions at undervalue and preferences during the wind-down, both reviewable and reversible by a later liquidator</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/122\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 122 and 124</strong> &mdash; the grounds and petition route for compulsory winding-up by the court, usually triggered by creditor action after cessation</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/1003\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 1003</strong> &mdash; voluntary strike-off on Form DS01, with the three-month no-trading and no-disposal preconditions referenced in this guide</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/section/6\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, section 6</strong> &mdash; the unfitness ground for disqualification orders of 2 to 15 years following a liquidator's D-report</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/strike-off-your-company-from-companies-register\"><strong>Companies House: strike off your company from the register</strong> &mdash; official DS01 guidance on eligibility, creditor notification, and gazette processing referenced for the informal closure route</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/claim-redundancy\"><strong>Redundancy Payments Service (RPS)</strong> &mdash; the route for employees to claim statutory notice, redundancy and holiday pay when an insolvent employer cannot pay on cessation</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions About Ceasing to Trade</h2>\n\n<!-- normalized wp:ub/content-toggle FAQ accordion -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-26279\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does ceasing trading mean my company is immediately dissolved?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-26279\">\n<p>No. Ceasing trading is a factual position: the company has stopped operating. The company itself stays on the Companies House register and remains legally alive until you formally close it through strike-off, MVL or liquidation. Filing duties, debts and director duties continue until that point.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I still pay existing creditors after ceasing trade?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-26279\">\n<p>You can, but you must pay them in the right order. If the company is insolvent, paying a connected creditor or a favoured supplier ahead of HMRC creates a preference under section 239 of the Insolvency Act 1986, which a later liquidator can reverse. Take advice before clearing any post-cessation debts.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What if I want to restart trading in the future?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-26279\">\n<p>While the company is on the register and not in a formal procedure, you can restart trading at any point. You will need to bring filings up to date, register again for VAT and PAYE if relevant, and notify HMRC that activity has resumed. If the company has been struck off, restarting requires either administrative restoration or court restoration first.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How does ceasing trading affect staff redundancy?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-26279\">\n<p>Permanent cessation usually triggers redundancy. Statutory notice, redundancy pay and outstanding holiday pay must be settled, and consultation rules apply once headcount thresholds are crossed. If the company is insolvent and cannot pay, employees can claim statutory amounts from the Redundancy Payments Service, which then ranks as a creditor in the liquidation.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Do I need to notify HMRC if I have stopped trading?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-26279\">\n<p>Yes. Tell HMRC the cessation date in writing, file a final corporation tax return for the shortened accounting period, and de-register for VAT and PAYE if the cessation is permanent. HMRC will issue a final assessment; ignoring it does not make the liability disappear, it just delays the moment a personal liability notice becomes a possibility.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What if the company cannot pay its debts in full?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-26279\">\n<p>Then the company is insolvent on the cashflow test under section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986, and strike-off is not the right route. Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation is the orderly choice: directors keep the choice of liquidator, the conduct review happens in a structured forum, and creditors get a defined process. Compulsory winding-up by court order is the alternative if you wait too long.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-6\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-6-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Am I personally liable for debts if I am a director?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-6-faq-26279\">\n<p>Limited liability protects you in normal circumstances. It does not protect you against personal guarantees you have signed, against an overdrawn director's loan account, against wrongful trading findings under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986, or against an HMRC personal liability notice for unpaid PAYE. Cessation does not insulate any of these; in some cases it accelerates them.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26279-7\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-7-faq-26279\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26279\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How long can a company stay ceased trading without closing?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-7-faq-26279\">\n<p>There is no statutory ceiling. Practically, the longer the company sits ceased on the register, the more late-filing penalties accumulate and the more pattern is created for any later conduct review. Most directors should choose an exit route within three to six months of stopping, and earlier if creditors are pressing.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-26279\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->"
  },
  "/advice/debt-management-guide/": {
    "id": 26234,
    "slug": "debt-management-guide",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your company owes more than it can comfortably pay, debt management is not about hoping it resolves itself. It is about understanding which debts are dangerous, which creditors will escalate first, and what tools are available to restructure what you owe before a creditor decides for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We work with directors managing business debt every day. The ones who come through it best are not the ones with the smallest debts. They are the ones who faced the numbers honestly, prioritised the creditors who could hurt them most, and took action while they still had options.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The ones who come through it worst are the ones who treated every creditor equally, paid whoever shouted loudest, and left HMRC until last. We cannot tell you how many times we have seen that sequence end with a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-how-to-manage-business-debt\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-how-to-manage-business-debt\">Quick Answer: How to Manage Business Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>List every debt your company owes. Separate them into three categories: (1) debts that can trigger enforcement or insolvency proceedings (HMRC, creditors over \u00a3750 who could petition), (2) debts that are operationally critical (rent, key suppliers, payroll), and (3) debts that can wait (unsecured creditors without enforcement powers).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Deal with category 1 first. Contact HMRC for a Time to Pay arrangement. Negotiate directly with critical suppliers. Then assess whether the business is viable with restructured debt or whether formal insolvency is the appropriate route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"step-1-know-exactly-what-debt-the-business-owes\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"step-1-know-exactly-what-debt-the-business-owes\">Step 1: Know Exactly What Debt the Business Owes</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that most directors in debt trouble do not have an accurate total. They know the big debts but not the accumulation of small ones. Pull together a complete list: every creditor, every amount, every due date, and whether the debt is secured, preferential, or unsecured. Your accountant can help if you do not have the figures to hand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress this because the total often surprises directors. They know they owe HMRC \u00a330,000 and a supplier \u00a315,000, but when they add the other 20 creditors, the total is \u00a390,000, not the \u00a345,000 they had in their head. The accurate number changes the strategy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"step-2-prioritise-business-debt-by-danger-not-by-who-calls-most\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"step-2-prioritise-business-debt-by-danger-not-by-who-calls-most\">Step 2: Prioritise Business Debt by Danger, Not by Who Calls Most</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Not all creditors are equally dangerous. We advise prioritising by enforcement power:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>HMRC:</strong> can serve statutory demands, petition to wind up, send enforcement agents, and issue personal liability notices to directors. Always deal with HMRC first.</li>\n<li><strong>Secured creditors (banks):</strong> can appoint receivers, enforce charges over property, and call in personal guarantees. If you have a bank loan with a debenture, the bank is your second priority.</li>\n<li><strong>Landlord:</strong> can forfeit the lease and lock you out of your premises. If your business depends on the premises, this is operationally critical.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade creditors over \u00a3750:</strong> can serve a statutory demand and petition for compulsory liquidation. The \u00a3750 threshold is low. Almost any supplier can petition.</li>\n<li><strong>Employees:</strong> unpaid wages create immediate operational problems and are preferential debts in insolvency.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We tell directors: the supplier who phones you five times a day may be annoying, but if they are owed \u00a32,000 and have no security, they are less dangerous than HMRC who has not called yet but is compiling an enforcement referral. Prioritise by power, not by volume.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"step-3-contact-your-most-dangerous-business-debt-holders-first\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"step-3-contact-your-most-dangerous-business-debt-holders-first\">Step 3: Contact Your Most Dangerous Business Debt Holders First</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>HMRC:</strong> Call 0300 200 3835 (Business Payment Support Service) and negotiate a Time to Pay arrangement. This is the single most effective debt management tool available to UK businesses. We cover it in detail in our TTP guide.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Bank/secured lender:</strong> Contact your relationship manager. Explain the position. Banks have restructuring teams that can adjust repayment terms, extend facilities, or agree interest-only periods. We find that banks engaged early are cooperative. Banks engaged after a missed payment are defensive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Landlord:</strong> Negotiate a rent reduction, a payment holiday, or a longer lease in exchange for a period of reduced rent. Landlords prefer a paying tenant to an empty premises and a forfeiture that costs them money.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Key suppliers:</strong> Offer a payment plan. Explain the situation honestly. Suppliers who understand the position and see a realistic plan are more likely to continue supplying than suppliers who are left in the dark.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"step-4-assess-whether-the-business-is-debt-viable\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"step-4-assess-whether-the-business-is-debt-viable\">Step 4: Assess Whether the Business Is Debt-Viable</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Debt management only works if the business can sustain itself going forward. If you restructure the debt but the business still loses money every month, you are delaying insolvency, not preventing it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The test is simple: can the company generate enough cash to cover its operating costs, meet current obligations, AND service the restructured debt? If yes, manage the debt. If no, the question becomes whether to close or save the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are honest about this because we see directors who spend 12 months managing debt that should have been resolved through a formal insolvency process. Every month of debt management on an unviable business increases the total debt, narrows the options, and increases the director's personal wrongful trading exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"formal-business-debt-management-options\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"formal-business-debt-management-options\">Formal Business Debt Management Options</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If informal negotiation is not enough, formal options exist:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA):</strong> A binding deal with creditors to repay a proportion of the debt over 3-5 years. Requires 75% creditor approval. You keep trading. See our CVA guide.</li>\n<li><strong>Administration:</strong> Court protection while the business is restructured or sold. Stops all creditor enforcement immediately.</li>\n<li><strong>Other alternatives to liquidation:</strong> Refinancing, invoice finance, asset-based lending. Injecting new capital to bridge a cash-flow gap.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise directors to explore informal options first (Steps 1 to 3) and move to formal options only if informal negotiation fails or creditor pressure is too intense to manage informally.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-directors-should-do-right-now-to-manage-debt\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-directors-should-do-right-now-to-manage-debt\">What Directors Should Do Right Now to Manage Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>List every debt.</strong> Total it. Face the number.</li>\n<li><strong>Prioritise by danger.</strong> HMRC first, secured creditors second, everyone else after.</li>\n<li><strong>Call HMRC today</strong> if you owe them. 0300 200 3835.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess viability.</strong> Can the business fund restructured debt AND current obligations? If not, the problem is bigger than debt management.</li>\n<li><strong>If you are not sure, speak to a licensed insolvency practitioner.</strong> Company Debt connects directors with regulated IPs who can assess your debt position and recommend the right approach. A free, confidential consultation will tell you whether informal management is realistic or whether you need a formal route.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-26234-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about your debt position and the right route\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Managing Business Debt</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-26234\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26234-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-26234\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26234\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Should I <a href=\"/hmrc/pay-hmrc-or-suppliers-first/\">pay HMRC or suppliers first</a>?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-26234\">\n<p>HMRC first. They have the strongest enforcement powers: statutory demands, winding-up petitions, enforcement agents, and personal liability notices for PAYE. A supplier can sue you. HMRC can close your company. Prioritise by enforcement power, not by who calls most.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26234-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-26234\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26234\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I negotiate with creditors myself?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-26234\">\n<p>Yes. Informal negotiation costs nothing and should always be tried first. Call each creditor, explain the position honestly, and propose a realistic payment plan. The key is acting early. Creditors who are engaged before enforcement starts are significantly more willing to negotiate.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-26234-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-26234\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-26234\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>When does debt management become insolvency?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-26234\">\n<p>When the company cannot pay its debts as they fall due (cash-flow test) or its liabilities exceed its assets (balance-sheet test). If you are choosing which creditors to pay each month, you have almost certainly crossed that line.</p>\n<p>At that point, debt management becomes insolvency management, and your duties as a director shift to protecting creditors. See our guide on how to <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">check if your company is insolvent</a>.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-26234\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on HMRC enforcement guidance, the Insolvency Act 1986 (CVA, administration, liquidation), and practical experience from debt management cases handled by licensed practitioners in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/difficulties-paying-hmrc\"><strong>HMRC &mdash; Business Payment Support Service and Time to Pay</strong> &mdash; how to negotiate instalment arrangements for PAYE, VAT, and corporation tax before HMRC escalates to enforcement</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; Part I (CVAs), Schedule B1 (administration), and section 123 cash-flow and balance-sheet insolvency tests directors must apply</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006</strong> &mdash; statutory framework for directors' duties under sections 171&ndash;177, including the duty to promote success and consider creditors when solvency is in doubt</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service &mdash; guidance on rescue and restructuring</strong> &mdash; official guidance on company rescue routes, administration, and director conduct reviews following insolvency</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/courts-tribunals/insolvency-and-companies-court\"><strong>HMCTS &mdash; Insolvency and Companies Court</strong> &mdash; procedure for statutory demands and winding-up petitions, the enforcement route any unpaid creditor owed more than &pound;750 can use</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; basis for disqualification orders of two to fifteen years where conduct during the debt period falls below statutory standards</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; regulator and licensed IP search; verify a practitioner's licence before instructing on CVA, administration, or liquidation</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/company-cash-flow-problems/": {
    "id": 26139,
    "slug": "company-cash-flow-problems",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Cash flow problems rarely announce themselves as a crisis. They announce themselves as a VAT bill we will pay next week, a supplier invoice that slides a few days past terms, a PAYE payment delayed until the large client settles. Each looks manageable. The compounding effect, three or four months in, is what actually sinks the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This hub page covers the early-warning signs, the legal consequences of persistent arrears, the informal and formal solutions available at each stage of distress, and the director-liability exposure that compounds as your cash position worsens. Each section links to the deeper guide on the specific topic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Understanding Cash Flow and Insolvency Basics</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Cash flow is the timing of money moving in and out of your business. Profit is an accounting concept; cash flow is an operating one. Your business can be profitable on paper and insolvent in practice. Most companies that enter formal insolvency in the UK were profitable at some point in the 12 months before they failed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The two statutory tests for corporate insolvency under section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Cash-flow test</strong>, is the company unable to pay its debts as they fall due? Persistently missed tax, rent, supplier or payroll obligations fail this test directly.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Balance-sheet test</strong>, do the company's liabilities (including contingent and prospective) exceed its assets? This test is slower to trip and often follows the cash-flow test by months.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Failing either test triggers a specific shift in your director duties under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006. You are no longer acting primarily for shareholders; you are acting for creditors as a whole. Everything that follows on this hub assumes that shift is in view.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Common Causes of Cash Flow Problems in UK Businesses</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The causes repeat across almost every case we're instructed on:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Late-paying customers</strong>, the single largest source of small-business cash-flow distress. The Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998 entitles commercial creditors to charge 8% plus Bank of England base rate on qualifying late payments, a tool most small businesses under-use.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Rising tax liabilities</strong>, VAT and PAYE payments that were affordable at old trading levels become unmanageable at contracted trading levels.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Overhead growth outrunning revenue</strong>, staff, premises, or equipment commitments made on optimistic forecasts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Unexpected costs</strong>, equipment failure, compliance fines, lost key-person time, sudden supplier price increases.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Concentrated customer risk</strong>, one customer representing 30%+ of revenue whose payment timing the business cannot influence.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Impact of Delayed Invoices</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An invoice paid 45 days late on 60-day terms has consumed 105 days of your working capital. Multiply that across a full aged-debtor ledger and the gap between \"profitable on paper\" and \"insolvent in fact\" is exactly the size of your late-payment book. Disciplined credit control is the single largest lever your business has on its own survival.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Early Warning Signs Directors Must Not Ignore</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The signals that a company's cash-flow problem is material, in rough order of severity:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Overdraft sitting at ceiling most months.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>HMRC correspondence shifting from reminder to formal.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Rates or rent arrears accumulating.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Supplier credit terms shortening or being withdrawn.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Directors injecting personal cash to fund payroll.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>A statutory demand received from any creditor.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Any one of these is a reason to look at your forecast. Two or three together is a reason to instruct a licensed insolvency practitioner for advice on your solvency position and your director duties.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Immediate Risks and Legal Consequences of Cash Flow Failure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Legal consequences escalate on a specific timeline once arrears are formalised:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>County Court Judgments</strong> obtained by creditors, 6 years on the company's public credit record.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Enforcement by bailiffs or Sheriff Officers</strong>, compliance fee \u00a375, enforcement fee \u00a3235 plus 7.5% over \u00a31,500 in England and Wales; 10% statutory surcharge in Scotland.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Statutory demand</strong>, \u00a3750+ unpaid for 21 days creates a presumption of insolvency.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">Winding-up petition</a></strong>, once advertised in The Gazette, company bank accounts freeze under section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Compulsory liquidation</strong>, court-ordered wind-up where the petition succeeds. Loss of director control; Official Receiver appointed; director conduct reviewed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Each stage narrows your options. The restructuring window for a CVA or administration closes sharply once a petition is advertised, because the company bank account freezes under section 127 the moment the notice hits The Gazette, and payroll on the Friday becomes a problem you cannot solve by phoning the bank.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors who pick up the statutory demand the week it lands still have a CVA or administration open to them. The ones who leave it in the in-tray until the petition is served usually discover the choice has been made for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Informal Cash Flow Solutions Before Formal Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the cash-flow pressure is recoverable without formal process, the useful tools:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC Time to Pay</strong>, structured arrangement to repay tax arrears over 6\u201312 months. Accepted where the arrears are a one-off and future compliance is credible.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Creditor negotiation</strong>, specific, dated, written payment plans offered with immediate partial payment. Suppliers and landlords routinely accept where the alternative is formal enforcement.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Short-term finance</strong>, overdraft extension, invoice finance, asset finance. Useful only where the underlying business is viable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Business restructuring</strong>, cost reduction, contract renegotiation, strategic change. The earliest and cheapest intervention where the business is structurally sound but operationally stretched.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Formal Insolvency and Rescue Options</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When informal solutions are insufficient, four formal routes address the position:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</strong>, binding agreement to repay creditors a percentage over 3\u20135 years while continuing to trade. Requires 75% creditor approval by value.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Administration</strong>, statutory moratorium while administrator pursues rescue or sale.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a> (CVL)</strong>, orderly wind-down where rescue is not viable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Members' Voluntary Liquidation (MVL)</strong>, solvent closure where the company can pay all debts; typically on retirement or exit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The choice depends on whether the underlying business is viable if historic debt is restructured. A licensed IP answers that question from the management accounts in an hour.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Directors' Duties and Personal Liability on Cash Flow Problems</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once insolvency is likely, director duties shift. The routes to personal liability that matter:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading</strong> under section 214, personal contribution to losses caused by continuing to trade past the point where insolvency was unavoidable.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Fraudulent trading</strong> under section 213, carrying on with intent to defraud creditors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Misfeasance</strong> under section 212, breach of specific fiduciary duty.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC Personal Liability Notices</strong>, unpaid NICs transferred to director personally where non-payment is attributable to fraud or neglect.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Director disqualification</strong> under the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, 2 to 15 years.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Documented contemporaneous advice from a licensed IP is the single best defence against these routes. If you take advice while the business is still trading, your options remain open; directors who wait until the petition has been advertised have closed most of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Costs and Financial Consequences of Cash Flow Failure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The direct costs compound quickly:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC interest</strong> at 7.75% on late corporation tax (current rate; tracks Bank of England + 2.5%).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC penalties</strong> for late filing (\u00a3100 fixed) and late payment (5% of tax due at 30 days, repeated at 6 and 12 months).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Supplier late fees and credit-reference damage</strong>.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Court fees and enforcement costs</strong> on each creditor action.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Professional fees</strong> on formal insolvency, a CVL for a small company typically \u00a33,000\u2013\u00a36,000 in IP fees.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The indirect costs are often larger: loss of key staff, loss of key customers, reputational damage, director time absorbed in firefighting. Each compounds the original cash-flow problem.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Next Step on Company Cash Flow Problems</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Cash-flow problems are almost always solvable if addressed early and decisively. The consistent pattern across businesses that recover is not financial wizardry; it is disciplined engagement with your numbers, open communication with creditors, and timely professional advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed insolvency practitioners and business rescue specialists can assess the position in an hour, outline both informal and formal options, and provide the documented advice that protects both the business and the director's personal position. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Company Cash Flow Problem FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"26139-cf-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow do I know if my company is insolvent?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Apply the two tests in section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986. Cash-flow: can the company pay debts as they fall due? Balance-sheet: do total liabilities (including contingent) exceed total assets? Failing either is insolvency. Persistent missed tax, rent, supplier or payroll obligations fail the cash-flow test directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a profitable company be cash-flow insolvent?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. And this is the most common pattern we see. Profit is an accrual concept; cash flow is a timing concept. A company can be profitable on the P&amp;L while being unable to pay its debts as they fall due, because the customers owe money that has not yet arrived. Most insolvent businesses were profitable within the previous 12 months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes HMRC automatically agree to Time to Pay?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. HMRC grants Time to Pay where the arrears are a one-off, future compliance is credible, and the proposed schedule is realistic. Serial applicants, businesses with a history of breaching previous TTP agreements, and proposals that do not fit current HMRC policy are declined. Early application, with supporting cash flow evidence, has the best success rate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens if I ignore a CCJ or statutory demand?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>CCJs enable enforcement (bailiffs, charging orders, third-party debt orders). Statutory demands, unpaid for 21 days, enable winding-up petitions. Ignored, both lead to mechanical escalation: enforcement fees, <a href=\"/advice/frozen-bank-account/\">frozen bank account</a>s on petition advertisement, loss of director control on compulsory liquidation. Respond, paying, disputing, or negotiating, before day 21.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eAre government-backed loans still available to help with cash flow?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The main COVID-era schemes (Bounce Back Loan Scheme, CBILS) have closed to new applications. The Recovery Loan Scheme, and its successor the Growth Guarantee Scheme, continue to support commercial borrowing with a partial government guarantee. Check current gov.uk guidance for the scheme in force in your reference year.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Any scheme borrowing creates commitments of its own and should be part of a broader financial plan for your business, not a substitute for one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This hub is written by our editorial team, reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners, and reflects UK insolvency, tax, and commercial law as at the last-reviewed date. Statutory references are drawn from the Insolvency Act 1986, Companies Act 2006, and Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Where we recommend a CVA, Administration, CVL, or MVL, we can act as the licensed Insolvency Practitioner under separate engagement. Our 0800 number is a free confidential consultation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; sections 123 (cash-flow and balance-sheet tests), 127 (void dispositions after winding-up petition), 212 (misfeasance), 213 (fraudulent trading) and 214 (wrongful trading) frame director exposure once arrears persist</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/172\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, section 172</strong> &mdash; the duty that pivots from shareholders to creditors as a whole the moment your business fails the cash-flow test</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/20/contents\"><strong>Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998</strong> &mdash; statutory right to charge 8% plus Bank of England base rate on overdue commercial invoices, the lever for the largest cause of SME cash-flow distress</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; conduct review and disqualification consequences for directors who keep trading through worsening cash-flow insolvency</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Further reading</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-afford-to-pay-suppliers-what-are-the-options/\">Can't afford to pay suppliers</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-staff-wages/\">Can't pay staff wages</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-a-commercial-lease-or-rent/\">Can't pay a commercial lease or rent</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-business-rates/\">Can't pay business rates</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-business-energy/\">Can't pay business energy</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-pay-a-company-mortgage/\">Can't pay a company mortgage</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/cant-afford-to-repay-business-loan/\">Can't afford to repay a business loan</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/what-is-a-statutory-demand-against-a-company/\">What is a statutory demand against a company</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/company-is-having-financial-difficulties/\">Company is having financial difficulties</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/when-employers-cant-afford-redundancy-payments/\">When employers can't afford redundancy payments</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/biggest-struggles-for-small-business-owners/\">Biggest struggles for small business owners</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/why-debt-is-not-always-a-bad-thing-for-your-business/\">Why debt is not always a bad thing for your business</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/challenge-a-statutory-demand/\">Challenge a statutory demand</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/validation-order/": {
    "id": 25310,
    "slug": "validation-order",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is Wednesday morning. The <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> was advertised in the London Gazette yesterday. The accounts team logs in to approve the BACS run for next week's payroll and the Barclays interface returns an error. The account has been frozen overnight. Forty-two staff are due to be paid on Friday.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What has happened, mechanically, is section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986. From the moment the petition is presented, and certainly once it has been advertised, any disposition of company property is void unless the court orders otherwise. The bank has frozen the account to protect itself from being treated as having facilitated a void payment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A validation order is the remedy. You make an urgent application to the Companies Court, on written evidence, asking the court to permit specific categories of payment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below, we set out what a validation order does, the test the court applies under <em>Re Gray's Inn Construction Co Ltd</em> [1980], the typical scope of permitted payments, the costs and timing, and the practical steps we take with clients in the first 48 hours after the Gazette advertisement.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-a-validation-order-is\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-a-validation-order-is\">What a Validation Order Is</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A validation order is a court order under section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986 that permits a disposition of your company's property, which would otherwise be void, to proceed lawfully. You, or the company through its solicitors, apply for it in the Companies Court.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three features matter:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Prospective or retrospective</strong>, the order can validate payments you have already made as well as payments you need to make. Prospective orders are easier for you to obtain; retrospective ones carry more scrutiny.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Specific or categorical</strong>, the order can permit named transactions (your payment to a specific supplier) or categories (all payments in the ordinary course of business up to a set threshold).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Conditional</strong>, the court can attach conditions, for example requiring regular reporting or a limit on total disposals pending the petition hearing.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Without the order, your bank will continue to freeze the account, suppliers will refuse to accept your payments for fear of clawback, and any transaction you complete is legally null. The validation order is the only mechanical fix available to you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"why-section-127-freezes-the-company-bank-account\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"why-section-127-freezes-the-company-bank-account\">Why Section 127 Freezes the Company Bank Account</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986 provides that, in a winding-up by the court, any disposition of your company's property made after commencement is void, unless the court orders otherwise. Commencement, under section 129, is deemed to be the date the petition was presented, not the date of the winding-up order.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The statutory freeze serves a creditor-protection purpose. Without it, directors could drain the company account between petition and order, preferring favoured creditors or moving value out of reach of the liquidator. The rule is rigid by design.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your bank is a joint target of the rule. If the bank honours payments from your frozen account without a validation order and a winding-up order then issues, the bank may have to refund the sums to the liquidator. That is why the account freeze is immediate and not negotiable bank-by-bank.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-re-gray-s-inn-test-when-the-court-grants-a-validation-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-re-gray-s-inn-test-when-the-court-grants-a-validation-order\">The Re Gray's Inn Test: When the Court Grants a Validation Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The leading authority on when a validation order should be granted is <em>Re Gray's Inn Construction Co Ltd</em> [1980]. The court asks whether the proposed disposition is likely to benefit, or at least not harm, the interests of the unsecured creditors as a whole.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The factors the court typically weighs:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Ordinary course of business</strong>, payments that keep the company trading on a normal footing, not speculative or unusual transactions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Arm's-length terms</strong>, the transaction is at market rates, not a preference or a transaction at undervalue.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Good faith</strong>, the company, the directors, and the counterparty are acting honestly, with full knowledge of the petition.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>No preferential effect</strong>, the payment does not advantage one creditor over another in a way that would be reversed as a preference under section 239 Insolvency Act 1986.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Net benefit to the estate</strong>, the disposition preserves or enhances value rather than dissipating it. Paying essential suppliers to keep a contract live, for instance.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The court will not validate a transaction that looks like you paying a connected party ahead of the general creditor body. A validation order is not a mechanism for quietly closing out your preferences.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-a-validation-order-typically-covers\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-a-validation-order-typically-covers\">What a Validation Order Typically Covers</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The typical scope of a validation order for a trading company:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Payroll and PAYE</strong>, wages, holiday pay, employer NIC, and tax deducted at source, up to a stated weekly or monthly figure.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Essential supplier payments</strong>, utilities, rent, and contract-critical suppliers where non-payment would interrupt trading.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Current-period tax</strong>, VAT and Corporation Tax accruing on ongoing trading, which is itself a new liability post-petition.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Receipt of customer payments</strong>, validation is needed not only for outgoings but for incoming receipts, since the bank freezes both directions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Professional fees</strong>, legal and insolvency advice, up to a capped amount.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The order will typically require regular reporting to the petitioner and the court, either weekly or fortnightly, showing the payments made under the order. In some cases the court will require an independent accountant's certificate of the position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-to-apply-for-a-validation-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-to-apply-for-a-validation-order\">How to Apply for a Validation Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You make the application to the Companies Court, typically on an urgent basis. The procedural framework sits within CPR Part 8 and the Insolvency Rules. Your mechanics:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Witness statement</strong> from you as director, setting out the petition background, your company's current trading position, the categories of payment needed, and the rationale for each under the <em>Gray's Inn</em> test.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Supporting financial information</strong>, usually the latest management accounts, a cash-flow projection for the period covered by the order, and a list of proposed payees and amounts.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Notice to the petitioning creditor</strong>, unless urgency justifies a without-notice application. Practice is usually to give short notice and invite the petitioner's position.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The hearing</strong>, often by remote video, can be listed within days where urgency is made out. The court hears the application, considers the evidence, and grants the order with any conditions it thinks fit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Urgency is the entire point. The longer your account is frozen, the more damage accrues. In the petitions we see, where you ring a specialist on the day of the advertisement, a validation order is usually in place inside a week. Where you hesitate for ten days hoping the petition will go away, the business has lost suppliers and staff before the application is even issued.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"costs-and-timing-of-a-validation-order-application\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"costs-and-timing-of-a-validation-order-application\">Costs and Timing of a Validation Order Application</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The costs of a validation order application fall into three buckets:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Court fee</strong>, the application fee under the EX50 schedule, typically \u00a3313 on notice or \u00a3123 by consent in the Companies Court.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Solicitor's fees</strong>, the witness statement, evidence preparation, and hearing advocacy. A straightforward application runs to several thousand pounds; a contested one to substantially more.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Accountant or IP support</strong>, where management accounts or cash-flow projections need to be produced or verified.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The overall timeline from instruction to order, where no complications arise, is typically 5 to 10 business days. Urgent hearings can bring that forward where genuinely essential. The petitioner may seek costs against the company in some cases, though in straightforward validation orders each side usually bears its own costs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-a-validation-order-is-not-enough\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-a-validation-order-is-not-enough\">When a Validation Order Is Not Enough</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A validation order is a tactical tool. It keeps your lights on while the underlying problem (the winding-up petition) is resolved. It does not in itself solve your insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A validation order is not the right tool when:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The underlying debt cannot be paid or compromised</strong>, in which case the petition will result in a winding-up order regardless of how the account is managed in the interim.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Trading has already collapsed</strong>, without orders coming in, no payroll to protect, the validation order is an expense with no benefit.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The company is clearly heading for a <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">creditors' voluntary liquidation</a></strong>, where the right move is to propose CVL and withdraw the petition, not to fund continued trading.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The diagnostic conversation with your insolvency practitioner will flag which is which. The validation order is a Band-Aid; the underlying wound may need a different procedure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-a-validation-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-a-validation-order\">Your Next Step on a Validation Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The real question is rarely \"do I need a validation order?\". It is \"can I realistically keep trading through the petition, or is the validation-order application just throwing good money after bad?\". Two groups read this page, and your next call is different.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If the petition has been advertised, the account is frozen, and the business is fundamentally viable</strong>, call an insolvency practitioner and a specialist solicitor today. A validation order is achievable inside a week and payroll can be protected.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If the account is frozen and the business has no realistic trading future</strong>, the correct move is usually not validation but a creditors' voluntary liquidation. Trying to trade through a petition on validated payments, without a route out, is an expensive way to reach the same end point.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our licensed IPs can review the petition position, coordinate with a specialist Companies Court solicitor on the validation-order application, and advise whether CVL is the better route. Call us free on 0800 074 6757 for confidential advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-on-validation-orders\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-on-validation-orders\">FAQs on Validation Orders</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"25310-validation-order-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhen is a validation order needed?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Once a winding-up petition has been presented against the company, section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986 voids any disposition of company property unless the court validates it. Banks typically freeze the company account on becoming aware of the petition, which usually means on Gazette advertisement. A validation order is the court mechanism to permit necessary payments to continue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat does the court consider when granting a validation order?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Under <em>Re Gray's Inn Construction Co Ltd</em>, the court looks at whether the proposed disposition benefits, or at least does not harm, the unsecured creditors as a whole. Ordinary-course payments, arm's-length terms, good faith, and no preferential effect are the usual gateway factors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow quickly can a validation order be obtained?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Straightforward applications can be heard within 5 to 10 business days of instruction. Genuinely urgent cases, where payroll is at immediate risk, can be listed inside a few days on short notice. The court is accustomed to urgency on validation applications.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat does a validation order typically cost?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Court fees of \u00a3123 (by consent) or \u00a3313 (on notice) under EX50, plus solicitor's fees of several thousand pounds for a straightforward application, and accountant or IP support to produce the supporting evidence. A contested application can run substantially higher.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a validation order cover payments already made?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, a retrospective validation order can confirm payments made between petition and order. The court will scrutinise retrospective applications more closely than prospective ones, since the payments have already happened and the creditor community cannot now be consulted before the event.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eShould I apply for a validation order or propose a CVL?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Validation orders make sense where the business is viable and the petition is expected to be paid, compromised, or withdrawn. Where the business has no realistic trading future, the cost of the validation-order application is better spent on a creditors' voluntary liquidation that preserves more value for the creditor body. A licensed IP can diagnose which is which.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by our editorial team and reviewed by our licensed insolvency practitioners. We reflect UK insolvency law as at the last-reviewed date.</p>\n<p>Statutory references are drawn from the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 127, 129, 239), the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, and Civil Procedure Rules Part 8. The case authority cited is <em>Re Gray's Inn Construction Co Ltd</em> [1980].</p>\n<p>Company Debt is an insolvency advisory firm. Validation-order applications are issued by specialist Companies Court solicitors; we coordinate the IP-side evidence and manage the underlying insolvency strategy. Our 0800 number is a free confidential consultation, not a transaction.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 127, 129 (commencement of winding up), 239 (preferences)</strong> &mdash; the statutory basis for void dispositions after a winding-up petition and the framework validation orders operate within</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents\"><strong>Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016</strong> &mdash; procedural rules governing validation applications, evidence requirements, and service in the Companies Court</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.justice.gov.uk/courts/procedure-rules/civil/rules/part08\"><strong>Civil Procedure Rules Part 8</strong> &mdash; the alternative procedure used for validation applications made on written evidence without a substantial factual dispute</a> - justice.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/fees-in-the-civil-and-family-courts-main-fees-ex50\"><strong>EX50 Court Fees</strong> &mdash; current issue fees for application notices and certificates payable when filing a validation application</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/courts-tribunals/insolvency-and-companies-court\"><strong>HMCTS - winding-up petitions and statutory demands</strong> &mdash; the Insolvency and Companies Court list, urgent applications process, and listing arrangements after a petition is advertised</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service - guidance for directors</strong> &mdash; official guidance on duties once a winding-up petition is on foot and the consequences of trading through it</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association - regulator and IP search</strong> &mdash; verify the licence of any practitioner advising on validation strategy or shadow directorship risk before instructing</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/advice/personal-guarantee-insurance/": {
    "id": 24780,
    "slug": "personal-guarantee-insurance",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- TITLE: Personal Guarantee Insurance (PGI) -->\n<!-- POST ID: 24780 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70156 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/advice/personal-guarantee-insurance/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Personal guarantee insurance covers the financial exposure you take on when you sign a personal guarantee for your company's debts. If the company fails and the guarantee is called in, the insurance pays out instead of you. It is the only product that directly protects your personal assets from guarantee claims.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise directors on personal guarantees every week, and the most common regret is not having insured the guarantee before the company got into trouble.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>PGI is not cheap. Premiums typically run 3 to 5% of the guaranteed amount per year. But for directors who have guaranteed six-figure facilities secured against their home, the premium is a fraction of the potential loss. We explain below how PGI works, what it covers, what it does not, and whether it is worth the cost for your situation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Quick Answer: How Personal Guarantee Insurance Works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You take out a PGI policy that covers a specific personal guarantee (or multiple guarantees). You pay an annual premium. If the company enters insolvency and the creditor calls in the guarantee, you submit a claim to the insurer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the claim is valid, the insurer pays the guaranteed amount (or a proportion of it, depending on the policy) directly to the creditor or reimburses you. Your personal assets are protected up to the policy limit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress \"if the claim is valid\" because PGI policies have exclusions. The most important: the policy typically does not cover guarantees where the company was already insolvent at the time the policy was taken out, guarantees called in due to fraud or deliberate default, or claims made outside the policy period. We cover the exclusions in detail below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Personal Guarantee Insurance Covers</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Bank loan guarantees.</strong> The most common use case. You guaranteed a business loan and the company enters liquidation.</li>\n<li><strong>Commercial lease guarantees.</strong> You guaranteed the rent on a commercial property and the company cannot pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade credit guarantees.</strong> You guaranteed a supplier credit facility.</li>\n<li><strong>CBILS and similar facility guarantees.</strong> Where the loan terms included a personal guarantee (CBILS loans above \u00a3250,000 could include guarantees).</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Coverage is typically 70 to 80% of the guaranteed amount, not 100%. This co-insurance element means you bear some risk yourself, which keeps premiums manageable and prevents moral hazard. We see policies that cover 80% of the first \u00a3500,000 of guarantee exposure as the most common structure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Personal Guarantee Insurance Does NOT Cover</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Pre-existing insolvency.</strong> If the company was already insolvent when you took out the policy, the insurer will reject the claim. You must take out PGI while the company is solvent.</li>\n<li><strong>Fraud or deliberate default.</strong> If the company's failure resulted from fraudulent trading, deliberate asset stripping, or your own misconduct, the policy will not pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Guarantees given after the policy inception.</strong> New guarantees signed after the policy starts are not automatically covered. You need to notify the insurer and add them.</li>\n<li><strong>Claims made after the policy lapses.</strong> PGI is a claims-made policy. If the guarantee is called in after the policy has lapsed (because you stopped paying premiums), you are not covered.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading orders and misfeasance claims.</strong> PGI covers personal guarantee calls. It does not cover other personal liabilities arising from insolvency. For those, you need directors' and officers' (D&amp;O) insurance.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Personal Guarantee Insurance Costs: Is It Worth the Premium?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Premiums vary by risk profile but typically range from 3 to 5% of the guaranteed amount per year. For a \u00a3100,000 guarantee, expect to pay \u00a33,000 to \u00a35,000 annually.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise directors to run the maths: if you have guaranteed \u00a3200,000 and the guarantee is secured against your home, a \u00a310,000 annual premium protects a \u00a3200,000 exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company fails after 5 years of premiums, you have paid \u00a350,000 in premiums to avoid a \u00a3200,000 guarantee call. If the company trades successfully for 10 years and you never claim, you have paid \u00a3100,000 in premiums for peace of mind.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find PGI makes most sense for: directors with large guarantees (\u00a3100,000 plus) secured against their home, directors in high-risk industries (construction, hospitality, retail), and directors approaching retirement who have more to lose personally.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It makes less sense for: small unsecured guarantees under \u00a325,000, directors of very stable businesses with low insolvency risk, or companies where the guarantee can be renegotiated or removed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">PGI vs D&amp;O Insurance: What Is the Difference?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>PGI and D&amp;O insurance are different products that cover different risks:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>PGI</strong> covers personal guarantee calls. The creditor demands payment under the guarantee you signed.</li>\n<li><strong>D&amp;O insurance</strong> covers claims against you for breach of director duties: wrongful trading, misfeasance, negligence, regulatory investigations.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise directors with significant personal exposure to consider both. PGI handles the guarantee risk. D&amp;O handles the conduct risk. Together, they cover the two main routes to personal liability in insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Get Personal Guarantee Insurance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Audit your guarantee position.</strong> Know what you have guaranteed, the amounts, and whether they are secured against your property.</li>\n<li><strong>Approach a specialist broker.</strong> PGI is a niche product. General insurance brokers may not have access to it. Specialist business insurance brokers who work with SME directors are the right starting point.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply while the company is solvent.</strong> PGI underwriters assess the company's financial health. If the company is already struggling, the premium will be higher and coverage may be declined.</li>\n<li><strong>Review the exclusions carefully.</strong> Understand what is not covered before you rely on the policy.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the policy active.</strong> If you stop paying premiums and the policy lapses, you lose coverage. Claims made after lapse are not covered.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-24780-purbeck\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet Personal Guarantee Insurance from Purbeck\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Purbeck is a specialist personal guarantee insurance provider. Get a quote for cover on the guarantees you have signed.\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 Specialist PGI provider | \u2713 Online quote | \u2713 No obligation\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a PGI Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://csp.purbeckinsurance.co.uk/referral-landing-page?rid=4e264482-b784-4c0f-ad32-73c2fca40766\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><em><small>Company Debt is a referral partner of Purbeck Personal Guarantee Insurance and may receive a commission for introductions made through this link. This does not affect the price you pay.</small></em></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt connects directors with licensed insolvency practitioners who can assess your personal guarantee exposure. A confidential consultation will clarify what you need to protect and whether PGI is the right tool for your situation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Personal Guarantee Insurance</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-24780\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24780-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-24780\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24780\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>How much does personal guarantee insurance cost?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-24780\">\n<p>Typically 3 to 5% of the guaranteed amount per year. A \u00a3100,000 guarantee costs approximately \u00a33,000 to \u00a35,000 annually to insure. Premiums vary by the company's financial health, industry, and the nature of the guarantee.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24780-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-24780\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24780\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I get PGI if my company is already in financial difficulty?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-24780\">\n<p>Unlikely. PGI underwriters assess the company's financial health. If the company is already struggling or insolvent, the premium will be prohibitive or coverage will be declined. PGI is a product you take out when the company is healthy, not when it is already in trouble.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24780-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-24780\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24780\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Does PGI cover 100% of the guarantee?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-24780\">\n<p>Most policies cover 70 to 80% of the guaranteed amount, not 100%. The co-insurance element means you retain some personal risk. Check the policy terms for the exact coverage percentage and any caps or sub-limits.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24780-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-24780\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24780\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I take out PGI for a guarantee my spouse signed?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-24780\">\n<p>Generally no. PGI is taken out by the guarantor in their own name. If your spouse co-signed the guarantee as a director or shareholder, they would need to be on the policy too. If only your spouse signed and you did not, you are not the insured party and cannot claim.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-24780\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on current PGI market information from UK underwriters, personal guarantee law, the Insolvency Act 1986, FCA insurance regulation, and practical experience from guarantee cases handled by licensed insolvency practitioners and insurance specialists in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. This does not influence our editorial content. References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; governs the corporate liquidation events that trigger most personal guarantee calls, including the moment a creditor can pursue the director personally</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/insurance-distribution-directive\"><strong>Financial Conduct Authority: Insurance Distribution</strong> &mdash; regulator setting the disclosure and conduct standards PGI providers and brokers must meet when selling cover to directors</a> - fca.org.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/47/contents\"><strong>Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996</strong> &mdash; sections 14 and 15 are the route creditors use to force the sale of a guarantor's home</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1979/53/contents\"><strong>Charging Orders Act 1979</strong> &mdash; framework allowing a guaranteed creditor with a judgment to secure the debt against your property before enforcement</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/contents\"><strong>Companies Act 2006</strong> &mdash; establishes the limited liability principle that personal guarantees deliberately override, and the director duties relevant to guarantee decisions</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.british-business-bank.co.uk/\"><strong>British Business Bank</strong> &mdash; publishes the personal guarantee terms attached to CBILS, Recovery Loan Scheme and Growth Guarantee facilities directors commonly insure</a> - british-business-bank.co.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; regulator and licensed IP search, useful when checking the practitioner advising on guarantee exposure during insolvency</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/": {
    "id": 8414,
    "slug": "time-to-pay-hmrc",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A Time to Pay arrangement is an instalment plan with HMRC that lets you spread an overdue tax bill over 6 to 12 months. It is the single most effective tool for managing HMRC debt, and the single most wasted opportunity when directors leave it too late to call.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We facilitate TTP conversations between directors and HMRC every week, and the pattern in our caseload is consistent. Directors who call before the deadline, or within a few weeks of missing it, get arrangements agreed in a single phone call.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors who call six months late, after statutory demands have been served and field officers instructed, face a much harder negotiation with a much lower success rate from our experience. The tool is the same. The timing changes everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The director who calls on a Tuesday morning while the VAT is still only a fortnight late tends to come off the phone with an agreement and a payment schedule. The one who calls after the bailiff letter has arrived is starting from much further back.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--summary\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>Time to Pay at a Glance</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What it is:</strong> a binding instalment plan agreed with HMRC for an overdue tax debt, typically 6 to 12 months long.</p>\n<p><strong>Who calls:</strong> the director or their accountant calls HMRC's Business Payment Support Service on 0300 200 3835.</p>\n<p><strong>What it does:</strong> suspends enforcement, freezes most penalties, and binds HMRC to the agreed schedule for the duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Cost:</strong> interest continues to accrue at Bank of England base rate plus 4% throughout the arrangement.</p>\n<p><strong>Director priority:</strong> call early, propose a specific monthly figure you can actually pay, and meet every current tax obligation during the TTP. Missing one collapses the lot.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Quick Answer: How HMRC Time to Pay Works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call HMRC's Business Payment Support Service on 0300 200 3835. Tell them you cannot pay your tax bill in full. Propose a specific monthly repayment schedule, typically 6 to 12 months. If HMRC agrees, enforcement is suspended, late-payment penalties may be frozen, and you repay the debt in instalments while continuing to meet current tax obligations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Interest continues to accrue on the outstanding balance at the HMRC late-payment rate (Bank of England base rate plus 4%, since 6 April 2025). If you miss a payment or fall behind on current obligations, HMRC can terminate the arrangement and resume enforcement immediately.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Which Tax Debts Qualify for HMRC Time to Pay?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>TTP is available for all HMRC business tax debts. The five most common are below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VAT:</strong> quarterly or monthly returns. The most common TTP debt by volume.</li>\n<li><strong>PAYE and employee NICs:</strong> monthly payroll deductions. HMRC treats these as money you collected on its behalf and negotiates least flexibly.</li>\n<li><strong>Corporation Tax:</strong> annual company tax. HMRC is more flexible here because the debt is the company's own.</li>\n<li><strong>CIS deductions:</strong> Construction Industry Scheme payments.</li>\n<li><strong>Self Assessment:</strong> personal tax for directors paid through SA.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC treats different tax types with different urgency, and the reason matters when you decide which TTP to ask for first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>PAYE and employee NICs are pursued hardest because HMRC treats these as money the company collected from employees and held as a fiduciary, not as a company debt. VAT sits in the middle for similar reasons: collected from customers and remitted on. Corporation Tax is the most flexible because the debt is the company's own.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you owe more than one tax type, request the TTP for Corporation Tax first while you sort current PAYE and VAT, not the other way round.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Negotiate an HMRC Time to Pay Arrangement</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before You Call HMRC</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Know exactly what you owe. Check your Business Tax Account at tax.service.gov.uk; the balance updates daily.</li>\n<li>Prepare a breakdown of your company's monthly income and expenses. The HMRC adviser will ask for this.</li>\n<li>Have a realistic repayment proposal ready. Not what you hope to pay. What you can guarantee.</li>\n<li>Make sure your tax returns are filed up to date. HMRC will not agree a TTP while returns are outstanding; the adviser cannot calculate the total debt without them. If you cannot pay the tax shown on the return, file the return anyway and call about the TTP straight after. Filing first is what unlocks the conversation.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">On the Call With HMRC</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Be honest about why you cannot pay. HMRC hears every excuse. Honesty builds credibility.</li>\n<li>Propose a specific monthly amount and duration. \"I can pay \u00a33,000 per month for 10 months\" lands better than \"I need help.\"</li>\n<li>Confirm you can meet current obligations during the TTP. This is non-negotiable. If you cannot pay the old debt and keep up with new liabilities, TTP is not viable.</li>\n<li>Ask for the agreement in writing. HMRC will send a confirmation letter. Keep it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The call typically takes 20 to 30 minutes. The adviser has authority to agree arrangements up to 12 months on the spot. Longer terms or larger debts may be referred to a manager, which adds a few days.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Happens If HMRC Rejects Your Time to Pay Application</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC rejects TTP when they conclude the business is not viable, the proposed repayment is unrealistic, or you have a history of broken arrangements. We see rejections most commonly in the four situations below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Reason for rejection</th><th>What it usually means</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Director called too late</td><td>Enforcement has already started; HMRC has lost confidence in the company's ability to pay.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Multiple tax types overdue</td><td>The arrears across VAT, PAYE, and CT together suggest the business is structurally insolvent, not cash-strapped.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Previous TTP broken</td><td>HMRC's system records every prior arrangement. A broken TTP is treated as a credibility problem.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Numbers do not support the proposal</td><td>The income and expenses breakdown shows the company cannot sustain the proposed repayments.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A rejection narrows the options in a specific order. The cheapest move is to propose revised terms straight back: a lower monthly amount, a longer period, or both.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The next step is to engage a licensed insolvency practitioner to make the call, because HMRC sometimes responds differently to a regulated firm putting forward a documented assessment of viability. The third move is a formal restructuring like a CVA, which binds HMRC to a creditor-approved repayment plan through the 75% vote.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If none of those work and HMRC proceeds to enforcement, the next step is typically a statutory demand followed by a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> under section 124 of the Insolvency Act 1986. At that point the question shifts from how to pay the tax to whether to close or save the company, and the calendar is no longer yours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Happens During the Time to Pay Period</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>What stops</th><th>What carries on</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Enforcement is suspended: no statutory demands, no field officers, no winding-up petitions for the agreed debt.</td><td>Interest accrues on the outstanding balance at base rate plus 4% throughout.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Most late-payment penalties freeze for the duration.</td><td>You must make every TTP payment on time. Miss one and HMRC can terminate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HMRC will not chase the debt outside the agreed schedule.</td><td>You must meet current tax obligations: this quarter's VAT, this month's PAYE, this year's CT all paid on time.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The current-obligations requirement is where most TTPs collapse. A director negotiates a TTP for last year's VAT, then misses this quarter's VAT because the repayments consumed the cash that was supposed to cover the new bill. The TTP terminates and the total debt is now larger than it was at the start.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress-test the numbers before we let a director sign. Can the business fund the TTP repayments and meet current obligations from forecast cash? If the answer is no, the TTP is a displacement exercise, not a solution, and the company needs a different conversation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Time to Pay vs Other HMRC Debt Options</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Best used when</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Time to Pay</td>\n<td>Single or limited HMRC debt, business is otherwise viable, called early.</td>\n<td>Only covers HMRC. Will not solve a broader creditor problem.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Informal creditor negotiation</td>\n<td>You have other creditors beyond HMRC who are open to instalments.</td>\n<td>Not legally binding. A single creditor can withdraw and force the issue.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Company Voluntary Arrangement</td>\n<td>Multiple creditors including HMRC; business viable with restructured debt.</td>\n<td>Requires 75% creditor vote; HMRC's vote often decisive. Failure ends in liquidation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administration</td>\n<td>HMRC has filed a winding-up petition or business needs the statutory moratorium.</td>\n<td>Heavier process; administrator takes control; outcome depends on saleable value.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</td>\n<td>HMRC debt is unmanageable and the business cannot be rescued.</td>\n<td>Closes the company; preserves IP choice and timing if you act before petition.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What You Should Do Right Now About HMRC Time to Pay</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you owe HMRC any tax and cannot pay by the deadline, call 0300 200 3835 today. Not tomorrow. The acceptance rate drops measurably with every week of delay.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Call before the deadline:</strong> highest success rate. HMRC treats this as the company managing its position responsibly.</li>\n<li><strong>Call within 30 days of the deadline:</strong> still strong. The debt is recent, the file clean.</li>\n<li><strong>Call after 3 months:</strong> noticeably harder. Penalties have applied; HMRC's system flags the case.</li>\n<li><strong>Call after a statutory demand:</strong> hardest of all. The case is on HMRC's enforcement track and a TTP usually requires professional representation to be considered.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have already been rejected, or if the debt is too large for TTP, Company Debt connects directors with licensed insolvency practitioners who can negotiate with HMRC on your behalf or advise on alternative routes. Take our 30-second insolvency test first if you are not sure where the company actually stands. The cases that go badly are rarely the ones where the director called too soon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/\">HMRC Tax Debt Hub</a>: overview of the HMRC debt-collection process and the routes available at each stage.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-vat/\">Cannot Pay VAT</a>: VAT-specific TTP guidance and risks.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-paye/\">Cannot Pay PAYE</a>: why HMRC negotiates least flexibly on payroll deductions.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/problems-paying-corporation-tax-hmrc/\">Problems Paying Corporation Tax</a>: CT-specific TTP and rescue options.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">Winding-Up Petitions</a>: what happens if HMRC moves to enforcement.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">Company Voluntary Arrangement</a>: the route when TTP alone cannot solve the creditor picture.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">30-Second Insolvency Test</a>: confirm where your company actually stands before negotiating.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions About HMRC Time to Pay Arrangements</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"8414-ttp-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow long can an HMRC Time to Pay arrangement last?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Typically 6 to 12 months. HMRC advisers can agree arrangements up to 12 months on the call. Longer terms are possible for larger debts but require manager approval and stronger evidence of viability.</p>\n<p>The shorter the arrangement, the more likely HMRC is to agree. A 6-month TTP signals confidence in the business; a 24-month TTP usually signals the business needs more than a payment plan.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes Time to Pay stop penalties and interest?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Interest continues to accrue throughout the TTP at the HMRC late-payment rate, which has been Bank of England base rate plus 4% since 6 April 2025.</p>\n<p>Late-payment penalties may be frozen while the TTP is active, depending on the tax type and your compliance history. The TTP stops enforcement action but does not eliminate the cost of being late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I get a second Time to Pay if my first one failed?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Possible, but significantly harder. HMRC's system records broken arrangements and the adviser will see the history. You will need to explain why the first arrangement failed and demonstrate that circumstances have changed.</p>\n<p>A second TTP with professional representation (an IP or tax adviser making the call) sometimes carries more weight than a director calling alone, particularly if the first failure was procedural rather than viability-driven.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDo I need an accountant or IP to negotiate a Time to Pay?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. You can call 0300 200 3835 yourself. For straightforward arrangements (single tax type, realistic repayment, first-time TTP), the call is simple.</p>\n<p>For complex situations (multiple tax types, large debts, previous broken arrangements, or an active statutory demand), professional representation can improve the outcome. The IP makes the same call, but with the credibility of a regulated firm and a documented assessment of the company's viability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill HMRC accept a Time to Pay if I have a personal guarantee on the debt?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC tax debts do not normally involve personal guarantees in the way bank debt does. The relevant personal-liability mechanisms for HMRC are Personal Liability Notices (under the Social Security Administration Act 1992 for unpaid NIC) and Joint and Several Liability Notices (under Finance Act 2020 for tax-avoidance and certain insolvency cases).</p>\n<p>A TTP is a company-level arrangement and does not change personal liability under those notices. If a Personal Liability Notice has been issued or is threatened, the conversation needs to cover both the company TTP and the personal exposure separately.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association.</p>\n<p>Statutory and procedural references draw on HMRC's Business Payment Support Service guidance, HMRC's late-payment interest framework (base rate plus 4% from 6 April 2025), the Insolvency Act 1986 (s.124 winding-up petition as the enforcement endpoint), and Finance Act 2020 (Joint and Several Liability Notices).</p>\n<p>Practical observations on TTP acceptance reflect cases handled by our practice and partner IP network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner and may receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network. Last reviewed May 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/difficulties-paying-hmrc\"><strong>HMRC: Pay your tax bill in instalments</strong> &mdash; official guidance on the Business Payment Support Service and TTP eligibility</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/rates-and-allowances-hmrc-interest-rates-for-late-and-early-payments\"><strong>HMRC interest rates for late and early payments</strong> &mdash; current late-payment rate of Bank of England base rate plus 4% since 6 April 2025</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; s.124 (winding-up petition presented by HMRC as creditor), Schedule 6 (preferential debts including reinstated Crown preference for VAT, PAYE, employee NIC, CIS, student-loan deductions)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/14/contents\"><strong>Finance Act 2020</strong> &mdash; Joint and Several Liability Notices and Crown preference reinstatement (1 December 2020)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/5/contents\"><strong>Social Security Administration Act 1992</strong> &mdash; section 121C (Personal Liability Notices for unpaid NIC)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tax.service.gov.uk\"><strong>HMRC Business Tax Account</strong> &mdash; check current balance, file returns, request TTP</a> - tax.service.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/find-an-insolvency-practitioner\"><strong>Find an Insolvency Practitioner (gov.uk register)</strong> &mdash; verify a liquidator's licence before instructing</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/feedback/": {
    "id": 24801,
    "slug": "feedback",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:gravityforms/form {\"formId\":\"6\",\"ajax\":true} /-->"
  },
  "/advice/what-are-fixed-and-floating-charges/": {
    "id": 24760,
    "slug": "what-are-fixed-and-floating-charges",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A fixed charge gives a lender a claim over a specific asset. A floating charge gives them a claim over a class of assets that can change.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The distinction determines who gets paid first when your company enters insolvency, and it directly affects how much is available for other creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We explain this to directors because most do not understand what their bank actually holds over the company. You signed a debenture when you took out the facility. That debenture almost certainly contains both a fixed charge over specific assets (property, specific equipment) and a floating charge over everything else (stock, debtors, cash, general equipment).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fixed charge holder ranks first in the creditor priority order. The floating charge holder ranks after preferential creditors and the prescribed part. That difference can mean the bank recovers everything while unsecured creditors get nothing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-fixed-vs-floating-charges-explained\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-fixed-vs-floating-charges-explained\">Quick Answer: Fixed vs Floating Charges Explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"hasFixedLayout\":true} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\">\n<thead>\n<tr><th></th><th>Fixed Charge</th><th>Floating Charge</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>What it covers</td><td>A specific, identified asset (property, machinery, IP)</td><td>A class of assets that changes (stock, debtors, cash)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Company's freedom</td><td>Cannot sell or dispose of the asset without consent</td><td>Can deal with the assets freely in the ordinary course</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Priority in insolvency</td><td>Ranks first, paid from the specific asset before anyone else</td><td>Ranks after costs, preferential creditors, and the prescribed part</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Crystallisation</td><td>Already attached to the specific asset</td><td>Floats over assets until a trigger event crystallises it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Registration</td><td>Must be registered at Companies House within 21 days</td><td>Must be registered at Companies House within 21 days</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-fixed-charges-work-in-practice\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-fixed-charges-work-in-practice\">How Fixed Charges Work in Practice</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A fixed charge attaches to a specific asset that the company cannot sell, transfer, or deal with without the charge holder's consent. The most common examples are charges over commercial property and specific high-value equipment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In insolvency, the fixed charge holder has a direct claim over the charged asset. The liquidator sells the asset and pays the fixed charge holder from the proceeds before anyone else sees a penny from that asset.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the asset sells for more than the secured debt, the surplus goes into the general estate. If it sells for less, the shortfall becomes an unsecured claim.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see directors who assumed their bank's charge was floating when it was actually fixed. The distinction matters because a fixed charge holder can also appoint a receiver over the specific asset (an <a href=\"/insolvency/lpa-receivership/\">LPA receiver for property</a>), bypassing the liquidator entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your bank holds a fixed charge over your commercial premises and you default, they can sell the property without waiting for the liquidation to conclude.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-floating-charges-work-in-practice\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-floating-charges-work-in-practice\">How Floating Charges Work in Practice</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A floating charge covers a class of assets rather than specific ones. It floats over the assets, allowing the company to deal with them freely in the ordinary course of business: buying and selling stock, collecting debts, spending cash, without needing the lender's consent for each transaction.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The charge crystallises (becomes fixed) when a trigger event occurs: the company enters insolvency, the company breaches the loan terms, or the lender serves a crystallisation notice. Once crystallised, the charge attaches to whatever assets are in the class at that moment, and the company can no longer deal with them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In insolvency, floating charge holders rank lower than fixed charge holders. They are paid after <a href=\"/liquidation/how-much-does-liquidation-cost/\">liquidation costs</a>, preferential creditors (employees for wages and holiday, HMRC for PAYE, VAT and employee NICs since 2020), and the prescribed part (a portion ring-fenced for unsecured creditors under section 176A). Only then does the floating charge holder receive their share.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that the restoration of HMRC's preferential status in 2020 significantly reduced what floating charge holders recover. Before 2020, HMRC ranked equally with unsecured creditors, and the floating charge holder recovered more.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Now, HMRC's preferential claims for PAYE, employee NICs, and VAT are paid before the floating charge holder. We see banks recovering 30 to 40% less from floating charges than they did before the change.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-prescribed-part-what-it-means-for-floating-charge-holders\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-prescribed-part-what-it-means-for-floating-charge-holders\">The Prescribed Part: What It Means for Floating Charge Holders</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Section 176A of the Insolvency Act requires the liquidator to set aside a portion of floating charge realisations for unsecured creditors. This is the prescribed part: 50% of the first \u00a310,000, plus 20% of anything above \u00a310,000, capped at \u00a3800,000.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The prescribed part comes out of the floating charge recovery, not the general estate. This means the floating charge holder loses a slice of their recovery to unsecured creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We mention this because it is the mechanism that occasionally produces a small dividend for unsecured creditors in cases where the floating charge would otherwise consume everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-a-bank-debenture-actually-contains\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-a-bank-debenture-actually-contains\">What a Bank Debenture Actually Contains</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most bank debentures contain both a fixed charge and a floating charge. The fixed charge typically covers freehold and leasehold property, specific plant and machinery, intellectual property, and book debts (though whether a charge over book debts is truly fixed is disputed in case law).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The floating charge typically covers stock, cash, general equipment, and any other assets not subject to the fixed charge.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We advise directors to read their debenture. Most directors signed it years ago and have never looked at it since. The debenture tells you exactly what the bank holds security over, which assets you can and cannot deal with, and what triggers crystallisation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are approaching insolvency, knowing what your bank can take is essential to understanding what will be left for other creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"why-fixed-vs-floating-matters-for-directors-personally\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"why-fixed-vs-floating-matters-for-directors-personally\">Why Fixed vs Floating Matters for Directors Personally</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The distinction affects your personal position in two ways.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>1. What is left for creditors determines wrongful trading exposure.</strong> If the bank's fixed charge consumes the most valuable asset and the floating charge (after preferential creditors and the prescribed part) consumes the rest, unsecured creditors get nothing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The liquidator will assess whether you should have recognised this earlier and stopped trading to prevent further losses to unsecured creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>2. A qualifying floating charge holder can appoint an administrator.</strong> Under Schedule B1 of the Insolvency Act, a lender holding a qualifying floating charge (one created after 15 September 2003 that covers substantially all of the company's assets) can appoint an administrator directly, without a court hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This means your bank can take control of the company's affairs through administration without your consent. We have seen banks appoint administrators over directors' objections when they lost confidence in the management.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-to-do-if-your-company-has-secured-debt\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-to-do-if-your-company-has-secured-debt\">What to Do if Your Company Has Secured Debt</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Read your debenture.</strong> Understand what charges exist, what they cover, and what triggers crystallisation.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Companies House.</strong> All charges are registered publicly. Search your company and verify which charges are on the register.</li>\n<li><strong>Engage with your bank early.</strong> If the company is in difficulty, tell the bank before they discover it from missed loan payments. Banks that are engaged early restructure. Banks that are surprised enforce.</li>\n<li><strong>Understand the priority order.</strong> Our guide on who gets paid first in liquidation explains how fixed charges, floating charges, preferential creditors, and unsecured creditors interact.</li>\n<li><strong>Take insolvency advice.</strong> A licensed insolvency practitioner can assess how the security structure affects your options. A free, confidential consultation will clarify your position.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-on-fixed-and-floating-charges\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-on-fixed-and-floating-charges\">FAQs on Fixed and Floating Charges</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"6f3d8b91-2c54-4a17-9e8b-d4f1c2a8b605\",\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the difference between a fixed and floating charge?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A fixed charge attaches to a specific asset (property, equipment) and prevents you from dealing with it without the lender's consent. A floating charge covers a class of changing assets (stock, debtors, cash) and allows you to deal with them freely until a trigger event causes it to crystallise. Fixed charges rank first in insolvency. Floating charges rank lower.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes my bank hold a fixed or floating charge?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Most bank debentures contain both. The fixed charge covers specific assets (property, IP) and the floating charge covers everything else. Check your debenture document or search your company's charges at Companies House to see exactly what is registered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a floating charge holder appoint an administrator?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, if the floating charge was created after 15 September 2003 and covers substantially all of the company's assets. The charge holder can appoint an administrator directly without a court hearing. This gives the bank significant power to take control of the company without your consent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the prescribed part?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A portion of floating charge realisations ring-fenced for unsecured creditors: 50% of the first \u00a310,000, plus 20% above that, capped at \u00a3800,000. This comes out of the floating charge recovery and is the main reason unsecured creditors occasionally receive a small dividend in cases where a floating charge exists.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat triggers a floating charge to crystallise?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The most common triggers are entry into insolvency (administration or liquidation), breach of the loan covenants, cessation of trading, or the lender serving an automatic crystallisation notice (where the debenture provides for one). Once crystallised, the charge attaches to whatever assets are in the class at that moment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow do I check what charges are registered against my company?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Search your company on Companies House (free) and open the \"Charges\" tab. You will see every registered charge, the date it was created, the chargee (the lender), and whether the charge is satisfied or still outstanding. The actual debenture wording is held by you and the lender, not Companies House. If you cannot find your copy, ask the lender to send one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association.</p>\n<p>Statutory references draw on the Companies Act 2006 (Part 25 on the registration of company charges), the Insolvency Act 1986 (section 175 on preferential debts, section 176A on the prescribed part, Schedule B1 on administration appointments by qualifying floating charge holders), and the Finance Act 2020 (HMRC secondary preferential creditor status from 1 December 2020).</p>\n<p>Last reviewed May 2026.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner. We may also receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/part/25\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, Part 25</strong> &mdash; the registration regime for company charges, the 21-day filing deadline, and the consequences of failure to register</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/175\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, section 175</strong> &mdash; preferential debts in liquidation, including employees and HMRC's secondary preferential claim</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/176A\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, section 176A</strong> &mdash; the prescribed part of floating charge realisations ring-fenced for unsecured creditors</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/schedule/B1\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, Schedule B1</strong> &mdash; administration framework, including paragraph 14 (appointment by a qualifying floating charge holder)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/14/section/98\"><strong>Finance Act 2020, Section 98</strong> &mdash; HMRC secondary preferential creditor status for PAYE, employee NICs and VAT from 1 December 2020</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2003/2097/contents/made\"><strong>Insolvency (Prescribed Part) Order 2003 (and 2020 amendment)</strong> &mdash; the formula for the prescribed part and the \u00a3800,000 cap</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/\"><strong>Companies House &mdash; Search the register</strong> &mdash; public access to registered charges on UK companies</a> - find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/what-is-a-freezing-order-or-injunction/": {
    "id": 24434,
    "slug": "what-is-a-freezing-order-or-injunction",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A freezing order, sometimes still called a Mareva injunction after the 1975 case that invented it, is a court order that locks your assets in place before trial. You cannot dispose of them, transfer them, charge them, or spend more than the ordinary-living allowance the order sets.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is granted under CPR 25.1(1)(f) and section 37 of the Senior Courts Act 1981, usually without notice, and usually because a judge has been persuaded there is a real risk you will dissipate assets before the claimant gets judgment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have just been served one, the calendar matters. You normally have seven days to the return-date hearing at which the order is reviewed. Every movement of money during that window is scrutinised. Breach is contempt of court, punishable by fine, seizure, or imprisonment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What follows covers what the order actually locks down, what the ordinary-course-of-business exception really allows, how the order interacts with insolvency proceedings, and what your options are for challenging or varying it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-a-freezing-order-actually-does\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-a-freezing-order-actually-does\">What a Freezing Order Actually Does</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A freezing order is not a seizure, you still own the assets and they stay where they are. The order prohibits you from \"removing from the jurisdiction or in any way disposing of, dealing with or diminishing\" assets up to a stated maximum value. The standard form is annexed to Practice Direction 25A.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Domestic freezing order</strong>, covers assets inside England and Wales. Bank accounts, real property, vehicles, company shares, inventory.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Worldwide freezing order (WFO)</strong>, covers assets everywhere. Courts grant WFOs where there is evidence the defendant has assets abroad and a real risk of dissipation. The claimant usually has to undertake not to enforce the order in a foreign jurisdiction without the English court's permission.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two consequences follow immediately. Your bank will freeze accounts the moment it is served or notified (banks are on hair-trigger notice). And you must serve an asset-disclosure affidavit, normally within 48 hours, listing every asset above a stated threshold, with values and locations.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The affidavit is evidence in the main proceedings; false disclosure is perjury as well as contempt. In our experience, directors underestimate how quickly the bank acts, and how complete the affidavit needs to be.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"why-courts-grant-a-freezing-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"why-courts-grant-a-freezing-order\">Why Courts Grant a Freezing Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The test the claimant must meet is set out in the authorities, principally <em>The Niedersachsen</em> and <em>Fiona Trust v Privalov</em>. Four elements:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A good arguable case</strong> on the substantive claim. Not a trial on the merits, a reasonably arguable set of allegations.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Assets within the jurisdiction</strong> (or, for a WFO, abroad) that could satisfy an eventual judgment.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A real risk of dissipation</strong>. This is the decisive element. The court wants evidence that the defendant is not merely an ordinary debtor but someone who will move assets to defeat a judgment. Evidence often cited: offshore structures, sudden restructurings, unexplained transfers to family members, dishonesty allegations in the underlying claim.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The balance of convenience</strong>. The claimant undertakes in damages, to compensate the defendant if the order turns out to be wrong, and the court weighs harm to the defendant against harm to the claimant.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Freezing orders are usually made on a without-notice application. The claimant has a duty of full and frank disclosure, every material fact, including facts adverse to the claimant's case. If the claimant omits something material, the order can be set aside at the return date purely on that ground, irrespective of the underlying merits.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our experience is that this duty is breached more often than defendants realise, and pursuing it early is usually worthwhile.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"immediate-risks-for-directors-and-businesses\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"immediate-risks-for-directors-and-businesses\">Immediate Risks for Directors and Businesses</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A freezing order against a trading company is a commercial emergency even when the substantive claim is weak. We see directors focus on the litigation and overlook the solvency question it triggers, often to their cost. The order itself triggers a chain:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Bank disruption</strong>, accounts frozen immediately. Wages, VAT payments, supplier DDs all stop until the ordinary-course-of-business exception is clarified with the bank in writing.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Supplier panic</strong>, word travels. Factors call in facilities, suppliers demand pro-forma terms, insurers review cover.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Contempt exposure</strong>, every payment you make from now on is potentially a breach. Paying an old creditor who shouts loudest is not the \"ordinary course of business\"; it is a preference and a contempt.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading exposure</strong>, if the company was already near the line on <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">the insolvency test</a>, a freezing order often tips it over. Trading on while unable to pay debts as they fall due is a section 214 wrongful trading exposure for every director who knows the position.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal assets</strong>, if the freezing order names you personally (common in fraud claims, dishonest-assistance claims, and Serious Fraud Office applications), your salary, home equity, and family savings are all in scope.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"responding-to-a-freezing-order-the-first-72-hours\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"responding-to-a-freezing-order-the-first-72-hours\">Responding to a Freezing Order: The First 72 Hours</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Read the order in full</strong>, paying particular attention to the asset cap (the maximum value frozen), the return date, the ordinary-course exception, the living-allowance figure, and the penal notice at the foot. Do not act on memory or on a summary someone else read to you.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Instruct specialist counsel the same day</strong>. Freezing-order work is a specialism; general commercial litigators will assemble a team but a silk familiar with <em>JSC BTA Bank v Ablyazov</em> and the Commercial Court freezing-order lists is what you want steering decisions.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Contact your bank in writing</strong>, acknowledging the order and proposing which outgoing payments fall within the ordinary course of business (wages, rent, statutory obligations, existing contractual commitments). The bank's compliance desk wants paper, not phone calls.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Prepare the asset disclosure affidavit</strong>. Full and frank. Include assets held by nominees, trusts, offshore entities. A defective affidavit is the easiest route to contempt and the hardest to recover from.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Preserve documents</strong>. Email, WhatsApp, accounting system records. Standard litigation holds apply.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Assess solvency honestly</strong>. If the order makes the company unable to pay debts as they fall due, directors' duties shift under <em>BTI v Sequana</em>, creditor interests must now be considered. A licensed IP conversation is prudent in parallel with the litigation response.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-ordinary-course-of-business-exception-what-it-really-covers\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-ordinary-course-of-business-exception-what-it-really-covers\">The Ordinary Course of Business Exception, What It Really Covers</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every standard-form freezing order permits payments \"in the ordinary and proper course of business\" and payments on reasonable legal advice and for reasonable living expenses. The limits of the exception are tighter than companies tend to assume.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Wages, PAYE, VAT, Corporation Tax, rent, utilities, existing contractual commitments</strong>, generally within the exception, provided they are routine, quantified, and not advanced to defeat the order.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Paying down specific pre-existing debts to preferred creditors</strong>, generally outside. This is the preference trap. A payment to your best supplier because you want to keep the relationship is not \"ordinary course\".</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Legal fees</strong>, within the exception up to a cap (often \u00a3250,000 on substantial matters, but set on the face of the order). Bills above the cap require a separate application.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Personal living expenses</strong>, the order names a weekly figure. Going over it is a breach unless varied by consent or by application.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Large capital items</strong>, new vehicles, property purchases, investments: outside the exception. Needs an application to the court.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ambiguity is resolved by application, not by assumption. A CPR 23 application to vary the order takes days, not months, and is materially cheaper than a contempt finding. Our team works with your litigation solicitors on these applications where the solvency angle needs to be factored in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"freezing-orders-and-insolvency-where-they-overlap\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"freezing-orders-and-insolvency-where-they-overlap\">Freezing Orders and Insolvency, Where They Overlap</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Freezing orders and insolvency proceedings interact in three practical ways that catch directors out.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If you present a petition while frozen</strong>, a winding-up petition, an administration application, or your own <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a> resolution, the freezing-order regime continues to bind you personally. The insolvency practitioner, once appointed, steps into the assets, but the director remains bound by the disclosure obligations and contempt risk.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If a creditor presents a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> against the company</strong>, the Insolvency Act 1986 section 127 then bites in parallel with the freezing order. Post-petition dispositions are void unless the court validates them, which means the company cannot safely transact even on \"ordinary course\" payments without a validation order from the Companies Court.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>If the company is placed into administration or liquidation</strong>, the moratorium does not automatically discharge the freezing order against the company. The appointed IP usually applies to vary or discharge it so the estate can be realised.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A freezing order against a director personally survives corporate insolvency. The director's personal disclosure obligations, personal living allowance, and personal contempt exposure continue regardless of what happens to the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For directors running several companies, this is the detail that tends to surprise most, and it is the point where our involvement alongside your litigation team adds the most value.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"challenging-or-varying-a-freezing-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"challenging-or-varying-a-freezing-order\">Challenging or Varying a Freezing Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You have three standard routes:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Set aside at the return date</strong>. The claimant must justify the order afresh. Your grounds: weak underlying claim, no real risk of dissipation, material non-disclosure at the without-notice hearing, disproportionate sum, availability of a less intrusive remedy (security for costs, caveat on land).</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Vary</strong>. Keep the order in place but change specific terms: raise the living allowance, uncap legal fees, permit a specific transaction, release a specific asset, reduce the sum frozen.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Provide alternative security</strong>. A bank guarantee, payment into court, or charge over a specific asset up to the frozen sum. Once accepted, the freezing order is discharged.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Material non-disclosure is, in our experience, the most productive ground to push on in the first 14 days. Claimants applying without notice routinely overstate the risk of dissipation; they under-disclose settlement correspondence, commercial explanations for transactions the claim makes sinister, or prior failed attempts at security.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An affidavit from the defendant's side reconstructing what the claimant knew and did not say is often decisive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"state-freezing-powers-poca-uwos-and-hmrc\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"state-freezing-powers-poca-uwos-and-hmrc\">State Freezing Powers: POCA, UWOs, and HMRC</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Freezing orders granted to private claimants under CPR 25 are only part of the picture. State-agency freezes operate under separate statutory regimes and carry different tactical considerations:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Proceeds of Crime Act 2002</strong>, restraint orders in criminal cases; account-freezing orders (AFOs) in civil-recovery proceedings. Both bypass the standard CPR 25 framework and have their own disclosure regimes.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Unexplained Wealth Orders</strong> (Criminal Finances Act 2017), used by the NCA, SFO, HMRC, and others against politically exposed persons and suspected serious-criminality defendants. Reverse the burden of proof on asset provenance.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>HMRC Security Deposits and Direct Recovery of Debts</strong>, HMRC can freeze accounts under the DRD regime (Finance (No. 2) Act 2015) for unpaid tax above \u00a31,000 held in accessible funds above \u00a35,000, without a court order.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A state freeze is not a private-party litigation move and you should not respond to it as if it were. For HMRC specifically, our <a href=\"/hmrc/hmrc-enforcement-action/\">HMRC enforcement action</a> guide covers the DRD regime in detail. Where our involvement is relevant, it is usually on the insolvency and creditor-management side rather than the criminal-defence side, where specialist counsel leads.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-a-freezing-order\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-a-freezing-order\">Your Next Step on a Freezing Order</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two immediate calls, to specialist counsel and to an insolvency adviser, plus a written communication to the bank, are the minimum viable response within 24 hours of being served. A freezing order is reversible, narrowable, and sometimes discharged entirely on the return date; none of that happens without early, experienced input.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We have seen orders discharged in full at the return date where the without-notice application was inadequately supported; do not assume the order will stand.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where the freezing order overlaps with cash-flow distress, creditor pressure, or a pending <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>, call us free on 0800 074 6757. Our licensed IPs work alongside your litigation team so solvency, validation orders, and the ordinary-course-of-business exception are handled coherently rather than in isolation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"freezing-order-faqs\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"freezing-order-faqs\">Freezing Order FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"24434-fo-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I still pay wages under a freezing order?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, wages are squarely within the ordinary-course-of-business exception in every standard-form order, and PAYE/NIC on those wages follows. Tell your bank in writing that the payroll run is within the exception, with a copy of the order, and keep the exchange on file. If the bank resists, a one-paragraph application to the court will clarify it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens if I accidentally breach a freezing order?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Contempt of court is a strict-liability exposure on the act but the penalty reflects intent. An honest mistake, a standing order that should have been cancelled, a routine payment that turned out to be outside \"ordinary course\", is usually dealt with by prompt self-report, explanation, and undertaking not to repeat.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Concealment or repetition moves it toward imprisonment. The rule in practice: if you realise a breach has occurred, tell your solicitor within the hour and inform the court through the claimant's solicitors the same day.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow long does a freezing order last?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Until judgment in the substantive claim, unless earlier discharged. A without-notice order is always reviewed at the return date (normally 7 days after grant); from there it runs to trial or discharge. Large commercial freezing orders routinely remain in force for two to four years while the main claim is litigated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill a freezing order appear on public records?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The order itself is not automatically public, but claimants routinely apply to notify third parties (banks, registries, corporate counterparties) who then act on it. Companies House is sometimes notified where a directorship is relevant; HM Land Registry is typically notified where real property is in scope. Reputationally, freezing orders rarely stay confidential once a WFO is in effect.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes insolvency discharge a freezing order?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not automatically. Administration and liquidation create a moratorium over most legal process against the company, but freezing orders are typically pursued to variation or discharge by application. A freezing order against a director personally is not affected by the company's insolvency; the director's disclosure and spending obligations continue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I get my legal fees out of frozen assets?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, up to the cap on the face of the order (commonly \u00a3150,000\u2013\u00a3500,000 in substantial cases). Bills above that cap need an application to vary. The claimant is entitled to see invoices in redacted form. Fees for defending criminal proceedings run alongside are treated on their own standard, normally more generously.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"methodology-amp-disclosure\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"methodology-amp-disclosure\">Methodology &amp; Disclosure</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This guide is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by licensed insolvency practitioners who work alongside specialist commercial litigators on freezing-order cases.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Statutory and procedural references are to section 37 of the Senior Courts Act 1981, CPR Part 25 and Practice Direction 25A, the Insolvency Act 1986, the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, the Criminal Finances Act 2017, and the Finance (No. 2) Act 2015 (DRD).</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Case references cited reflect leading authorities at the time of writing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt does not litigate freezing orders. Our role is to work with the director's litigation team to handle the solvency, validation-order, and enforcement interaction. The 0800 number is a free confidential consultation on the insolvency and creditor-action elements; our licensed IPs can act the same day where the position is urgent.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1981/54/section/37\"><strong>Senior Courts Act 1981, section 37</strong> &mdash; the statutory power under which the High Court grants freezing injunctions, cited as the foundational authority throughout this guide</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.justice.gov.uk/courts/procedure-rules/civil/rules/part25\"><strong>Civil Procedure Rules, Part 25 and PD 25A</strong> &mdash; procedural framework governing interim injunctions, including CPR 25.1(1)(f) under which freezing orders are granted and the return-date timetable</a> - justice.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/127\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, section 127</strong> &mdash; void-disposition rule that bites once a winding-up petition is presented, running in parallel with a freezing order and discussed in the insolvency-interaction section</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2002/29/contents\"><strong>Proceeds of Crime Act 2002</strong> &mdash; statutory basis for restraint orders and account-freezing orders used by state agencies outside the CPR 25 framework, covered in the state-freezing-powers section</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2015/33/contents\"><strong>Finance (No. 2) Act 2015 (Direct Recovery of Debts)</strong> &mdash; statutory basis for HMRC's DRD regime, which can freeze bank accounts for unpaid tax without a court order</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/advice/what-are-the-duties-and-responsibilities-of-a-company-director/": {
    "id": 24402,
    "slug": "what-are-the-duties-and-responsibilities-of-a-company-director",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every UK company director has seven statutory duties under the Companies Act 2006. They are not guidelines. They are legal obligations, and breaching them creates personal liability that limited liability does not protect you from.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that most directors of small companies cannot name more than one or two of these duties. That is a problem, because the duties are the standard against which the liquidator measures your conduct if the company enters insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director who can demonstrate they understood and followed their duties has a defensible position. A director who did not know what their duties were has a harder case to make. We have set out all seven below in plain English, with the practical implications for directors of companies in financial difficulty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"quick-answer-the-seven-duties-of-a-uk-company-director\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"quick-answer-the-seven-duties-of-a-uk-company-director\">Quick Answer: The Seven Duties of a UK Company Director</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Act within your powers</strong> (section 171)</li>\n<li><strong>Promote the success of the company</strong> (section 172): shifts to creditor interests when insolvency is probable</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise independent judgement</strong> (section 173)</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise reasonable care, skill, and diligence</strong> (section 174)</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid conflicts of interest</strong> (section 175)</li>\n<li><strong>Not accept benefits from third parties</strong> (section 176)</li>\n<li><strong>Declare interest in proposed transactions</strong> (section 177)</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These duties are owed to the company, not to individual shareholders or creditors. But when the company is insolvent or approaching insolvency, section 172 is modified: you must act in the interests of creditors. This is the duty shift that underpins every wrongful trading claim and every conduct investigation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duty-1-act-within-your-powers-section-171\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duty-1-act-within-your-powers-section-171\">Director Duty 1: Act Within Your Powers (Section 171)</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You must act in accordance with the company's constitution and exercise your powers only for the purposes they were given. In practice, this means: do not use company money for personal expenses, do not exceed the authority the articles give you, and do not take actions that the shareholders have not authorised.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see breaches of this duty most commonly in small companies where the distinction between the director's personal money and the company's money has blurred over years.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director who uses the company debit card for personal shopping, pays personal bills from the company account, or takes cash without proper documentation is breaching section 171 as well as creating an overdrawn director's loan account that the liquidator will pursue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duty-2-promote-the-success-of-the-company-section-172\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duty-2-promote-the-success-of-the-company-section-172\">Director Duty 2: Promote the Success of the Company (Section 172)</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the most important duty for directors of companies in financial difficulty. You must act in the way you consider, in good faith, would be most likely to promote the success of the company for the benefit of its members (shareholders) as a whole.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When the company is solvent, \"success\" means shareholder value. When the company is insolvent or bordering on insolvency, the duty shifts: you must act in the interests of creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Supreme Court confirmed this in <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022]. The closer the company is to insolvency, the more weight creditors' interests carry. At the point of insolvency, they become paramount.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We stress this because the duty shift is what creates wrongful trading exposure. A director who pays a dividend to shareholders when the company is insolvent is prioritising shareholder interests over creditor interests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director who continues to trade, accumulating more debt, when insolvent liquidation is unavoidable is failing to minimise creditor losses. Both are breaches of the modified section 172 duty.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duty-3-exercise-independent-judgement-section-173\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duty-3-exercise-independent-judgement-section-173\">Director Duty 3: Exercise Independent Judgement (Section 173)</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You must not simply follow instructions from others without applying your own judgement. This applies even if the instruction comes from a majority shareholder, a co-director, or a professional adviser. You can take advice, but the decision must be yours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see this duty breached in family companies where a dominant founder gives instructions to a nominee director (a spouse, a child, a friend appointed to the board). The nominee rubber-stamps every decision without questioning it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company enters insolvency, the nominee director is held to the same standard as the founder. \"I just did what they told me\" is not a defence. It is a confession of breaching section 173.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duty-4-exercise-reasonable-care-skill-and-diligence-section-174\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duty-4-exercise-reasonable-care-skill-and-diligence-section-174\">Director Duty 4: Exercise Reasonable Care, Skill, and Diligence (Section 174)</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The standard is both subjective and objective. You are measured against: (a) your actual knowledge, skill, and experience, and (b) the knowledge, skill, and experience that a reasonably diligent person carrying out your functions would have. The higher of the two applies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find this is the duty that catches directors who say \"I did not know the company was insolvent.\" The objective test asks: should you have known? If a reasonably diligent director in your position, with access to the same information, would have recognised the insolvency, your lack of awareness is not a defence. It is a failure of diligence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We tell directors: read your management accounts. Ask questions when the numbers do not look right. If you do not understand the accounts, get them explained. The duty requires you to be engaged with the company's financial position, not to be a financial expert, but to be a diligent non-expert who asks the right questions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--review\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Case Review</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure Which Route Fits Your Company?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Every situation is different. Get a free, no-obligation review from a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner and understand your options before deciding anything.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/contact/\">Book Free Review &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free review</span><span>No obligation</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"director-duties-5-to-7-conflicts-benefits-and-declarations\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"director-duties-5-to-7-conflicts-benefits-and-declarations\">Director Duties 5 to 7: Conflicts, Benefits, and Declarations</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These three duties deal with conflicts of interest and personal benefits. We cover them in detail in our conflicts of interest guide.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In summary: avoid situations where your interests conflict with the company's, do not accept benefits from third parties that arise from your position, and declare any interest you have in a proposed transaction with the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"what-happens-when-directors-breach-their-duties\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"what-happens-when-directors-breach-their-duties\">What Happens When Directors Breach Their Duties</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Breaches of directors' duties can result in:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Misfeasance claims (section 212, Insolvency Act):</strong> The liquidator recovers losses caused by the breach. This is a personal liability.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrongful trading (section 214):</strong> A personal contribution order for the increase in creditor losses during the period you should have stopped trading.</li>\n<li><strong>Director disqualification:</strong> 2 to 15 year ban from acting as a director.</li>\n<li><strong>Account of profits:</strong> You hand over any personal profit made from the breach.</li>\n<li><strong>Transaction reversal:</strong> Conflicted transactions can be voided by the company or the liquidator.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We find that most directors who face consequences in insolvency are not charged with one dramatic breach. They face a pattern of small failures: late filings, poor records, continued trading past the tipping point, selective creditor payments, and undeclared conflicts. The pattern, assessed cumulatively, is what triggers disqualification and misfeasance claims.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"how-directors-fulfil-their-duties-when-in-difficulty\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"how-directors-fulfil-their-duties-when-in-difficulty\">How Directors Fulfil Their Duties When in Difficulty</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Recognise the duty shift.</strong> When insolvency is probable, you must act in creditors' interests. Stop paying dividends. Stop prioritising shareholders.</li>\n<li><strong>Seek professional advice.</strong> The date you sought advice is evidence of diligence under section 174.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep proper records.</strong> Board minutes, financial accounts, decision documentation. These are your evidence of compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not make selective payments.</strong> Pay in the ordinary course only. No preferences to connected parties.</li>\n<li><strong>Declare conflicts.</strong> If you have any interest in any transaction with the company, declare it formally.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt connects directors with licensed insolvency practitioners who can assess your conduct position. A free, confidential consultation will tell you whether you are fulfilling your duties and what to do if the company is approaching insolvency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-24402-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about your director conduct and the duty shift\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs on Directors' Duties and Responsibilities</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-24402\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24402-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-24402\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24402\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What happens if I breach my duties as a director?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-24402\">\n<p>You face personal liability for losses, account of profits, transaction reversal, misfeasance claims in liquidation, and potential director disqualification. The consequences depend on the nature and severity of the breach.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24402-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-24402\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24402\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>When do my duties shift from shareholders to creditors?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-24402\">\n<p>When the company is insolvent or bordering on insolvency. The Supreme Court in Sequana (2022) confirmed this is a sliding scale: the closer to insolvency, the more weight creditors' interests carry. At the point of insolvency, creditors' interests become paramount.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24402-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-24402\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24402\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I be disqualified for breaching my duties?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-24402\">\n<p>Yes. Persistent or serious breaches of directors' duties contribute to a finding of unfitness under the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986. Disqualification orders range from 2 to 15 years and prevent you from acting as a director of any company.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-24402-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-24402\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-24402\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Do these duties apply to non-executive directors?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-24402\">\n<p>Yes, in full. The seven general duties under the Companies Act 2006 apply equally to executive and non-executive directors, and to shadow directors. The objective standard under section 174 is moderated by the role you accepted: a non-executive director is not expected to run the day-to-day, but they are expected to read papers, ask questions, and engage with the financial position.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-24402\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This article was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the IPA.</p>\n<p>It is based on the Companies Act 2006 (sections 170 to 177 directors' general duties), <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25, the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 212 to 214), the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, and practical experience from cases handled by licensed practitioners in our network.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial service that connects business owners with insolvency professionals. We may receive a fee when you engage a practitioner through our service. References are current for England and Wales as of April 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/part/10/chapter/2\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, sections 170&ndash;177</strong> &mdash; codified general duties of directors, including the duty to promote the success of the company and the shift to creditor interests when insolvency is in prospect</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.supremecourt.uk/cases/uksc-2019-0046.html\"><strong>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA [2022] UKSC 25</strong> &mdash; Supreme Court ruling confirming the creditor-duty shift under section 172 and when it engages</a> - supremecourt.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, sections 212&ndash;214</strong> &mdash; misfeasance, fraudulent trading, and wrongful trading provisions used to pursue directors after liquidation</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, sections 6&ndash;8</strong> &mdash; statutory framework for disqualification orders following conduct reports from liquidators and the Insolvency Service</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/companies-house\"><strong>Companies House</strong> &mdash; official guidance on running a limited company, filing obligations, and the public director record</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/insolvency-service\"><strong>Insolvency Service</strong> &mdash; guidance for directors of insolvent companies and the body that investigates conduct after liquidation</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\"><strong>Insolvency Practitioners Association</strong> &mdash; recognised regulator with a licensed IP search to verify a liquidator before instructing</a> - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/winding-up-petitions/": {
    "id": 7687,
    "slug": "winding-up-petitions",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A winding-up petition is a creditor asking the court to close your company down and sell what it owns to pay the debt. It is a request, not the decision. The decision is a separate thing called a winding-up order, and it comes later, at a hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That difference is your whole position at the moment. Until the court makes the order, the company is still yours to run and you still have choices about how this ends.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The date of that hearing is printed on the petition you were given, the copy carrying the court's stamp. That date is the one that governs your case, and no general rule of thumb does. If you read anywhere that a hearing always falls a set number of days after the petition arrives, that page is guessing at something the court decides case by case.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The hearing is not the deadline to work to, though. There is an earlier one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At some point after the petition is delivered to you, the creditor has to publish a formal notice about it in The Gazette. The Gazette is the government's official journal of public notices, it is free, and anyone can search it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Publishing that notice is what the law calls advertising the petition. It is a phrase we end up explaining on almost every call, because nothing about the word advertising suggests what it actually means.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once the notice is out, your problem stops being private. Banks check The Gazette as a matter of routine, so yours may see it and restrict the account. Other creditors may see it too, and can join in behind the first one. Most of the real damage happens in that period, well before anyone gets near a courtroom.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--scope\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>This Guide Covers England and Wales</strong></p>\n<p>Scotland and Northern Ireland run separate court procedures with their own rules and terminology. If your company is registered there, use the <a href=\"https://www.mygov.scot/wind-up-company\">Scottish Government guidance</a> or the <a href=\"https://www.economy-ni.gov.uk/articles/winding-company-owes-you-money\">Northern Ireland Department for the Economy guidance</a> instead.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Find Your Stage First</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors ring us unsure whether the thing on the desk is a threat, a live court proceeding, or the end of it. Those are three different situations with three different answers, and which one you are in decides who you should be calling this afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>And if the envelope sat unopened for a day or two first, that is ordinary rather than negligent. Worth knowing before you read on: the position is usually less final than it feels at the moment of opening it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>What has happened</th><th>What it means</th><th>Immediate action</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Creditor warning or threat</td>\n<td>No formal step has been taken. There may be no insolvency deadline running at all yet.</td>\n<td>Check the deadline the creditor has actually stated, the debt, and whether the company can pay. You have the most room you will ever have.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statutory demand received</td>\n<td>A formal written demand giving the company 21 days to pay. Serious, but nothing has been filed at court yet.</td>\n<td>Act inside those 21 days. Look at paying, at agreeing terms, or at whether the demand can be challenged.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Petition filed at court and delivered to you</td>\n<td>Court proceedings are live. You are still in control of the company.</td>\n<td>Write down the hearing date. Get insolvency and legal advice before the advertisement window opens.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Petition advertised, or the bank has restricted the account</td>\n<td>The problem is public and trading may be disrupted.</td>\n<td>Coordinate an insolvency practitioner and a specialist solicitor together. Deal with cashflow and the petition as two separate problems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Winding-up order made</td>\n<td>The company is in compulsory liquidation and your powers as director have ended.</td>\n<td>Cooperate with the Official Receiver, the government officer who now controls the company. Take advice at once if you want to ask the court to cancel the order.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Speak to a licensed insolvency practitioner now</strong> if a petition has been presented against your company. We arrange specialist insolvency-solicitor support where court action is needed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--key\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>A Petition Has Been Served: Do These Five Things Now</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the petition properly, all of it. Note the <strong>hearing date</strong>, which court it is in, which creditor has brought it, and the exact amount they say they are owed.</li>\n<li>Stop any unusual payments. Nothing moved or sold, nothing paid to a creditor connected to you or your family, no clearing a director's loan account, until you have taken advice.</li>\n<li>Work out which this is: a debt you can genuinely dispute, a debt you can pay, or a debt beyond the company's means.</li>\n<li>Instruct an <strong>insolvency solicitor</strong> if you dispute the debt, or if you need to ask the court to stop the public notice being published.</li>\n<li>Instruct a <strong>licensed insolvency practitioner</strong> on viability, negotiation, rescue, or closure.</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What a Winding-Up Petition Is</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A winding-up petition is a formal application to the court asking it to wind up a company compulsorily. The grounds sit in <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/122\">section 122 of the Insolvency Act 1986</a>, the insolvency tests in <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/123\">section 123</a>, and the question of who may petition in <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/124\">section 124</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A creditor generally needs to be owed <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/wind-up-a-company-that-owes-you-money\"><strong>at least \u00a3750</strong></a>, and to show the court that the company cannot pay its debts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The familiar way of showing that is an unpaid statutory demand: a formal written demand giving the company 21 days to pay. It is not the only way, though, and it is not required. An unpaid county court judgment will do it, and so will bailiffs who came away with nothing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That trips directors up regularly. Someone who never saw a demand decides the petition must be defective, waits for the mistake to be conceded, and spends the window that mattered waiting.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it is</th><th>Immediate effect</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Statutory demand</td><td>A formal written demand for payment, and one way of evidencing that a company cannot pay.</td><td>Not a court order. It does not liquidate anything.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Winding-up petition</td><td>A court application asking for compulsory liquidation.</td><td>Proceedings are active, but directors remain in control until an order is made.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Winding-up order</td><td>The court's decision to wind the company up.</td><td>Compulsory liquidation begins and directors' powers cease.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compulsory liquidation</td><td>The insolvency procedure that follows the order.</td><td>The Official Receiver, and later any appointed liquidator, controls and sells the assets.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Petitions are heard in the <strong>Insolvency and Companies List</strong> of the High Court, or in a District Registry or County Court hearing centre with insolvency jurisdiction. You will still see the old name, Companies Court, in older guidance and correspondence.</p>\n<p>Our guide to <a href=\"/liquidation/winding-up-petition-vs-compulsory-liquidation/\">winding-up petitions versus compulsory liquidation</a> sets out the difference between the application and the outcome in more detail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC petitions often. Directors who have negotiated with HMRC before are usually surprised by how much narrower the room becomes once a petition exists, which is why we cover it separately in our guide to <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/dealing-with-an-hmrc-winding-up-petition/\">dealing with an HMRC winding-up petition</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--key\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>How to Check Whether a Petition Exists</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Search <a href=\"https://www.thegazette.co.uk\">The Gazette</a> using the company name and company number.</li>\n<li>Check Companies House and the official insolvency information via <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/find-out-if-a-company-is-in-financial-trouble\">GOV.UK's guidance on finding out if a company is in financial trouble</a>.</li>\n<li>Compare anything you find against the court-stamped petition you were given, and the hearing details printed on it.</li>\n<li>Do not treat absence from one public source as proof that no petition exists. A petition can be presented and served well before it is advertised.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Happens After the Petition Is Served</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below is the shape of it. Some of these points are fixed by the rules and you can plan around them. The rest depend on your case, your court's list and your bank, and anyone quoting you dates for those is inventing them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The stretch between service and advertisement is the one directors squander, usually on phone calls to the creditor that go nowhere while the advertisement window opens behind them. It is also the stretch where the options are widest. Not a coincidence: the options are wide precisely because the problem is still private.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Your risk or decision</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Petition presented (filed at court)</td><td>The creditor lodges the petition and the court gives it a hearing date. This can happen without you knowing.</td><td>This is the date that counts later. If an order is eventually made, payments made from here on can be undone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Petition served (delivered to you)</td><td>A copy is formally delivered to the company, usually at its registered office.</td><td>Record the hearing date. Take advice that week, while there is still something to work with.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gazette advertisement</td><td>The public notice appears in The Gazette. Normally no earlier than seven business days after the petition was delivered, and at least seven business days before the hearing.</td><td>Banks and other creditors may become aware. This is usually when the pressure becomes public.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Before the hearing</td><td>Debt, dispute, rescue, closure, and banking all have to be dealt with in parallel.</td><td>Evidence takes time to assemble. So does coordinating an IP and a solicitor.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The hearing</td><td>The court considers the petition, the company's position, and any supporting creditors.</td><td>An order, dismissal, adjournment, or other directions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Winding-up order</td><td>The Official Receiver takes control of the company.</td><td>Directors lose management authority and must cooperate with the investigation.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--legal\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>The Advertisement Rule, in Full</strong></p>\n<p>Unless the court directs otherwise, the Gazette notice for a creditor's petition must normally be published:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least <strong>seven business days after</strong> the petition has been served on the company; and</li>\n<li>at least <strong>seven business days before</strong> the hearing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/rule/7.10\">Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, rule 7.10</a>. Both limbs matter. The first is the gap in which urgent work to restrain advertisement is realistically possible.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is also a certificate the creditor has to file confirming they published the notice properly, which is due at least <strong>five business days before the hearing</strong>. And at the far end, if an order is made against the company, <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/cancel-winding-up-order\">an application to cancel it</a> should be made within <strong>five working days</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What nobody can fix in advance is when the court lists the hearing, when your bank finds out, whether it restricts the account, or how long a settlement, restraint, validation or rescue application takes to run. Those are the parts people most want a date for. They are exactly the parts that do not have one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your Options After Receiving a Petition</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Dealing with the petition and keeping the business running are separate problems, and running them together is the most common mistake we see. They take different remedies, and usually different professionals.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Put all the effort into the cashflow side and you can reach the hearing having made payroll and having done nothing whatever about the petition. Both need running at once, from the same week.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ways to Resolve, Oppose, or Restructure</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Possible route</th><th>The qualification that matters</th><th>Who you need</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds</td>\n<td>Urgent advice on opposing the petition, or on asking the court to block the public notice</td>\n<td>A petition is not the right forum for deciding a genuinely disputed debt, but the evidence and the timing are everything</td>\n<td>Insolvency solicitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The company can pay or secure the debt and the costs</td>\n<td>Negotiate settlement and formal withdrawal or dismissal</td>\n<td>Payment alone does not end the court process. Costs and other creditors still have to be dealt with</td>\n<td>Solicitor, with an IP where solvency is uncertain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The business is viable but cannot pay right now</td>\n<td>Negotiate, or seek an adjournment where there is something concrete to adjourn for</td>\n<td>Neither the petitioner nor the court has to accept a proposal, and a vague one will not earn an adjournment</td>\n<td>IP and solicitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administration could preserve a viable business</td>\n<td>Assess an administration application urgently</td>\n<td>An outstanding petition closes off the ordinary out-of-court appointment route, so a court application may be required</td>\n<td>Administrator and solicitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A CVA could work: a Company Voluntary Arrangement, where creditors formally agree to accept repayment over time</td>\n<td>Prepare a credible proposal and deal with the petition separately</td>\n<td>Proposing a CVA does not by itself pause or end the petition</td>\n<td>IP and solicitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rescue is not realistic</td>\n<td>Consider whether a CVL is still achievable before an order is made: a Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation, where you close the company yourselves rather than the court doing it</td>\n<td>A pending petition complicates the route. It does not simply disappear because you have resolved to liquidate</td>\n<td>Liquidator and solicitor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The administration row is the one that catches people. Directors read that <a href=\"/company-administration/\">administration</a> brings a statutory moratorium, which is a legal freeze on creditors taking action, and reasonably conclude that the freeze will deal with the petition.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It does not work that way once a petition is already before the court. The usual do-it-yourself route into administration is closed off, so check this with a solicitor before you rely on it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--risk\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>Paying the Debt Is Not the End of It</strong></p>\n<p>Do not assume that settling the original petition debt automatically closes the case. The petition costs still have to be dealt with, formal withdrawal or dismissal steps may be required, and another creditor may support the petition or seek to take it over.</p>\n<p>Clearing the original debt, breathing out, and then finding the hearing still listed because another creditor has stepped into the petitioner's shoes is a common and thoroughly unpleasant surprise.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Keeping Essential Payments Moving</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A <a href=\"/insolvency/validation-order/\">validation order</a> is the court giving permission for particular payments to be made despite the petition, so the bank can release them without risk. It is often necessary. But it is a cashflow fix and nothing more: it settles no debt, ends no petition, and moves no hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who You Actually Need: Solicitor, IP, or Both</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>No one profession covers all of this, and starting with the wrong one costs days you have not got. When we take a petition call, the first thing we are working out is which of the two you need in the room first, and whether you need both.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>The problem</th><th>The main professional</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Genuine debt dispute, restraining advertisement, opposing at the hearing</td><td>Insolvency solicitor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Viability, negotiation, CVA, administration, or CVL assessment</td><td>Licensed insolvency practitioner</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frozen account and validation-order evidence</td><td>Solicitor to apply, with IP or accountant evidence on the financial position</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A winding-up order has already been made</td><td>Specialist solicitor, alongside the Official Receiver's process</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Company Debt is a licensed insolvency practice. We assess viability, negotiate with petitioning creditors, and handle formal insolvency appointments.</p>\n<p>Court work is a different thing. Opposing a petition, applying to restrain advertisement, or arguing at a hearing is reserved legal work, and we instruct and coordinate specialist insolvency solicitors and counsel for it rather than doing it ourselves. Be wary of any practitioner who lets that line blur.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Happens to the Company Bank Account</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the first question nearly everyone asks, and it is nearly always payroll behind it. The answer is that it depends on your bank, and nobody can promise you the timing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A restriction tends to announce itself as a declined payment rather than as a letter. If that happens, treat the wages run as at risk from that moment rather than from whenever the paperwork catches up.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nothing in the petition instructs any bank to freeze anything, which surprises people. What happens is that banks watch The Gazette, and a bank that spots a petition will often restrict the account to protect itself. Some move within the day, others take a good deal longer to notice, and the variation is wide enough that we would not want to give you a number.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reason sits in <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/127\">section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986</a>. If the court later makes a winding-up order, money paid out and property moved in the meantime can be reversed, unless the court has approved it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>And under <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/129\">section 129</a>, the clock generally starts on the day the petition was filed at court, not the day you found out about it. So one of the first things we ask for is that filing date, because it can be well before anything landed on your desk.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>So the risk works backwards, and only if things go badly. Nothing is undone on the day the petition arrives. It can be undone later, if an order follows, which is why a bank would rather stop the account than be the one explaining afterwards where the money went.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A validation order is the remedy here, and you can apply for one at any point after the petition is filed, including after the account has already been restricted. So do not wait for a freeze before taking advice, but equally do not assume the door has shut because one has already happened.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An application normally needs evidence showing which transactions should be authorised and why they are consistent with creditors' interests: the financial position, a cashflow forecast, the specific payments, and supporting documents. A broad request simply to continue trading is unlikely to be sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are not certain a petition is the cause, start with our guide to a <a href=\"/advice/frozen-bank-account/\">frozen business bank account</a>. For the position once a company is in liquidation, see <a href=\"/liquidation/business-bank-account-in-liquidation/\">business bank accounts in liquidation</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Can Happen at the Hearing</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<table class=\"cd-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Winding-up order</td><td>The court orders compulsory liquidation. The Official Receiver normally becomes liquidator initially.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dismissal</td><td>The petition fails, commonly where the debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds or the process has been misused.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adjournment</td><td>The hearing is put off to a later date, usually because something concrete is already under way. Courts do not adjourn on hope.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Withdrawal</td><td>The petitioner is given permission to withdraw, where the procedural requirements for that have been met.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Substitution or support</td><td>Another creditor backs the petition, or takes the first creditor's place, and the case carries on without the original one.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other directions</td><td>Case-specific orders, including where another insolvency process is in play.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A winding-up petition is not defended the way an ordinary claim is, and there is no standard response form to post back. Opposing it means evidence filed and served in the proper form, plus attendance or representation at the hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An email to the petitioner's solicitors the night before is not a defence, however reasonable it reads. Whether your case needs a witness statement, counsel, or simply somebody in the room is a judgement for your solicitor on your facts, not something to settle from a website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Petitions are generally dealt with in busy lists, and the hearing itself may be short. What you filed beforehand therefore tends to count for more than anything said on the day, although the precise procedure varies by case and by court.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If an order has already been made, an application to rescind is possible, and GOV.UK indicates it should be made within five working days using Form IAA and a witness statement. That is a very tight window. It needs a solicitor the same day, and it is the one point on this page where we would tell you to stop reading and pick up the phone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Directors Should and Should Not Do</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Do</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Preserve every record, including the ones that do not flatter you.</li>\n<li>Note the hearing date and check whether the petition has been advertised.</li>\n<li>Get current management accounts and a short cashflow forecast together. You will need both for almost any route.</li>\n<li>Separate what the company admits from what it can properly dispute.</li>\n<li>Keep minuting board decisions. The record of what you considered, and when, is worth having later.</li>\n<li>Take advice before moving money or assets anywhere.</li>\n<li>Run IP and solicitor advice in parallel where court action is involved, rather than one after the other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Do Not</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Ignore the petition. The process runs to its date whether or not you engage with it.</li>\n<li>Sell, move, or hide company assets without advice. Depending on the facts, a sale for less than something was worth can be reversed by a court, a director who has breached their duties can be made to pay the money back personally, and in some cases there is criminal liability.</li>\n<li>Pay one creditor ahead of the others, particularly anyone connected to you. A payment like that can later be challenged as a preference under <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/239\">section 239</a>, or undone under section 127, depending on the timing, the relationship, and why it was made.</li>\n<li>Promise creditors money the company has no realistic means of paying.</li>\n<li>Take new credit without a reasonable basis for repaying it.</li>\n<li>Rely on a proposed CVA, CVL, or administration as though it automatically stops the court process.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On wrongful trading: continuing to trade after there is no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation may expose directors to a claim for a contribution under <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\">section 214</a>.</p>\n<p>It depends on what you knew, when, and what steps you took to minimise loss to creditors. It is not automatic. A court has to be satisfied of the statutory elements before any contribution is ordered.</p>\n<p>In the cases we handle, the directors who come out of this best are rarely the ones who did everything right. They are the ones who can show what they weighed up, and when. That record either exists by the time somebody asks for it or it does not, and it cannot be built afterwards and made to look contemporaneous.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What a Petition Means for Employees and Directors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Employees</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A winding-up order normally brings employment to an end where the business stops and the Official Receiver takes control. For a good many of the directors we deal with, this is the part that has actually been costing them sleep, rather than the debt.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What happens next is governed by two systems that get run together constantly, including on other insolvency sites. The difference is worth holding onto.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first is the <strong>Redundancy Payments Service</strong>. Eligible employees can claim statutory redundancy pay, arrears of wages, holiday pay, and notice pay from the National Insurance Fund, subject to service requirements and category limits. The statutory weekly cap is <strong>\u00a3751 for relevant claims from 6 April 2026</strong>. GOV.UK sets out <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/your-rights-if-your-employer-is-insolvent/what-you-can-get\">what employees can get</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The second is the order in which people get paid out of whatever the company has left. Some of what employees are owed counts as a preferential debt, which means it is paid ahead of ordinary creditors, and it has its own separate limits. Different system, different limits. A figure from one tells you nothing about the other.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Directors</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Service of the petition does not end your powers. The winding-up order does. Until then you are still running the company and still carrying the duties that go with it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Compulsory liquidation takes control away and puts the Official Receiver in charge to begin with. That is a genuine difference from the voluntary route, where you pick the practitioner and keep some say over timing and over how staff and customers hear about it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What it is not is a softer outcome, whatever you are told. Director conduct is reported on in <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">a CVL</a> exactly as it is in a compulsory liquidation, and the <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/insolvent-company-investigations-what-happens-when-a-company-is-liquidated\">Insolvency Service may investigate further</a> where the report or other evidence raises concerns. A practitioner pitching a CVL on the basis that it means less scrutiny is selling something they are not in a position to deliver.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Disqualification for up to 15 years is possible under the <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\">Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</a> where misconduct is established, and personal liability where the statutory tests are met. Neither follows from the petition. Neither follows from liquidation by itself. Both follow from conduct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Information to Gather Before You Call</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Have this to hand and the first conversation goes a great deal further. It is also the quickest way for us to tell you whether your position is better than you have been assuming, which reasonably often it is.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>The petition itself and the hearing notice</li>\n<li>The date and method of service</li>\n<li>Whether the petition has been advertised in the Gazette, and when</li>\n<li>Any statutory demand or judgment behind the debt</li>\n<li>Correspondence with the petitioning creditor</li>\n<li>The amount admitted and the amount genuinely disputed</li>\n<li>Current bank access: working, partly restricted, or frozen</li>\n<li>Latest management accounts</li>\n<li>An aged creditor list</li>\n<li>A 13-week cashflow forecast if you have one</li>\n<li>Secured lending and debenture details</li>\n<li>Payroll dates and any urgent payments due</li>\n<li>Details of any asset sale or unusual payment made since the petition was presented</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The last item is not a trap. It is the thing most likely to need attention first, and it goes far better coming from you than surfacing on its own later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where Petition Numbers Currently Sit</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-callout cd-callout--summary\">\n<p class=\"cd-callout__label\"><strong>Two Separate Series, Not One</strong></p>\n<p>Our <a href=\"/data/winding-up-petition-tracker/\">winding-up petition tracker</a> recorded <strong>482 petition notices</strong> in the Gazette in May 2026, and <strong>373 winding-up orders</strong> in the same month.</p>\n<p>Those are two different populations. Orders made in May largely relate to petitions advertised in earlier months, so dividing one by the other does not give you the proportion of petitions that end in an order. We report them separately for that reason.</p>\n<p>Separately, the <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/company-insolvencies-june-2026\">Insolvency Service recorded <strong>276 compulsory liquidations in England and Wales in June 2026</strong></a>. Across 2025, compulsory liquidations were at their highest annual level since 2012 and 15% above 2024. Those are orders, not petitions.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of which tells you anything about your own case. That turns on your debt, your evidence, and what you do between now and the hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">If You Are the Creditor Considering a Petition</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A petition is a collective insolvency process, not debt collection with sharper teeth. Winning it does not mean getting paid.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/wind-up-a-company-that-owes-you-money\">GOV.UK shows a <strong>\u00a3352 court fee</strong> and a <strong>\u00a32,600 petition deposit</strong></a>, checked 29 July 2026, with legal, service and Gazette costs on top. The petitioner normally files Forms Comp 1 and Comp 2 with evidence such as an unpaid demand or judgment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What you recover depends on what assets exist and where you rank. Secured and preferential claims come ahead of unsecured creditors, and the costs of the process come out of the estate first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Petitioning a company with no assets is an expensive way of closing it down for nobody's benefit in particular. The deposit goes either way.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"7687-wup-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs a winding-up petition the same as a winding-up order?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. The petition is the creditor's application to the court. The order is the court's decision to wind the company up.</p>\n<p>Until an order is made you are still a director with control of the company. That distinction is the reason acting before the hearing matters so much.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow do I find the hearing date?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>It is printed on the court-stamped petition delivered to the company, along with the name of the court and the creditor bringing it.</p>\n<p>If it has been mislaid, the court office that issued it can confirm the listing. Do not rely on a general estimate: listing times vary and there is no statutory period between service and hearing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes a creditor need to serve a statutory demand first?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not necessarily. A creditor generally needs to be owed at least \u00a3750 and to show the company cannot pay its debts.</p>\n<p>An unpaid statutory demand is one common way of establishing that. An unpaid judgment or failed execution can also do it, so the absence of a demand does not make a petition invalid.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan the petition be withdrawn once the debt is paid?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Payment may allow the petitioner to withdraw or the court to dismiss the petition, but it does not happen automatically.</p>\n<p>The costs still need dealing with, withdrawal or dismissal has to be done formally, and a supporting creditor may want to continue. Get the position in writing before assuming it is over.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan another creditor take over the petition?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. Other creditors can give notice of intention to appear and support the petition, and the court can substitute a supporting creditor as petitioner. This is why settling with the original petitioner does not always end the matter, particularly once the petition has been advertised.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan the company keep trading after a petition is presented?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>There is no automatic prohibition on trading, and directors remain in control until an order is made. What constrains it in practice is money and risk.</p>\n<p>Money, because the bank may restrict the account. Risk, because of section 127: if a winding-up order follows later, payments made after the petition was filed at court can be reversed unless the court has approved them. Take advice before trading on, and before taking on new credit.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan we still do a CVL after a petition has been presented?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>It may still be possible before an order is made, but it is not a conversion and the petition does not fall away because you have decided to liquidate. The petitioning creditor, any supporting creditors, and the court timetable all still have to be dealt with.</p>\n<p>Whether a CVL is achievable in the time available is a judgement for a licensed insolvency practitioner on your facts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":7,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes proposing a CVA stop the petition?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. Proposing a <a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">CVA</a> does not by itself stay or dispose of a winding-up petition.</p>\n<p>The proposal still has to go to creditors and be approved, and the petition handled separately, usually by persuading the court to adjourn while the proposal is put. Neither is guaranteed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":8,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan directors appoint an administrator once a petition has been presented?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Not by the ordinary out-of-court route. Schedule B1 of the Insolvency Act 1986 restricts an appointment by the company or its directors while a winding-up petition is outstanding.</p>\n<p>Where administration is genuinely the right answer, that generally means an application to court instead, which takes longer and costs more. Check this with an insolvency solicitor before you file anything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":9,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is a validation order?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A court order authorising specified payments or transactions despite the petition, so they are not caught by section 127 if a winding-up order later follows.</p>\n<p>It reaches the transactions and leaves the debt and the petition exactly where they were. It can be applied for after an account is already restricted. Our <a href=\"/insolvency/validation-order/\">validation order guide</a> covers the evidence and the application.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":10,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens if a winding-up order has already been made?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The company is in compulsory liquidation and the Official Receiver normally acts as liquidator initially. Your powers as a director have ended and you have a duty to cooperate.</p>\n<p>An application to rescind is possible, and <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/cancel-winding-up-order\">GOV.UK indicates it should be made within five working days</a> using Form IAA and a witness statement. That goes quickly while you are also dealing with the Official Receiver, so get legal advice the same day. Our <a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">compulsory liquidation guide</a> explains what follows.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide is written for directors of limited companies in England and Wales. Scotland and Northern Ireland operate separate regimes and are signposted above.</p>\n<p>Written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association, for the insolvency and viability content.</p>\n<p><strong>Legal review pending.</strong> The court, dispute, validation, hearing, and administration-route sections require review by a named insolvency solicitor. That review has not yet taken place and the reviewer will be named here once it has.</p>\n<p>Primary sources checked on 29 July 2026: the Insolvency Act 1986, the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, and current GOV.UK guidance, including the \u00a3352 fee, \u00a32,600 deposit and \u00a3751 weekly cap.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the processes described here. If you instruct us we act as your insolvency practitioner, and we may receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network.</p>\n<p>Court work is carried out by specialist insolvency solicitors and counsel whom we instruct. This guide is general information and does not amount to case-specific legal advice.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/dealing-with-an-hmrc-winding-up-petition/\">Dealing With an HMRC Winding-Up Petition</a>: where the petitioner is HMRC.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/insolvency/validation-order/\">Validation Orders</a>: the evidence, the application, and when one is worth making.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/winding-up-petition-vs-compulsory-liquidation/\">Winding-Up Petition vs Compulsory Liquidation</a>: the difference between the application and the order.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">Compulsory Liquidation</a>: what happens once the order is made.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: whether it remains achievable before a hearing.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-administration/\">Company Administration</a>: the rescue route, and how an outstanding petition affects it.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">Company Voluntary Arrangement</a>: a creditor-approved plan, which does not itself stop a petition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/what-is-a-statutory-demand-against-a-company/\">Statutory Demands</a>: the stage before a petition, where you still have room.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/data/winding-up-petition-tracker/\">Winding-Up Petition Tracker</a>: our monthly Gazette petition and order data.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong> &mdash; sections 122 (grounds), 123 (inability to pay), 124 (who may petition), 127 (avoidance of dispositions), 129 (commencement of winding up), 214 (wrongful trading), 238 to 239 (undervalue and preferences), Schedule 6 (preferential debts) and Schedule B1 (administration)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/rule/7.10\"><strong>Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, rule 7.10</strong> &mdash; advertisement of a winding-up petition, both the seven-business-day period after service and the seven-business-day period before the hearing</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/wind-up-a-company-that-owes-you-money\"><strong>GOV.UK: Wind up a company that owes you money</strong> &mdash; the \u00a3750 creditor threshold, the \u00a3352 court fee and the \u00a32,600 petition deposit</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/wind-up-a-company-that-owes-you-money/the-court-hearing\"><strong>GOV.UK: The court hearing</strong> &mdash; advertisement deadlines, the certificate of compliance, attendance and possible outcomes</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/liquidate-your-company/access-to-your-bank-account\"><strong>GOV.UK: Access to your bank account</strong> &mdash; frozen-account risk and the validation-order route</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/cancel-winding-up-order\"><strong>GOV.UK: Cancel a winding-up order</strong> &mdash; the five-working-day rescission application, Form IAA and witness statement</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/find-out-if-a-company-is-in-financial-trouble\"><strong>GOV.UK: Find out if a company is in financial trouble</strong> &mdash; checking Companies House and Gazette notices</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/insolvent-company-investigations-what-happens-when-a-company-is-liquidated\"><strong>Insolvency Service: Insolvent company investigations</strong> &mdash; director-conduct reporting in both compulsory and voluntary liquidation</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/your-rights-if-your-employer-is-insolvent/what-you-can-get\"><strong>GOV.UK: Your rights if your employer is insolvent</strong> &mdash; Redundancy Payments Service entitlements and the \u00a3751 weekly cap from 6 April 2026</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/46/contents\"><strong>Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986</strong> &mdash; disqualification of up to 15 years where misconduct is established</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/company-insolvencies-june-2026\"><strong>Insolvency Service: Company insolvencies, June 2026</strong> &mdash; 276 compulsory liquidations in England and Wales, and the 2025 annual comparison</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mygov.scot/wind-up-company\"><strong>Scottish Government: Wind up a company</strong> &mdash; the separate Scottish procedure</a> - mygov.scot</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.economy-ni.gov.uk/articles/winding-company-owes-you-money\"><strong>NI Department for the Economy: Winding up a company that owes money</strong> &mdash; the separate Northern Ireland procedure</a> - economy-ni.gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/can-we-trade-out-of-insolvency/": {
    "id": 24080,
    "slug": "can-we-trade-out-of-insolvency",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is Thursday night. You are at the kitchen table with the quarterly VAT return open on the laptop and a Barclays statement showing \u00a34,200 available against a \u00a338,000 VAT bill due on the seventh. The order book for next month is solid, there are two invoices worth \u00a322,000 sitting at 45 days, and a repeat customer has just confirmed a six-figure contract for Q3.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The question you are asking is not \"can I pay this quarter\" but \"can I trade through this and come out the other side?\" The honest answer is: sometimes yes, sometimes no, and the difference is decided long before the court is involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The law in the UK does not tell you that an insolvent company must cease trading overnight. What section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 tells you is that once you know (or ought to know) there is no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation, you must take every step to minimise creditor loss.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trading on without that, without the paperwork and the professional advice behind it, is what crystallises personal liability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We see two kinds of director on this call. The first has a genuine case for trading through and simply needs the documented process that protects them. The second has been hoping the numbers will turn around for six months and does not have a reasonable prospect, they have a wish.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This page walks you through the legal framework, the evidence a defensible trade-on decision requires, and the formal routes that let you trade under protection if informal trading is no longer safe.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"can-you-legally-trade-out-of-insolvency-in-the-uk\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"can-you-legally-trade-out-of-insolvency-in-the-uk\">Can You Legally Trade Out of Insolvency in the UK?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Yes, but only under conditions. English and Welsh law does not impose an automatic duty to cease trading the moment the balance sheet goes negative or you miss a payment. What it does is shift the legal framework around your decisions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once the company is insolvent, or insolvency is imminent or probable, the Supreme Court in <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25 confirmed that your directors' duty under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006 shifts.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You must now have regard to creditor interests alongside (and as the position worsens, ahead of) shareholder interests. Every decision to keep trading is measured against that creditor-interest lens.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On top of that sits section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986, the wrongful trading provision. If the company goes into insolvent liquidation and it is shown you knew, or a reasonably diligent director in your position ought to have concluded, there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding that outcome, the court can order you to contribute personally to the estate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>\"Every step\" is not \"every heroic step\". It means the reasonable steps a competent director would have taken. Getting specialist advice on file. Minuting the board's assessment. Stopping fresh supplier credit that you cannot honestly pay. Paying PAYE and VAT as they fall due rather than robbing them to fund trading losses. Our guide to <a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-wrongful-trading/\">wrongful trading</a> goes through the defence in detail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-it-is-defensible-to-trade-out-of-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-it-is-defensible-to-trade-out-of-insolvency\">When It Is Defensible to Trade Out of Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A defensible trade-on case has three ingredients, and all three need to be in the board file before the decision is taken, not reconstructed afterwards.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>A genuine prospect of recovery.</strong> A live, confirmed order book that, on realistic collection assumptions, clears the current payables shortfall within a defined window. A lender who has indicated fresh facility, in writing. A one-off loss (bad debt, contract cancellation, litigation settlement) that is identifiably behind you rather than a pattern that continues.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Documented board decisions at each step.</strong> Full board minutes that record the management accounts reviewed, the 13-week cash-flow forecast considered, the creditor-interest weighing done, and the specific decision made. Not \"noted and approved\". The minute should read like something you would be comfortable handing to a liquidator on day one.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Professional advice on file.</strong> A licensed IP or an insolvency-specialist solicitor consulted before the trade-on decision, with the advice recorded in writing. The point is not to outsource the decision; it is to show the decision was taken with proper input against the section 214 test.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Without those three, a director who trades through and then ends up in liquidation is arguing against the tide. With them, the file tells the story of a board that took every step a reasonable director would. The paper trail is the defence, and it has to be contemporaneous.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:test -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--test\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">30-Second Insolvency Check</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Not Sure If Your Company Is Actually Insolvent?</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Answer a few questions to see whether your company may be insolvent and whether you should take action now.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Check My Company &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free</span><span>Confidential</span><span>Regulated</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:test -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"when-trading-out-of-insolvency-crosses-into-wrongful-trading\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"when-trading-out-of-insolvency-crosses-into-wrongful-trading\">When Trading Out of Insolvency Crosses Into Wrongful Trading</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The line is crossed when there is no longer a reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation and you keep going anyway. In our casework, the tell-tale signs of a director who has quietly slipped across that line are recognisable:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The cash-flow forecast has not been updated for three months, or updates rely on \"pipeline\" revenue rather than contracted orders.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Supplier invoices are being paid selectively, with the quiet ones (HMRC, the landlord, the pension scheme) falling further behind.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>New customer deposits are being used to pay last month's wages rather than held against delivery.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Director loans are going in rather than coming out, and the board has stopped asking when the injections will stop.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>The conversation at board level has shifted from \"how do we fix this\" to \"how long can we hold on\".</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When a liquidator reconstructs the timeline afterwards, they look at exactly those indicators. The management pack you signed six months before liquidation is either the section 214 defence or the section 214 smoking gun.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-sequana-duty-shift-why-it-matters-when-you-trade-out-of-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-sequana-duty-shift-why-it-matters-when-you-trade-out-of-insolvency\">The Sequana Duty Shift: Why It Matters When You Trade Out of Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Until the Supreme Court decided <em>Sequana</em> in October 2022, the exact timing of the duty shift to creditor interests was uncertain. The Court held that the shift is triggered where the directors know or ought to know that the company is insolvent, or that insolvency is imminent, or that an insolvent liquidation or administration is probable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That means the shift can happen well before formal insolvency, well before a statutory demand, well before a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>. It happens at the point where a diligent director looking at the numbers would conclude that the company's creditors are now the group whose interests need protecting.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Practically, if you are already two quarters into sustained losses, already factoring at 70% rather than 85%, already behind on HMRC, and already asking yourself \"can we trade out of this?\", the <em>Sequana</em> trigger is probably already pulled. The question is not whether you have crossed the line, it is whether your board minutes show you recognised that you had.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:mid -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--service\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">CVL Liquidation Service</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Considering Liquidation? Talk to an IP First.</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Speak to an IPA-regulated Licensed Insolvency Practitioner. The first call is free and there is no obligation to proceed.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">See Our CVL Service &rarr;</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Licensed IPs</span><span>Nationwide</span><span>CVL specialists</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:mid -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"formal-routes-that-let-you-trade-under-protection\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"formal-routes-that-let-you-trade-under-protection\">Formal Routes That Let You Trade Under Protection</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If informal trading is no longer defensible but the business still has a future, the Insolvency Act and the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 give you structured routes that let you keep operating under creditor protection.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Company Voluntary Arrangement (Part I Insolvency Act 1986).</strong> A proposal to unsecured creditors to compromise historic debt, typically paid from future trading profits over three to five years. Requires 75% in value of creditors voting to approve. You stay in day-to-day control. See our <a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">CVA</a> guide.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Part A1 Moratorium (inserted by CIGA 2020).</strong> A standalone 20-business-day moratorium from creditor enforcement, extendable, overseen by a licensed IP as \"monitor\". Directors remain in control while a rescue plan is developed.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Administration (Schedule B1 Insolvency Act 1986).</strong> An administrator takes over the company with statutory purposes: rescue the company, or achieve a better result for creditors than liquidation, or realise property to distribute. A powerful automatic moratorium protects the business during the process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Restructuring Plan (Part 26A Companies Act 2006).</strong> A more recent tool, used on larger matters, that can cram down dissenting creditor classes with court sanction.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Each route has trade-offs. A CVA binds unsecured creditors but demands a disciplined five-year delivery and kills flexibility on new lending. A moratorium buys 20 working days, not a lifetime. Administration transfers control from you to an administrator. None of them is painless, and the one that fits depends on your specific cash, creditor, and lender position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"the-practical-test-before-you-decide-to-trade-out-of-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"the-practical-test-before-you-decide-to-trade-out-of-insolvency\">The Practical Test Before You Decide to Trade Out of Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The honest test a director can run at the kitchen table, before ringing us, is this. Take the 13-week cash-flow forecast you actually believe, not the optimistic one. Subtract the contracted order-book cash from the contracted payables. If the gap closes inside 13 weeks without further director loans, new overdraft, or unpaid HMRC, you have a case for trading through.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the gap closes only by assuming new contracts you have not yet signed, or by continuing to stretch HMRC, or by taking a further injection the family cannot really afford, you do not have a case for trading through. You have a preference for doing so. The Supreme Court test in <em>Sequana</em> is about probabilities, not preferences.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our page on <a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">the statutory insolvency test</a> sets out how a licensed IP applies section 123 to your specific numbers, and our guide to <a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/\">company cash flow problems</a> covers the 13-week forecast discipline in more detail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"your-next-step-on-trading-out-of-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"your-next-step-on-trading-out-of-insolvency\">Your Next Step on Trading Out of Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The verdict splits into two camps, and the call between them is the most important commercial decision you will take this year.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you have a confirmed order book, a realistic 13-week forecast that closes the gap, and a willingness to paper the board decisions properly, you have a trade-on case. Your next step is a documented board review with a licensed IP's advice on file, not a \"let's see how next month goes\" conversation. The IP advice plus the minuted decision is the section 214 defence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the honest forecast does not close, if HMRC is drifting further behind, or if the conversation at board level has become about surviving another fortnight rather than fixing the underlying problem, informal trading is no longer safe.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Your next step is a formal conversation this week, not next month, about whether a CVA, a moratorium, an administration, or a <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">creditors' voluntary liquidation</a> is the right protected route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Call Company Debt free on 0800 074 6757 for a confidential review with one of our licensed insolvency practitioners. We will walk through your 13-week forecast, test whether the section 214 \"reasonable prospect\" threshold is honestly met, and set out the formal protections available if it is not. Nothing is charged until you instruct us.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n\n\n<!-- cd-cta:phone -->\n<div class=\"cd-cta cd-cta--phone\">\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__body\">\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__eyebrow\">Free Director Helpline</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__title\">Talk to a Licensed Insolvency Practitioner Today</p>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__intro\">Get straight through to a Licensed IP for a confidential, no-obligation conversation. We take HMRC and creditor pressure off you from the first call.</p>\n      <a class=\"cd-cta__button\" href=\"tel:08000746757\">0800 074 6757</a>\n      <p class=\"cd-cta__trust\"><span>Free call</span><span>UK based</span><span>Confidential</span></p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"cd-cta__icon\"></div>\n  </div>\n<!-- /cd-cta:phone -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"anchor\":\"faqs-on-trading-out-of-insolvency\"} -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"faqs-on-trading-out-of-insolvency\">FAQs on Trading Out of Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"24080-trade-out-faqs\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs it legal to keep trading if my company is insolvent?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, provided the board can honestly conclude there is a reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation, takes every step to minimise creditor loss, and documents that assessment with professional advice on file. Section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986 punishes trading that continued after no reasonable director could have seen recovery.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat is the section 214 wrongful trading test?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A director commits wrongful trading if, before winding-up, they knew or ought to have concluded there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation and did not take every step a reasonably diligent director would take to minimise creditor loss. The court can order a personal contribution to the estate equal to the increase in net deficiency.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat does it mean to take every step to minimise creditor loss?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>In practice it means taking specialist advice, documenting board decisions against the section 214 test, stopping fresh supplier credit you cannot honestly service, keeping current HMRC payments up to date, and considering whether a formal process would produce a better outcome for creditors than continued informal trading. The test is the reasonable-director standard.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow does a CVA allow me to trade out of insolvency?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>A Company Voluntary Arrangement under Part I of the Insolvency Act 1986 binds unsecured creditors to accept a compromise of their debt (often 30p to 50p in the pound) typically paid from future trading profits over three to five years. You stay in control of day-to-day management. Approval requires 75% by value of creditors voting, and a supervising IP monitors delivery throughout.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan a company in liquidation still trade?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No, not in the ordinary course. Once a liquidator is appointed, their statutory duty is to realise the assets and distribute the proceeds. Any trading is limited to what is necessary for a beneficial winding-up, typically completing specific work-in-progress contracts. The Part A1 moratorium and administration are the procedures designed for continued trading under protection; liquidation is not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhen exactly does the Sequana creditor duty kick in?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\"} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The Supreme Court held in <em>BTI v Sequana</em> [2022] UKSC 25 that the duty to have regard to creditor interests is engaged where directors know or ought to know the company is insolvent, or that insolvency is imminent, or that an insolvent liquidation or administration is probable. It engages earlier than the section 123 formal insolvency threshold and bites on every trading decision thereafter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\" aria-label=\"Methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This guide was written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, who oversees referrals to our panel of licensed insolvency practitioners.</p>\n<p>Statutory references are to the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 123, 214, Part I, Part A1, Schedule B1), the Companies Act 2006 (section 172, Part 26A), and the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020. Case authority: <em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency referral service. The initial consultation is free and confidential, and nothing is charged until a licensed IP is formally instructed.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\" aria-label=\"Sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/section/214\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986, s.214 (wrongful trading)</strong> &mdash; the personal-liability test directors must meet when continuing to trade once insolvency becomes unavoidable</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/46/section/172\"><strong>Companies Act 2006, s.172</strong> &mdash; directors' duty to promote company success, with the creditor-interest shift that applies once insolvency is imminent or probable</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/12/contents\"><strong>Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020</strong> &mdash; introduced the standalone Part A1 moratorium and restructuring plan, the formal routes for trading on under court protection</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2022/25.html\"><strong><em>BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA</em> [2022] UKSC 25</strong> &mdash; Supreme Court ruling that fixed when the creditor-duty shift engages and how it weighs against shareholder interests</a> - bailii.org</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/insolvency/": {
    "id": 17686,
    "slug": "insolvency",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Facing potential insolvency can be daunting for UK company directors, with serious legal and personal implications. When a company begins to struggle financially, swift and informed action becomes critical. Ignoring <a href=\"/debt-creditor-pressure-hub/\">creditor pressure</a> or delaying decisions can significantly increase risks, including personal liability and regulatory consequences.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors must understand when insolvency arises, how their duties change as financial distress deepens, and which lawful options are available. This guide explains the core insolvency tests, director duties, and formal procedures, helping you make informed decisions to protect both the company and your own position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":67521,\"sizeSlug\":\"full\",\"linkDestination\":\"media\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full\"><img src=\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/A-Guide-to-Insolvency.png\" alt=\"What is Insolvency?\" class=\"wp-image-67521\" /></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Understanding Company Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under UK law, company insolvency is assessed primarily by reference to section 123 of the Insolvency Act 1986. Insolvency is not a single event but a financial state that can be identified using two statutory tests: the <strong>cash-flow test</strong> and the <strong>balance-sheet test</strong>. Satisfying either test can indicate insolvency for legal purposes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where a company is insolvent, or approaching insolvency, directors must pay close attention to their statutory and fiduciary duties, particularly where creditor interests become increasingly relevant.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Cash-Flow Test</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cash-flow test considers whether a company is able to pay its debts as they fall due. This includes both current liabilities and debts that are due in the reasonably near future. A persistent inability to meet payment obligations\u2014such as rent, taxes, or supplier invoices\u2014can indicate insolvency under this test.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This assessment focuses on the company\u2019s real ability to meet obligations in practice, rather than temporary cash shortages alone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Balance-Sheet Test</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The balance-sheet test examines whether the value of a company\u2019s assets is less than the amount of its liabilities, taking into account contingent and prospective liabilities. These may include guarantees, ongoing litigation risks, or future contractual commitments.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This test provides a broader view of financial health by assessing whether the company\u2019s overall obligations outweigh its available resources.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meeting either test may indicate insolvency, requiring directors to act with heightened care and to consider appropriate steps to minimise potential losses to creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key Signs and Risks of Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Early recognition of insolvency warning signs is essential. Common indicators include mounting creditor pressure, overdue tax liabilities, persistent cash-flow shortfalls, and difficulty meeting routine financial commitments.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Continuing to trade during financial distress can expose directors to serious risks. In particular, directors may face allegations of <strong>wrongful trading</strong> if, before the start of an insolvent liquidation, they knew or ought to have concluded that there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation and failed to take every step to minimise losses to creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>More serious misconduct, such as deliberately deceiving creditors, can result in <strong>fraudulent trading</strong>, which carries both civil and criminal consequences.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Once insolvency is suspected, directors must act with care and should consider seeking advice from a licensed insolvency practitioner. Early professional guidance can expand available options and reduce personal exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Overview of Formal Insolvency Procedures</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>UK insolvency law provides several formal procedures designed to address financial distress, depending on whether rescue or orderly closure is achievable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Administration</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Administration aims to rescue the company as a going concern, achieve a better result for creditors than liquidation, or realise property to make distributions to secured or preferential creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An insolvency practitioner is appointed as administrator and assumes control of the company\u2019s affairs. A statutory moratorium applies, restricting most creditor enforcement actions unless the administrator consents or the court grants permission. Directors remain in office but their powers are significantly limited.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Creditors\u2019 Voluntary Liquidation (CVL)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A <a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">CVL</a> is used when an insolvent company cannot continue trading. Shareholders must pass a special resolution (at least 75% by value) to wind up the company voluntarily. A licensed insolvency practitioner is appointed as liquidator and takes control of the company.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors\u2019 powers cease, and the liquidator investigates company affairs and director conduct before realising assets for creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Compulsory Liquidation</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Compulsory liquidation occurs when the court orders a company to be wound up, most commonly following a creditor\u2019s <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a>. The Official Receiver initially acts as liquidator and may later be replaced by an insolvency practitioner.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The process involves investigation of company affairs, realisation of assets, and distribution to creditors in accordance with statutory priorities.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Company Voluntary Arrangement (CVA)</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A CVA allows an insolvent but viable company to reach a binding agreement with creditors to repay debts over time. The proposal must be approved by at least 75% (by value) of creditors voting, and more than 50% (by value) of unconnected creditors who vote.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors usually remain in control, subject to supervision by an insolvency practitioner acting as supervisor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Moratorium</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Introduced under the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020, a moratorium provides temporary protection from creditor action while rescue options are explored. An insolvency practitioner acts as monitor, overseeing the process while directors retain day-to-day control.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The moratorium is obtained by filing prescribed documents with the court and meeting statutory eligibility criteria.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Receivership</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Receivership typically involves appointing a receiver over specific charged assets, usually under a fixed charge. Administrative receivership (covering all or substantially all assets under a floating charge) is now largely restricted to floating charges created before September 2003, subject to limited exceptions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A receiver\u2019s primary duty is to the appointing secured creditor, not to the company as a whole.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Directors\u2019 Duties and Potential Liabilities</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As financial distress deepens, directors must increasingly consider creditor interests when making decisions. Failure to do so can result in personal liability, particularly in wrongful trading claims.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors may also face disqualification under the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 if their conduct is found to make them unfit to manage a company. Investigations are typically carried out by the liquidator, administrator, or the Official Receiver, often involving the <strong>Insolvency Service</strong>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Cooperation with office-holders, maintaining proper records, and seeking early professional advice can help mitigate risks.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Practical Steps to Take if You Suspect Insolvency</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If insolvency is a concern, prompt and measured action is essential:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Seek Professional Advice - </strong>Contact a licensed insolvency practitioner as early as possible for case-specific guidance.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Avoid Worsening Creditor Losses -</strong> Carefully review ongoing trading decisions to ensure they do not increase losses to creditors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Review Financial Information -</strong> Prepare up-to-date accounts, cash-flow forecasts, and a full list of liabilities.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Maintain Transparency -</strong> Communicate responsibly with stakeholders while avoiding preferential treatment of particular creditors.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These steps can help protect both the company and directors while lawful options are assessed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Common Misunderstandings and Mistakes</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A common misconception is that insolvency automatically ends the business. In reality, procedures such as administration or CVAs may allow viable operations to continue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Another misunderstanding is that directors are automatically disqualified when a company becomes insolvent. Disqualification only follows where misconduct or unfitness is established through the statutory process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Serious mistakes include delaying advice, poor record-keeping, and preferring certain creditors. These actions can significantly increase personal exposure and worsen outcomes for all parties.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">FAQs</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"ad72d72a-9841-4163-91ab-e143072fdaf1\",\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e1) What if I cannot afford to pay for an insolvency practitioner?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Many insolvency practitioners offer an initial consultation at no cost. While the <strong>Insolvency Service</strong> provides general information about insolvency processes, it cannot give legal or financial advice on individual cases.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e2) Can directors continue trading when the company is insolvent?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Insolvency alone does not automatically prohibit trading. However, directors risk wrongful trading liability if they continue when there is no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation and fail to take every step to minimise creditor losses.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e3) What happens if a creditor has already petitioned for winding up?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The court will determine whether to make a winding-up order. Directors should seek immediate professional advice and act carefully, as asset dispositions after petition presentation may be void.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e4) Does insolvency affect my personal credit score?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Company insolvency does not directly affect a director\u2019s personal credit record. However, <a href=\"/advice/directors-personal-guarantees/\">personal guarantees</a> or other personal liabilities may have credit consequences.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e5) Will HMRC support a CVA if I owe taxes?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC may vote in favour of a CVA where it considers the proposal viable and compliant, but approval depends on the specific facts and creditor voting outcomes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e6) Can the business still operate under administration?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. An administrator may continue trading where this supports the statutory objectives, such as business rescue or sale as a going concern.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e7) Do employees automatically lose their jobs in insolvency?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>No. Outcomes vary by procedure. Some jobs may be preserved in administration, while liquidation often leads to redundancies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":7,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e8) What is the role of the Official Receiver in compulsory liquidation?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>The Official Receiver initially acts as liquidator, investigating company affairs and director conduct and managing asset realisation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":8,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e9) How does company insolvency differ in Northern Ireland or Scotland?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Core insolvency law is UK-wide, but procedural rules and institutions differ. Local guidance should always be consulted.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":9,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e10) Does seeking advice early help avoid formal insolvency?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Early advice can identify rescue or restructuring options before creditor action escalates.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":10,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e11) Will I lose my directorship once insolvency proceedings begin?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Directors often lose control of management in administration or liquidation but do not automatically lose their status unless disqualified.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":11,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003e12) Are there government schemes to help pay redundancy?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. Where an employer is insolvent, employees may claim statutory payments through the Redundancy Payments Service.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Next Steps</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If your company is experiencing financial distress, engaging a licensed insolvency practitioner without delay is critical. Early professional advice can help identify rescue options, manage risks, and ensure compliance with UK insolvency law. Whether restructuring is possible or an orderly wind-down is required, timely action is essential to protect both the company and your position as a director.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Further reading</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency-calculator/\">Insolvency test</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-are-the-warning-signs-of-an-insolvent-company/\">What are the warning signs of an insolvent company</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-happens-if-a-company-cannot-pay-its-debts/\">What happens if a company cannot pay its debts</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/check-if-a-company-is-insolvent/\">Check if a company is insolvent</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/how-to-save-a-struggling-business/\">How to save a struggling business</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/how-to-reduce-insolvency-risk/\">How to reduce insolvency risk</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/can-we-trade-out-of-insolvency/\">Can we trade out of insolvency</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/stop-or-avoid-insolvency/\">Stop or avoid insolvency</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/dealing-with-creditor-pressure/\">Dealing with creditor pressure</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/creditor-negotiations/\">Creditor negotiations</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-wrongful-trading/\">What is wrongful trading</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/antecedent-transactions/\">Antecedent transactions</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/cease-trading/\">Cease trading</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/what-is-an-insolvency-practitioner/\">What is an insolvency practitioner</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/insolvency/business-recovery-services/\">Business recovery services</a></li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->"
  },
  "/accessibility/": {
    "id": 24032,
    "slug": "accessibility",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Below is more detail about the accessibility of the CompanyDebt.com website. </p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Links</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>Links on the CompanyDebt.com open in new windows.</li><li>All links can be followed, regardless of the internet browser you are using. There are no \u2018JavaScript pseudo-links\u2019.</li><li>As much as possible, the links included on CompanyDebt.com are written to make sense out of context. There are numerous browsers, such as Opera, Lynx, JAWS and Home Page Opera, which can extract the links so the user can browse the list away from the page.</li><li>Many of the links on this website have title attributes which help to describe the link in more detail. That\u2019s not the case where the link already accurately describes the target.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Visual design</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>Cascading style sheets are used on CompanyDebt.com for visual layout.</li><li>The content on the website is still readable even if the internet browser being used does not support style sheets. </li><li>We have endeavoured to maintain a high background-foreground contrast across CompanyDebt.com.</li><li>CompanyDebt.com only uses font sizes that are compatible with the user-specified \u2018text size\u2019 option in visual browsers.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Images</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>All of the images on CompanyDebt.com include ALT attributes which describe the images in more detail. If images are purely decorative then they have null ALT attributes. </li><li>More complex images include descriptive LONGDESC attributes or inline descriptions which are used to explain the significance of the images to non-visual readers. &nbsp;</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Scripting</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>CompanyDebt.com uses scripting for visual effects. However, even if scripting is unavailable, all content remains fully accessible. </li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Forms</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul><li>HTML label tags are used to associate labels with fields.</li><li>All forms follow a logical Tab sequence.</li></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Disability Discrimination Act</strong></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>CompanyDebt.com complies with the requirements of the British Disability Discrimination Act. However, if you need any assistance reading or understanding the content on the website or encounter any accessibility issues, please do not hesitate to get in touch with our team and we will do our best to fix them. <br></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  "/hmrc/cant-pay-vat/": {
    "id": 9443,
    "slug": "cant-pay-vat",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<article>\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you cannot pay your VAT bill, the situation is more common, and more fixable, than it feels right now. We talk to directors in exactly this position every week: the bill sat there for a month, then two, then a penalty notice arrived, and now a firmer letter has landed. What you do in the next few days matters more than how you got here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Handled early, unpaid VAT can often be dealt with through a payment plan, provided the company is viable and the repayments are affordable. Left alone, it escalates: HMRC may agree a plan if you contact them before they start enforcing, but their willingness to deal narrows sharply once they do. Below, we set out what happens, what it means, and what to do now.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can't Pay VAT? Quick Answer</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you cannot pay your VAT, file the return anyway and contact HMRC before enforcement starts. Most viable companies clear VAT arrears through a <a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">Time to Pay arrangement</a>, an instalment plan HMRC agrees where the business can sustain the repayments. If the company cannot pay its VAT or its other debts as they fall due, that is an insolvency question, and it is worth taking advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-answer\">\n<p class=\"cd-answer__label\"><strong>If you cannot pay your VAT bill</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>File the VAT return on time, even if the company cannot pay it.</li>\n<li>Contact HMRC before enforcement escalates, not after.</li>\n<li>Ask for a Time to Pay arrangement if the company can afford instalments.</li>\n<li>Late payment interest runs from the first overdue day.</li>\n<li>Penalties escalate at day 16, and again at day 31.</li>\n<li>If the company cannot pay its VAT or its other debts as they fall due, take insolvency advice.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-decision\">\n<p class=\"cd-decision__label\"><strong>Work out your position in 30 seconds</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Can you pay in full within 15 days?</strong> Pay now and you avoid the late payment penalty, though interest still applies.</li>\n<li><strong>Can you afford monthly instalments?</strong> Ask HMRC for Time to Pay before enforcement starts.</li>\n<li><strong>Are other creditors also overdue, or has HMRC refused Time to Pay?</strong> Treat this as a possible insolvency issue and take advice.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Directors Should Do First If You Cannot Pay VAT</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A handful of actions in the first few days do most of the work. They cost nothing, and in our experience they are what separate a payment plan from a court petition.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>File the VAT return on time.</strong> Late filing and late payment are penalised separately, so a filed return you cannot pay is far better than filing nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the actual amount due.</strong> Confirm the figure, and any adjustments or corrections, before you negotiate anything.</li>\n<li><strong>Work out what the company can afford.</strong> Set a realistic monthly figure from your cash flow over the next quarter, not just today's bank balance.</li>\n<li><strong>Contact HMRC before enforcement starts.</strong> Phone the Business Payment Support Service on 0300 200 3835. Early contact keeps every option open.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep records of cash flow and decisions.</strong> If the company's solvency is later questioned, notes made at the time protect you.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not promise payments the company cannot make.</strong> A broken arrangement is harder to renegotiate than an honest, smaller one.</li>\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can You Get a VAT Time to Pay Arrangement?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A <a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">Time to Pay arrangement</a> is HMRC's formal instalment plan for tax it is owed. In plain terms, you pay the VAT off in monthly chunks instead of one lump. For most directors it is the single most effective tool available, and worth pursuing before any formal insolvency route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Setting up a plan online.</strong> Some companies can set up a VAT payment plan online without calling HMRC.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Eligibility depends on HMRC's current conditions, which generally require that you have filed your outstanding VAT returns, owe no more than the self-serve limit, are within a set window of the payment deadline, have no other HMRC payment plans or debts, and can clear the balance within the maximum term.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Check the current thresholds on the VAT payment plan service before relying on them, because they change. Above those limits, or once enforcement has started, you arrange Time to Pay by contacting HMRC directly through the Business Payment Support Service on 0300 200 3835.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>What HMRC will ask for.</strong> Have these ready before you call. HMRC respond far better to a specific, sustainable proposal than to a request for unspecified patience.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>the VAT amount owed;</li>\n<li>why the company cannot pay;</li>\n<li>what the company can afford each month;</li>\n<li>current and forecast cash flow;</li>\n<li>any other tax debts;</li>\n<li>whether future VAT returns can be paid on time.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC also expects you to reduce the debt as far as you reasonably can first, for example by releasing assets such as stock or vehicles, and it may ask directors to put in personal funds or arrange lending before it agrees a plan. Go in expecting that conversation.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Acceptance is far more likely if you are up to date on PAYE, <a href=\"/hmrc/problems-paying-corporation-tax-hmrc/\">Corporation Tax</a>, and current VAT returns. Behind on everything at once, and a plan is much harder to win.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-answer\">\n<p class=\"cd-answer__label\"><strong>Before you call HMRC, have your offer ready</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>the monthly amount you can commit to;</li>\n<li>the date of your first payment;</li>\n<li>a short, honest reason for the arrears;</li>\n<li>how you will keep future VAT returns paid on time.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A specific, affordable offer lands far better with HMRC than a vague request for more time, and putting one together is something we help directors with every week.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>One thing we always flag: a plan can wrap up the VAT, penalties and interest together, and it can reduce or remove the penalties, but interest keeps running on the unpaid balance until it clears. Miss a payment and HMRC can cancel the arrangement and charge the penalties as if it had never existed, so only agree to what you can genuinely keep to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>If Time to Pay is refused.</strong> HMRC can decline a plan, or agree one and then cancel it if you miss an instalment or fall behind on a current return. If that happens, they may press for faster payment or move to recovery action.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A refusal also reframes the question. The directors we help who get a flat no are usually looking at a solvency problem rather than a timing one. That is the point to take advice, not to make a second offer you cannot keep.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">VAT Penalties and Interest If You Pay Late</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Late VAT carries two separate charges: a late payment penalty and late payment interest. The penalty is banded by how late you are, and a Time to Pay request made in time can stop it rising or prevent it altogether. Interest runs from the first overdue day regardless.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>Up to 15 days late.</strong> There is no late payment penalty if you pay the VAT, or agree a Time to Pay arrangement, within 15 days of the due date. Late payment interest still applies from the first day the payment is overdue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>16 to 30 days late.</strong> From day 16 a first late payment penalty applies, calculated at 3% of the VAT still owed at day 15. Requesting Time to Pay within this window can stop the penalty rising further.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>31 days or more late.</strong> If the VAT is still unpaid after day 30, the first penalty rises to 6% in total: 3% of the balance owed at day 15 plus 3% of the balance owed at day 30. From day 31 a second penalty then builds up daily, at an annual rate of 10% on whatever remains outstanding, until the debt is cleared. HMRC enforcement risk becomes serious at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>How late the VAT is</th><th>Penalty position</th><th>Interest position</th><th>What the director should do</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Up to 15 days</td><td>No late payment penalty if you pay or agree Time to Pay in this window</td><td>Interest accrues from the first overdue day</td><td>Pay, or agree Time to Pay now</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16 to 30 days</td><td>First penalty: 3% of the VAT owed at day 15</td><td>Interest still accruing daily</td><td>Request Time to Pay to stop it rising</td></tr>\n<tr><td>31 days or more</td><td>First penalty 6% total (3% at day 15 plus 3% at day 30), then a second penalty at 10% per year, accruing daily</td><td>Interest still accruing daily</td><td>Take advice; agree Time to Pay or a formal route</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The exact penalty and interest rates are set by HMRC and change over time. Interest is charged at the Bank of England base rate plus 4% per year. Confirm the current figures on GOV.UK before you rely on them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong>A quick example.</strong> Say a company owes &pound;20,000 in VAT and pays 40 days late. Nothing is charged for the first 15 days. At day 15 a first penalty of 3% (&pound;600) applies, and as it is still unpaid at day 30 another 3% (&pound;600) is added: &pound;1,200 in penalties.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>From day 31 a second penalty starts building daily at 10% a year on what is left, and interest has been running on the &pound;20,000 since day one. We show directors this maths for one reason: paying sooner shrinks all three charges at once.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Happens If You Still Do Not Pay VAT?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If nothing is in place, HMRC's response follows a predictable path, and we see the same sequence play out again and again. The table below is what it looks like in practice, and where each stage sits on the risk scale.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it means</th><th>Director action</th><th>Risk level</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>VAT payment missed</td><td>The debt is live from the day after the deadline; HMRC's system flags it automatically</td><td>Contact HMRC now</td><td>Low, but rising</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reminder or demand</td><td>A penalty notice, then formal demand letters as the tone shifts to enforcement</td><td>Respond and propose Time to Pay</td><td>Medium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Debt management contact</td><td>The debt is referred to HMRC's debt management team, or to a collection agency acting for them</td><td>Engage and agree a plan</td><td>Medium to high</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Payment plan discussion</td><td>HMRC may still agree Time to Pay if the business is viable</td><td>Put forward a realistic offer</td><td>Reducible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enforcement or recovery</td><td>Enforcement agents under Taking Control of Goods (HMRC can seize company assets), or direct recovery from the company's bank account</td><td>Take advice the same week</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Statutory demand</td><td>A formal demand for a debt over &pound;750. If the company does not pay, agree terms, or dispute it within 21 days, the creditor may use this as evidence the company cannot pay its debts</td><td>Take advice immediately</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Winding-up petition</td><td>HMRC asks the court to shut the company down; HMRC is a frequent petitioning creditor in compulsory liquidation cases</td><td>Urgent insolvency advice</td><td>Severe</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The trigger for a <a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">winding-up petition</a> is rarely the size of the debt. It is HMRC's read on whether you intend to pay voluntarily. VAT arrears, like PAYE, signal that you may already have crossed that line. If a <a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/what-is-a-statutory-demand-against-a-company/\">statutory demand</a> arrives, treat it as a deadline, not a warning, and take advice the same week.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can Unpaid VAT Lead to Insolvency?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>In our experience, unpaid VAT is often the first hard evidence that a company is insolvent, and HMRC treat it that way. The money was never really yours: you collected it from your customers to pass on. Owing it is a different kind of signal from owing a supplier.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If the company cannot pay its debts as they fall due, including VAT, it meets the cash-flow test for insolvency. VAT debt rarely stands alone, and usually sits alongside PAYE, Corporation Tax, supplier arrears or loan arrears.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since December 2020, VAT is a secondary preferential debt, which means HMRC gets paid ahead of banks holding a floating charge and ahead of ordinary unsecured creditors in the <a href=\"/liquidation/which-creditors-get-paid-first/\">order creditors get paid</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC now recover more from a liquidation than they used to, so they are less patient and quicker to petition. Where there is no realistic route to catch up, that is the point to take formal insolvency advice.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-warning\">\n<p class=\"cd-warning__label\"><strong>Signs VAT debt may now be an insolvency issue</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The company cannot keep up with current VAT while repaying old VAT.</li>\n<li>HMRC has refused or cancelled a Time to Pay arrangement.</li>\n<li>Other creditors are also overdue.</li>\n<li>The company is using new VAT receipts to cover old debts.</li>\n<li>Cash-flow forecasts show no realistic recovery.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Directors Risk When VAT Is Unpaid</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>First, the reassuring part: limited-company VAT is normally a company debt, not a personal one. Director conduct only becomes relevant where the company is insolvent, or close to it, and the exposure then usually comes through wrongful trading rather than a personal VAT bill.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>HMRC can issue a personal liability notice for VAT, but that is reserved for deliberate default or evasion, not for a company that simply ran short of cash.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most of the damage we see was not caused by the original bill. It was caused by what the director did, or failed to do, in the weeks after it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Continuing to trade when the company cannot pay its debts.</strong> If the VAT debt grows while the company is insolvent, a liquidator can seek a personal contribution under section 214. See <a href=\"/liquidation/what-happens-to-directors-in-liquidation/\">what happens to directors in liquidation</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Paying some creditors while ignoring HMRC.</strong> Preferring other creditors once insolvency is likely can be unwound and counted against you.</li>\n<li><strong>Using VAT money to fund ongoing losses.</strong> Spending VAT you have collected to prop up a loss-making company deepens both the debt and the exposure.</li>\n<li><strong>Making unrealistic promises to HMRC.</strong> A plan you breach is worse than a smaller one you honour, because a broken arrangement is harder to renegotiate.</li>\n<li><strong>Failing to keep proper board or cash-flow records.</strong> Without notes made at the time, it is harder to show your decisions were reasonable.</li>\n<li><strong>Taking dividends or repayments when the company is insolvent.</strong> Dividends drawn from a company that cannot pay its debts can be reclaimed, and directors' loans called in.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These matter beyond the debt itself. This is also where director disqualification can start: the Insolvency Service looks at conduct like trading on when the company cannot pay, failing to keep proper records, not paying tax that is owed, or taking company money for personal use. Clean records and early engagement are what keep you on the right side of that line.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Options If the Company Still Cannot Pay VAT</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If a standard Time to Pay arrangement is not enough on its own, a few routes remain. Which one fits depends on whether the business is fundamentally viable, and that is the first thing we look at with you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Option</th><th>When it fits</th><th>Main risk</th><th>Next step</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Renegotiate Time to Pay</td><td>The company remains viable and can afford revised payments</td><td>HMRC may decline and resume enforcement</td><td>Call HMRC before you miss an instalment, not after</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short-term VAT funding</td><td>The VAT problem is temporary and repayment is realistic</td><td>Deepens the hole if the business is not viable</td><td>Check the borrowing is serviceable first</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">Company Voluntary Arrangement</a></td><td>A viable company that needs a formal creditor repayment deal</td><td>Requires creditor approval and disciplined delivery</td><td>Take advice from a licensed insolvency practitioner</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a></td><td>The company cannot recover</td><td>Ends the company; director conduct is reviewed</td><td>Take advice on an orderly closure</td></tr>\n<tr><td><a href=\"/company-administration/\">Company Administration</a></td><td>There may be a rescue or sale, or serious creditor pressure</td><td>Control passes to an administrator</td><td>Move quickly; options narrow under petition pressure</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-decision\">\n<p class=\"cd-decision__label\"><strong>Which way to turn</strong></p>\n<p>If the business is viable, focus on payment plans, cash flow and controlled repayment. If the business is not viable, stop relying on short-term fixes and get insolvency advice before HMRC decides the outcome for you.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practitioners we work with deal with HMRC VAT debts every day, and a free, confidential conversation will clarify your position before HMRC forces it. Where a rescue is still possible, the earlier you talk to us, the more <a href=\"/liquidation/alternatives-to-company-liquidation/\">options remain open</a>.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"3a8f2c91-7e4d-4b62-9a3e-d1c2f5b8e203\",\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I pay VAT in instalments?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Often, yes, through a Time to Pay arrangement. Call the Business Payment Support Service on 0300 200 3835 with a realistic repayment proposal. Acceptance is more likely if you call before enforcement starts and are current on your other taxes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":1,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eShould I file my VAT return if I cannot pay?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes, always. Late filing and late payment are penalised separately, so filing on time and paying late is less damaging than doing neither. A filed return also shows HMRC you are engaging rather than hiding.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":2,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWill HMRC charge penalties if I cannot pay VAT?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>There is no late payment penalty if you pay or agree Time to Pay within 15 days. From day 16 a first penalty of 3% of the VAT owed at day 15 applies; if still unpaid after day 30 that rises to 6% in total, and a second penalty then accrues daily at 10% a year. Interest also runs from the first overdue day.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":3,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan HMRC close my company for unpaid VAT?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Yes. HMRC can ask the court to wind up your company, meaning shut it down, for any debt over &pound;750. They are a frequent petitioning creditor in compulsory liquidation cases, and VAT debts do not need to be large for them to act.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":4,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan directors be personally liable for unpaid VAT?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Normally VAT is a company debt, not a personal one. Personal exposure usually comes through wrongful trading, where you kept trading while insolvent and the VAT debt grew. HMRC can issue a personal liability notice, but only for deliberate default or evasion. VAT group registrations can carry joint and several liability.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":5,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat happens if HMRC refuses Time to Pay?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>HMRC may press for faster payment or move to recovery action. A refusal usually means the company cannot fund a realistic plan, which is a solvency question rather than a timing one. That is the point to take insolvency advice rather than make a second offer you cannot keep.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":6,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I get business funding to pay VAT?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>Where the business is viable and the shortfall is temporary, short-term funding can clear the VAT and replace an HMRC debt with a commercial one on terms you control. It only makes sense if the underlying business can service the borrowing. Using finance to prop up a company that is not viable usually deepens the hole.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block {\"index\":7,\"theme\":\"#f8f8f8\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\",\"panelTitle\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhen should I speak to an insolvency practitioner?\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"toggleIcon\":\"plus\",\"showOnlyOne\":true} -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"placeholder\":\"Panel content\"} -->\n<p>When the VAT debt is beyond what a realistic Time to Pay plan can clear, when HMRC has refused or cancelled a plan, or when a statutory demand arrives. The earlier the conversation happens, the more options remain open.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-panel-block -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-related\">\n<p class=\"cd-related__label\"><strong>Related Guides</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/time-to-pay-hmrc/\">HMRC Time to Pay</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/problems-paying-corporation-tax-hmrc/\">Can't Pay Corporation Tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/hmrc/cant-pay-paye/\">Can't Pay PAYE</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-cash-flow-problems/what-is-a-statutory-demand-against-a-company/\">Statutory Demands</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/winding-up-petitions/\">Winding-Up Petitions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/company-rescue-solutions/company-voluntary-arrangement/\">Company Voluntary Arrangements</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a></li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>This page is written by the Company Debt editorial team and reviewed by Chris Andersen, a licensed insolvency practitioner regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association. Last reviewed July 2026.</p>\n<p>Statutory references draw on the VAT Act 1994, the Finance Act 2020 (HMRC secondary preferential creditor status for VAT), and the Insolvency Act 1986 (sections 124 and 214), alongside HMRC debt-management, enforcement and director-disqualification guidance. VAT late payment penalty and interest figures reflect HMRC guidance current as of July 2026.</p>\n<p>Company Debt is a commercial insolvency practice. We are not independent of the process described on this page. If you instruct us, we act as your insolvency practitioner. We may also receive a fee where you engage another practitioner through our network.</p>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources and References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1994/23/contents\"><strong>VAT Act 1994</strong>: registration, payment obligations, and the penalty framework</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2020/14/section/98\"><strong>Finance Act 2020, Section 98</strong>: HMRC secondary preferential creditor status for VAT from 1 December 2020</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\"><strong>Insolvency Act 1986</strong>: section 124 (winding-up petition), section 175 (preferential debts), section 214 (wrongful trading)</a> - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/pay-vat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>HMRC, Pay your VAT bill</strong>: deadlines, payment methods, and what to do if you cannot pay</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/difficulties-paying-hmrc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>HMRC, If you cannot pay your tax bill on time</strong>: Time to Pay and payment plans</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-late-payment-penalties-work-if-you-pay-vat-late\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>HMRC, How late payment penalties work if you pay VAT late</strong>: the 3%, 6%, and 10% penalty bands</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/late-payment-interest-if-you-do-not-pay-vat-or-penalties-on-time\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>HMRC, Late payment interest if you do not pay VAT or penalties on time</strong>: interest at the Bank of England base rate plus 4%</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/what-will-happen-if-you-do-not-pay-your-tax-bill\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>HMRC, What will happen if you do not pay your tax bill</strong>: the enforcement sequence, with insolvency as a final course of action</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/debt-management-and-banking/dmbm530170\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>HMRC DMBM530170</strong>: directors and managing officers are normally not personally liable for company debts</a> - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/company-director-disqualification\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Company director disqualification</strong>: unfit conduct including trading while unable to pay and not paying tax owed</a> - gov.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n</article>"
  },
  "/liquidation/how-much-does-liquidation-cost/": {
    "id": 23698,
    "slug": "how-much-does-liquidation-cost",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- TITLE: How Much Does It Cost to Liquidate a Company in the UK? -->\n<!-- POST ID: 23698 / TYPE: pages / AUTHOR: 34 / FM: 70038 / TEMPLATE: templates/take-the-test-template.php -->\n<!-- LINK: https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/liquidation/how-much-does-liquidation-cost/ -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The first question on almost every call is: how much?</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For a straightforward Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation, our practitioner fee is a fixed \u00a33,500 plus VAT, with a further \u00a3500 to \u00a31,500 of external expenses. In practice that means a director usually needs to fund somewhere between \u00a34,000 and \u00a35,000 to get the liquidation under way.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Read that as the funding requirement, not as a guaranteed final cost. Whether a quoted fee covers the whole anticipated case, or only the work needed to get a liquidator appointed, varies between firms and between cases. Establish which one you are being offered before you instruct anyone.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Having no money left in the company does not prevent it entering liquidation. Most directors who reach this page have already worked out that they cannot pay the fee from what is in the account, and there are established routes through that.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are licensed insolvency practitioners, so we have a commercial interest in you instructing an IP. We have set out our fee basis in full anyway, so you can test any quote you are given, including ours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Liquidation Costs at a Glance</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost depends on the route. Statutory fees are fixed by law and are the same wherever you go. Practitioner fees are commercial and vary between firms, which is why the two are separated here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Typical cost</th><th>Fee type</th><th>Who funds it</th><th>When it applies</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation (CVL)</td><td>\u00a33,500 + VAT for a straightforward case, plus \u00a3500 to \u00a31,500 of external expenses. Directors typically fund \u00a34,000 to \u00a35,000 to get under way</td><td>Professional (commercial)</td><td>Company assets or director funding, which may in some cases be supported by an anticipated director redundancy claim</td><td>Company is insolvent and directors act voluntarily</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Members' Voluntary Liquidation (MVL)</td><td>\u00a33,500 + VAT</td><td>Professional (commercial)</td><td>The solvent company's assets</td><td>Company is solvent and closing tax-efficiently</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compulsory liquidation</td><td>\u00a3352 court fee + \u00a32,600 petition deposit, plus the creditor's own legal costs (indicatively \u00a31,500 to \u00a34,000)</td><td>Statutory (fixed), plus the creditor's legal fees</td><td>The petitioning creditor, upfront</td><td>A creditor petitions the court to wind the company up</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Strike-off (DS01)</td><td>\u00a313 online, \u00a318 by paper</td><td>Statutory (fixed)</td><td>The company or its directors</td><td>Company has ceased trading and has no creditors likely to object. Not a substitute for dealing properly with insolvency</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The strike-off fee is the one that misleads people most. \u00a313 sits next to several thousand pounds in that table and looks like an obvious choice. It is not a substitute for dealing with an insolvent company and its creditors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An insolvent company can submit a DS01 application. What it cannot do is rely on it. Creditors, including HMRC, may object where money remains outstanding, and the company can be restored to the register afterwards.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Since 2021 the Insolvency Service has also been able to investigate the conduct of directors of dissolved companies directly. A strike-off that unravels two years later costs considerably more than the liquidation it was meant to avoid.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How Much Does a Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation Cost?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The CVL Fee for a Straightforward Company</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We charge a fixed \u00a33,500 plus VAT for a straightforward CVL: a single-director company with no employees, no leases and a clean set of books. VAT is charged on top, so \u00a33,500 becomes \u00a34,200 payable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quoting a fixed fee means you know the number before you commit, rather than watching it climb as the case goes on. Where a company has staff, a debtor book to chase or equipment to sell, the case is not straightforward and costs more. We say so at the first call and explain exactly what drives the figure higher.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A low starting price is difficult to evaluate unless the firm has first asked about your creditors, your employees, your records, your assets and any director-related balances. Ask for the assumptions behind the figure, the exclusions, and the circumstances in which it could change.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"cd-callout cd-calloutu002du002dcost-reality\"} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-group cd-callout cd-callout--cost-reality\">\n  <p class=\"cd-callout__label\">Before You Commit</p>\n  <p class=\"cd-callout__title\">What You Need to Pay Before the Liquidation Starts</p>\n  <!-- wp:paragraph -->\n  <p>In addition to the practitioner's fee, the case may incur an insolvency bond, required Gazette notices, postage and other external expenses. For a straightforward Company Debt CVL, these normally total between \u00a3500 and \u00a31,500.</p>\n  <!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n  <!-- wp:paragraph -->\n  <p>Your quotation should identify these separately and state whether they are included in the amount you must fund. Compare quotes on the practitioner's fee alone and the figure you actually have to find can be materially higher than the one you agreed, before VAT is even added.</p>\n  <!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Pushes a CVL Above the Standard Fee</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Complexity drives the fee, not the size of the debt. A company with nothing in the bank, fifty creditors and an overdrawn loan account costs more to liquidate than a company with \u00a3100,000 in the account and two suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The fee reflects the work required to verify claims, reconstruct records and complete the investigation, not the balance sitting in the account. Directors find that counter-intuitive, and it is worth naming rather than pretending the logic is obvious.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Does a Liquidation Quote Cover?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A liquidation has two cost stages, and the answer depends on which one a firm is pricing. Some quote a fixed fee for the whole anticipated case. Others price the work needed to get a liquidator appointed, with remuneration for the rest approved separately and drawn from what the liquidation recovers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>The work</th><th>Stage</th><th>How it is normally paid</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Advising the board, preparing the Statement of Affairs, convening the shareholders' resolution and the creditors' decision procedure</td><td>Pre-appointment</td><td>Funded upfront by the company or the director. This is the work a quoted fee most reliably refers to.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Realising assets: chasing debtors, selling stock and equipment, collecting bank balances</td><td>Post-appointment</td><td>Liquidator's remuneration. May sit inside a fixed fee agreed for the whole case, or be approved separately and drawn from realisations. Ask which.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adjudicating creditor claims and distributing in the statutory order of priority</td><td>Post-appointment</td><td>As above.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Investigating the directors' conduct and reporting to the Insolvency Service</td><td>Post-appointment</td><td>As above. Required in an insolvent liquidation, and not optional.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Statutory returns to Companies House and the Insolvency Service, and final dissolution</td><td>Post-appointment</td><td>As above.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On straightforward cases, we normally agree a fixed fee covering the anticipated case. Additional remuneration would only arise if the scope changed materially, and would need to be explained and approved through the appropriate process.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Under the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, post-appointment remuneration can be fixed as a set amount, charged on a time-cost basis, taken as a percentage of assets realised, or a combination. The basis must be approved, normally by creditors or a liquidation committee, and in some cases by the court.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Statement of Insolvency Practice 9 requires the practitioner to explain the proposed basis, the work expected and the likely return to creditors before that approval is sought.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Before appointing a firm, establish exactly which work the fee covers: the appointment only, or the whole expected case. The two behave very differently if the case turns out to be more complicated than anyone expected.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of that is the same thing as a debt you owe. The company's own liabilities are paid from what the liquidation recovers, not from the practitioner's fee. And the largest sum a director ends up paying is frequently not a liquidation cost at all: it is the personal guarantee signed on the lease four years ago. No quote will ever include it, and no practitioner can make it go away.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Compare Liquidation Quotes</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>You should get more than one quote, and you should compare them on more than the headline number. Ask every practitioner the same eight questions, and get the answers in writing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>Is this a fixed fee or an estimate?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>Does it cover the pre-appointment work, or only the appointment itself?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>What basis of post-appointment remuneration will you propose to creditors?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>Is VAT included in the figure you have quoted me?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>Which external expenses are included, and which will be billed on top?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>What specifically would trigger an additional charge?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>Is my director's loan account being dealt with separately from your fee?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li>What has to be paid before you will accept the appointment?</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A practitioner who answers all eight without hesitation is quoting you a real number. One who cannot is quoting you a starting position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Changes the Cost of Liquidating a Company?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Why it adds cost</th><th>What you can do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Quality of the records</td><td>Incomplete books mean the liquidator rebuilds the company's position from bank statements and third-party records, and that rebuilding is billed.</td><td>The most useful thing on this list. The director who arrives with the last two years reconciled pays less than the one who arrives with a carrier bag.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Number of creditors</td><td>More correspondence, more proofs of debt to adjudicate, and a higher chance of a creditor requisitioning a meeting or forming a committee.</td><td>Little, but have the ledger complete and the addresses current.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Employees</td><td>Redundancy notifications, employee claims to the Redundancy Payments Service, and TUPE considerations if any part of the business is sold on.</td><td>Have payroll records and contracts to hand.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Assets and the debtor book</td><td>Property, vehicles on hire purchase, retention-of-title stock and disputed debtor balances all take longer to realise than a bank account and a laptop.</td><td>List them honestly and early. Concealment costs far more than disclosure.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leases and financed equipment</td><td>Ongoing lease liabilities and hire-purchase agreements need separate handling and often separate advice.</td><td>Gather the agreements before the first meeting.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overdrawn director's loan account</td><td>The liquidator must pursue it, which is work. It is also a sum you will be asked to repay.</td><td>Raise it yourself at the first call. It will be found regardless.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disputed transactions</td><td>Where possible preferences or transactions at undervalue are identified, the investigation lengthens and the conduct report becomes more detailed.</td><td>Take advice before making any further payments to connected parties.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Litigation</td><td>If a creditor challenges the liquidator's decisions, or the liquidator must go to court to recover an asset, legal costs escalate and come out of the estate.</td><td>Largely outside your control once it starts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overseas assets</td><td>Cross-border realisation is slow, and slow is expensive.</td><td>Flag them at the outset so the fee reflects reality.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"cd-key-takeaway\"} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-group cd-key-takeaway\">\n  <p class=\"cd-key-takeaway__label\">Key Takeaway</p>\n  <!-- wp:paragraph -->\n  <p>Acting voluntarily is usually cheaper for the estate than a compulsory liquidation forced by a creditor. It gives directors greater control over timing and allows the company to nominate a practitioner, although creditors retain the right to nominate an alternative.</p>\n  <!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n  <!-- wp:paragraph -->\n  <p>What it does not do is soften the scrutiny of your conduct. A report on the directors' conduct is required in an insolvent liquidation, whether the company enters a CVL or is wound up by the court, and the practitioner appointed is independent of you.</p>\n  <!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who Pays for Company Liquidation?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two different things get muddled together here, and separating them is the fastest way to understand your actual exposure.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The cost of the liquidation.</strong> In a CVL this comes from the company's assets, or from director funding where there are none. In a compulsory liquidation the petitioning creditor pays the court fee and deposit upfront, and where the company has sufficient assets, allowable petition costs and the Official Receiver's fees may ultimately be met from the estate.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>What you may choose to contribute.</strong> Where the company cannot fund the process, a director often funds it personally. This is a commercial decision, not a liability, and it buys greater control over timing and the ability to nominate a practitioner.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Personal and Director-Related Liabilities That Are Separate From the Fee</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These are not liquidation costs, they are not owed to the practitioner, and no quote will include them. They are also the sums that hurt most, so it is worth being precise about who is owed what.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Liability</th><th>Who is owed</th><th>What happens in the liquidation</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Overdrawn director's loan account</td><td>The company</td><td>Money you owe the company. The liquidator will normally pursue repayment for the benefit of creditors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personal guarantee</td><td>A third party: a lender, landlord or supplier</td><td>A contingent liability of yours, not the company's. It survives the liquidation and the creditor can pursue you personally.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Preference or transaction at undervalue</td><td>The company, if a claim succeeds</td><td>Not a debt today. A claim the liquidator may bring against you following investigation, if payments or transfers are found to be recoverable.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Directors who cannot afford to liquidate are usually the ones who most need to. Drifting on, trading while insolvent, or attempting an unlawful strike-off all carry far higher personal risk than the fee being avoided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Director Redundancy: A Funding Route Many Directors Miss</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A director who was also genuinely employed by the company may be able to claim redundancy pay, unpaid wages, holiday pay and notice pay from the Redundancy Payments Service. It is paid from the National Insurance Fund rather than out of company assets, and it is the same statutory scheme that protects your staff.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The key question is whether you were genuinely employed by the company. Being a director does not create the entitlement, because a director is an office-holder who may or may not also be an employee.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Redundancy Payments Service assesses whether a genuine employment relationship existed, looking at the working relationship as a whole: your duties, your hours, how you were paid, PAYE records, and whether there was a contract of employment, which may be written or implied by conduct.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two years of continuous employment is the usual qualifying threshold. Paying yourself only in dividends, with no employment contract and no PAYE record, makes a successful claim considerably less likely, because there is little evidence of employment to point to. It is an evidential problem rather than an automatic bar, but it is a serious one and it catches a lot of one-person consultancies.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What a successful claim is worth depends on your age, your length of service and your weekly pay, capped at \u00a3751 a week from 6 April 2026, giving a statutory redundancy maximum of \u00a322,530. The <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/calculate-your-redundancy-pay\">gov.uk calculator</a> will give you your own figure in about two minutes. We assess eligibility at the first call and tell you honestly if we think a claim will not stand up.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Where a director does qualify, the claim is commonly used to fund the CVL. The mechanics vary between firms, so ask exactly how yours would work: whether the claim is assigned, whether funding is advanced against it, what happens if the Redundancy Payments Service reduces or refuses it, and whether any charge is deducted.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Do not assume the money will be available until the claim has been accepted.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What If You Cannot Afford to Liquidate the Company?</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Beyond the redundancy claim above, three routes remain, and most directors who think they are stuck are on one of them without knowing it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Asset realisations.</strong> Companies that feel penniless often are not. The van on the drive, the unbilled work, the debtor who has been dodging you since March, the stock in the lock-up: all of it has realisable value, and all of it can fund the process.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Instalments.</strong> Some practitioners, ourselves included, will agree staged payment for a director-funded CVL where the circumstances warrant it. Ask at the first call, not after the quote lands.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>The fallback.</strong> If no voluntary solution is funded, continued creditor action may ultimately lead to a winding-up petition and compulsory liquidation. The petitioning creditor pays the \u00a3352 court fee and the \u00a32,600 deposit upfront. What it costs you is control: the timing, the chance to get records in order, and the ability to nominate a practitioner.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Will HMRC Find Out If I Start Asking Questions?</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is the fear that keeps directors sitting on an insolvent company for another six months, so it deserves a plain answer. An initial consultation with an insolvency practitioner is confidential. It does not notify HMRC, does not itself trigger an investigation, and involves no filing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nothing is submitted anywhere unless you instruct us and your shareholders resolve to wind the company up, and that decision remains yours.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The instinct to stay under the radar is understandable, and it tends to work against you. HMRC is generally already aware of what it is owed. A liquidator's conduct report considers what the directors did once the company's position became apparent, so taking advice and acting on it is easier to evidence than a further six months of silence.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Striking the company off is not a way round any of this either. Our full guide on <a href=\"/liquidation/cant-afford-to-liquidate/\">what to do when you cannot afford to liquidate</a> sets out every option in detail.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Common Liquidation Cost Misunderstandings</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>What directors assume</th><th>The position</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>\"It should cost less because the company has no money.\"</td><td>Cost is driven by complexity, not the bank balance. Fifty creditors and a conduct issue cost more to deal with than \u00a3100,000 in the account and two suppliers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"I can just shop around for the cheapest liquidator.\"</td><td>Compare quotes, but the cheapest headline is not the best value. A low figure that escalates on a time-cost basis can cost more than a higher fixed fee for the whole case.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"The creditors pay for the liquidation.\"</td><td>Not directly. Liquidation costs are normally paid from company assets, reducing what remains available for creditors.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Liquidation Costs You, and What Waiting Costs You</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A straightforward CVL usually needs \u00a34,000 to \u00a35,000 of funding to get under way. If you were genuinely employed by the company on PAYE, a redundancy claim may cover part or all of that, and it is worth establishing this week rather than assuming you cannot afford it. Any remaining assets fund the rest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Waiting can increase both the financial damage and the risks for directors. A creditor petitions, control of the timing passes to the court, the Official Receiver takes the case rather than a practitioner the company nominated, and your records are examined as you left them. Directors generally retain more options by acting before a creditor petitions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/call-to-action-block {\"blockID\":\"a1b2c3d4-23698-pre-faq\",\"ub_call_to_action_headline_text\":\"\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet a Quick and Easy Liquidation Quote\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_content_text\":\"Talk to a licensed IP about your liquidation cost and funding\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\u2713 100% Confidential | \u2713 No Obligation | \u2713 Licensed and Regulated Advice\\u003c/strong\\u003e\",\"ub_cta_button_text\":\"Get a Quote\",\"headColor\":\"#002856\",\"contentFontSize\":17,\"buttonColor\":\"#ff6600\",\"buttonTextColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"buttonWidth\":300,\"ctaBackgroundColor\":\"#e7f6fd\",\"ctaBorderColor\":\"#ffffff\",\"url\":\"https://comdebstage.wpengine.com/quick-quote/\",\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"},\"margin\":{\"top\":\"0\",\"bottom\":\"0\",\"left\":\"0\",\"right\":\"0\"}} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions About Liquidation Costs</h2>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle wp-block-ub-content-toggle-block\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-block-faq-23698\" data-mobilecollapse=\"false\" data-desktopcollapse=\"true\" data-preventcollapse=\"false\" data-showonlyone=\"false\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-23698-0\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-23698\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-23698\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What is the cheapest way to liquidate a company?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-0-faq-23698\">\n<p>For an insolvent company, a CVL funded by a director redundancy claim is often the cheapest route for the director, where they qualify and the claim is large enough to meet the fee. Whether it does depends on your age, length of service and weekly pay, and the claim is assessed independently by the Redundancy Payments Service.</p>\n<p>If you do not qualify, the cheapest safe option is a fixed-fee CVL from a licensed practitioner.</p>\n<p>Strike-off costs \u00a313 online, but it is not a cheap liquidation. It is a different thing entirely. An insolvent company can submit an application, but creditors can object, the company can be restored to the register afterwards, and the Insolvency Service can investigate the conduct of directors of dissolved companies.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-23698-1\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-23698\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-23698\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Can I pay the liquidator in instalments?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-1-faq-23698\">\n<p>Some practitioners offer staged payment for director-funded CVLs, and we do where the circumstances warrant it. The fee has to be agreed and committed before the process begins, because the liquidator cannot start work without certainty of funding. Ask about payment terms at the first consultation rather than after the quote lands.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-23698-2\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-23698\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-23698\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What happens if the final cost exceeds the estimate?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-2-faq-23698\">\n<p>It depends on the basis you agreed. A fixed fee for the whole anticipated case holds even if it proves harder than expected, which is the point of paying for one. Additional remuneration would only arise if the scope changed materially, and would have to be explained and approved through the appropriate process.</p>\n<p>On a time-cost basis, the practitioner must seek approval for their remuneration, normally from creditors, and Statement of Insolvency Practice 9 requires them to explain the work done and the fees drawn. This is why question three on the comparison checklist matters: establish the fee basis before you appoint.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-23698-3\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-23698\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-23698\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>What happens if the company has no assets and I cannot pay personally?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-3-faq-23698\">\n<p>Check the director redundancy route first, if you were genuinely employed by the company. For directors who qualify, it is the route that most often resolves the funding problem.</p>\n<p>If you do not qualify and there is genuinely nothing to realise, continued creditor action may ultimately lead to a winding-up petition and compulsory liquidation. That does not cost you the practitioner's fee, but it removes your control over timing, records and the nomination of a practitioner. Our guide on <a href=\"/liquidation/cant-afford-to-liquidate/\">what to do when you cannot afford to liquidate</a> covers every option.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-23698-4\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-23698\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-23698\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Do I have to pay VAT on the liquidator's fee?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-4-faq-23698\">\n<p>Yes. Practitioner fees carry VAT at the standard rate of 20%. Our \u00a33,500 CVL fee plus VAT means \u00a34,200 payable. Always confirm whether a quote includes or excludes VAT before committing, because a quote that omits it is understating the real figure by a fifth.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion\" style=\"border-color: #f1f1f1\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-block-faq-23698-5\">\n\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title-wrap\" style=\"background-color: #f1f1f1\" aria-controls=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-23698\">\n\t\t<p class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-title ub-content-toggle-title-faq-23698\" style=\"color: #000000\"><strong>Is compulsory liquidation really free for the director?</strong></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-toggle-wrap right\" style=\"color: #000000\"><span class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-state-indicator wp-block-ub-chevron-down\"></span></div>\n\t</div>\n\t<div role=\"region\" aria-expanded=\"false\" class=\"wp-block-ub-content-toggle-accordion-content-wrap ub-hide\" id=\"ub-content-toggle-panel-5-faq-23698\">\n<p>The director does not pay the practitioner's fee. The petitioning creditor pays the \u00a3352 court fee and the \u00a32,600 deposit upfront, and where the company has sufficient assets, allowable petition costs and the Official Receiver's fees may ultimately be met from the estate.</p>\n<p>But compulsory liquidation usually costs the estate more in total and leaves less for creditors. It also removes your control over timing, over the chance to get records in order, and over the nomination of a practitioner. A report on the directors' conduct is required either way: that is a statutory requirement in an insolvent liquidation, however the company gets there.</p>\n\t</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->\n\n<!-- wp:ub/content-toggle-block {\"blockID\":\"faq-asset-loader-23698\",\"theme\":\"#f1f1f1\",\"collapsed\":true,\"titleColor\":\"#000000\"} -->\n<!-- /wp:ub/content-toggle-block -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Guides</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/creditors-voluntary-liquidation/\">Creditors' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: the full CVL process, timeline, and what directors need to prepare.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/cant-afford-to-liquidate/\">What to Do When You Cannot Afford to Liquidate</a>: every funding option, in detail.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/compulsory-liquidation/\">Compulsory Liquidation</a>: what happens once a creditor petitions the court.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/members-voluntary-liquidation/\">Members' Voluntary Liquidation</a>: the solvent route, and when it is worth the fee.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/liquidation/company-strike-off-and-dissolution/\">Company Strike-Off and Dissolution</a>: when the DS01 route is lawful, and when it goes badly wrong.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n<!-- wp:list-item --><li><a href=\"/advice/overdrawn-directors-loan-accounts/\">Overdrawn Directors' Loan Accounts</a>: why this is a debt you owe, not a fee you pay.</li><!-- /wp:list-item -->\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:html -->\n<aside class=\"cd-methodology\">\n<p class=\"cd-methodology__label\"><strong>Methodology and Disclosure</strong></p>\n<p>Company Debt is a firm of licensed insolvency practitioners regulated by the Insolvency Practitioners Association. If you instruct us, we act as your IP and our fees are paid from company assets, director funding, or an accepted director redundancy claim. This page is written from that position and is not independent legal advice.</p>\n<p>Every figure on this page falls into one of three categories, and we have set out which is which so you can weigh them accordingly.</p>\n<p><strong>Our own pricing (commercial, and will differ between firms).</strong> Our fixed \u00a33,500 plus VAT fee for a straightforward CVL, the same \u00a33,500 plus VAT for an MVL, and the \u00a3500 to \u00a31,500 range of external expenses, which includes an insolvency bond typically between \u00a3200 and \u00a3400.</p>\n<p>These are Company Debt's prices for work in England and Wales, current as of July 2026. They are not a market survey, and complex cases are quoted higher.</p>\n<p><strong>Observed ranges (indicative, not ours to set).</strong> The \u00a31,500 to \u00a34,000 for a petitioning creditor's own legal costs is an indicative range based on what we see in practice. It is charged by that creditor's solicitors, not by us, and it varies considerably.</p>\n<p><strong>Statutory figures (fixed by law, sourced below).</strong> The winding-up petition court fee (\u00a3352) comes from the HMCTS civil court fees schedule (EX50). The \u00a32,600 petition deposit comes from the Insolvency Proceedings (Fees) Order 2016 as amended.</p>\n<p>The DS01 strike-off fees (\u00a313 online, \u00a318 paper) come from the current Companies House schedule. The \u00a3751 weekly redundancy cap and the \u00a322,530 statutory maximum are set by the Employment Rights (Increase of Limits) Order 2026, effective 6 April 2026.</p>\n<p>Where we describe what a redundancy claim is likely to be worth, or whether it will cover a CVL, we are describing what we see in the cases we handle. Every claim is assessed independently by the Redundancy Payments Service on its own evidence, and we cannot guarantee an outcome.</p>\n<p>We do not publish an average director redundancy claim figure. The amount depends entirely on your age, length of service and weekly pay, and any single average would mislead more directors than it helped.</p>\n<p>Last reviewed by Chris Andersen, Licensed Insolvency Practitioner (IPA regulated), July 2026.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"cd-sources\">\n<p class=\"cd-sources__label\"><strong>Sources &amp; References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/fees-in-the-civil-and-family-courts-main-fees-ex50/civil-court-fees-ex50\">HMCTS civil court fees (EX50)</a> &mdash; the \u00a3352 fee for entering a winding-up petition - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/692\">Insolvency Proceedings (Fees) Order 2016</a> &mdash; the \u00a32,600 winding-up petition deposit - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/companies-house-fees/companies-house-fees\">Companies House fees</a> &mdash; voluntary strike-off (DS01) at \u00a313 online and \u00a318 by paper - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1024/contents/made\">Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016, Part 18</a> &mdash; the basis on which liquidator remuneration is fixed and approved - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1986/45/contents\">Insolvency Act 1986, sections 100 and 176ZA</a> &mdash; CVL liquidator appointment and the priority of liquidation expenses - legislation.gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/calculate-your-redundancy-pay\">GOV.UK redundancy pay calculator</a> &mdash; work out your own statutory entitlement - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/strike-off-your-company-from-companies-register\">GOV.UK: strike off your company from the register</a> &mdash; eligibility for voluntary strike-off and creditor objections - gov.uk</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://insolvency-practitioners.org.uk/\">Insolvency Practitioners Association</a> &mdash; verify that the practitioner quoting you is licensed - insolvency-practitioners.org.uk</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<!-- /wp:html -->"
  },
  "/case-studies-hub/": {
    "id": 23471,
    "slug": "case-studies-hub",
    "type": "page",
    "endpoint": "pages",
    "raw": "<!-- wp:acf/hub-box {\"name\":\"acf/hub-box\",\"data\":{\"hub_boxes\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes\":\"field_63db7922eff54\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_0_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_0_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_0_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_0_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_0_heading\":\"Our Sector Specific Experience\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_0_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_0_style\":\"description\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_0_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_0_content\":\"In this section, we showcase a small selection of sector-specific case studies. You can also read our testimonials to hear the directors themselves speaking about their experience.\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_0_content\":\"field_63e0c6153a571\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_0_list_item\":0,\"hub_boxes_boxes_1_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_1_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_1_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_1_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_1_heading\":\"Travel Sector\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_1_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_1_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_1_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_1_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_1_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_1_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_1_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_heading\":\"Property \\u0026 Construction\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_list_item_0_title\":\"Subcontractor Agency\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_list_item_0_title\":\"field_63db79a9eff59\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_list_item_0_url\":\"/case-studies-hub/\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_list_item_0_url\":\"field_63db79b9eff5a\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_2_list_item\":1,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_2_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_3_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_3_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_3_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_3_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_3_heading\":\"Professional Services\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_3_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_3_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_3_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_3_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_3_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_3_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_3_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_4_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_4_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_4_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_4_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_4_heading\":\"Removals\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_4_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_4_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_4_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_4_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_4_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_4_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_4_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_heading\":\"Engineering\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_list_item_0_title\":\"Creditor Negotiations\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_list_item_0_title\":\"field_63db79a9eff59\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_list_item_0_url\":\"/insolvency/creditor-negotiations/\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_list_item_0_url\":\"field_63db79b9eff5a\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_5_list_item\":1,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_5_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_6_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_6_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_6_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_6_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_6_heading\":\"Courier\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_6_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_6_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_6_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_6_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_6_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_6_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_6_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_7_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_7_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_7_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_7_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_7_heading\":\"Retail\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_7_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_7_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_7_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_7_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_7_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_7_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_7_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_8_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_8_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_8_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_8_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_8_heading\":\"Education\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_8_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_8_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_8_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_8_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_8_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_8_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_8_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_9_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_9_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_9_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_9_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_9_heading\":\"Logistics\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_9_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_9_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_9_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_9_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_9_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_9_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_9_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_10_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_10_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_10_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_10_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_10_heading\":\"Information Technology\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_10_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_10_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_10_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_10_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_10_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_10_list_item\":0,\"_hub_boxes_boxes_10_list_item\":\"field_63db7994eff58\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"background\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_bcg_image_or_icon\":\"field_63db793eeff55\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_icon\":\"\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_icon\":\"field_63db796beff56\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_heading\":\"Restaurants \\u0026 Leisure\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_heading\":\"field_63db7984eff57\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_style\":\"list\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_style\":\"field_63e0c5d43a570\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_view_all\":\"0\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_view_all\":\"field_63db79c8eff5b\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_list_item_0_title\":\"Chinese Takeaway\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_list_item_0_title\":\"field_63db79a9eff59\",\"hub_boxes_boxes_11_list_item_0_url\":\"/case-studies/chinese-takeaway/\",\"_hub_boxes_boxes_11_list_item_0_url\":\"field_63db79b9eff5a\",\"hub_boxes_boxe